# Changelog

Changes in reverse chronological order.

## Many code bodies per isolate

Making `db` awaitable took the *thread* out of a host call. This takes the *isolate* out of a run:
one isolate now serves hundreds of code bodies at the same time, and the number of `run_js_code`
requests this server can answer at once stopped being the size of the isolate pool.

A run used to be what the isolate was doing. It is now an **entry in a table**, keyed by a token
minted per run — 128 bits from the operating system, bound as a `const` in that run's own function
scope, which the prelude closes over and sends with every plan. Random rather than an index on
purpose: two resident runs may carry different authority (`db.asUser()` delegates to *this*
event's caller), so a body that could name another run's token could ask the database questions in
that caller's name. A token that names nothing is a named error — *"this database call belongs to a
code run that has already finished"* — which is also what an abandoned continuation resuming after
its run was answered now gets, in place of the unwind-before-the-next-job dance the previous phase
needed.

Which moves where a run ends. `execute_script` **starts** one — the body is an async function, so
it returns at the first `await` — and the answer is delivered by a **completion op**, `__scDone`
with the JSON text of the result or `__scFail` with `e.stack`, once the event loop has carried the
body to it. The run's oneshot lives in its table entry rather than in the worker's loop, because
the loop no longer waits for one run. A syntax error still surfaces synchronously from the call.

The worker is one `block_on` around a loop that waits on three things at once: the isolate's
**event loop**, which advances every resident run; the **job channel**, which admits new ones
without waiting for the resident ones to finish; and a **timer** at the next deadline, which is
what notices a run's wall clock while every run is suspended and nothing is executing. It parks on
the channel when nothing is resident, so an idle worker still costs nothing. Dispatch is
least-outstanding across per-worker channels (an `AtomicUsize` each) in place of the shared
`Mutex<Receiver>`, so a job goes to the worker with room rather than to whichever one happened to
be waiting.

`CodeRuntime::run` serves a run's host calls in a `FuturesUnordered` instead of awaiting each
inline, so a body's `Promise.all([…])` issues its queries in parallel for real. The host is still
borrowed, with no spawn and no `'static` requirement.

Occupancy is what is bounded now that threads are not: each resident run holds its scope, its
bindings and a capped read in the V8 heap, so a worker admits at most 256 runs
(`CodeRuntime::with_workers_and_inflight`) and the rest queue exactly as they did when the number
was one — with the queue time still inside the run's own deadline. Past that the ceiling is the
database connection pool, which is where it belongs and already bounds itself.

**The deadlock is gone.** A body whose `.insert()` fires a second `run_js_code` trigger completes
on a **one-worker** pool, which has a test that would have failed before this. Two more measure
what the milestone claims: 200 bodies on one isolate, each a read and a write against a host that
takes 50 ms a call, finish in seconds rather than the 20 the serialised version would need — with
the fake host recording over a hundred calls in flight at the same instant, because a fast wall
clock could be a fast host while a hundred outstanding calls can only be concurrency. And 50
concurrent runs with distinct bindings each answer their own values, with one run exhausting its
call budget leaving the others' untouched.

One thing multiplexing had to be taught: an unhandled promise rejection halts `deno_core`'s event
loop, which with runs resident is every body punished for one body's dropped `db` call. A run's own
failure never arrives that way — `__scRun` attaches a rejection handler to the body's promise — so
the isolate now says such a rejection is handled and means it.

The watchdog is the honest gap. It is still one instrument for the whole isolate, armed at the
earliest JS deadline any resident run has, so a body that spins without yielding still takes its
co-residents down with it. Splitting the wall clock from a JS slice, naming the body that actually
overran, and re-queueing only the co-residents that provably have no side effects yet, is the next
phase.

## `db` is awaitable

`run_js_code`'s `db` was synchronous, and that bought correctness with the one currency a server
cannot spend: a host call blocked its isolate thread for the whole round trip to the database, so
the number of code bodies this server could serve **at once** was the size of the isolate pool.
Two. Worse than a throughput ceiling, it was a deadlock: a body whose `.insert()` fires a second
`run_js_code` trigger needs a second worker while still holding its own, and the pool size that
avoids that is `1 + max nesting depth × concurrency`, which is not a number anyone can pick.

The guest surface is now asynchronous. Every terminal answers a promise, `.iter()` is an async
generator, and a body is the inside of an `async function` — so `await` is legal at its top level
and a returned promise is the answer rather than something refused:

```js
const overdue = await db.invoices
  .where({ paid: false, due: { lt: payload.today } })
  .orderBy("due")
  .limit(50)
  .rows();

for await (const inv of db.customers.where({ active: true }).iter()) { … }

const [chased, owed] = await Promise.all([
  db.reminders.insert(overdue.map((i) => ({ invoice: i.id }))),
  db.invoices.where({ paid: false }).sum("amount"),
]);
```

That last one is real parallelism, not a formality: `op_sc_db` is an ordinary async op awaiting
the host, so a run in a host call costs a pending promise rather than a thread and the isolate is
free while the database works. **The chain is untouched** — `db.invoices.where(…).orderBy(…)`
still builds a plan and touches nothing — so `await` goes at the front of a whole chain and never
inside one, which is what keeps the surface recognisable.

**A forgotten `await` is not silent.** This is the tax the change levies, and it is paid in the
runtime rather than by every trigger author: `JSON.stringify(promise)` is `{}`, `` `${promise}` ``
is `[object Promise]`, and `for (const r of promise)` is a bare `TypeError` — three ways for a
missing `await` to look like a wrong answer instead of a mistake. So a terminal answers a
`DbPromise`, a `Promise` subclass whose `toJSON`, `Symbol.toPrimitive` and `Symbol.iterator` all
throw *"this database call was not awaited — write `await db.invoices.rows()`"*. It costs nothing
when the body is right, because `await` reaches none of them. The editor catches it earlier still:
the `.d.ts` Monaco loads now declares `rows(): Promise<Row[]>` and
`iter(): AsyncIterableIterator<Row>`.

**Nothing about the seam changed** (and deliberately: §15's other guest languages inherit this).
`CodeHost` still takes one JSON plan and answers one JSON value; the plan language, authority, the
row cap and the cascade guard are all as they were. What changed inside the crate is that
`handle.block_on` and `RunState::handle` are gone, and each worker now owns a current-thread tokio
runtime that drives the isolate's own event loop after `execute_script` — because with an async
body, what the script evaluates to is a pending promise and the event loop, not the call, is what
finishes a run. A syntax error still surfaces synchronously from the call, and still should.

The run's wall clock now bounds that event-loop wait too. Both bounds inside the isolate — the
watchdog, and the deadline checked on entry to a host call — need the guest to be *doing*
something to see it, and a body suspended is doing nothing; without the outer bound the worker
would be lost rather than the run. Time in the host still does not count against the JS watchdog,
so a slow query is never reported as "your code timed out". Two failures the event loop makes
newly visible get their own messages rather than the wall clock's: a body awaiting something
nothing will ever settle (`await new Promise(() => {})`) is told that `db` is the only awaitable
thing in the sandbox, at once rather than at its deadline; and a run abandoned at its wall clock
is **unwound before the worker takes another job**, because a continuation left resident would
resume during the next run's event loop and reach that run's host and call budget.

**No backwards compatibility for the synchronous spelling** (prototype status): every existing
body, test, doc example and `.d.ts` declaration is updated to `await`, and the sync form is gone
rather than deprecated.

This is the first phase of the milestone. One isolate still serves one run at a time — making
runs resident, splitting the wall clock from a JS slice, and the admission bound are the phases
that follow, and they are what turn "a run costs a promise" into "one isolate serves hundreds".

## `.iter()` — reads a code body can walk

`db`'s reads answer at most 1000 rows, and the cap refuses rather than truncates, which is right:
a body handed 1000 of 4000 rows computes a wrong answer out of a right-looking one. But it left a
body that has to *visit* a large table with nowhere to go. Now there is `.iter()`:

```js
for (const invoice of db.invoices.where({ paid: false }).orderBy("due").iter()) {
  db.reminders.insert({ invoice: invoice.id, sent_to: invoice.customerⱵemail });
}
```

It yields the same rows `.rows()` would, in the same order, but reads **one batch per database
call** — so the isolate holds a batch and never the result. Nothing is fetched before the loop
asks for it, and a `break` (or a `return`) stops: a body that wanted the first match pays for one
batch. `.iter(n)` sets the batch size; a `.limit(n)` bounds the **iteration** rather than a batch.

**Ordered streaming, which is the part that has to be exactly right.** Each batch is a read of
its own, resumed from where the last stopped by a keyset on the query's own ordering — with the
**primary key appended to it**, so that no two rows tie and no batch boundary can skip or repeat
one. Ties are not a corner case: fifty invoices sharing a due date will straddle a boundary. Null
placement is stated rather than inherited (`NULLS LAST` ascending, `NULLS FIRST` descending) so
the resume predicate and the `ORDER BY` cannot disagree, and each direction has its own answer
for resuming *from* a null. The cursor is the sort values of the last row read, projected into
the same `SELECT` under reserved aliases — because a `.select()` narrows what comes back and a
Ⱶ-path is no column of the table at all — and it is coerced on the way back in against the column
it sorts on, exactly as a `.where()` literal is.

Three things it will not do, each refused by name rather than approximated:

- **`.orderBy()` must name a column, a calculated field or a Ⱶ-path**, never an expression:
  resuming binds the last row's value into a `WHERE`, and a value needs a column's type.
- **The table needs a single-column primary key** — the same requirement `.update()` and
  `.delete()` already have, for the same reason.
- **A grouped aggregate cannot be streamed**; groups have no key to break a tie on.

**Delegated**, a stream needs its ownership rule to reach inside the statement, because that is
what bounds a batch. RLS policies do and a translatable ownership formula does; a formula only
the evaluator can decide does not — a batch of it would fetch the whole table, hand back what
survived and do it again — so it is refused naming `.rows()`, which is §5's asymmetry exactly as
an aggregate meets it.

**An iteration is not a snapshot.** Batches are separate statements and no transaction spans them
(§6, as for a bulk write), so a loop that changes a row's *sort key* may see that row twice or not
at all; changing any other column is safe. And because each batch is a database call, what bounds
a stream is the **call budget** rather than the row cap — 200 batches of 1000 by default.

The editor knows it: `iter(batchSize?): IterableIterator<Row>` is in the `.d.ts` the code editor
loads, so the loop variable completes as a row of that table.

## `db.sql` — the body's own SQL

`run_js_code`'s `db` could ask everything the row layer's read expresses and nothing else, so a
window function, a recursive CTE or an `ON CONFLICT` meant leaving the trigger. Now the body can
write the statement:

```js
const ranked = db.sql(
  "select owner, title, rank() over (partition by owner order by pages desc) as r \
   from books where pages > $1",
  [200],
);
const mine = db.sql("select * from books", [], { asUser: true });  // or db.asUser().sql(…)
```

**The text is the author's; the values are binds.** The SQL runs as written — placeholders are
the database's own (`$1` on Postgres) and the second argument is what they stand for, so a value
that spells SQL is a value. This is the same admission §13.4's custom SQL queries are, on the
same grounds: a code body is server-side configuration written by an administrator, which is
what `Statement::Raw`'s rule ("authored, never assembled from what a caller sent") requires.

**Whose authority, said twice because both readings are natural.** A third argument —
an *object*, so what it can say may grow — takes `{ asUser: true }`, and `db.asUser().sql(…)`
is the same thing said fluently; the option wins where both appear. Delegation means less here
than on a chain, and the docs say so rather than implying otherwise: the statement runs at the
caller's role and user, which is exactly what row-level security reads, and an ownership
*formula* is applied by a row layer that raw SQL does not go through.

**What is given up is listed where an author will read it** — in `run_js_code`'s own
documentation, the code editor's completions and the host module: no ownership formula filters
it, no rich type coerces what it returns, and a write inside one raises **no table event**. What
still holds: the caller-context transaction (an RLS table's policies decide), the row cap (these
rows are materialised into the isolate like any others) and the call budget.

A `db.sql` request is a shape of its own rather than a sixth `Op` — it shares nothing with a plan
but the authority — and `TableHost::call` tells the two apart by `op` before deserialising
either, so a malformed request is refused in the words of the shape it was trying to be. Two
things are read out of the text, both by `rewrite_named_params` (the one reader of admin SQL,
which knows a `;` inside a literal separates nothing): a **second statement** is refused, and so
is a `:name` parameter — that is the custom query's spelling, where the admin also declares each
type, and left alone it would reach the database as a syntax error pointing at a colon.

## `db` in a code body can group

`run_js_code`'s tables could answer "how many" and "how much" for a whole table; they could not
answer "per customer" without reading every row into the isolate and counting them there. Now
`.groupBy()` says what makes a group, `.aggregate()` says what to compute for each one, and
`.rows()` answers **one row per group** with the keys beside the values:

```js
db.invoices
  .where({ paid: false })
  .groupBy("customerⱵemail")                        // a field, a Ⱶ-path or a formula
  .aggregate({ n: "count()", owed: "sum(amount)" })
  .having({ n: { gt: 1 } })
  .orderBy("owed", "desc")
  .rows();
```

**The scalar terminals became sugar for it.** `.count()`, `.sum(f)`, `.avg(f)`, `.min(f)` and
`.max(f)` are the same plan, the same host function and the same statement with nothing to group
by — so the filter, the row cap, the caller context and the delegated refusal are decided in one
place rather than in two that could drift. `rows::aggregate_grouped` is that place, and
`rows::aggregate_values` is now a call to it that takes the one row a scalar aggregate has.
`RowQuery` grew `group` and `having` to carry the question, which the existing `Select` already
knew how to render — grouping is the only part of this milestone that touched the read struct at
all.

**`having` is the filter object, over the aggregate's own names.** `{ n: { gt: 1 } }` means what
it means everywhere else, because it goes through the same walk in `sc_api::filter` — which grew
an `Operand` for the one thing that is new here: `count()` stands behind no column, so its
operand is read by its own JSON shape instead of being coerced against a type it does not have.
A `having` key that is not one of *this* aggregate's aliases is refused naming the ones that are,
and says that a condition on the rows belongs in `.where()`, which means the same thing for less
work. An `.orderBy()` may name an alias too, and the aggregate is repeated in the clause rather
than named, since an output alias is not in scope in a `HAVING` on any database and not in an
`ORDER BY` on some.

**The bounds and the authority did not move.** Groups are rows, so the row cap refuses a read
that would materialise more of them than a body may hold; a `.groupBy()` on a row read or a write
is refused rather than ignored, as an unfiltered `.update()` is; two values under one alias is
refused rather than silently answered as one; and a delegated grouped aggregate over a table
whose ownership formula the translator cannot lower is refused for exactly the reason an
ungrouped one is — the guard narrows the *rows*, and the grouping only partitions what is left.

Tested where each claim lives: the prelude lowering one chain to one plan (and refusing
`.count()` over groups, and `.groupBy()` with nothing to compute); the plan becoming a `SELECT`
with the Ⱶ-path group key in both the projection and the `GROUP BY`; the statement answering, per
group, against a real Postgres; the delegated case counting only the caller's rows and refusing
where §5 says it must; the tutorial's own grouped example, executed; and the whole thing through
the admin's **Run** button.

## The admin copilot writes an application's SQL endpoints, and is scoped by area

`admin_copilot` reached the catalog, then the trigger set. It now reaches the third thing an admin
asks for in a sentence: **an application's custom SQL queries** (§13.4) — the escape hatch for the
report the row layer's read cannot express. Asked for "an endpoint that returns each author with
their book count", the agent writes the SQL, and comes back with the columns *Postgres* said the
statement produces.

Three tools, in the shape the other two halves already have. `describe_applications` reads what is
served — each application's tables, exposed triggers and APIs, and each API's queries with their
SQL, parameters, role floor and result columns. `save_api_query` writes one, by name, so saving
under a name that exists edits that query and omitting an argument leaves it as it was.
`delete_api_query` removes one.

**One authority, as everywhere else.** `sc_app::save_application` is the only way in, so the agent
runs the validation the admin's form and `saltcorn api add-query` run — one statement, declared
parameters matching the used ones, a path no table route already answers — and then the save
**prepares every query against the database**. That is the last validation and the typing at once:
a statement Postgres will not prepare comes back carrying Postgres's own message with *nothing*
stored, and one it will is stored with the columns the database reported, which reach the model as
`returns` in the same turn. It is told the shape of what it wrote without running it against
anybody's rows.

**Which API a query belongs to now has one home.** `select_api` moved from `sc-cli` down into
`sc-app`, beside `registered_api_provider_info` — the command line and the trait resolve it with
the same function, so a query cannot land in one place from a terminal and another from a
conversation. Its ambiguity refusal takes a parameter for *how this caller's user names an API*
(`--api`, or the `api` argument), because a refusal that sends a model to a command line it is not
standing at is a refusal it cannot act on. It also gained a check it was missing: naming an API
that does **not** serve custom queries — the app's GraphQL one — is now refused there, where the
mistake was made, rather than surviving to the save as "unknown setting `queries`", which is a true
message about the wrong thing. `saltcorn api add-query` gets that fix with it.

**What is live afterwards, stated rather than implied.** The generated TypeScript client is
rewritten on every save, as it is on the admin API's — the source tree must never disagree with the
definition. The mount is not: a mounted app's providers are built from its record when it is
mounted, so the tool result says the endpoint is served from the app's next build rather than
letting the model report a live endpoint that is not answering yet.

**Two area checkboxes, on top of the four grants.** The grants say *what this agent may do*; the
new `allow_triggers` and `allow_applications` say *to which of the three*. Both default on. An area
that is off removes its tools from the model's list entirely — the opposite of how a grant behaves,
and deliberately: a model that may not drop a table still has to be able to say that dropping one
is what the request needs, whereas an area that is off is not part of this agent's job at all, and
a tool the model can see is a tool it will try. An agent scoped to the schema should not spend a
turn, and the admin's money, discovering that it is not the trigger editor. The areas compose with
the grants rather than replacing them: applications on with `allow_drop` off is an agent that can
write an SQL endpoint and cannot remove one.

The tests are against a real Postgres, a real stored application and a real file store, because
what is under test could not survive a mock: the `returns` a query comes back with are `describe`'s
answer; a `select publisher from books` is refused with Postgres's message and leaves the stored
application exactly as it was; and the app's `api.ts` grows the `topAuthors` method in the same
call — and loses it again on delete. Beside them, the grants deciding add/edit/delete and who may
call the endpoint, a non-admin refused by all three tools, and the areas deciding which of the nine
tools an agent is offered.

## An OpenAI agent run survives its first tool call

A run of the admin copilot against an OpenAI provider failed the moment it came
back from a tool: *"the LLM provider failed: RequestError: Assistant tool call
`call_id` is required for OpenAI Responses API"*. The first turn worked, the
tool ran, and the second turn — the one carrying the call and its result back —
was refused before it left the process.

**Two ids, and we were keeping the wrong one.** The Responses API gives a tool
call an item id (`fc_…`) *and* a correlation id (`call_id`), and pairs the call
with its output by the latter. `rig_bridge` read only rig's `id`, so it kept
`fc_…` and sent rig's `call_id` as `None` on both the assistant's call and the
tool result — and rig, which cannot invent one, rejects the request rather than
send a history OpenAI would silently mis-pair. `ToolCall::id` now holds the
correlation id where a provider has one, and fills both of rig's fields on the
way out. Anthropic is unaffected either way: it has one id, which is both.

One field rather than a second one threaded through `LlmMessage`, `_sc_runs` and
every caller: an id a provider does not correlate by is an id nothing in the
loop can use. rig drops a non-native item id from the payload by itself, so
Anthropic's `toolu_…` travelling in that field costs nothing.

The test is the one that was missing: a capturing stub replays a real
`function_call` item whose `id` and `call_id` differ, and asserts on the *second*
request's body — that the `function_call` and its `function_call_output` both
carry `call_id: "call_1"`. Asserting on our own delta types could never have
caught this, because the shape that was wrong only exists on the wire.

Runs that already failed this way stay failed: their stored history holds the
item id, and replaying it is a request OpenAI has no call to match.

## A code body is edited in a code editor, with the sandbox's own types

`run_js_code` is now a program worth writing — it reads and writes tables — and it
was being written in a one-line text input. It is now written in **Monaco**, with
JavaScript highlighting and completions over `db`, this server's tables and their
columns, and the event's own `row`, `old`, `user` and `payload`.

**The coupling is a declaration, not a screen.** `FormField` gains
`code_language`, set with `.code("javascript")`, beside the `multiline`/`secret`/
`create_only` hints it already carries; `run_js_code` declares it on its `code`
setting, the API reports it in every `config_spec`, and the admin UI renders any
field that has it as an editor. No screen knows that a particular action's
particular setting holds a program — which is the property the whole
settings-as-data vocabulary exists for, and the reason the next code setting
needs no UI change at all.

**The types are generated in the browser, from the catalog.** `ui/admin`'s
`codeTypes.ts` builds an ambient `.d.ts`: a row interface and a column union per
table (including one `keyⱵcolumn` name per column of each table a key field
points at, so `.select("customerⱵemail")` completes), the fluent chain
transcribed from `sc-expr`'s `DB_PRELUDE`, the comparison operators from
`sc_api::filter`, and the `{ updated, ids }` shape a bulk write answers with.
`row` and `old` are declared exactly where the event has them — a `login` body
gets no `row`, because naming it there is a `ReferenceError` in the sandbox.
Building it here rather than on the server costs no endpoint and no schema, and
`listTables`/`listFields` are already what the screen reads; a server-side emitter
is the right move the moment a second consumer wants the same text (§15's
adapters).

**No diagnostics, deliberately.** A body is the inside of a function, so
top-level `return` — what the action's own documentation tells an admin to write
— is a syntax error to a parser that does not know that, and the sandbox is not a
browser, so half of what a checker would say is not knowable. Completions, hovers
and signature help are what the declarations are for; errors come from **Run**,
which really runs it. The test suite takes the opposite setting: it compiles the
generated declarations against the milestone's own example body with `checkJs`
on, so a chain method that returns the wrong builder is a failing test rather than
a wrong completion.

**The language service is started before anything asks it a question.** The
TypeScript service lives in a 6 MB worker that builds a program before it can
answer, and nothing starts it until the first request — so the first `.` an admin
typed paid for all of it, showing the word-based guesses from the document in the
meantime, which is indistinguishable from completions that do not work. The
editor now hands the model to the worker as soon as it is created; the
declarations that arrive later with the catalog are pushed to the running worker
by Monaco itself. The suite asks what an admin asks — the names offered after
`db.`, `row.`, `db.invoices.` and `orderBy("` — through that same language
service, because declarations can type-check a finished body and still offer
nothing useful to someone halfway through writing one.

**One CSP relaxation, named in the policy.** Monaco writes the theme that colours
the syntax into a `<style>` element it creates at runtime and offers no nonce hook
to sign it with, so the admin policy is now `style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'`.
Nothing about where *code* may come from moves: `script-src 'self'`, no `eval`, no
`blob:`, and `default-src 'self'` leaves injected CSS nowhere to send anything.
Monaco is loaded as a dynamic import — a chunk fetched when a code setting is
opened and never on the way to another screen — its workers are same-origin
scripts Vite emits, and a fetch that fails falls back to a text area, because an
admin who cannot load an editor must still be able to fix a trigger.

## `db` in `run_js_code`: a trigger's code body reads and writes tables

The fifth phase of "tables in code", and the one an admin can see. `run_js_code`
builds a `TableHost` from its context and binds `db`, so the milestone's own
example — read the overdue invoices with a joined column and a child count, write
a reminder per row, return a total — is a trigger you can paste into the form and
press **Run** on. Everything the four phases below built was unreachable until
this one; nothing about them changed to make it reachable.

**A host borrows the catalog, because everything that has one has a reference to
it.** `TableHost` took an `Arc<Catalog>`, which no firing trigger has: the row
layer, an `ActionContext` and every request handler hold a `&Catalog`, and
threading an `Arc` down to a trigger would mean threading one through the write
that fired it. So `TableHost<'a>` borrows, and `CodeCall::host` borrows with it —
which the pool cannot carry, since a job crosses a channel to an isolate thread
and must be `'static`. `CodeRuntime::run` therefore **bridges**: the job takes a
`'static` proxy, the plans come back over a channel, and the future that holds the
borrow is the future serving them, which is the same future awaiting the run. The
guest API is unchanged and the isolate thread still blocks on each call; what
changed is that the thing it blocks on is a channel rather than the catalog, and
the borrow can therefore end when the run does.

**One new setting: `timeout_ms`** (max 60000), validated at save time as `fetch`'s
is — refused when out of range rather than clamped, because an admin who typed
five minutes should be told, not quietly given a minute. Left blank it means
*whatever this server's default is* (5 s) rather than a resolved 5000: the pool
has that default already, and turning "unset" into a number here would overrule a
process started with another one. A configurable timeout is only possible at all
because a code body runs on its own isolates — the same knob on the formula
evaluator would have been a configurable hold on every authorization decision in
the process.

**Authority is the event's, as specified.** The host carries the event's role and
user, so `db.asUser()` in a body delegates to whoever caused *that* event: the
signed-in person for a table event or a `none` trigger called from an app, and
nobody — the public role — for a scheduled or startup one. The trigger's chain
rides along too, so a write from a body is an event at the depth it belongs to and
the cascade bound applies to it exactly as it does to `insert_row`.

**Documented where it will be looked for.** §10.1 of the technical design gains
the whole `db` specification (the chain and its terminals, the two spellings of a
filter, formula projections, the write rules, the authority rule, the three bounds
and the plan JSON), and §15 gains `CodeHost` as *what an adapter implements* —
with the reason a Python adapter will need its own runtime and not its own data
layer. `docs/tutorial-triggers.md` gains step 5, "reading and writing tables from
code", whose body is executed by `tutorial_triggers.rs` like every other formula
the tutorial prints.

**Found on the way: a run could hold its caller without running at all.** The
wall clock was enforced in the isolate — the watchdog while JavaScript executes,
the deadline checked when the guest asks for a host call — and neither can see a
run that is not executing. Two now-reachable shapes are exactly that: a run
waiting for a free isolate while every isolate is blocked in a host call of its
own (which is what a code body whose write fires *another* code body looks like to
a two-worker pool), and one host call that never comes back. Both used to wait for
ever, which is precisely the unbounded hold on the firing request that `timeout_ms`
exists to prevent. `CodeRuntime::run` now stops waiting shortly after the run's own
deadline (a 250 ms grace, so the isolate's two bounds keep reporting their more
specific messages), and giving up drops the serving loop — so the abandoned run
fails at its next host call and its worker comes back rather than being held for
as long as the database takes.

**Tested through the button.** `sc-server`'s `code_body_tables.rs` creates the
trigger over HTTP, runs it over HTTP and reads the written rows back over HTTP:
the definition-of-done body against a real database, plus the three mistakes an
admin makes while writing one (an unknown table, an unknown column, an unfiltered
update) coming back as sentences rather than as a 500. The action's own tests add
what only the action can be wrong about: that a body reads and writes its tables,
that `asUser()` obeys an ownership formula over *this* event's caller while the
trigger's own authority does not, that the configured timeout is the bound on a
run, and that `db` is still the only host surface — no `fetch`, no timers, no
`Deno`.

## `asUser()`: a code body's access delegated to the event's caller

The fourth phase of "tables in code". A plan carrying `authority: "user"` was
refused; now it is answered — as the person who caused the event, and no row
more. Still nothing an admin can see (no action binds `db` until phase 5), but
both authorities the milestone specifies now work.

**One rule, not a second copy of it.** Every delegated operation goes through
`sc_api::ownership`'s `*_as` functions at the event's own role and user — the
same functions the agent tools call, where "meets the floor OR the ownership
formula grants it" already lives. So without this phase implementing any of it: a
read is narrowed in the `WHERE` where the formula translates and row by row where
it does not (with the bound applied after the evaluator has spoken, never
before); an update is checked twice, on the row as it is and on the row as it
would become, so it cannot move a row out of the caller's own ownership; a row
the formula withholds is the same **not found** an absent row gets, so a
delegated `.get(pk)` cannot become a way to probe which rows exist; and an
RLS-enforced table is read and written inside a caller-context transaction where
the database's own policies decide. A bulk update or delete resolves its rows
through the *delegated* read, so a row the caller cannot see is never a row they
can reach by predicate.

**The authority also decides how names resolve.** It is settled once per plan
into an `Actor`, which is the role the plan's own Ⱶ-paths and Ↄ-aggregations are
resolved at — a delegated read through a key goes through `ownership::join_guard`
as the caller, exactly as a REST embed does, rather than as the admin.

**Who to delegate to, said honestly.** `User::from_json` reads the caller back out
of the JSON an event carries (beside `from_row`, and the inverse of the rendering
`caller_context` produces), with the event's role as the role. An event with no
caller — scheduled, startup — has nobody to act as, so `asUser()` there reads as
**public**: the honest answer to "on whose behalf", and the reason `asAdmin()` is
the default. A caller object with no usable `id` is an error rather than an
anonymous fallback.

**§5's one asymmetry keeps its message.** A delegated aggregate over a table
whose ownership formula only the evaluator can decide is refused — a count over
rows it cannot filter would be a number nobody can check — and the refusal now
names the way out: read the rows and aggregate them in the code body, which can
decide row by row.

**A delegated write is still an event at the depth it belongs to.**
`ownership::insert_row_as` / `update_row_as` / `delete_row_as` build their own
`CallerContext` and therefore used to drop the trigger chain; they now carry it.
Without that a delegated write would restart the cascade at zero, and
`db.asUser().t.insert(…)` from a trigger on `t` would never stop — the bound
phase 3 established for the admin path. A request is not a firing and passes an
empty chain (REST, GraphQL, agent tools); a code body passes the chain that led
to it.

**Tested against a real database, real policies and the real engine**, because
every claim above is a claim about code this phase calls rather than code it
contains. Including the deadlock regression decision 1 exists for: a body running
on a *code* isolate, whose thread blocks on a host call, whose delegated read
needs the *formula* isolate to decide its ownership formula — two runtimes, so
the blocked thread is never the one the formula needs. One isolate serving both
would hang, so that test has a timeout around it rather than a comment.

## A code body's writes are events

The third phase of "tables in code": `insert`, `update` and `delete` plans are
answered. Still nothing an admin can see — no action binds `db` until phase 5 —
but a `TableHost` handed a write plan now writes, and what it writes is an
event.

**Through the row layer, one row at a time.** `insert` goes to
`rows::create_row_ctx`, and a bulk `update`/`delete` resolves its matched rows
first and then writes each one by primary key through `rows::update_row_ctx` /
`delete_row_ctx` — exactly as the `update_rows` and `delete_rows` actions do,
because the events are the point. So a code body's write is coerced against its
columns, validated, File-field-checked and **observed by the table's own
triggers**, and it carries this run's caller and trigger chain: an audit trigger
on it can say who caused it, and `Event::firing`'s cascade bound stops a body
that writes the table that fired it exactly where it stops an action that does.
`.insert(v)` answers the written row and `.insert([…])` an array of them — the
answer's shape follows the call's — while `update` and `delete` answer
`{ updated | deleted, ids }`, the shape those two actions already return.

**An omitted `.where()` cannot empty a table.** The prelude refuses an
unfiltered `update`/`delete` in front of the author; the host refuses it again,
because the prelude is a convenience and the host is the rule. A chain method
that only shapes a read (`.select()`, an aggregate, `.get()`) is refused on a
write rather than ignored — a body that wrote one meant something the write
cannot do — while `.orderBy()` and `.limit()` are kept, so "update the oldest
ten" says what it means.

**A bulk insert's rows are all checked before any of them is written.** There
are no transactions in this milestone, so a bad third row discovered on the
third `INSERT` would leave the first two written and their events already out.
Every key is checked as a writable field (a calculated one is refused by name)
and every value coerced against its column up front. `rows::row_key` is now the
one implementation of "which row is this" shared by the row actions and the code
host, rather than two spellings of the same coercion.

## The host behind `db`: a plan in, rows out

The second phase of "tables in code". `sc-expr` learned to *send* plans last
time; `sc_api::code_host` is what answers them, for reads. Still nothing an
admin can see — no action binds `db` until phase 5 — but a `TableHost` handed a
plan now returns the rows.

**Nothing in a plan is trusted, and nothing in it is SQL.** The table is
resolved through the catalog, every column against that table, every Ⱶ-path by
walking the keys (each hop through `ownership::join_guard`, the same check a
REST embed and a GraphQL key field go through), every formula through the one
translator, and anything else is refused naming it. What the guest sends is
names and values; the statement is built here, and every literal is a bound
parameter. `deny_unknown_fields` on the plan, so a guest and a host that
disagree about the seam say so rather than answering a question nobody asked.

**One `where`, in one place.** The object DSL walk moved out of
`sc-core-traits::table` — where it was written for the agent tools — into
`sc_api::filter`, beside the comparison vocabulary it already called. A code
body's `.where({ … })` is an agent's `where` argument is a REST query string's
comparisons: one meaning, one implementation. Moving it is what let `and`, `or`
and `not` be added **once**, so the agent tools gained boolean logic at the same
moment the code bodies did, and `nin` stopped being a comparison that existed
in a URL but not in an object. A field wins over a combinator, so an
application with a column called `or` keeps filtering on it — the trade the
REST query string already makes. The agent traits' tests came along unchanged
and still pass.

**Two spellings of a filter, one predicate.** `{ status: "draft" }` and
`'status === "draft" && ordersↃcustomer.length > 3'` lower to the same
`sc_query::Expr`; a Ⱶ-path may be a filter *key*, and its literal is coerced
against the column it belongs to on the **target** table rather than on the one
being read. A projection is the same choice — a name, a path, or an
`{ alias, formula }` — and `.orderBy(…)` and `.sum(…)` resolve a name exactly
the way a projection does, because there is one expression language.

**A formula the database cannot compute is an error that says where to put it.**
`TranslateError::Untranslatable` becomes "compute it in your code body instead,
which is JavaScript and can" — which costs the author nothing, and is the whole
reason this seam is worth having. A formula naming `user`, `row`, `old` or
`payload` is refused for the same reason from the other direction: those are the
code body's own bindings, and the body can splice the value into the plan.

**The row cap refuses; it never truncates.** A read with no `.limit()` asks the
database for one row more than the cap, and if that row arrives the read fails
saying so. Handing a body 1000 of 4000 rows would let it compute a wrong answer
out of a right-looking one and never know — the one failure mode this whole
milestone is careful not to have. A `.limit()` *above* the cap is refused before
any statement runs, rather than quietly lowered to it. The call budget is
counted on the host as well as in the guest: the guest's stops the loop before a
plan is built, and the host's is the number that is true.

**What is honestly not here yet** says so rather than doing something plausible:
`asUser()` is refused rather than silently answering with the admin's rows
(phase 4), writes are refused (phase 3), and `.groupBy()` is refused rather than
ignored (phase 6).

## A code body gets a runtime of its own

The first phase of "tables in code". Nothing an admin can see yet — no action
binds `db` — but the machinery underneath it is here, and it is a *second*
JavaScript engine rather than a new power granted to the first.

**Why two.** The formula evaluator is one V8 isolate on one thread serving every
ownership check in the process. A code body is different in kind: it calls the
host, and the host call blocks the thread until the database answers. Giving
*that* isolate a blocking call would put every authorization decision on the
server behind whatever some trigger's code is doing — and it would **deadlock**
the first time a delegated read's ownership formula needed the JS evaluator,
because the thread waiting on the host is the thread the formula would have to
run on. So `sc_expr::CodeRuntime` is a small pool of isolates of its own (two by
default), and `DenoEvaluator`'s stays exactly as pure as it was: a test asserts
the formula isolate has no `Deno`, no op handle, no run wrapper and no `db`.

**The seam is JSON, both ways.** `CodeHost` is one method — a JSON request in, a
JSON value out — and that is deliberately the whole interface. `sc-expr` sits
below `sc-catalog` and `sc-api` and does not learn what a table is; the catalog
lookups, the formula translation and the row layer stay where they already are.
It is also what makes this the seam §15's other guest languages implement rather
than a JavaScript feature: a Python adapter answers the same plans.

**The fluent surface is JavaScript.** `db.books.where({…}).select(…).rows()` is
a prelude, not generated code. Chain methods are pure and return a new builder;
a terminal builds one plain plan object and makes one round trip. `db.table(n)`
is the general form and `db.n` is a Proxy over it; `.asUser()` / `.asAdmin()`
set one field of the plan, so it does not matter where in the chain they are
said; `.update()` or `.delete()` with no `.where()` throws before anything is
sent. Adding a chain method from here touches no Rust.

**Three bounds, three messages.** Wall clock for the run (checked when the guest
asks for a host call, default 5 s, hard max 60 s), a call budget (200, so an
accidental N+1 cannot hammer the database quietly), and the JS watchdog — which
is **paused for the duration of every host call**. A slow query is the
database's time, not the guest's, and reporting it as "your code timed out"
sends an admin to rewrite code that was never the problem. A test holds the host
for longer than the whole run's budget and asserts the error names the time
limit rather than the watchdog.

**The globals cannot be poisoned.** Runs share an isolate, so `__scDbCall` and
`__scRun` are installed non-writable and non-configurable, and `db` is built
fresh inside each run's own function scope. Tampering could never *escalate* —
the host re-validates every plan — but a body that broke the next trigger's `db`
would be a bug nobody could find.

**One latent process abort, fixed on the way past.** `deno_core` registers each
isolate against the tokio runtime that was current when it was built, and
**aborts the process** if V8 later posts a delayed foreground task (its GC
memory reducer does, under load) against an isolate that has none. Both engines
now build their isolates inside a runtime context and drop the guard
immediately, which is also what leaves a code thread free to block on a host
call. The formula evaluator had been constructing its isolate outside any
runtime since it was written; under the extra heap pressure of a second engine
it started aborting the test binary roughly one run in three.

## The log learns to describe a model call

Answering "what does an agent log?" — which, before this, was: nothing at all.
`sc-llm` and `sc-agent` printed not one line between them, so an agent that
answered the wrong question left no trace of what it had been asked.

**Every configured provider is now wrapped in a `LoggedProvider`.** The wrap
happens inside `connect_provider`, which is the one place a stored provider
record becomes something callable, so the agent loop, the chat socket and the
*Test connection* button are all covered by construction rather than by three
call sites remembering to log.

At **`info`**, one line per finished call: which provider and model, how big the
request was, why the model stopped, **what it cost in tokens** and how long it
took. This is the line an installation can leave on — it carries no message
text, which is what makes it safe to.

At **`verbose`**, a line as the call is *issued*. A model call is the longest
wait in the system, and a run that is thinking and a run that is wedged look
identical without it.

At **`trace`**, the whole thing: the entire `LlmRequest` and the entire
assembled response, as JSON. The system prompt with whatever the traits appended
to it, every message of the history, every tool schema that was offered, and the
answer with its reasoning, its tool calls and their arguments. What is logged is
**our** request value rather than the vendor's wire body — it is the same for
every backend, and it is the level the interesting mistakes live at: a tool
schema that is wrong, a history that lost a tool result, a system prompt a trait
never got to.

**The loop logs its own half.** `sc_agent::driver` prints each tool call with its
outcome and duration at `info`, its **arguments and its result in full** at
`trace`, and one closing line per run naming the run id, how it ended and how
many steps it took. Together with the request dump that is the whole
conversation: what the model was told, what it decided, what the tools actually
did, and what it said back.

**What is not in it:** the API key, which lives in the adapter and is not on this
path at any level. What *is* in it at `trace` is everything the people using an
agent typed — a trace log is a transcript — which is why it is at `trace` and
why the setting says so.

Below `info` the decorator hands the call straight through: no line, no clone, no
accumulation.

**A line is the message and nothing else.** No program name, no level tag: the
terminal, `journald` or the container runtime reading these already knows which
process wrote the line and which stream it came out of, and a prefix on every one
is width spent saying so twice. It also leaves the SQL echo as *valid SQL* — the
binds ride in a trailing `--` comment — so a redirected stdout is a script rather
than a log to be unwrapped:

```
SELECT * FROM "_sc_config" WHERE ("key" = $1) LIMIT $2 -- binds: [Text("log_sql"), Int(1)]
UPDATE "_sc_config" SET "value" = $1 WHERE ("key" = $2) -- binds: [Json(Bool(false)), Text("log_sql")]
```

The consequence, taken knowingly: an `info` line and a `warning` line look alike,
so a message that wants to be recognisable as a failure says so in its own words
(`llm ✗ house (gpt-5.1): …`). The boot-time `saltcorn: …` announcements are
untouched — they are direct `eprintln!`s from the CLI, not messages through this
facility.

**`sc-log` grew a `capture` feature** so this could be tested as behaviour rather
than as string formatting. It installs a thread-local sink that collects lines
instead of printing them; a crate adds it to `[dev-dependencies]` and asserts
what was *logged*. Four tests now drive a real provider through `connect_provider`
against a stub SSE server and read the log back — the trace dump contains the
system prompt, the user's question and the tool's parameter schema; the `info`
line contains the token counts and none of the transcript; `warning` logs
nothing. The same sink turns the earlier request-logging and SQL-echo tests from
assertions about formatters into assertions about output.

## Settings → Development: a log verbosity, and an SQL echo

A third settings tab, and the two switches an admin reaches for when they cannot
explain what a running installation is doing.

**Log verbosity** is the ladder every other daemon on the machine already uses —
`error`, `warning`, `info`, `verbose`, `trace` — and **`info` logs every server
request**: one line each, with its method, target, status and duration. It is a
middleware wrapped *outermost* around the router rather than a line inside
`dispatch`, which is what makes "every request" true: the upload route, the two
backup routes, the language-server and chat upgrades and every application
subdomain do not go through `dispatch`, and the status a request finished with is
the one the header and CSRF layers produced, not the one the handler returned.
`verbose` adds a line as a request *arrives* — a request that hangs has no
completion line, so without it the interesting case is the invisible one — and
`trace` adds nothing at all for a request. It did, briefly: every header, with
`cookie`, `authorization` and `x-csrf-token` redacted. Two lines of signal per
request arrived wrapped in eight of `accept-encoding` and `sec-fetch-mode`, which
buried the level that carries the model transcripts under the level's own
boilerplate. Headers are a debugging need the browser's network panel already
serves.

**Log SQL** echoes every statement to stdout, with its bind values. It is hooked
in the Postgres driver, at the two functions every statement already funnels
through, so it covers queries, DDL, introspection, `prepare`-time description and
the transaction verbs without anything being routed through a second path to be
seen. The bind *values* are printed, unlike in a query error — which is returned
to a client and so carries only their count — because a statement without its
parameters does not tell you what ran. That includes password hashes and session
tokens, and the setting says so.

**Both are settings, not flags, and both take effect immediately.** The moment
you want the SQL is the moment the server is already running and doing the thing
you cannot reproduce; a flag would mean restarting it and throwing that away. So
the switches live in a new layer-0 crate, **`sc-log`**, as two process-wide
atomics: `sc_config::development` declares the keys, `DevelopmentSettings::apply`
is the one place a stored value becomes process behaviour, and it is called at
boot (from `connect_catalog`, so a CLI command run against an installation with
the echo on prints its SQL too) and again at the end of every settings save.

Globals, deliberately. The thing that wants to print a statement is the driver at
layer 2, the thing that knows what was ticked is the config store at layer 5, and
the six layers between them have no business carrying a logging handle from one
to the other. What it costs is a relaxed atomic load per statement and per
request; below `info` the middleware does nothing else at all.

The default stays `warning` and no SQL: `info` is defined as "log every request",
and a server that does that by default is a server whose logs nobody reads.

**The admin UI needed no changes.** The tab is the section — the settings screen
renders whatever sections the server declares — so a checkbox, a five-option
dropdown and their help text are a Rust declaration and nothing else. The tests
that landed here are the model's: an unticked checkbox travels as `false` rather
than being dropped (dropped, it would read as "leave it as it was", and turning
the echo *off* is the half an admin does more often), and the level round-trips
with `warning` as its default.

## `admin_copilot`: the schema agent grows the triggers, and learns to ask

`manage_table_admin` is now **`admin_copilot`**, and it configures triggers as
well as tables. The rename is the honest one: the trait was never about tables
in the sense the other traits are about a table, and the four grants it is
scoped by turned out to be exactly the four a trigger needs too. Four tools join
its two — `describe_triggers`, `describe_action`, `save_trigger` and
`delete_trigger` — so an agent asked to "email the client when a matter closes"
writes the trigger that does it, in the same conversation it created the tables.

**Everything goes through `save_trigger`**, the same authority the admin's own
form goes through: the same `validate_trigger` resolves every configured formula
in the scope the event will actually give it, against the same action registry
the dispatcher fires with. An agent cannot save a trigger an admin could not
have, and is refused in the words the admin would have read. The dispatcher is
reloaded before the tool result comes back, so what was just written is live —
the test that matters here is the one where the trigger the agent wrote fires on
the next insert, through the row layer, with nobody reloading anything.

**The hard part is the action's configuration, and this is the decision.** There
is an open-ended set of actions, each declaring its own settings, and some of
those declarations are not even fixed — `send_email` grows a checkbox per File
field of the trigger's table. Putting every action's every setting into one
tool's JSON schema would be a large, mostly-irrelevant declaration re-sent on
every model call of every conversation, stale the moment a plugin registers an
action.

Saltcorn 1 answered this with a **nested inference call**: `create_action` chose
the action and the trigger conditions, and a second, ad-hoc model call filled in
its parameters with a tool built for that one action. v2 answers it with
**progressive disclosure inside the one loop** — the pattern the ecosystem
converged on for the same problem, a discovery tool beside an execution tool with
schemas fetched on demand rather than carried. `describe_action` returns one
action's settings, resolved for the table the trigger will fire on;
`save_trigger` takes `configuration` as an open object; and a configuration that
does not validate comes back as a refusal **carrying the settings it should have
used**. Four reasons, in the order they mattered:

- **The parameters are exactly what the conversation decides.** A nested call has
  to be re-briefed, by the model that is about to guess: "email the client, not
  the fee earner" lives in the transcript the sub-call cannot see. It is
  `subagent`'s own argument for why a child does not inherit its parent's
  context, read the other way round — delegation pays when the child's *work* is
  long and noisy, and filling in one form is neither.
- **A refusal has to reach the model that can fix it.** Validation is real, so a
  first attempt is often wrong. In one loop that is a tool result and the next
  turn corrects it; inside a nested call it is either an error nobody can
  attribute or a retry loop nobody can see.
- **A hidden second inference is a run nobody can read.** A run is a transcript
  with one subject, a step budget and a row in `_sc_runs`. A tool that quietly
  calls the model again has none of those, and would be the one place in the
  system where tokens are spent off the record. Where a task genuinely wants its
  own context window, `subagent` already gives it one, visibly.
- **It costs less.** Saltcorn 1 spends two inferences on every action, always.
  This spends one extra *tool* round trip, only when the model does not already
  hold the settings — and because the refusal carries them, a model that guesses
  well pays nothing.

What was kept from Saltcorn 1 is the sequencing it was reaching for — choose the
action, then configure it — made explicit and cheap by `describe_action`'s two
levels: every action with one line, then one action in full.

**The four grants cover both halves, and are not four more checkboxes.** Creating
a table and creating a trigger are both `allow_create`; deleting a trigger is
`allow_drop`; and a trigger's `min_role` — which decides who may `POST
/actions/{name}` — is an **access rule**, so it needs `allow_access_changes`
exactly as a table's role floors do. Creating and editing being two grants over
one tool is also what makes the name-is-the-identity upsert safe: an agent
allowed only to build new triggers is refused rather than quietly rewriting one
an admin wrote by reusing its name. Omitted means unchanged and `null` clears, as
`alter_table` already did; changing the action without new settings is refused
rather than half-applied; and changing the event to one that has no row clears
the table and the condition, saying so in the result rather than leaving the
model to notice.

**Secrets are masked on the way out and merged back on the way in.** A tool
result is written into `_sc_runs` and re-read into the provider's context on
every later turn, so a key that reaches one has been copied somewhere nobody
thought about. `redact_attrs`/`merge_secrets` apply here for that reason rather
than as an access control — the caller is an admin either way, since every tool
still refuses a conversation whose user is not role 1.

Underneath, `sc-action` grew a **`TriggerObserver`**, the sibling of
`SchemaObserver` and installed the same way: a mounted application projects its
exposed triggers as endpoints at mount time, and that projection has to follow a
change whether the change came from a handler, a restore or an agent. It is
notified from `TriggerDispatcher::reload` — the one thing every writer calls
afterwards — and the handlers' own `refresh_triggers` calls are gone rather than
double-firing beside it.

## `subagent`: an agent an agent can be given

An agent can now hand a task to **another agent**. `subagent` joins the built-in
trait set: configured with one agent by name, it offers the model one tool —
`delegate_to_researcher` — that runs that agent's whole loop, with its own system
prompt, its own tools, its own step budget and its own row in `_sc_runs`, and
hands back what it concluded. So "the agent that reads the library" and "the
agent that talks to people" are two records with two role floors, and the second
is composed of the first rather than being given its tools.

**Delegation, not handoff.** The other shape — transfer the conversation, let the
specialist own every message from then on — is deliberately not what this is. A
run has one subject and one authority recorded on it, and a transcript that
changed agent halfway would be two agents' work under one heading; it would also
have to swap the tool set mid-conversation, which is the one thing a `Turn`
refuses. Here the parent stays answerable for the answer and the child is its own
run, linked to its parent by `parent_run` and `delegated_by` on its row.

**The child inherits the caller and nothing else.** It runs as the parent run's
own `RunCaller`, so §7.3's ownership and row-level security answer with the
chatting person's rows one level down and delegation cannot become an escalation;
the sub-agent's own `min_role` gates it on top of that, which is `run_trigger`'s
rule read for an agent. What it does *not* inherit is the conversation — and that
is the point rather than a limitation. The whole reason to delegate is that the
specialist's twenty tool calls happen in a context window that is not the
parent's; a child handed its parent's history would spend exactly the tokens the
delegation was meant to save.

**Which makes the briefing the entire channel, so the tool asks for one
properly.** Not a sentence: a `task` (required), the `context` the sub-agent
cannot see for itself, and the `output` wanted, assembled under headings with a
framing line naming the parent and saying that only the final message travels
back. Both halves answer failure modes that are well documented in systems built
this way — a model handed one free-text field writes "look into that", and a
sub-agent that does not know its last message is the deliverable does the work and
reports none of it.

**A cycle is refused by name; a chain is refused by number.** The runner carries
the delegation chain, so `a → b → a` comes back with the path in it rather than as
a budget message — the depth limit would eventually stop it too, and sending the
admin to a number when the fault is a loop they can see is the worse error. Depth
is a per-instance setting (default 3, ceiling 5) and bounds *cost*, since
termination is already handled. The one cycle visible without running anything —
an agent naming itself — is refused on save, where it is cheapest to fix.

**A sub-agent that reported nothing is a failed delegation, not an empty
answer.** Out of steps, or having called tools all turn and then said nothing, it
comes back as a tool **error** naming its run and telling the parent what to do
differently. Handing that back as `answer: ""` is how a parent ends up telling
somebody there was nothing to find. What did come back comes back *verbatim* — a
paraphrase is a second chance to lose the finding — beside the child's run id, so
the working is read out of `_sc_runs` rather than shipped into the parent's
context.

Underneath, `sc-agent` grew a **`Delegator` seam** and `TraitContext` a third
capability beside the JavaScript evaluator and the trigger dispatcher, offered by
`Runner::with_subagents(connector)`. A trait that names a sub-agent cannot start a
run itself — it has no trait registry, no provider connector and no idea how deep
the chain already is, and all three live on the runner already driving it. A
context without one answers with `require_delegate`'s configuration error rather
than finding a second way to run an agent. It takes the *connector* rather than a
connected provider because the sub-agent names a provider and a model of its own;
delegating to a cheaper model is much of the point. Both places a run is created
in production pass it: the chat socket and the `run_agent` action, so an agent a
trigger started delegates exactly as one a person is chatting with does.

The child does not stream to the parent's observer — its deltas are a different
conversation, and interleaving them would render one agent's thinking as
another's. The chat panel shows the delegation as a tool call and its result; the
sub-agent's transcript is `getRun`-able.

Ten tests against a real database and scripted providers: the whole shape end to
end, the isolation in both directions (the child's request carries the briefing
and no part of the parent's message; the parent's transcript carries the answer
and none of the child's working), the caller travelling down into an ownership
formula, the role floor, the cycle, the depth, the empty report, the calling-side
step budget, and the configuration checked on save and again on load.

## The `send_email` action, MJML bodies and file attachments

A trigger can now send an email. `send_email` joins the built-in set, and every
part of the message an admin configures — `to`, `cc`, `bcc`, `from`, the subject
and both bodies — is a `{{ }}` **template** in the formula language everything
else here is written in, validated in the event's scope when the trigger is saved
and rendered against the row when it fires. So a receipt is configured by writing
what it says: `{{ customerⱵemail }}` as the recipient, `Receipt for order
{{ id }}` as the subject, and the order's own values in the body — with the Ⱶ-path
*prefetched*, which is the whole reason `render_event_template` exists.

**The escaping follows the part, not the sigil.** The recipients, the subject and
the text body render as text; the HTML body renders as HTML. A subject put
through the HTML rule turns `Tea & Coffee` into `Tea &amp; Coffee`, and a
plain-text receipt full of entities is what nobody wants.

**The HTML body can be written as MJML.** Tick `mjml` and the body is compiled
through [mrml](https://crates.io/crates/mrml) — the Rust reimplementation of
[MJML](https://mjml.io) — into the nest of tables and conditional comments a
message has to be for Outlook to lay it out. Three things about that are
deliberate. It is compiled **after** interpolation, so the `{{ }}` tokens are
written into the MJML *source* and escaped by the ordinary HTML rule on the way
in; interpolating into the generated tables instead would drop a rendered value
somewhere the author never looked. It is a **declared flag**, never inferred from
markup that happens to start with `<mjml>`, because guessing what a body is would
make a plain HTML body's meaning depend on its first tag. And a **static** MJML
body is compiled at save as well as at send, so a missing `</mj-section>` is a
message on the form rather than a Tuesday-night failure. Compiling in process is
the point of choosing mrml over MJML's own implementation: v1 needs a Node
sidecar for this, and this is a function call.

`mj-include` is refused, because the parser's default loader refuses it: a
template is data an admin typed, and it must not be able to make the server read
a file or fetch a URL.

**A file the row points at can travel with the message.** Every **File field**
of the trigger's table becomes a checkbox on the action's form, and a ticked one
attaches the file that row's path names — read from the store that field is
declared against, named after the file rather than the column, and typed from its
extension by the same `mime_for_path` the File field's own `mime_allow` check
uses.

That checkbox is the first place a setting *declaration* depends on the table.
`Action::config_spec_for(catalog, channel)` is the seam: the default is the
static spec every other action has, and `send_email` adds one boolean per File
field. `validate_trigger` then validates against **that** spec, which is what
makes `attach_invoice` on a table with no `invoice` file field an unknown setting
rather than a value carried quietly along — so the check happens when the table
changes underneath a trigger, not at 3am. `listActions` grew an optional
`?table=`, and the trigger form re-reads the declarations whenever the table
changes; the screen still knows nothing about attachments, which is the whole
point of settings-as-data.

The rules around a file are the ones the rest of this system already follows. A
**null path attaches nothing and is not an error** — an order with no invoice yet
is data, and failing the receipt over it would break the trigger on every row
that is merely incomplete. Anything else *is* an error naming the field: a store
that is not connected, a path that points at nothing, a file past 20 MB. That
limit is not arbitrary — providers cap a message near 25 MB and base64 adds a
third on the way in, so past it the message is one the mail server will refuse
after it has been built, and being told which field and how big beats reading
"552 message size exceeds fixed limit".

Structurally, attachments make the message a `multipart/mixed` whose first part
is the body — the two bodies staying `alternative` *inside* it. Getting that
nesting backwards is how a receipt arrives as three files and no text, so it is
asserted, including once across a real SMTP socket with bytes that would
otherwise end the DATA section early.

**The mailer reaches the action the way the evaluator does** — through
`ActionContext`, absent in contexts that have none, so an action asking for one
out of context gets a named configuration error rather than doing nothing. The
two are now one `ActionServices` on the dispatcher, because they have the same
shape and the same rule, and the next one is a field rather than another argument
at every call site.

What a server installs is **`SettingsMailer`**, which reads the saved settings for
every message rather than being built from them once at boot. That is not
fastidiousness: the Email settings section promises that changes take effect on
the next message and need no restart, and a transport captured at boot would make
that false. Building one opens no socket, and the settings come from a catalog
that already caches them.

`Mailer` grew a second method, `sender()`. The from-address is a property of the
**transport** — the configured `email_from` for SMTP, an authorised mailbox for
the Graph transport that does not exist yet — so the action asks for it instead
of reading settings it otherwise has no business knowing about. It is also the
one place "this installation sends no mail" is discovered, which is why an
unconfigured installation fails with a sentence naming Settings → Email before
anything is rendered or connected to.

**The `fetch` action's URL is a template too**, which is what makes "one central
interpolation facility" a fact rather than a claim. A URL with no `{{` is parsed
and checked exactly as before; one with tokens has its tokens resolved against the
event's table at save and its URL parsed at send, with the same "not a valid URL"
message and the same refusal of any scheme that is not http(s) — which matters
more now that part of the URL is data.

Tested against a real database, a real V8, a real local file store and the
recording mailer, so the assertions are about the *message that would have gone
out*: the joined recipient, the escaped HTML and unescaped text, a null column
rendering as nothing, the compiled MJML with its interpolated values inside the
generated tables, the invoice's own bytes as an attachment, every way the
configuration can be wrong refused while the admin is still looking at the form,
and the unconfigured installation's error.

## Email settings, a transport, and a button that proves they work

An admin can now open **Settings → Email**, type an SMTP host, port, security
mode, credentials and from-address, save, press **Send test email** and receive
one. Four pieces, and each is where it is for a reason.

**The settings are ordinary declared config keys** (`sc-config::email`), beside
`ssl.rs`, so the settings screen renders them with no code that knows what an
SMTP port is. The password is a `secret` like the TLS private key: redacted on
read, and a save that posts the sentinel back keeps what is stored. Like it, it
is *not* encrypted at rest — it sits in the primary database, and saying so is
better than implying a protection a database dump would disprove.

**The cross-field rules live with the keys**, and every one of them is refused by
the name of the setting that is wrong: a host with no from-address, a username
with no password, a password with no username, a from-address that is not a
mailbox. One of them is a security rule rather than a coherence rule —
`smtp_security = none` *with* credentials is refused outright, because it would
put the password on the wire in the clear, and the message says that rather than
just "invalid". `EmailSettings::load` answers `Option`: an installation with no
`smtp_host` has no transport, which is a different answer from a transport whose
settings are wrong, and the two get different messages.

**The Settings screen's tabs are now the sections the server declared**, plus
Backup. `SETTINGS_TABS` was a constant naming `ssl`, and adding Email under a tab
labelled "SSL" is the answer that arrangement gives. Deriving them means a
section added server-side gets a tab with no change in the SPA — the point of
declaring settings as data — and it means a tab with no section cannot exist,
because there is nowhere for one to come from. Saving stays **one act** over the
whole bag from whichever tab is showing: the tabs partition the display, not the
transaction, because the server validates the settings together and a per-tab
save would be a way to leave half a configuration applied.

**The transport is a trait with one implementation.** The new `sc-email` crate
(layer 6, beside `sc-llm`) holds `Email`, the `Mailer` trait, `SmtpMailer` over
[lettre](https://lettre.rs), and `RecordingMailer`. The trait is not speculation
about future providers: it is what will let the `send_email` action's tests
assert *what would have been sent* without an SMTP server, and it is the seam the
OAuth2/Graph transports and the system emails plug into later. lettre's feature
set is chosen to add **no new crates to the tree** — `aws-lc-rs` is the crypto
provider the workspace's rustls pin already compiles in, and
`rustls-platform-verifier` is the root store reqwest already brings, so a mail
server's certificate is checked against exactly the roots a webhook's is.

This crate arrives a phase early because the test-email button has nothing to
send through without it.

**Addresses are parsed, not concatenated**, and parsed *once*: `Mailbox` and
`parse_mailbox` are in `sc-config` beside the setting they check, and the
transport reuses them rather than having a second grammar for what an address is.
`parse_recipients` splits a rendered list on the commas that separate addresses
and not on the ones inside a quoted display name — `"Lovelace, Ada"
<ada@example.com>` is one recipient — and names the address it rejected, because
a recipient string will be a *template* and knowing which of three addresses a
row produced badly is the difference between a report and a shrug.

**`sendTestEmail` sends through what is stored, not what is typed**, and the note
under the button says to save first. A button that tested the boxes would report
success on a configuration that is not the one this installation sends with. The
transport's error comes back **verbatim**: "connection refused", "authentication
failed" and "relay access denied" are three different problems with three
different fixes, and a friendlier summary would throw away the only thing that
makes the button worth pressing.

Tested against a **real SMTP conversation** — `sc_test_harness::TestSmtp` binds a
listener on loopback and speaks enough of the protocol to accept a message — for
both the transport and the button, because a trait-only test proves nothing about
whether lettre was wired up correctly. It asserts what the server *received*: the
envelope, the headers, that a blind copy is a recipient and not a header, and
that credentials are offered only when a username is configured.

## One interpolation facility: a `{{ }}` token is a formula

Saltcorn 1 has an interpolator *and* a formula evaluator — two scopes, two
parsers, two failure modes. v2 has one. `sc_expr::Template` splits a string into
literal runs and `{{ }}` tokens and hands every token to `Formula::parse`, so a
subject line is the same language as an ownership rule, a calculated field and a
trigger's `only_if`:

```
Receipt for order {{ id }} — {{ customerⱵemail }}
```

The consequences are the reason for doing it this way. A template
**validates at save time** against the same `SchemaShape` an `only_if` does, so
`{{ totl }}` is refused in front of the admin — naming both the identifier and
the token it is in — rather than at 3am. Its free variables come out of the
ordinary `Analysis`, so `{{ customerⱵemail }}` is prefetched by the same
`sc_catalog::prefetch_bindings` that resolves a Ⱶ-path anywhere else. And
rendering is the one `JsEvaluator`, so `{{ price * quantity }}` means in a
message exactly what it means in a column.

**v1's three sigils, kept exactly**, because a v1 application's templates are the
corpus this has to accept: `{{ x }}` renders the value, `{{! x }}` renders it
without escaping, and `{{= x }}` renders it and then interpolates the result
again in the same scope. The sigil is the character straight after `{{`, which
leaves `{{ !x }}` the negation it reads as.

Two deliberate departures. **Re-interpolation is bounded** at five passes: v1's
is not, so a row holding `{{= self }}` renders forever, and here the bound is hit
and the error names the template. **A missing binding is an error, not an empty
string** — every name was classified against the schema when the template was
saved, so an unresolvable one at render time means the schema changed underneath,
which this system reports rather than absorbs. A **null column** is still the
empty string: that is data, not a mistake.

**Escaping is a property of the render, not only of the sigil.** `render_html`
escapes bare tokens and `render_text` does not, because a subject line put
through the HTML rule reads `Tea &amp; Coffee` in the mail client and a
`text/plain` body becomes a page of entities. `{{! }}` and `{{= }}` mean the same
in both.

Above it, `sc-action` gains the two helpers an action's templates go through —
`check_template`, run on save *and* on load like every other configuration check,
and `render_event_template`, which renders against the event with every Ⱶ-path
**prefetched**. That prefetch is why it is a new helper rather than a call of
`event_formula_value`, which does not prefetch and would render a customer's
address as an unbound-name error. A template's bare scope is the event's **row**
(`template_scope`), not the empty `EVENT_SCOPE` a configured formula gets:
`Receipt for order {{ id }}` is how a person writes a subject line, and the
ambiguity that makes a bare identifier wrong in an `insert_row` value does not
exist where there is exactly one row in view. A constant template — most subject
lines — costs one string search and never reaches the isolate.

**`EventBindings` now binds `row` when the event *has* one**, rather than when
its kind is a table event. That is the honest rule either way, and it is the one
a trigger run against a row of its table needs: the row button that milestone is
about carries a row on a `none` event, and its templates must read it exactly as
an `update` trigger's do. `old` stays a property of the kind — only a table event
has a "before".

## The generated TypeScript client: one object per table, typed rows, and a typed `select`

The client an application gets had four methods per table — `listTasks`,
`createTasks`, `updateTasks`, `deleteTasks` — and every one of them spoke in
`unknown`, because an endpoint knows how a request is made and nothing about the
table behind it. Both halves of that are fixed here.

**An `EndpointSet` now carries the tables behind its endpoints.** A new
`ResourceModel` (`crates/sc-api/src/resource.rs`) records a table's columns and
their wire types, which of them a write may set, which are keys into *another
projected table*, and the endpoint name each operation is performed by. It
restates nothing an endpoint already knows: it names its endpoints rather than
copying their paths or auth, so an operation the projection did not register — a
keyless table has no `update` — is simply absent, and `register_resource` refuses
a model naming an endpoint the set does not have. `RestProvider::project` builds
one per table; `app_endpoints_with` carries them across providers.

**So a table is one object with methods, over a row type of its own.**

```ts
const posts: PostsRow[] = await api.posts.list();
const one = await api.posts.get(1);
const created = await api.posts.create({ title: "hello", author: 1 });
await api.posts.update(created.id, { title: "goodbye" });
await api.posts.delete(created.id);
await api.posts.cover.upload(1, "cover.png", file);
```

**There is one interface per table, and the write shapes are derived from it.** A
row and an insert hold the same columns with the same types and differ only in
which of them the *caller* has to supply, so the client says that difference as
two lists of column names rather than as a second copy of the table:

```ts
export interface PostsRow { id: number; title: string; published: string | null; … }
export type PostsInsert = Insertable<PostsRow, "title" | "author", "word_count">;
export type PostsUpdate = Partial<PostsInsert>;
```

`Insertable<Row, Required, ReadOnly>` — `Pick` the required, `Partial` the rest,
`Omit` the read-only, flattened so a hover shows the object — lives in the helper.
A `NOT NULL` column with no default is required; a key that numbers itself is not;
a calculated column is in the row and in no write, which is the rule the row layer
already enforced at runtime. Rename a column and the two lists cannot go on
describing a table that no longer exists. `get(id)` is `list` with the key filtered and
one row asked for, so it needs no endpoint of its own. Endpoints with no table
behind them — the app's own `login`/`logout`/`whoami`, exposed triggers, custom SQL
queries — stay loose methods, because that is what they are. A table whose name
collides with one of those takes a `Table` suffix rather than shadowing it.

**A read's query string is typed against the table's own columns**: `filter` is one
entry per column and each value is `` `${FilterOp}.${string}` ``, `order` is a
column optionally suffixed `.asc`/`.desc`. A column that is not there is now a
compile error rather than the 400 it used to be.

**And the `select` is read by the compiler.** `?select=` changes the shape of the
answer, so the answer's *type* is computed from the string:

```ts
const rows = await api.posts.list({ select: "id,author(name,country)" });
rows[0].author?.name; // string | undefined — the key is nullable, so the embed is
```

That is a small parser written in TypeScript's type system (split on top-level
commas, `alias:column`, `key(…)` embeds to any depth), emitted once per client and
only when the set has tables. A select assembled at runtime — typed `string`, not a
literal — degrades to the whole row plus unknown extras rather than to a lie about
it. This is why there is one `list` rather than one method for rows and another for
embeds.

**The React runtime follows.** `hooks.ts` calls `api.tasks.list()` and re-exports
the client's row types instead of declaring a second copy of them; the primary key
type a hook takes is read off the resource model, so the hook and the client method
it calls cannot disagree about it.

**A new generated file, `src/saltcorn/store.ts`: the optimistic layer every app
was going to write anyway.** `useTasksStore()` answers `rows` with the writes in
flight already applied, plus `add` / `update` / `remove` that show the change at
once, take it back if the server refuses it, and drop the local copy when the
server's own answer arrives. A row that is not saved yet carries `$pending`, and
`remove` answers the row **as it was**, which is what an undo has to put back. It
is general because nothing in it knows which table it is looking at: a table says
only which row is which and what an unsaved one looks like. Policy — toasts, undo
timers, sorting — is deliberately not in it. A table whose rows cannot be addressed
gets no store, and the file says so rather than importing a runtime it cannot use.
The scaffolded page for a writable table is now built on the store, so a new row
appears as it is typed.

**The generic half of the client is a file of its own, `helper.ts`.** How a
request is made, how a failure is reported, `ClientOptions`, and the types a
read's `select`/`filter`/`order` are expressed in are the *same text* in every
application — so they are no longer a hundred and fifty lines of preamble in
front of the part that is about this application. `client.ts` imports exactly
what it names (an import of a helper it has no use for would be a
`noUnusedLocals` failure in somebody else's project) and re-exports the shared
vocabulary, so a consumer still has one module to import from. Every emitter
writes the pair: the scaffold's `src/saltcorn/`, an app's configured client path,
and the two checked-in admin clients (`ui/admin/src/helper.ts`,
`ui/ide/src/helper.ts`, regenerated by the same one command). The import is
extensionless — `"./helper"` — because a `.ts` extension is an error in a
TypeScript project unless `allowImportingTsExtensions` is on, and no consumer
should be made to turn that on for a generated file.

Tested by `tsc` rather than by string matching: `crates/sc-api/tests/typescript_typecheck.rs`
type-checks a two-table client and asserts the *negative* half with
`@ts-expect-error` — a filter on a column that does not exist, a selected row
reaching for a column it did not select, a string where the key is a number, an
insert missing a required column, a write to a calculated column. The scaffold's
own `npm run build` type-check covers the hooks, the store and the generated page,
and both admin SPAs type-check against the regenerated pair.

## Git file stores: choose the checkout directory, or adopt one that exists

A git store now has a **working-copy directory** setting, set when the store is
created and fixed thereafter, and the repository URL is no longer required. The
two together make a case that was previously impossible: a repository someone had
already cloned onto the server could not be connected as a store — the admin had
to hand Saltcorn a URL and let it clone a second copy somewhere it chose.

**A directory that already holds a checkout is adopted, not cloned over.** That
was already `ensure_cloned`'s rule for a store's own second save; it is now the
rule whether the working tree got there by Saltcorn's first clone or by someone
typing `git clone` on that box a year ago. So a store created with a directory and
**no URL** connects to the tree as it stands, uncommitted work included, and
pushes to the remote that checkout already has. A directory with neither a
checkout nor a URL to clone from is the one failure, and it names both ways out.

**Neither `url` nor `directory` is declared required, and the pairing is checked
by the backend.** A per-field spec can say "this is required"; it cannot say "one
of these two". So `git_config_spec` marks neither, and `validate_git_config` — run
from `validate_file_store_config` after the generic field-by-field pass — rejects a
store that has said nothing about where its repository comes from. It is still a
*structural* check in the sense the backend module means: it asks whether the admin
has described a store, not whether the directory is there, so a named directory
that has not been cloned into yet still saves and is still editable.

**`create_only` is a new `FormField` property, not a git special case.** The
directory says *where the working tree was put*, so an edit that changed it would
abandon the tree rather than move it. Marking that on the declaration reaches both
consumers at once, exactly as `secret` does: the admin form renders the control
read-only on an edit, and `preserve_create_only` puts the stored value back over
whatever a save submits — so a request that went round the form cannot repoint a
working tree either. The form is the courtesy; the save path is the rule. Any
backend, including one supplied by a plugin, gets the same behaviour by declaring
the flag.

**A store that left the directory blank is shown the one it got.** Blank stays
blank in the row, because the location is derived and a stored copy of a derived
answer is a second answer that can disagree — but the control is read-only on an
edit, so an empty one would leave the admin with nowhere to learn where their
files are. `display_config` fills it in on the way out only, and the round trip
cannot pin it: the form hands the value straight back on the next save and
`preserve_create_only` drops it again, there being nothing stored for it. It is a
registry function rather than a line in the serialiser, so a backend added later
that settles a blank setting for itself gets the same treatment by saying so.

**`clone_path` gained a middle term.** The recorded `clone_path` attribute still
wins, then the configured directory, then the Saltcorn-owned
`<data dir>/git-stores/<name>`. The attribute outranks the setting because the two
answer different questions — the setting is where the admin asked for the
checkout, the attribute is where one *is* — and following the attribute is what
keeps a renamed store's working tree reachable.

## Table constraints: rules the database keeps

A table can now carry the four things Saltcorn 1's Constraints screen offers —
**jointly-unique** keys, **indexes**, a **full-text search** index and **row
constraints** — on a Constraints card on the table page. What is different is
where they end up: in the database, where every route to the data meets them.

**A constraint is not stored anywhere.** There is no `_sc_constraints` table, and
that is §9's own rule rather than an omission: a unique constraint, an index and a
trigger are facts the database already holds, and a metadata table for them would
be a second answer to a question introspection answers. So they are created as the
objects they are and **read back** — `PhysicalTable::constraints` from
`introspect()`, `Table::constraints` after the merge, beside the primary key and
the foreign keys. Three things follow, all of them the point: a `UNIQUE` somebody
added in `psql` is listed on the screen (marked **External**, since Saltcorn did
not make it), a `pg_dump` and restore keeps a table's constraints with no metadata
to restore beside them, and there is no second copy that can disagree.

**What the database cannot hold, the comment holds.** Two things are Saltcorn's
rather than Postgres's — the error message an admin writes for a violation, and
the formula a row constraint was generated from, since a `plpgsql` body is not a
formula — and both ride in the object's own `COMMENT` as one JSON object under a
`saltcorn_constraint` key. A comment is attached to the object, dropped with it
and carried by a dump, so the metadata cannot outlive what it describes. A comment
that is absent, is prose, or is JSON without that key leaves a constraint that
still works and is still listed. A *trigger* without it is not treated as a
constraint at all: a trigger is also how the rest of the world implements auditing
and denormalisation, and offering somebody's audit trigger as a rule to delete
would be offering it as something it is not.

**A row constraint is a constraint trigger, not a `CHECK`.** GOALS requires
constraint formulae to use join fields and aggregations, and a `CHECK` may not
query another table. Saltcorn 1 emitted a `CHECK` when the formula was simple
enough and *silently enforced nothing* when it was not; principle 5 does not allow
the second half. So there is one mechanism whatever the formula says: a generated
`plpgsql` function evaluating the **same** `sc_query::Expr` the ownership
translator produces, over `(SELECT (NEW).*)` aliased to the table's own name — so
the expression needs no rewriting and a formula means one thing whether a policy,
the runtime or this trigger enforces it. It is `DEFERRABLE INITIALLY IMMEDIATE`
for the reason the foreign keys are: unchanged at the statement, and deferrable to
commit by a caller holding the transaction, which is what lets rows that point at
each other arrive in a file's order. `user` and the operation flags are refused by
name, because a trigger has no session and fires the same way on every write.

**The admin's sentence reaches whoever broke the rule.** A unique violation used
to surface as `duplicate key value violates unique constraint "sc_uq_…"`, naming
an object the user has never heard of. The generated trigger names itself in the
error's `CONSTRAINT` field exactly as Postgres does for a unique violation, so
`sc-api::rows` has one lookup for both and a broken rule is a 400 carrying the
admin's own words rather than a 500 carrying the database's. For that the Postgres
driver now keeps the **SQLSTATE** and the constraint name in the text it formats
(`<message> [23505] (constraint "…")`) — which is also what `sc-catalog`'s
policy-violation mapping had been reduced to matching message text for want of,
and what a deferred failure at `COMMIT` had no way to report at all.

**Names say what a constraint is** — `sc_uq_<table>_<fields>`,
`sc_ix_<table>_<field>`, `sc_fts_<table>`, `sc_ck_<table>_<name>` — so adding the
same rule twice collides by name instead of quietly creating two constraints
enforcing one rule, and a name past Postgres's 63 bytes is truncated with a hash
suffix rather than silently truncated by the backend into another name.

**A backup carries them.** Constraints live in the database and nowhere else, so
the backup writes what the table *reported* — a hand-made `UNIQUE` included — and
the restore adds them **after the rows**, the order `pg_dump` uses: a unique
constraint over restored data is checked in one pass rather than once per insert,
and a row constraint created first would have judged every row as it arrived,
against a table whose other rows were not in yet. Without this a restore would
have handed back a table that accepts what the original refused and said nothing
about it. The one thing it will not do is approximate: an index over an
expression that Saltcorn did not create is reported as skipped, because what
could be rebuilt from the listing is an index over no columns.

**The schema editor knows what a rule needs.** A field a constraint names cannot
be dropped from under it: Postgres would drop the unique constraint and the index
along with the column without a word, and would leave a row constraint's trigger
to fail at the next write with an error about a column nobody can find. And a
table's full-text index is **rebuilt** whenever its text fields change, because it
is over every text field and an index that quietly stopped covering the table it
claims to cover is the failure nobody notices until a search comes back empty.

## Settings picks its section with pills

The Settings screen's SSL/Backup switcher is `nav nav-pills` rather than `nav
nav-tabs`. Tabler styles both, and nothing but the class changes: the folder-tab
strip becomes a row of rounded buttons with the showing one filled.

## A key that is switched on afterwards fills itself in too

The primary-key tick box gave a table its key but not the means to write a row into
it. A field *created* with the box already ticked got a generator — an `int` key
stored as an identity column, a `uuid` key given `DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()` — but
ticking the box on a column that already existed emitted `SET PRIMARY KEY` and
nothing else. The same checkbox therefore meant two different things depending on
when it was ticked, and the later meaning was "a key somebody types by hand for
every row". Since a table getting its key late is the *ordinary* way a table gets
one (nothing invents an `id`), that was the common case.

**A column can now be given a generator after the fact.** `SchemaChange::SetColumnGenerator`
(new) renders as drop-identity-if-exists, `DROP DEFAULT`, then whichever generator was
asked for — identity first because Postgres refuses `DROP DEFAULT` on an identity column
outright. Adding an identity is followed by a `setval` past the largest key already in
the table: Postgres does not look at the data when adding one, and a sequence starting
at 1 would collide with the first row on the very next insert. The schema editor emits
it after `SET PRIMARY KEY`, which is what makes the column `NOT NULL` — Postgres will
not make a nullable column an identity.

**Generation is one concept now, in one place.** A column carries an optional
`ColumnGenerator` — `Identity` or `Default(sql)` — rather than a `default` string beside
an `identity` flag that could disagree. `schema_edit::key_generator` is the single answer
to "what does a key of this type do about its own value", asked both when the field is
created and when the box is ticked later. The `IDENTITY_PK_SQL_TYPE` hack that smuggled
`generated by default as identity` through a field's *SQL type* string — and the
`.split(" generated ")` in `key_storage_type` that had to undo it before a foreign key
could point at such a key — are both gone.

**Introspection reads it back**, so the admin UI can say what a key actually does.
`information_schema.is_identity` is read alongside `column_default` (an identity column
has no default to report, so reading only the second made every identity key look like a
key nobody fills in), and `listFields` reports `generated` per field. Three consequences:
the note under the **Primary key** tick box now describes an existing key by what its
column does and a key about to be made by what ticking the box will do to it — which
differ for a table created outside the admin UI, whose `id` is an ordinary `bigint`
nobody fills in; the row editor leaves a blank box out of the insert for any generated
column rather than assuming the key is one; and the generated `schema.sql` an app carries
now shows the identity clause instead of a bare `int8`.

**A restore winds the numbering past the rows it wrote.** This was already broken and
had no way to be fixed: a backup carries its rows' keys and inserts them as given (the
identity is `BY DEFAULT`, which is the whole reason it is not `ALWAYS`), but the sequence
behind the column never saw those inserts and stayed at 1 — so the first row written
after a restore was handed a key a restored row already had, and the insert failed on the
primary key. The restore now re-applies the generator per identity column once its rows
are in, which is the same change the schema editor emits when a key is switched on over
rows that are already there. Reported rather than raised: a restore that got the rows in
is worth having even if one sequence could not be wound.

**Taking the key off leaves the generator alone**, for the reason the `NOT NULL` is left
alone: dropping it would rewrite the column, and a former key that still numbers itself
is a perfectly ordinary column.

Tests: the backup round-trip test now furnishes its table with a key that numbers itself
and adds a row on the far side, which fails with `duplicate key value violates unique
constraint "books_pkey"` without the rewind. Three new primary-key API tests — an `int` column made the key numbers itself *from where the
rows left off* (rows with keys 4 and 7 already in the table, next insert gets 8), a
`uuid` column made the key generates itself, and a `text` key reports `generated: false`
and is refused when a write omits it. Plus DDL rendering tests for all three
`SetColumnGenerator` shapes, and UI tests for the three things the note under the tick
box can say.

## The primary key is a field like any other

GOALS has always said it: *"do not create primary key fields when table is created.
User must create primary key fields like other fields."* The schema editor did the
opposite — every created table got a `bigint generated by default as identity`
column called `id`, unasked, and declaring one was an error. That is gone.

**A created table has no columns and no key.** The field editor has a **Primary
key** tick beside Nullable, and that is what gives a table its key. Tick it on more
than one field and the key is composite, in field order — which the goals ask the
whole system to work with, and which `SchemaChange::SetPrimaryKey` (new) is what
makes reachable: Postgres has no `ALTER PRIMARY KEY`, so it renders as
drop-constraint-if-exists, `SET NOT NULL` per column, `ADD PRIMARY KEY`. No columns
means no key, which is the state every table starts in and must be able to return
to.

**A key still fills itself in**, or "create the key yourself" would mean "and now
type one for every row": a `primary_key` field of type `int` is created as
`bigint generated by default as identity`, and one of type `uuid` gets
`DEFAULT gen_random_uuid()`. Any other type is created as itself and the writer
supplies the value — a text key typed into the row editor, or a CSV's own column.
Both accept an explicit value, so an import still chooses its own keys.

**A table without a key says so, in red**, on the field list where it is fixed: its
rows cannot be edited or deleted one at a time, nothing can reference it, and an
import cannot replace a row it already has. The row editor now addresses rows by
the table's *actual* key rather than by a column literally called `id` — a key
called `code` works, a blank key input means "the database assigns it", and with no
key the Edit and Delete buttons are gone rather than broken.

`primary_key` is also the one column property `updateField` may change. That is a
deliberate exception to "an edit writes only the overlay" (§3.3): since nothing
invents a key, a table that has none — imported from a CSV with no key column, or
built a field at a time — could otherwise only get one by being dropped and
recreated with its rows thrown away. The agent's `manage_table_admin` tool carries
the same flag and its description says a key is never invented; a restored backup
now keeps the key it had instead of losing it to an invented one.

## CSV import in one transaction, with deferred foreign keys

Every generated reference is now `DEFERRABLE INITIALLY IMMEDIATE`: every write
behaves exactly as before — a bad key is refused at the statement — but a caller
holding a transaction can defer the check to its commit. A constraint that was not
declared deferrable can never be deferred later, so this is the default rather than
something asked for per table.

The CSV import is that caller. It runs as **one transaction** with `SET CONSTRAINTS
ALL DEFERRED` and **a savepoint per row**, which is two properties at once: a row
may reference another row of the same file that has not arrived yet — Saltcorn 1's
self-joining `project` CSV, a child listed before its parent, now imports — and a
row that will not go in still does not take the others down with it. What cannot be
reported per line is a *deferred* violation, a key matching nothing anywhere in the
file: Postgres raises that at the commit, so the import fails whole and says so.

Making that possible needed a seam in the row layer: `rows::Executor`, either the
pooled provider (everything that was there before) or a transaction the caller
holds open, threaded through `create_row_in` / `update_row_in` / `select_values_in`
/ `run_read_in`. Reads go through it too, so a `Key` column given as a **summary
value** resolves against rows earlier lines of the same file inserted and nothing
has committed. Two consequences the caller owns, and the type's docs state: events
fire as the rows are written, before the commit, and a rollback undoes rows whose
events have already gone out.

**A CSV's `id` column becomes the table's key of whatever type its values are** —
integer, UUID or text — which is Saltcorn 1's `String`/`UUID` primary keys, now
that a key is a field like any other. A gap in it is still refused: a key cannot be
null. A file with no `id` column makes a table with no key, and the red banner
says so.

## A table from a CSV file, and an import that behaves like Saltcorn 1's

The tables list asked for a name in a box beside a Create button, which could only
make one kind of table. It is now a **+ New table** button over a dialog: a name, a
**Type** — *New database table* or *Create from CSV* — and, for the second, the
file. Choosing a file into an empty name box fills the name in from the file's own
name. The dialog's rules (when Create may be pressed, what a file suggests the
table be called) are in `ui/admin/src/newTable.ts` so they can be tested without a
browser; the screen is the React around them.

**Creating from a CSV deduces the schema and imports the rows.** One field per
header cell, named the way a label names a field (`Item Name` → `item_name`, the
label kept), typed by what every non-empty cell in that column turns out to be
(`bool`, `int`, `float`, `date`, `timestamp`, `uuid`, `json`, else `text`), and
`NOT NULL` when the column has no empty cell at all. The **whole** file is read to
decide, not a sample of it: Saltcorn 1 looks at the first 500 lines, and a type the
501st contradicts is a row rejected for a reason nothing in the file explains. A
column called `id` is not a field — it is the identity primary key every created
table gets (§3.3) — and its values become the rows' keys.

It is **all or nothing**, unlike an import into a table that already exists: a row
the deduced schema will not take means the deduction was wrong, so the table is
dropped and the errors reported rather than leaving a half-filled table nobody
asked for. `POST /api/tables/csv` (`createTableFromCsv`) is the endpoint; a
duplicated header is one column, and a header that cannot make an identifier at all
is refused before anything is created.

**The import into an existing table now matches Saltcorn 1** where it did not, with
a suite ported from that project's own CSV tests (`table_csv_import.rs`, 22 tests):

- a header cell finds its field by **name, by label, or by the name its label would
  make**, so a spreadsheet titled for people imports without being edited first;
- a column the table does not have is **ignored** rather than refusing the file.
  What catches the wrong file is the new check that every **required** field is
  covered by some column — refusing a real export for carrying a column nobody
  asked for would refuse most real exports;
- a file naming the **primary key upserts**: a key that is there replaces that row,
  a key that is not is inserted with it, a blank key cell is an ordinary insert, and
  the same key twice in one file is that line's error. The identity sequence is
  moved past the keys the file chose afterwards, or the next ordinary insert would
  collide with a row the import placed;
- a **`Key` column may hold the summary value** — the author's name, not the
  author's id — which is looked up in the target table (and cached per file); a
  summary that matches nothing is the row's error, naming the value and where it
  was looked for;
- **cells are trimmed**: `Joe Celko, 856` is how a person writes a CSV row, and
  ` 856` is not an integer to anything that parses one.

`importTableCsv` now answers with `updated` beside `inserted`, and the table page
says "3 replaced" when a file rewrote rows rather than adding them — "0 rows added"
for a file that rewrote the whole table reads as nothing having happened.

Four behaviours are deliberately **not** Saltcorn 1's, and each is a test that says
so: a date is ISO-8601 only (there is no locale to read `15.03.2011` against); a
large integer is an `int` column, because `int` here is 64 bits and Saltcorn 1's
fallback to text was working around a 32-bit one; a text or UUID `id` column is
refused rather than becoming a non-integer primary key; and a `Key` pointing
*forward* in the file is rejected on its line rather than deferred, because an
import is not a transaction here and the constraint is not deferrable.

## Backup and restore, on a tab of their own

The Settings screen was one form — the SSL section — and there was no way to get an
installation out of a database or into another one. It is now tabbed: **SSL** is the
settings form exactly as it was, and **Backup** is the pair of operations that
produce and consume a file. The split is by verb rather than by subject; a form
whose Save button sat beside a Restore button would be a form with two very
different consequences on it.

**A backup is the admin API's own JSON, in a zip.** Every metadata record it
carries — a table's settings and its columns, an application, an agent, a trigger,
a role — is written in the shape the endpoint that *lists* one returns, and read
back through the parser the endpoint that *creates* one uses (`handlers.rs`'s
`*_json` / `*_from_body` pairs, now `pub(crate)` for exactly this). A second
serialisation of an application would have been a second thing to keep in step with
the first, and its failure would have been silent: a field added to the form and
forgotten here, restored as its default a year later. Table rows and file contents
are the two things with no admin-API shape to borrow, so they are written as what
they are — a JSON array of row objects, and bytes. The layout, one directory per
kind:

```
manifest.json                       what this file holds, and when it was taken
tables/<table>/table.json           { table, fields } — the overlay + the columns
tables/<table>/rows.json            [ { column: value, … }, … ]
applications/<subdomain>.json       one application
file-stores/<store>/store.json      { definition, files: [ { path, meta } ] }
file-stores/<store>/files/<path>    the bytes, as they are
users.json                          { roles, users } — password hashes included
agents.json / triggers.json         [ … ]
settings/ssl.json                   the SSL section's stored values
```

**Backup now** opens a dialog over what this server has: multi-selects (the
component the application form's table picker uses) for table definitions, table
data, applications and file stores, and a tick box each for users and roles, agents,
triggers and the SSL settings. Everything is included by default. Three rules are
enforced where they are made rather than left to the server to discover:

- **rows cannot be included without their table's definition** — the data picker
  offers what the definitions picker has left, so unticking a table takes its rows
  with it. The server narrows the payload as well, because a client bug must not
  produce rows nothing can read;
- **a trigger on a table that is being left out is left out with it**. Triggers are
  one choice, not one per trigger, but a trigger whose `insert` channel names an
  excluded table has nothing to fire on;
- **the file is as sensitive as the database**, and the dialog says so where the
  choice is made. Nothing is redacted: a store's credentials and the TLS private key
  travel in the clear, because a backup carrying the redaction sentinel restores an
  installation that cannot serve HTTPS and a store that cannot connect.

The choice is **remembered as what was left out**, in `_sc_config` under
`backup_include`, and it is saved by taking the backup rather than by a Save button
of its own — the answer to "what should a backup include" is the backup that was
just taken. Exclusions rather than inclusions because a stored list of what to
include would freeze the installation as it was the day the dialog was tuned: the
table added next week would be missing from every backup after it, and nothing would
say so. `sc-config` grew `internal_defs()` for keys like this one — stored
configuration that no settings *form* renders, declared so the write is still
type-checked and still refuses an unknown key, but kept out of `config_spec()` so no
form grows a control for a value it cannot edit.

**Restore** takes a file, and the same dialog comes back over the manifest's
contents with everything ticked. One dialog for both, because the two questions are
the same question asked of two sources; the server describes what is available in
one shape whether it is describing this installation or an uploaded file, so the
screen never asks which it is looking at.

A restore **adds; it does not destroy.** Nothing drops a table, deletes a row,
replaces an account or repoints a file store that is already defined — an admin
restoring onto a server with things in it gets what was missing and is *told* what
was left alone, in a report of two lists. The alternative, a restore that makes the
server match the file, would be an admin one click from deleting the installation
they are standing in, and the destructive verbs already exist elsewhere in the admin
UI where each is individually deliberate. Concretely: an account matching by id or
email is kept (so a backup cannot overwrite the password of the admin running the
restore and lock them out of the screen they are on), an existing store keeps its
definition and the backup's files are written into it (a backup's store points at a
path on the machine it was taken from), an existing column is left at the type the
database says it is, and rows are inserted **without raising the table's insert
triggers** — a restore puts data back, it does not replay an installation's history.
Every item is restored on its own, so an application naming a table that is not in
the file loses that application and nothing else; tables are created in one pass and
their columns added in a second, which is what lets two tables that reference each
other be restored at all, and rows go in in dependency order.

**A restored application is built and mounted, not merely stored.** Restore and go:
an installation that came back without its applications serving is not one anybody
has finished restoring, and the admin has no way to know which of them was waiting
on a button. Each one therefore goes through the same `build_and_mount` the Build
button does — the bundler over the source tree the file stores restored moments
earlier, which is why applications are restored *after* them, and then a live
remount, so the app answers on its subdomain as soon as the restore finishes. A
first build installs the project's dependencies, so a source tree that arrived
without its `node_modules` is not a special case. A build that fails is a warning
against an application that is *saved* — the bundler's own diagnostics, and pressing
Build once its source is in place is the repair — and the one cost is time: a restore
including applications takes as long as their builds do, which the Restore card says
before the admin starts.

Two of the four operations are typed endpoints (`getBackupOptions`,
`restoreBackup`) and two are routes outside the endpoint set — `POST /backup/create`,
whose response *is* a zip, and `POST /backup/upload`, whose request is one — for the
reason the binary file upload is outside it: a `TypeSchema` has no bytes shape. Both
still dispatch through the handler registry, so both are behind the same session
lookup, the same admin check and the same CSRF middleware as everything typed;
`HandlerResponse` gained an optional `Download` for the one that answers with a file.
The restore is deliberately two requests — hand the file over, then say what to take
from it — with the archive held in memory for half an hour between them, because
what to restore cannot be chosen until the file has been read and re-uploading a
large archive to say so doubles the wait on the operation an admin is already nervous
about.

`zip` (8.x, `deflate-flate2-zlib-rs` only) is the one new dependency: DEFLATE
through a pure-Rust zlib, so §16's no-C-dependencies posture holds here as it does
for TLS, and the file opens with `unzip` like any other.

The round trip is tested where it matters — `backup_api.rs` furnishes one server
with a configured table, rows, a file store with a file in it, a role, a trigger and
a pasted certificate, backs it up through the HTTP route a browser posts to, and
restores it into a *second* server, then reads what arrived back through the ordinary
admin API rather than out of the zip.

## The users screen manages users

The users screen could list users and create one, with a form beside the list that asked
for an email, a password and a role. Everything else an administrator does to an account
had no way in: a user could not be edited at all, a column added to the users table was
invisible to the form that creates users, and the role a new user got by default was
Public — the role of somebody who has not signed in, which is never what the person
filling in the form meant.

The form is now generated from the users table. §7.1 makes that table the one table an
admin is invited to add columns to, so a fixed three-box form is wrong the moment they do:
the fields come from `listFields("users")` minus the columns the system owns (`id`, `role`,
`email`, `password_hash`, `disabled` — named once, in `sc_auth::SYSTEM_USER_COLUMNS`, and
mirrored in `ui/admin/src/userForm.ts`). Those columns travel in an `extra` bag on the wire,
coerced through the same `column_value` every row write uses, and the bag refuses a system
column: writing a password hash through a map of column values would have been a way in.

The form is also a dialog now, opened from "Add user" or from each row's Edit button, the
same shape the fields list on the table page has — the list stays the page and the edit is
the interruption. The default role is the role nearest 80, breaking a tie towards the lower
number so that an installation with roles at 60 and 100 does not land back on Public.

Each row carries a menu of the things that are *not* edits, each an endpoint of its own
because each does something a column write cannot:

- **Set random password** replaces the password with 20 characters from the OS CSPRNG,
  drawn by rejection sampling from an alphabet with `l`, `I`, `O` and `0` left out — a
  generated password is transcribed into a chat message and out of one, so it is chosen to
  be read aloud and the length covers the entropy.
- **Force logout** drops every session the account holds, without touching the account.
- **Disable user** stops it signing in *and* ends the sessions it is in the middle of; the
  row, the role and everything the admin recorded stay, which is the difference from
  deleting. A disabled account collapses into the same "no" as a wrong password, so the
  login screen does not become a way to enumerate addresses.
- **Become user** swaps the caller's session for that user's with no password. An admin who
  can reset that password can already sign in as them, so this is convenience rather than
  new authority — and it costs the admin their admin session, which `SessionAction::Start`
  now guarantees by ending whatever session the request arrived with.
- **Delete user**, and disabling, refuse the account the caller is signed in as: locking
  yourself out of the screen you are standing on is a guard rail worth having.

Creating a user with the password left blank generates one too, and both paths end in the
one dialog that shows a password in plain text: a monospace block with the site, the email
and the password, and a copy button, because the hash is all that is stored and this is the
only moment anybody can read it.

Behind it: `users.disabled` (nullable boolean, `NULL` = enabled) joins the users schema;
`authenticate` and session resolution both refuse a disabled user; `SessionStore` gained
`end_user_sessions`, and `SessionAction` an `EndUser` variant so a handler can ask for it
without holding the store. `listUsers` returns the whole row rather than three columns, and
`createUser` answers `{ user, generated_password }`.

## Minimum role is always a select

Every minimum-role input in the admin UI was an `<input type="number">` between 1 and 100:
the agent's, the trigger's, the file store's and the individual file's. A role is a row in
`_sc_roles` with a name an admin chose, so typing its number was the wrong question twice
over — it invited a number no role has, and it told the admin nothing about which number
meant which role. Only the table page's read/write rules were selects.

They are all selects now, over the roles `listRoles` reports. The table page's local
`RoleSelect` moved to `ui/admin/src/roleSelect.tsx` and is the one place a role is picked,
in two shapes because the two cases differ in what an empty value means and that is worth
being a type error rather than a convention: `RoleSelect` for a field that must name a role,
and `OptionalRoleSelect` for one that may name none — with the caller naming what having no
role means there ("Admin only" for an agent or a trigger, "Unrestricted" for a store or a
file), since that sentence is different on every screen and is the whole meaning of the
choice. `roleOptions` accordingly takes `number | null`, and still keeps a current role the
server no longer lists, so a store pointing at a deleted role does not silently widen its
access on the next save. The four forms now hold their minimum role as `number | null`
rather than a string awaiting `Number()`, and the file manager's *effective* role badge
names the role instead of printing "Role 40".

The number boxes that remain are not roles and stay as they are: an agent's temperature and
step limit, a periodic trigger's hour and minute, and the number of a role being created —
which is the one place a role number is genuinely being chosen rather than referenced.

## Add Example to formula placeholders 

Prepended "Example: " to placeholders for formulas in the admin ui, because due to low 
contrast in dark-mode, it is difficult to see if the input contents is the placeholder 
or an entered formula

## A field can be edited from the fields list

The fields list on the table page could add a field and drop one, and nothing in between.
A field's label, its rich type, its attributes and its kind are all overlay settings that
`updateField` has always been able to change (§3.3) — there was simply no way to reach it
from the admin UI, so a `String` field created with the wrong `max_length` had to be
dropped, taking its data with it, and made again.

Each row of the list now has an **Edit** button, and it opens the **same modal** the Add
button does, seeded from the field. One modal rather than two, because a field is one thing
to describe however it got there, and two forms would be two places for "a File's parameters
live on the `kind` object, not in `attributes`" to be got right or wrong independently. The
form's model moved out of the screen into `ui/admin/src/fieldForm.ts`, where the round trip —
a field as `listFields` reports it, into the form, back out as a request that means the same
thing — is a set of plain functions and is asserted without a browser.

What an edit may change is narrower than what an add may, and the modal says so rather than
offering what it would then drop: the **name** and the **nullability** are shown disabled
(a rename or a NOT NULL is a migration, which is out of scope), and so is **Calculated**,
since whether a field has a column at all is settled when it is made. Everything else — the
type, a rich type's attributes, a File's store and folder, a Key's target and summary — is
editable, and the modal gained **Label** and **Description** inputs, which the add form never
had. Those two are not a flourish: `updateField` is whole-object like `updateTable`, so a
form that could not *show* a label would have cleared it every time an admin opened a field
to change something else.

One server-side fix came out of the round trip. `updateField` recorded whatever `type` it was
given as the overlay's rich type, including a **basic** type name — and the editor sends back
the type it read, which for a plain `text` column is `text`. That left the overlay naming a
rich type that is not registered: `listFields` went on describing the field correctly while
the catalog reported it as broken. It now records only a rich name, which is what `createField`
has always stored for the same field (`resolve_field_type` already returned exactly this).
The new `field_api` test writes every field of a table straight back as it read it and asserts
both that nothing changed and that the catalog has no merge issues — which is the half that
`listFields` alone cannot show.

## The default port is 3032

The server bound `127.0.0.1:3000` unless told otherwise. 3000 is the busiest port on a
developer's machine — it is the default for Create React App, Next.js, Rails, Grafana and
Saltcorn 1 itself — so the first thing a new install did was collide with whatever was
already running, and the second was to be reachable at a URL that might belong to something
else. The default is now `127.0.0.1:3032`.

This is one number in `sc_server::DEFAULT_BIND`, mirrored in `sc-cli`'s `DEFAULT_HTTP_PORT`
(the port an application's URL carries when nothing said which — the build commands never
construct a `ServerConfig`, so it is named in both places). `--bind` is unchanged, and the
README and every tutorial now say `:3032` in the URLs they tell you to open.

## The table page is laid out the way Saltcorn 1 lays it out, and a table's rows go in and out as CSV

The table page was two columns — fields on the left, the row editor on the right — with the
settings form above both and a Drop button in the header. It is now the shape Saltcorn 1
settled on, which is the order an admin actually works in:

1. **Fields**, full width at the top. Nothing below it means anything until the columns do.
   Adding one is a **modal** behind an "Add field" button rather than a form beside the list:
   the form is much the taller of the two — a Key's three dependent selects, a rich type's
   whole attribute spec — so side by side it either squeezed the list into half a page or left
   a column of white space under it, depending on which type was chosen.
2. **Table data**, a shallow strip: the row count, and four things one does with the *data*
   rather than with the table — Edit, Download CSV, Upload CSV, and a `⋯` menu holding the
   rare and irreversible (Drop table, and Forget settings for a table that has any).
3. **Triggers on this table** — what fires when its rows change, with Create trigger.
4. **Edit table properties** — the `_sc_tables` overlay, last, because it is set once.

The **rows have moved to a screen of their own** (`#/tables/<name>/data`), which is what the
Edit tile opens. That is the split Saltcorn 1 makes and it earns itself twice: the table page
is a page you read, the row grid is one you scroll and type in — and the table page now costs
a **count** instead of every row in the table. `countRows` is the new endpoint behind that,
answered by `aggregate_values` through the same read path as any other, so an RLS-enforced
table counts the rows the caller may see rather than the rows that exist.

**CSV in and out** is new, in `sc_api::csv` and two endpoints:

- The document crosses as a **string in a JSON envelope**, not as a file download and a file
  upload. Both could have been raw routes outside the `EndpointSet`, as the binary file upload
  is; neither has to be. CSV is text, so it fits the endpoint model exactly, and staying
  inside it means the typed client carries both and the CSRF and auth machinery applies with
  no second path to remember. The browser makes the download from a blob; the export names the
  file, because only the server knows the table.
- An import goes **row by row through `rows::create_row_ctx`** — the same function the row
  endpoints and an application's REST provider write through — so a CSV write is held to
  every rule an ordinary write is: coercion, rich-type attributes, `File` field paths,
  ownership, RLS, and the insert triggers that fire afterwards. A bulk loader that went
  straight to `INSERT` would be a second write path with none of that.
- An import is **not a transaction**. Each row stands or falls on its own and a refusal is
  reported with its line number — a 5000-line export with three bad dates in it is a file to
  fix three lines of, not a file to refuse whole. A *header* naming a column the table does
  not have is refused before anything is written, because that is a wrong-file mistake rather
  than a bad-row one. A blank cell is null, except in a text column where the empty string is
  a value someone may have meant.
- Calculated fields are left out of an export: they are refused on write, so including them
  would produce a file that could not be imported back, and a round trip is what an export is
  for.

The `csv` crate is a new workspace dependency (`csv` + `csv-core`; its other dependencies were
already in the tree). A hand-rolled splitter is a line of code until the first quoted field
with an embedded comma or newline in it — which is exactly what a spreadsheet exports.

"Create trigger" on a table's page opens the trigger form with that table already chosen
(`#/triggers/new/<table>`).

Tested: the CSV round trip over HTTP against real Postgres, through the quoting a comma and an
embedded newline force; a bad row reported by line while the good rows still land; a wrong
header refused with nothing written; the row count before and after inserts, and a 404 rather
than a zero for a table that is not there. In the SPA, that the triggers card shows the row
events on *this* table and not a scheduled job that happens to write to it, and that the new
data screen still belongs to the Tables section of the sidebar.

## The server can be reached over HTTPS, and settings have somewhere to live

Two things arrive together because neither is much use alone: a **settings table**, and the
first settings worth putting in it — **TLS certificates**, from Let's Encrypt or from the
admin's own paste (§13.5).

**`_sc_config`** is a new crate, `sc-config` (layer 5), and it is a key and a JSON value per
row. What makes it a settings table rather than a bag is that **every key is declared** — as
a `FormField`, the same vocabulary a file store's backend and an LLM provider declare their
settings in (§6.2), carrying a type, a label, a default, an optional set of allowed values,
`multiline` and `secret`. A write is validated against that declaration, so `https_port =
"yes"` is a message in front of the admin rather than a row, and a key nobody declared is
refused rather than stored where nothing would ever read it. Reading is strict for the same
reason; a row whose key no declaration describes is *reportable* (`stray_config_keys`) and
deletable rather than silently obeyed or silently ignored.

**TLS** is the first section. `ssl_mode` is `off`, `letsencrypt` or `custom`; `custom` takes a
PEM chain and key, `letsencrypt` takes a contact address and a **directory URL** — so "Let's
Encrypt" is a default rather than a hard-coding, and a staging directory or a private CA is a
value in a text box. `sc-server::tls` turns the stored settings into a serving plan and an
`axum-server` accept seam that both sources reach the listener through: a fixed
`rustls::ServerConfig` for a pasted certificate, `rustls-acme`'s acceptor for an ACME one. The
admin UI and every application are served by it, because they are one listener with a `Host`
router behind them.

Five decisions worth stating, each because the alternative looks reasonable until it bites:

- **TLS-ALPN-01, not HTTP-01.** Validation happens *inside* the handshake the server already
  terminates, so there is no `/.well-known/acme-challenge` route to keep in step, and nothing
  an application's own routes can shadow. The price is that the CA must reach the TLS port,
  which for Let's Encrypt means 443.
- **ALPN advertises `http/1.1` only.** A WebSocket over HTTP/2 needs RFC 8441's extended
  CONNECT, which axum's `ws` does not implement, so advertising `h2` would trade the agent
  chat and the IDE's language server for multiplexing on an admin console.
- **The ACME cache is a table** (`_sc_acme_cache`), keyed by the digest of the domain list and
  the directory URL. On disk it would mean each node ordering its own certificate — a
  rate-limit incident waiting for the deployment that scales out — and a key that does not
  distinguish orders would mean serving a staging certificate in production.
- **The certificate is checked where it is pasted.** A key that does not match its chain fails
  on Save, carrying rustls's own message, rather than at the next restart when nobody is
  watching and the symptom is a server that will not bind.
- **A stored setting that cannot serve stops the boot.** Falling back to plain HTTP would leave
  an admin who configured TLS serving an unencrypted admin UI and no way to find out.

With TLS on there are two listeners — the `--bind` address and the TLS port beside it — bound
before either serves and stopped by one signal. The plain one answers with a **308** (not a
301: a 301 lets a client turn a `POST` into a `GET`, which for an API request is a write
quietly becoming a read), unless the admin turns the redirect off to serve both ways. Session
cookies become `Secure` on their own, and the public origin carries the HTTPS port.

The admin UI gains a **Settings** entry in the sidebar — the installation's own section, one
entry however many sections of settings the server grows. The screen knows **nothing about any
particular setting**: it renders the declarations the API hands it and posts back what was
edited, so adding a setting is a Rust declaration and a redeployed server. The private key is a
`secret`, so it reaches the browser as the sentinel and a save that hands the sentinel back
leaves the stored key alone — the §11.1 rule an API key lives under, applied to a certificate's
key, and the thing that stops editing an unrelated setting from destroying a working
certificate.

Tests cover the type check on write against real Postgres (including a batch save that lands
*nothing* when one value in it is bad), the ACME cache as two nodes over one database, the
settings API's declarations and its secret round trip, the four ways a configuration is
refused — and, because a handshake is the one thing a `tower::oneshot` cannot exercise, a
**real HTTPS request** against a self-signed certificate generated in-process, with an
untrusted client refused beside it so the first test cannot pass against a server presenting
anything at all.

Not built, and named rather than implied: per-application config scope (§9 allows it; every key
today is installation-wide), certificate changes without a restart, and §13.5's other half, the
systemd readiness notification.

## A rebuilt UI is a UI the browser fetches

Both bundles pinned their entry points to `main.js` + `main.css`, because the server's
`BOOTSTRAP_HTML` constant named them. A stable name is a name the browser has already
cached: rebuild the admin UI, restart the server, reload — and the page is still running the
old bundle until someone opens devtools and disables the cache.

Both `vite.config.ts` files now keep Vite's default **content-hashed** output
(`assets/index-<hash>.js`), and the server serves the bundle's own `index.html`, including
as the history fallback for a client-routed deep link — the document is the only thing that
knows the hashed names, so it is the only thing that can name them. `serve_static` and
`serve_ide` share one `serve_file` helper, and both now set `Cache-Control`: a year and
`immutable` for anything under `assets/` (a change to that file is a change to its URL), and
`no-cache` — revalidate, not "don't store" — for the document, whose URL never changes.
`sc-cli`'s build script checks for `dist/index.html` rather than `dist/main.js`, that being
the one output whose name it can still know.

`BOOTSTRAP_HTML` is now only what is served when there is **no** bundle, and it links no
assets: it says the admin UI is not built. Naming a guessed entry point produced a blank page
and a MIME-type error in the console — the symptom README §9 used to have to explain — where
a sentence does. Tests cover the fallback document coming from the bundle, the two
`Cache-Control` values on both bundles, and the no-bundle document.

## Roles belong to Users, not to the sidebar

Roles had a top-level entry in the admin sidebar, which put a list nobody visits on its own next
to the sections that are actually worked in. It is now reached the way LLM providers are reached
from Agents: a **Roles** button in the Users page header. The Users entry claims `/roles` as well
as `/users`, so the sidebar still marks the section an admin is standing in, and the Roles screen
carries `Users` as its pretitle. A new `nav.test.ts` asserts the arrangement for both pairs — no
sidebar entry of its own, and the owning section still lit up — so a screen cannot end up
reachable with the sidebar pointing nowhere.

## A React app can be the whole file store

The `react` framework's **project directory** was required, and there was no way to say "the app
is the store". `/` was refused as a directory name (correctly — it is not one), and leaving the
box empty could not be saved, because the setting was declared `required` and the admin UI marks
the input so. That left an admin whose git store *is* the application's repository having to
invent a nesting level their repository does not have.

The setting is now **optional, defaulting to the store root**. `project` blank means every
derived path collapses one level: source `""`, output `dist`, runtime `src/saltcorn`, client
`src/saltcorn/client.ts`. One function, `react::project_path`, applies that rule, so the build,
the scaffold, the re-emit and the client path cannot disagree about it — and none of them can
produce the leading `/` that a store would otherwise have to forgive. `check_project_name`
accepts blank (the absence of a name is not a bad name) while still refusing `../..`, `a/b` and
the rest, and its message now says that leaving it empty is an option.

Two consequences worth naming:

- **`.git` no longer counts as content** when deciding whether a directory is empty enough to
  scaffold into. The project directory *is* the store root for a store cloned from the app's own
  repository, and an empty clone still has a `.git` — refusing that would refuse exactly the case
  this change is for, while losing nothing the admin wrote.
- **The npm package name falls back to the app's subdomain.** `package.json`, the `<title>` and
  the app's header took the project directory as their name; a blank one would have produced a
  package npm refuses to install. The subdomain is the app's other machine-readable identity and
  is already a legal package name, unlike its display name (`"My Blog"`).

Messages that used to interpolate the directory now say "the store root" where there is no
directory to name, rather than printing empty backticks.

The `code` framework already allowed this — its `source` setting has always been optional and
defaulted to the store root — so it needed no change; `react` was the odd one out.

## Sessions live in the database, so there can be more than one server

The session store was a `HashMap` behind an `RwLock`, and it was the single thing standing
between this and a second application server: a session minted on node A is not one node B has
heard of, so a load balancer in front of two processes would log people out at random. It is now
a row in **`_sc_sessions`** in the primary database — the one thing every node already shares —
with a per-node cache in front of it, so the common case is still a map lookup rather than a
round trip.

**The table is `UNLOGGED`** where the backend says it can be. A session is worth sharing between
nodes and not worth a write-ahead-log record; the price is that an unclean shutdown truncates it
and its rows never reach a physical standby, and both of those cost a re-login — which is what a
restart already cost. `SchemaChange::CreateTable` gained an `unlogged` flag and `DbCapabilities`
an `unlogged_tables` to gate it, so a backend without it gets an ordinary table rather than an
error: the flag buys throughput, never semantics.

**The cache is bounded twice**, because either bound alone is not enough: an LRU capacity
(10,000) bounds memory, and a per-entry freshness TTL (60s) bounds staleness. Three things fall
out of that and each is deliberate.

- **A miss is never cached.** Node A mints a session, the browser's next request lands on node B,
  and a node that had cached "no such token" would keep the user logged out until the entry aged
  out. An unknown token always asks the database — a primary-key lookup on an unlogged table.
- **The row names the user; it does not copy them.** It carries `user_id`, and a cache miss reads
  the user back. Serialising a `User`'s admin-defined columns into the session row would have
  frozen them for the session's whole 24 hours; instead a role change lands within the freshness
  TTL.
- **The token is stored SHA-256-hashed.** A fast hash, unlike a password's argon2id: the token is
  already 256 bits of uniform randomness, so there is no dictionary for a slow hash to defeat.
  What it buys is that a database dump is not a pile of live cookies.

**Logout is the one place this is weaker than a map, and the window is bounded rather than
hidden.** The node that handles a logout deletes the row and evicts its own cache entry, so it is
consistent immediately; another node holding a cached entry keeps honouring that cookie until the
entry goes stale. That window is the freshness TTL, it closes entirely once the message bus can
carry an eviction to every node (`SessionStore::invalidate` is the seam it will hook to), and a
deployment that will not accept it sets the cache TTL to zero, which turns every lookup into a
read. There is a test that watches the window open and close rather than a comment claiming it
is short.

`SessionStore::memory()` keeps the old in-process map as a test seam — the router's own tests
have no database at all — but `saltcorn serve` always builds the database-backed store.

### `saltcorn auth token` needs no server, and the grant is gone

The one-time session grant existed for one reason: a command line holding the database could not
reach a store that lived in the server's memory, so it wrote a row saying "the next caller who
presents this secret is this user" and asked a *running* server to turn it into a session. With
sessions in the database that bridge has nothing to span. `auth token` writes the session row
itself and mints both cookies, so it works against a stopped server and a screenshot script no
longer has to sequence itself behind one coming up.

This is not new authority. It is what `grant.rs` already said in its own header — whoever can
write a grant can already read every password hash and rewrite any of them — spelled once
instead of twice. Deleted with it: `_sc_session_grants`, the `POST /auth/token` route, the
`redeemSessionGrant` handler, and the CSRF priming request the exchange needed. The CSRF cookie
is minted locally because the double-submit check compares a cookie with a header and nothing
else — it is not bound to the session — and a `curl` restored from the file needs one or its
first POST 403s.

**`_sc_sessions.user_id` is deliberately not a foreign key**, which is the opposite of the rule
everywhere else in `sc-auth`, so: the schema layer renders a plain `REFERENCES` with no
`ON DELETE` action, and a constraint here would mean an administrator cannot delete a user who
happens to be signed in. Nothing is given up, because the guarantee is already unconditional —
`user_for` resolves a session by *reading the user*, so a session naming somebody who is gone
resolves to nobody, and the row it leaves behind is inert until the sweep collects it. A test
asserts both halves, including that the delete is not blocked.

## An application's tables and file stores are ticked, not typed

The application form asked for the two subsets an app is built out of — the tables it may
access and the file stores it may read — as **comma-separated text**. Both are lists the server
already has, and neither box checked anything as it was typed: a misspelt table came back as a
refusal after the save, and a table that had since been dropped was not checked at all until the
app failed to mount. It was the same shape of question the Key field used to ask, and it has the
same answer.

**Both are now multi-selects.** The box shows what is chosen as removable chips; the menu holds
a filter, an **All** and a **None** button, a count (`2 of 4`), and one tick box per table or
store with its label or description under it. A table's options come from `listTables` and a
store's from `listFileStores`, so what can be chosen is what exists, and a listing that fails
leaves an empty picker holding whatever the application already names rather than a screen that
will not open.

**A stored name the server does not offer stays visible and stays selected**, flagged in red with
the reason — the same treatment a trigger that no longer exists already got on this screen. It is
why the application will not mount, so the picker cannot be allowed to drop it silently on the
next save; unticking it is one click. "All" selects the list in front of the admin, that row
included, for the same reason.

**It is hand-built rather than `react-select`** (`ui/admin/src/multiSelect.tsx`, ~40 lines of CSS
in `admin.css`). The admin SPA is served under a strict Content-Security-Policy with `style-src
'self'` and no `unsafe-inline`, asserted by `admin_theme.rs`; `react-select` styles itself with
Emotion, which injects a stylesheet at runtime and would be blocked — the same reason the React
scaffolder avoids CSS-in-JS. For that reason there is no Popper-positioned menu and no `style`
attribute anywhere in the control either. It is a general control rather than two copies inside
the form, so the trigger list and the static directories' `store` column can take it next.

The model behind it is separate from the rendering and unit-tested (`multiSelect.test.ts`): which
rows a list of options and a stored selection produce, that a name the server did not offer is
kept and flagged at the end, what the filter matches, and that ticking is a pure function of what
was selected — so All, then None, then one tick cannot leave a duplicate in the saved request.

## A key field is chosen, not typed

Adding a `Key` field in the admin UI meant typing the target table's name, typing a field name
in it, typing a summary field name, and then answering "stored as" — four questions, three of
which the admin could only get right by remembering another table's columns, and one whose
only correct answer was already written down in the column being pointed at. Every wrong answer
came back as a refusal *after* the request: a table that does not exist, a column that does not
exist, or a foreign key Postgres will not create because the two types differ.

**Now all three are selects, and the fourth question is gone.** The target table is picked from
the catalog (this table included — a parent link is a key onto its own table), and choosing one
loads its fields so the target and summary fields are picked from what that table actually has.
A reference is therefore valid by construction rather than by validation, and changing the
target table re-checks both other selections against the new table instead of leaving a column
name that belongs to the old one.

**"Stored as" is not a question with an admin-supplied answer.** A key is stored as whatever it
points at, so the request no longer carries a type at all: `createField` takes `type` as
optional, and the schema editor — which already derived a key's storage from its target for
callers that omitted it, agents included — is now the only thing that answers it. The form
*reports* the type it will get (`Stored as int8, to match it`) beside the target field. Asking a
question whose wrong answers are the only ones the caller could give was the bug; the fix is to
stop asking rather than to validate harder.

The one thing the form still guesses is which column a new key should point at by default, and
`listFields` now reports `primary_key` alongside `unique` so it can guess from what the database
says rather than from the name `id`. A table introspected from elsewhere may have no primary key
at all, so the default falls back to the first unique column and then to the first column —
every field stays listed, and an impossible target is still the database's to refuse.

The Key form's model (`ui/admin/src/keyField.ts`) is separate from the screen and unit-tested:
which field is the default target, what a selection becomes when the target table changes under
it, that an unset summary field is omitted rather than sent as `""`, and that the request never
carries a storage type. The server side is covered end to end in `field_api.rs` — a key created
with no `type` gets its target's, is a real foreign key in the database, and a summary field the
target does not have is refused by name — along with the refusal that keeps the relaxation
honest: a field that is *not* a reference and names no type is still told it needs one.

## The password `saltcorn auth token` should never have asked for

`auth token` (below) asked for a user's password. That was the wrong question, and asking it
made the command worse than useless in the case it exists for: an agent that has just built an
application and wants to look at it does not have anybody's password, and the operator who
does now has a secret to leave in a file or a shell history.

**It was never buying anything.** The command runs where the server runs, from a shell holding
the primary database's connection string — an authority that can already read every password
hash, rewrite any of them, and grant itself any role it likes. A password checked *on top of*
that protected nothing. So the flag is gone, along with `--password @FILE` and
`SALTCORN_PASSWORD`, and what the command asks for now is not a secret but a **user**:
`--email EMAIL` as before, `--admin` for the first user holding the admin role, or `--role
NAME` for the first user holding that one. "First" is lowest email — the users table records no
creation time (§7.1) and an answer that changed between Tuesdays would be a bug that looked
like a flake. A name that is not a role is refused *with the list of the ones that are*, and a
role nobody holds is refused saying exactly that: the caller is at a shell, and "not found"
alone would send them to the admin UI to answer a question this command can answer.

**It still forges nothing**, and that was the interesting part to keep. A session cookie is one
the server minted into its own in-memory store (§7.2), so no other process can make one — and
this one does not try. It writes a **one-time grant** instead: a row in `_sc_session_grants`
naming the user, its secret half stored argon2id-hashed exactly as a password is, expiring two
minutes later, swept when the next one is minted. That is presented at `POST /auth/token`, a
fixed route outside every `EndpointSet` for the same reason `/upload` is one — it is nobody's
API contract, and no generated client should carry a method for it — where the server redeems
it and starts an ordinary session by the same code `login` does. The row is **deleted before
the secret is checked**, so a grant is good once whether or not that once succeeded, and a
guessed id buys one attempt rather than an unlimited supply. The authority is the database, not
the row: whoever can write it could already do everything it grants; what they could not do —
mint a session in another process's memory — is what this gives them, narrowly and briefly.

**The base domain is in the documentation only when the reader has to supply it.** An
application is served at `<subdomain>.<base-domain>` (§13.2), so `auth token` needs one, and it
is what the command stops on first when it has none. But a server that was told its base domain
has already answered that question for the environment its generated documentation is about:
the command as written in `AGENTS.md` and the README runs, and a paragraph explaining a setting
the reader does not have to touch is noise in the files they read first. So the generated
documents say nothing about it when this process knows the value. A document written by a
process that was **not** told one cannot name it, and says what to add to the command line —
`--base-domain <the server's base domain>` — which is the one place a reader inside a project
directory can supply it. Neither document sends anybody to edit the server's `saltcorn.toml`:
that file is the deployment's business, not the application project's.

**And the documentation now names this server's roles.** `--role` takes a name, and nothing
inside a project directory knows what an installation calls its roles, so a generated
`AGENTS.md` and `src/saltcorn/README.md` that merely *described* roles would send the reader
back to the admin UI. Both now list them — `Admin (1), Editor (40), Public (100)` — read out of
`_sc_roles` at scaffold time and re-read on every build, so a role added next month appears in
the README without anybody re-scaffolding anything. The README also gives each one its
description, because the reader is usually deciding which account an agent should get. A
database with no roles table (a test's, never a server's) documents no roles rather than
failing a build over a sentence.

## A session in a file, a URL in the documentation: the rest of the screenshot loop

`SIGHUP` (below) fixed the first half of "an agent changed something and wants to look at it".
Two things were still in the way, and neither is discoverable from inside a project.

**The screens are behind a sign-in.** A scaffolded app's routes require a user unless they say
otherwise (§13.3), so a script driving a browser photographs the sign-in page. What it needs
is the cookie a browser would have got; what it has is a shell. **`saltcorn auth token`** is
that shell's way to sign in: `--app`, `--email`, a password from `--password`, `--password
@FILE` or `SALTCORN_PASSWORD`, and out comes `.saltcorn-session.json` — Playwright's
`storageState`, which `browser.newContext({ storageState })` restores a logged-in browser
from. `--format netscape` writes a `cookies.txt` for `curl --cookie` instead.

It **signs in; it does not forge**. The only session the server accepts is one it minted into
its own in-memory store (§7.2), so nothing outside that process can mint one and this does not
try: it makes the same `POST {mount}/login` the sign-in screen makes, against the running
server, with a real account and its real password, and writes down what came back. The session
can do exactly what that account can do — which is what makes "give the agent its own
low-privilege account" a limit rather than a suggestion. Two requests rather than one, because
the CSRF check refuses a mutation whose `x-csrf-token` does not echo its `sc_csrf` cookie and a
first-contact client has neither: it primes with a GET, exactly as a browser does. The primer's
CSRF cookie is carried into the file, too — the login response does not re-set one, and a
`curl` restored without it would 403 on its first POST.

A session file is a password, and is treated as one: written `0600`, with the mode set before
the bytes so it is never briefly world-readable, and both default names are in the
`.gitignore` the scaffold writes.

**And the URL was nowhere.** An application is served at `<subdomain>.<base-domain>`, and the
base domain is the *server's* command line — it appears nowhere in the project, so the
generated documentation could only ever describe the URL rather than give it. Now
`Catalog::public_origin` carries it: base domain, bound port, TLS or not, recorded at boot by
`saltcorn serve` from its own flags. It rides on the catalog for the reason the schema
observer does — every generator already holds one, and the alternative was a parameter about
*documentation* in the signature of everything that builds.

The awkward half of that is that a build does not only happen in the server. So a
`saltcorn.toml` environment now carries `base_domain`, `bind` and `secure_cookies` beside its
connection parameters: an environment is a deployment, not a connection string. `saltcorn
serve --environment production` consequently needs no other flag — the file's settings are
parsed *before* the command line's, so a flag still wins by being read later — and, the reason
it was done, `saltcorn build-app` and `saltcorn api add-query` write the **same** URL the
server would. A build that rewrote `README.md` with the URL taken out would be worse than one
that never wrote it. Both also take `--base-domain` directly. A process that was told nothing
still says which setting is missing rather than inventing `localhost`: the generated
documentation is the file a reader trusts most, and a plausible wrong hostname in it is worse
than an obvious gap.

Both documents gained the whole loop, at the length each is for. `src/saltcorn/README.md` has
the procedure — the reload, the session command, where the password comes from, what the
session can and cannot do, how long it lasts (the server's TTL, or a restart, since sessions
are in memory — a `SIGHUP` reload deliberately does not end one), the Playwright snippet and
the `curl` form. `AGENTS.md` has the two commands, the URL, and a pointer here, because it is
read at the start of every session and what belongs in it is the loop and where the rest is.

The test that matters binds a real port, mounts an application on it, signs in over HTTP and
replays the cookie at `whoami` — because "the session it writes is one the server accepts" is
the entire claim, and nothing short of that tests it.

## `SIGHUP` reloads a running server, so a build on disk is a build in the browser

`npm run build` in an application's project directory changed nothing anybody could see, and
the reason was not obvious from anywhere: what a mounted app serves is the `AssetBundle` the
server read out of the output directory **when the server itself last built**, and every
request after that is answered from that map in memory. Only the admin UI's Build button and
a restart ever replaced it. So the loop a developer — and every coding agent working inside a
project — actually runs, build then reload the page, silently showed the previous build. The
same staleness had a second face: an application row edited by another process (`saltcorn api
add-query` from the project directory) is never re-read by the running server either, because
the re-projection that keeps mounts current is a schema observer inside *this* process.

Both are now one signal. `SIGHUP` reloads the catalog — re-introspection, both overlays — and
then every stored application: its row, the API providers projected from that row, and its
bundle re-read from the output directory the last build left. It **runs no bundler and no
installer**, which is the whole point: the caller has just built, and `npm install && npm run
build` again would be a slow duplicate of the work that prompted the signal. That makes it
milliseconds, and the reload logs its timings — the question it has to answer is how long to
wait before looking at the page, and the only honest answer is what it just took.

```sh
npm run build && pkill -HUP saltcorn
```

A signal rather than an endpoint because of who sends it: a shell in the project directory,
with no session, no CSRF token and no reason to acquire either, but with the run of the
machine. The Build button is untouched and still builds; this is its fast half.

The failure rule is the one boot already follows. An application whose output directory is
missing is *reported* and keeps serving what it was serving — the remount happens only after
the new bundle has loaded — so one unbuilt app cannot take the others down, and a reload
returns a report rather than a `Result` because a server that is already serving has no
business stopping over either half of it. A row deleted elsewhere is unmounted; a table
created elsewhere is in the catalog when the signal returns, which is what lets an app that
now declares it project endpoints for it.

The handler is registered **before** the task that services it is spawned, and that is not
tidiness: `SIGHUP`'s default disposition is to terminate, so a signal arriving in the gap
would have killed the process instead of reloading it. The test that sends a real
`kill -HUP` caught exactly that, which is why it exists in a test binary of its own — a
signal is process-wide, and raising one where other tests live would fire their reloads too.

Both generated documents now carry the loop, because it is the thing nobody guesses: the
project's `AGENTS.md` (written once, at scaffold) and `src/saltcorn/README.md` (rewritten on
every build) each say why a build alone is invisible, give the one pasteable line, say what
the reload deliberately does not do, and name the URL to look at — this app's own subdomain.
`docs/tutorial-react-todo.md` gains it beside `saltcorn build-app`, and the GraphQL
tutorial's "or `npm run build` in the project" — which was quietly wrong — now includes the
signal.

Not reloaded, each having its own live-updating admin API and each shared into the scheduler
or the catalog's write path at boot: the trigger set, the agents, the LLM providers, and the
file-store connections.

## Written down where the design lives, and a tutorial that opens the hole on purpose

The milestone's last phase, and the one that decides whether any of the rest of it is
findable a year from now. Three sections of the design document gained what this milestone
actually built, and there is a tutorial for the two features an application's developer
meets first.

**§13.1** gains the query parameter as part of the endpoint: the `QueryParam` shape, the one
options object the generator emits (optional when every parameter in it is, so an existing
call site does not grow an empty `{}`), `URLSearchParams` doing the encoding rather than
concatenation, and the `map` shape a filter vocabulary needs because a filter is keyed by
*column*. With it, the reason `ApiRequest.query` is an ordered list of pairs — a `HashMap`
turns a repeated key into a **silently dropped predicate**, which is rows the caller did not
ask for.

**§13.3** gains the generated directory's contract, because a boundary a developer has to
read the design document to discover is one they will cross: the `DO NOT EDIT` header in
each file's own comment syntax, what `README.md` and `schema.sql` are for, `schema.sql`
being the *driver's* DDL rather than a second writer's, and `AGENTS.md` living at the root
and never being rewritten. Plus what regeneration is and is not — not a build, so it can run
on every event that invalidates the endpoint set, logged and never fatal, with
`updateApplicationClient` as the same thing on demand.

**§13.4** gains the two halves it was missing. The REST query string as a *syntax over the
read layer*: the taken subset, the not-taken one refused by name, one statement per request,
`join_guard` on every embed, and the four reserved words. And custom SQL queries: the
`Statement::Raw` hole and the rule written on it, the `:name` rewriter, `describe` as the
source of the result type, and the authority paragraph — what a raw statement escapes
(ownership formulae, rich types, `File` rules, table events) and what still holds it (the
role floor, the caller's database context, `READ ONLY` for `GET`). Per-provider
configuration moved up to its own subsection, since it is now three providers' worth of
arrangement rather than a note under GraphQL's.

**[tutorial-rest-queries.md](docs/tutorial-rest-queries.md)** builds two tables and reads
them: `select` with an embed, the whole filter vocabulary, ordering and paging, then the
refusals — because the tutorial has to teach that *nothing is ever ignored* before a reader
meets it in production. Then the typed client, then a custom query written twice, in the
admin editor and from the command line, and called both ways. It does not stop at "it
works": an admin is the one caller no rule applies to, so it says which rules apply to
everybody else, and it says what a custom query is a hole in before showing how to open one.

Two hygiene tests keep this honest the way the GraphQL milestone's do: one asserting the
design still records each load-bearing decision across the three sections, one asserting the
tutorial still reaches the motivating query and every rule past the happy path — and the
tutorial cross-link chain now ends at this one.

## The generated directory explains itself, and keeps itself current

`src/saltcorn/` has been a generated directory since the React framework landed, but it
never said so anywhere a person — or a coding agent — would look, and it went stale
between builds. Both are fixed, and the two halves are the same idea: what the server
generates is the server's, and it follows the application's definition without anybody
asking.

**Two new generated files.** `src/saltcorn/README.md` says the directory is overwritten
without warning, lists what is in it, and spells out `saltcorn api add-query` for *this*
application — its subdomain, its mount — so the answer to "the client has no method for
what I need" is a command to paste rather than a search. It carries decision 6's
authority note too: raw SQL does not go through the row layer, what still applies is the
role floor and the caller's database context. `src/saltcorn/schema.sql` is the
`CREATE TABLE` of the tables the application declares, so an agent writing that query has
real column names and real types instead of guesses.

**The DDL is the driver's.** `schema.sql` is rendered through a new
`DatabaseDriver::render_ddl` over the existing `SchemaChange` renderer — the same text
`apply_schema` would run, and nothing applied. A second DDL writer in `sc-app` would have
drifted from the real one the first time a type or a constraint changed, and a file that
exists to be trusted cannot be the one that is wrong.

**`AGENTS.md` at the project root, written once.** It says what the project is, that
`src/saltcorn/` is generated and points at its README, that data reaches the browser
through the generated client and nothing else, and how to add a custom query. It is the
developer's file: the scaffold writes it and **never** rewrites it, because agents append
what they learn to it and clobbering that would destroy their work. That is the same
boundary §13.3 already draws — inside the generated directory is ours, the project root
is theirs — applied to one more file.

**Nobody regenerates anything by hand.** A re-projection of an app's providers — a column
added, a table's access changed, a trigger's role — now also re-emits that app's
`src/saltcorn/**`, and saving an application does the same. It is a background task and
its failures are logged, never fatal: the observer that triggers it is running inside
somebody's schema change, and an unreachable store must not fail their edit or take a
mounted app down (decision 10). No bundler runs; `npm run build` stays the build button's.

**And a button for the times you want it now**: `updateApplicationClient`, beside Build on
the applications screen. When the project directory is **empty** it scaffolds instead of
re-emitting — an app whose store was unreachable at creation, or whose tree somebody
deleted, would otherwise get a `src/saltcorn/` with no project around it — and the
response says which of the two it did, because writing a whole project is not the same
news as rewriting four files. The emptiness check is the scaffold's own; a second opinion
about what "empty" means is how the two would eventually disagree.

The opt-in `npm` end-to-end test now writes a caller of a **custom query** into the
scaffolded project, so `tsc --noEmit` type-checks a method whose result type came from
Postgres describing the statement — the whole chain, from `SELECT` to `rows[0].n`, fails
the build if any link of it moves.

## Writing a custom query: a screen, and a command line

The model landed last; this is how somebody actually adds one. Two ways, both of which
end in the same stored `queries` array, and neither of which asks anyone to regenerate
anything by hand.

**In the admin UI**, an API row that serves custom queries grows an editor: name, method,
sub-path, minimum role, the SQL, and the parameter list. The button that matters is
**Check**. It posts to a new admin endpoint, `describeCustomQuery`, which runs the same
two judgements a save runs — the model's rules, then `describe` — and answers with the
columns Postgres says the statement returns, storing nothing. So `column "titel" does not
exist` arrives in one round trip, in Postgres's words, while its author is looking at the
SQL; and the columns are also the **documentation**, because they are exactly what the
generated client method will hand back. Doing this at save time instead would mean
learning about a typo in one `SELECT` by having every other edit on the screen refused
with it.

**The authority note is on the screen**, not only in the module docs (decision 6): the
editor says, above the list, that raw SQL does not go through the row layer — so ownership
formulae, rich-type coercion and File-field rules do not reach it — and that what still
applies is the role floor (admin unless you say otherwise) and the caller's database
context, so RLS policies still decide what it can see. An admin opening a hole should be
told what it is a hole in, where they are opening it.

**The form still knows nothing about REST.** `ApiProviderInfo` gained
`supports_custom_queries`, so the editor appears because a provider declared it does this,
in the same spirit as the `config_spec` beside it. And `apiRowsToRequest` now carries the
queries back explicitly — `buildConfig` writes the provider's declared settings and
nothing else, which is right for settings and meant that opening an application to change
its mount silently saved its custom queries away. That failure has a test of its own.

**From a terminal**: `saltcorn api add-query --app blog --name topAuthors --path
/reports/top-authors --param since:int --sql @report.sql`, with `list-queries` and
`remove-query` beside it. Three commands rather than one because an add-only command is a
trap: the first typo would need a browser to fix, which is the situation the command
exists to avoid. A parameter is written `name:type`, or `name:type?` when the caller may
leave it out; `--sql @file` reads the statement from a file, because SQL is many lines and
a shell is a poor place to keep them. The role floor defaults to **admin** here too — a
command-line default of "public" would be the one place the rule did not hold.

Adding or removing a query **re-emits the application's generated client** (a new
`sc_app::emit_app_client`, which for a `react` app rewrites the whole `src/saltcorn/`
runtime, since its hooks are typed from the same endpoint set). A command that changed the
API and left the client describing the old one would be the drift §13.1 exists to prevent,
introduced by the tool meant to avoid it. The re-emit is reported but never fatal: the
query is already saved by then, and exiting non-zero would say the opposite. Validation is
the save itself, so a query that will not prepare exits non-zero carrying Postgres's
message with the stored application untouched — asserted end-to-end, through the real
binary against a real database, along with the endpoint set and the emitted `client.ts`
both gaining the method.

## An application's own SQL, as a typed endpoint

An administrator writes a statement, names its parameters and their types, picks an HTTP
method and a sub-path, and the application gains an endpoint — with a typed method on its
generated client whose **return type came from Postgres**, not from anything anyone
declared. It is the escape hatch for what the row layer's read cannot say: a window
function, a recursive CTE, a report nobody wants to assemble in a browser.

**The database types the result; the admin types the parameters.** Saving an application
prepares every custom query it declares (`DatabaseDriver::describe`, over
`prepare_typed`), and the columns the prepared statement reports become the endpoint's
`TypeSchema`. So a query that will not prepare **cannot be saved** — the refusal carries
Postgres's own message, and "column `titel` does not exist" arrives while its author is
still looking at the SQL. Describing runs on *every* save, in the same call that writes
the row, so the stored columns are never older than the stored SQL. The alternative —
the admin declaring the result shape too — is a second source of truth that goes stale
the first time anyone edits a `SELECT`.

**`Statement::Raw` is the one hole in the AST, and the rule is written on the variant.**
`sc-query`'s enum is the representation of a query; raw text is a hole in it, the goals
ask for the hole, so there is exactly one, it is named, and it says what it is for: a
`Raw` is constructed **only** from an admin-authored query definition, never from
anything a caller sent. Arguments are coerced to their declared type and **bound**;
`'; DROP TABLE books; --` supplied as an argument is a value that matches no rows, and
there is a test that then counts the rows still in the table.

**`:name` is rewritten once, by a scanner that knows what it is reading.** The admin
writes `:since`, not `$1`, and `sc_query::rewrite_named_params` turns it into the
dialect's placeholders — skipping single-quoted literals, dollar-quoted bodies, quoted
identifiers, line and block comments (which Postgres nests) and `::` casts. Each of
those is a way to corrupt somebody's query silently, and `x::text` read the other way
round would both lose the cast and invent a parameter called `text`; each has a test of
its own. The same scan counts statements, because "a custom query is one statement" has
to be decided on the same reading of the text — a `;` inside a literal separates nothing.
A repeated `:id` takes one placeholder and one bind, because two mentions of an argument
are one value.

**A custom query's authority is its own, and it is stated loudly** — in the module's own
documentation, because raw SQL does not go through the row layer and so ownership
formulae, rich-type coercion, `File`-field rules and table events do not reach it. What
does: a role floor that is **admin unless the admin said otherwise** (§10.2's trigger
rule — an access nobody has thought about must not be the one that turns out to be
public); and the **caller-context transaction**, the same one a row operation on an
RLS-protected table runs in, so the database's policies still decide what the query can
see. That last one is asserted against a real RLS table with two callers who each see
their own two rows and an admin who sees four — it would fail open if the context were
dropped.

**The method is the admin's choice; the read-only guarantee is not.** Nothing infers the
method from the SQL (GOALS asks for the manual choice), so an `UPDATE` behind a `GET` is
expressible — and the transaction refuses it, because a mutation a cache, a crawler or a
link prefetch can cause is not one anybody chose. `sc-catalog` grew
`run_in_context_read_only` for it, issuing `SET TRANSACTION READ ONLY` before the caller
GUCs (a `SET TRANSACTION` after the first statement is not a read-only transaction).

`Statement::Raw` carries its parameter types, which is the fix for a failure worth
naming: `WHERE (:q IS NULL OR title = :q)` has no inferable parameter type, so a query
that prepared happily against the admin's declared types would have failed the first
time somebody called it. Describing and running now derive the types from one place, so
a query is executed under exactly the types it was typed under.

The rest of the model is refused at the keyboard rather than discovered later: a name
that is not a usable client method name, or that a declared table's own endpoint already
holds; a sub-path a table's routes already answer (`resolve` takes the first match and
the tables are registered first, so the query would be an unreachable method in the
client) or that starts with `actions`/`login`/`logout`/`whoami`; a `:name` the query does
not declare, and a declared parameter the SQL never uses — which is an argument the
client would demand and the query would ignore, reading as a filter that does nothing;
more than one statement; and two result columns of one name, which would collapse into
one JSON property and lose a column silently. The same collision rules run again when the
provider is projected, so what would have been a duplicate-endpoint **panic** in a
running server is a mount error naming both.

Queries live in the API row's own configuration beside the provider's settings, but as a
typed `queries` array rather than a settings field — a list of records each carrying a
nested list of parameters is not a form, and `validate_api_config` lifts it out and
validates it as the value it is. `save_application` now **returns the application as it
was stored**, and the admin API answers with that: a create or update that reported the
body it was sent would be telling an admin their query returns nothing.

## An API provider has settings, and GraphQL's aggregates are one of them

An `ApiConfig` is now `{ provider, mount, config }`, and the third field is a settings bag
validated on save against the spec the **provider itself declares**. GraphQL's five settings —
the aggregation switch and the four bounds one operation is served under — live there, so an
application's limits come from the application rather than from the defaults at the call site,
and REST's `row_cap` does too.

**No provider-specific code in the form.** `ApiProviderInfo` gained `config_spec: Vec<FormField>`,
`listApiProviders` returns it, and the application form renders it with the same `SettingsFields`
a framework's settings go through (§13.3). Enabling GraphQL shows its switch and its four numbers;
enabling REST shows its one; a provider that grows a setting grows a control, and no screen is
edited. The APIs list stopped being a row of uniform text boxes to make room for it, and the
conversion — stored config to form strings and back to the types the spec declares — moved into
`ui/admin/src/apiRows.ts`, where it is tested without a browser. A row whose provider this server
does not register keeps its settings **exactly as they arrived**: they cannot be re-typed without
a spec, and rewriting them would destroy the configuration of a provider that is merely absent.

**Aggregates are off unless switched on**, which is the change with teeth. They are the most
expensive thing the schema can express — a correlated subquery per parent row, over rows the
caller may never see — so they are present because somebody asked for them. Off (the default),
`X_aggregate`, the `_aggregate` field on every relation, the `XAggregate`/`XNumericFields`/
`XComparableFields` objects and the `XSelectColumn` enum are **absent from the schema**. A
document naming one is therefore `async-graphql`'s own "field not found" over the document —
before a resolver runs and before a statement is issued — rather than a silent null or a refusal
that cost a round trip. A field that existed and refused would be a schema advertising what it
will not do.

**An undeclared setting is refused on save.** `validate_api_config` runs in `save_application`
beside the framework's own validation and the mount check, and names both the provider and the
setting. The failure it exists to prevent is a misspelled `aggregate` stored happily and read as
nothing: a switch that appears to work and does not. An unknown provider has no spec to be
configured against and says so, which is the same answer mounting one gives.

The SDL a build writes and the schema a mount answers still come from one projection
(`graphql_provider` now takes the whole `ApiConfig`), so the switch cannot turn the aggregates on
in one and not the other.

Tests: the SDL snapshot in **both** states — the existing file is now the switched-on one, beside
a new `graphql_schema_no_aggregates.graphql`, because "the aggregates are gone and nothing else
moved" is a claim only a whole-schema diff can make; a real-Postgres test that an aggregate
against a switched-off application is refused with **no statement recorded**; the limits
round-tripping through save/load and reaching the projection; a misspelled and an ill-typed
setting each refused on save naming the provider; unit tests for the config↔limits conversion in
both directions and for an unreadable value falling back to its default rather than to zero; and
a vitest suite asserting the form's round trip, including that editing only the mount does not
save the switch off.

## A REST read is a query string, and it is the same read GraphQL does

`GET /api/books?select=title,published,author(name,country)&published=gte.2020-01-01&order=published.desc&limit=20`
now answers it — in one statement, with the author's columns projected as Ⱶ-join
subqueries rather than fetched, and the caller seeing exactly the rows their role and
the tables' ownership formulae permit.

**Not a second data path**, which is the whole shape of this change. The query string
parses into the row layer's own `RowQuery` — the same value a GraphQL list field's
`where`/`order_by`/`limit`/`offset` lower to — and the read goes through the same
`ownership::read_row_values_as` the GraphQL root field and an agent's `query_table`
already use. So a filter, an ordering and a page apply *inside* the read that ownership
bounds, rather than to some wider set first, and the milestone's query and its GraphQL
equivalent are asserted to return the same rows against a real database. That is the
regression test worth having: if the two ever disagree, one of them has grown a reader
of its own. The REST list branch lost its own three-way (`list_rows` / `list_owned_rows`
/ RLS) dispatch in the process, and `ownership::list_owned_rows` is gone — the general
entry point subsumed it, and a second copy of "meets the floor OR the formula grants the
row" is a second place for it to be quietly wrong.

**One comparison vocabulary, two syntaxes.** `eq`/`ne`/`gt`/`gte`/`lt`/`lte`/`in`/`nin`/
`like`/`ilike`/`is_null` moved out of `graphql/args.rs` into `sc-api::filter`, and both
surfaces lower through it. A filter value is coerced against the *column* there, so
`?published=gte.2020-01-01` binds a **date** exactly as the GraphQL spelling does, and
`is_null` means SQL's nullness in both. The GraphQL suites passing unchanged is the proof
the move changed nothing; a new unit test asserts the two syntaxes build the identical
`Expr` for the same comparison, which is the property that would rot first.

**PostgREST's syntax, as a stated subset.** Taken: `select` with embeds through outgoing
keys to any depth, `alias:column` renaming, `column=op.value` filters (repeated keys ANDed,
so a range is two of them), `order=column.desc`, `limit`, `offset`. Deliberately not taken:
one-to-many embeds, `!inner`, the `...` spread, `::` casts, `or=(…)`, and filters on an
embedded resource. Everything not taken is refused **by name** — `!inner` says it is
`!inner` and why, an unknown operator lists the ones that exist, `books(…)` on a table
rather than a key says it is a one-to-many embed. Ignoring what we do not understand would
answer with rows the caller did not ask for, and a silently dropped filter is the worst
failure a data API can have. `limit` is clamped to the application's row cap and an absent
one *becomes* it (500 by default, `RestProvider::with_row_cap`), for the same reason the
GraphQL provider does it: an unbounded list is a request to stream a table into a response.

**An embed is a projection, not a fetch.** `author(name,country)` becomes one
`sc_expr::join_path_expr` correlated subquery per requested leaf, aliased by its join path,
carried in the same `SELECT` as the row and nested back into `{"author": {"name": …}}` on
the way out — to any depth, so `author(publisher(city))` is still one statement (asserted
by recording the SQL, not by timing it). A null key answers `null` rather than an object of
nulls, which is why the foreign key's own value is projected alongside the leaves.

**An embed is a read, so the target's read rule holds.** Every embed goes through
`ownership::join_guard` first: a caller who may not read the target table is refused
naming it, and so is a caller whose access to it comes from an ownership formula — a join
subquery has no `WHERE` this provider owns, so the formula cannot decide which rows are
reached, and answering anyway would hand over a withheld row one column at a time. An
RLS-protected target makes the whole statement a caller-context one instead, so its
policies decide as they would in a read of their own. `select` is therefore not a way
around anything the list endpoint enforces.

**The read's vocabulary is part of the endpoint.** Every list endpoint now declares
`select`, `order`, `limit`, `offset` and `filter` as query parameters, so `listBooks` takes
a typed options object instead of leaving callers to hand-write a `fetch` beside a generated
client. `filter` is a new *map* parameter shape (`QueryParam::map`): its keys are column
names, so it types as `Record<string, string>` and each entry becomes its own query-string
pair. Typing it as a map is honest about being a string vocabulary — pretending to a
narrower type would be a promise the query string cannot keep. The generated client
type-checks under `tsc --noEmit --strict` with a filtered, ordered, paged call in the usage
module.

Tests: unit tests for the query-string parser, the `select` grammar (aliases, nesting, each
not-taken feature, an unclosed paren, a nesting bomb), the null-key rendering, and the
cross-syntax `Expr` equality; and a new real-Postgres suite where the milestone's query
matches its GraphQL equivalent, a two-hop embed is one statement, the row cap holds however
large a `limit` asks for, a refused query issues no statement at all, and each authorization
rule has a test that would fail open without it — an ownership formula and RLS each still
bounding a filtered/ordered/paged read, and both embed refusals.

## A query parameter is part of the endpoint, and a query string is a list

The API milestone's first phase, and the thing both of its features need before either can be
built: an `Endpoint` can now declare the query-string parameters it accepts, and the generated
TypeScript client takes them as a typed options object.

Why it comes first. Two of this milestone's features — the REST read query string
(`?select=…&published=gte.2020&order=…`) and custom SQL queries, whose `GET` arguments ride in the
query string — are unexpressible in a generated client that can only type a path and a body. The
alternative to typing them is a hand-written `fetch` sitting beside a generated client, calling the
same server with none of the checking, and that is precisely the drift §13.1 exists to prevent. So
`Endpoint` gains `query: Vec<QueryParam>`, each parameter carrying a name, a `ValueType`, whether
it is `required`, and whether it is `repeated`. The field is skipped when empty, so every endpoint
written before this serializes exactly as it did and a stored endpoint set still round-trips.

`repeated` is the one worth explaining. A filter vocabulary spells a range as two values under one
key — `?published=gte.2020-01-01&published=lt.2024-01-01` — and both of them mean something. That
is also why `ApiRequest.query` is no longer a `HashMap<String, String>` but an ordered
`Vec<(String, String)>`, read through `query_get` (the first value) and `query_all` (all of them,
in order). A map keeps whichever pair arrived last and silently discards the rest; for a filter,
what it discards is rows the caller asked not to see. That is the worst failure a data API can
have, and it is not the kind of thing to leave sitting in a data structure waiting for Phase 2 to
walk into. `HandlerCtx.query` moves with it for the same reason, and gains the same two readers.

The server's `parse_query` therefore keeps order and duplicates, and now percent-decodes as well —
`+` as a space, `%XX` as a byte, which is what `URLSearchParams` (and so the generated client)
writes. Without decoding, a filter value containing a space or an `&` would reach a handler still
escaped, and a caller's `title=eq.rock & roll` would quietly become something else. A malformed
escape (`%zz`, a trailing `%`) is passed through as written rather than rejected: it is one
character of one parameter, and the endpoint reading it can say what is wrong with it far better
than a parser that knows only that a `%` was not followed by two hex digits.

On the client side an endpoint with query parameters takes one options object, last, after the path
parameters and any body. The object is *optional* when every parameter in it is, so declaring a
`select` nobody has to pass does not put an empty `{}` at every call site — and an endpoint that
declares no query parameters generates exactly the method it generated before, with no argument, no
`URLSearchParams` and no `?` in its URL. Encoding goes through `URLSearchParams` rather than string
concatenation, because a filter value legitimately contains `&`, `=`, `+` and spaces, and a client
that pasted those into a URL would send a different query than its caller wrote. A repeated
parameter types as an array and appends once per element; an optional one is skipped when
`undefined` *or* `null`, so a nullable value threaded through from a form does not arrive as the
string `"null"`.

Tests: the three parameter shapes' signatures and their emitted encoding, an endpoint with no query
parameters proving the no-argument case is untouched, serde round-tripping in both directions, the
ordered-pairs readers keeping both values of a repeated key, and `parse_query` doing the same over a
real URI — including the round trip of a value needing escaping (`eq.rock & roll = 1+1 ☕` survives
`URLSearchParams`'s encoding of it) and the awkward edges: a `=` inside a value, an empty value, a
malformed escape. The generated client type-checks under `tsc --noEmit --strict`, in a new case
alongside the admin and app-REST ones, where a usage module calls an all-optional endpoint with no
argument at all and a required one with its typed object. The checked-in admin clients are
unchanged and their drift test stays green: no admin endpoint declares a query parameter yet.

## `cargo test --workspace` no longer closes the terminal it runs in

Running the full suite on a desktop would, often enough to be a working hazard, make the whole
terminal window disappear — no failure, no scrollback, nothing left to read. The journal names the
culprit exactly: `systemd-oomd`, killing
`app-org.gnome.Terminal.slice/vte-spawn-….scope` "due to memory pressure for
`/user.slice/user-1000.slice/user@1000.service` being 54.80% > 50.00% for > 20s", with the scope's
`Current Memory Usage: 20.2G`. Two things about that are worth spelling out, because together they
turn a resource problem into a disappearing-evidence problem. oomd kills a **cgroup**, not a
process — and a terminal's shell, the cargo it launched, every rustc under that and the tab's own
process all share one `vte-spawn-*.scope`. So "the build used too much memory" is carried out as
"close the window", destroying the output that would have explained it.

The 20.2 GB is the workspace's own doing. A static V8 (`deno_core`, behind `sc-expr`'s `eval`
feature) links into **every one** of the ~110 integration-test binaries, and at rustc's default
`debug = true` each one came out at ~440 MB — overwhelmingly DWARF for dependency code — with a
peak of ~1.9 GB of linker RSS apiece. `cargo test -j8` runs eight of those at once. CI had already
met this and worked around it locally with `CARGO_PROFILE_DEV_DEBUG: 0`; a developer's machine
never got the same treatment, and has less headroom than a runner precisely because a desktop
session is running everything else too.

So the fix is a **debug-info budget** in the workspace `Cargo.toml`, which applies to every cargo
invocation without anyone having to remember a flag. Dependencies build with no debug info at all
(`[profile.dev.package."*"] debug = false`) and workspace crates keep line tables only. One key
covers `cargo test` as well as `cargo build`: `test` inherits from `dev`, and that inheritance
carries `package."*"` overrides — checked rather than assumed, since the opposite would have been
invisible. A `cargo test` build of a crate carrying only that key emits a dependency rlib with zero
`.debug_info` sections, against 18 with the key removed.

That draws the line where the value is: a panicking test still prints a backtrace with `file:line`,
which is the whole of what a test run reads debug info for, while nobody loses anything they were
using by not being able to single-step into V8's bindings. Full DWARF for a dependency is still one
flag away — `--config 'profile.dev.package."*".debug=true'`. Measured on this workspace, a test
binary goes 391 MB → 152 MB, the largest goes 440 MB → 173 MB, and a full
`cargo test --workspace --no-fail-fast` — build and run, 145 suites and 1334 tests — peaks at 3.3 GB
of toolchain RSS and never takes the machine below 23.8 GB of the 30 GB available. The run it
replaces reached 20.2 GB in one cgroup.

`scripts/cargo-guarded.sh` is the second layer, for whatever overruns anyway: it runs cargo under
`systemd-run --user --scope` with `MemoryHigh`/`MemoryMax`, which lands the build in a *sibling*
cgroup of the terminal's rather than a child of it. The cap is then the only thing that can be
killed, and it exits 137 with an explanation of which knob to turn instead of taking the window.
It falls back to plain `cargo` where there is no systemd, so it is safe to use unconditionally.

Three tests in `crates/sc-cli/tests/repo_hygiene.rs` keep the budget from being quietly dropped and
rediscovered the same way: one asserts both profile keys by name, one asserts the wrapper still caps
and still falls back, and one measures the *output* rather than the setting. That last one does not
weigh its own binary — `repo_hygiene` only reads files, so the linker collects almost everything and
it comes out around 7 MB with or without the budget, passing either way and proving nothing. It
scans the `deps/` directory the build just wrote and checks the largest binary there, which is one
that genuinely links V8: ~173 MB now against ~440 MB before, either side of a 300 MB ceiling.

Two things found on the way that are documented in the README rather than fixed in code: cargo
never garbage-collects `target/`, so every rebuild's hashed test binaries accumulate (this checkout
had 2287 stale executables, 423 GB, filling the disk to 94%); and `rust-lld` is already the default
linker on this toolchain, so there is nothing to gain from installing `lld` or `mold`.

## An app that cannot sign anyone in scaffolds an anonymous client, not a broken one

A React application with **only** the GraphQL provider enabled scaffolded happily and then failed
its build with four TypeScript errors in `src/auth.tsx` — `Property 'whoami' does not exist on
type 'ApiClient'`, and the same for `login` and `logout` — in a file the admin never wrote. The
cause is not a generation slip: the scaffold is authenticated by default (§13.3) and signs in
through the app's own `login` / `logout` / `whoami`, and those are projected by the **REST**
provider. The GraphQL provider projects its two endpoints and nothing else, by design (§13.4). So
that app has no way for any caller to stop being anonymous, and a login screen was generated for
it anyway.

The scaffold now has **two shapes**, chosen by
[`has_auth`](crates/sc-app/src/scaffold/files.rs) against the endpoint set: the authenticated one
§13.3 describes, and — when nothing projects those three endpoints — an anonymous one with no
`auth.tsx`, no `Login.tsx`, no `AuthProvider` around the router, no user cell in the header and
no per-route `public` flag. Authenticated-by-default is a rule about a scaffold's *routes*, and
it cannot lock a door that has no key: a wholly public API with a public client is a legitimate
app rather than a misconfigured one, so it gets a client that compiles instead of a diagnostic
telling it to become a different app. The names come from the new `sc_api::AUTH_ENDPOINTS`, so
the branch cannot go stale against a provider that changes what it projects, and it is all three
or none — half an auth layer is the failure this exists to prevent.

The other half of that story is an app scaffolded **with** REST and later left without it. Its
`src/auth.tsx` still calls the three methods, and `tsc --noEmit` type-checks every file in the
project whether or not anything imports it, so that build really does fail — and it now fails
first, with the reason, from `require_auth_endpoints_for_source` in
[`scaffold/mod.rs`](crates/sc-app/src/scaffold/mod.rs). The question it asks is about the
project's **source**, not the app's providers: an app that never had an auth layer is not broken
by not having one, and an `auth.tsx` the admin has replaced with their own is theirs to get
right. That is the same service [`require_api_provider`](crates/sc-app/src/scaffold/mod.rs)
performs one provider along.

The GraphQL scaffold tests enabled both providers throughout, which is why the combination was
never exercised. [`graphql_scaffold.rs`](crates/sc-app/tests/graphql_scaffold.rs) now scaffolds a
GraphQL-only app and puts it through a real `npm run build` — the claim the four errors disproved
— and covers the lost-provider refusal beside it, including the case that must *not* be refused.

Two things a GraphQL-only app still does not get, both by construction rather than oversight:
generated **pages**, which are written from the table endpoints REST projects (its data layer is
the typed `graphql()` call), and a login screen appearing later if REST is added, since the
project's own files are written once at scaffold time and never regenerated.

## `cargo build` now builds the admin UI, and `SC_BUILD_ADMIN=0` turns it off

The admin SPA and the file-store IDE were opt-in: `cargo build -p sc-cli` produced a binary that
served no admin UI unless you remembered `SC_BUILD_ADMIN=1`, and forgetting produced a *working*
binary whose browser page was blank — a failure that looks like a bug in the server and is
diagnosed by reading §9 of the README. The default is now the other way round. A plain
`cargo build` runs `npm ci && npm run build` in `ui/admin` **and** `ui/ide` and embeds both
paths, so the binary you get is the whole product.

`SC_BUILD_ADMIN` survives as the opt-**out**, for the builds that genuinely have no Node
toolchain: set it to `0`, `false`, `False` or `FALSE` and [the build
script](crates/sc-cli/build.rs) is a no-op beyond its `rerun-if-changed` lines. Four spellings
because a shell, a Makefile and a CI file each have their own habit, and a variable that
recognised only `0` would silently build the UI for someone who wrote `false`. Everything else —
`1`, `true`, unset, a typo — builds: an unrecognised value must fail towards the complete binary,
since that is the outcome whose failure mode is visible. [`tests/build_script.rs`
](crates/sc-cli/tests/build_script.rs) pulls the build script in as a module (cargo does not
compile one as a test target) and asserts that table against the function the build actually
calls.

CI sets `SC_BUILD_ADMIN: 0` workspace-wide. Its jobs check Rust — fmt, clippy, tests — and none
of them read a bundle, so two `npm ci` runs per job (the IDE alone is ~1 GB of monaco packages,
on a runner whose disk is already the binding constraint) would buy nothing. That is the
distinction the flip encodes: a person building the binary wants the product, and the pipelines
that want only the Rust half know it and can say so.

## Three databases, one binary: named environments in a configuration file

The server knew one way to be told where its database is: flags, or the environment. That works
for a container and works badly for a machine with a production database, a staging database and
a test database on it, where an operator either re-exports `DATABASE_URL` between runs or writes
three wrapper scripts. So the connection parameters can now live on disk, named, and the command
line picks one: `saltcorn serve --environment staging`, `--env test`, or whatever else
`[environments.*]` defines — the table is ordinary TOML, so a deployment may have as many
environments as it has databases. `build-app` takes the same flags, since deploying to staging
means building there too.

[crates/sc-cli/src/config_file.rs](crates/sc-cli/src/config_file.rs) is the reader. Where the
file lives is the operating system's business rather than ours, so it asks the platform — the
user configuration directory (`$XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, `~/Library/Application Support`, `%APPDATA%`)
and then the system one (`/etc`, `%PROGRAMDATA%`), the second mattering as much as the first
because a server started by systemd runs as a service account that may have no home directory at
all. `--config PATH` / `SALTCORN_CONFIG` names a file outright. TOML rather than YAML or JSON
because an operator can put a comment next to the line saying why staging points where it does,
and because its failure mode is a parse error with a line number rather than a silent coercion;
the crate is built `default-features = false` as a reader only, which is all anything here does.

**Nothing is stepped over.** A file that does not parse, a `--config` that does not exist, an
`--environment` the file does not define (the error lists the ones it does), or a key spelled
`databse` are all startup errors — a misconfiguration that got quietly ignored here would not
mean a missing feature, it would mean connecting to the wrong database and writing to it. The one
quiet path is *no file at all*, which is not a misconfiguration but the environment-variable
deployment this sits alongside. A section that holds passwords and is readable by other users
gets a warning on stderr, not a refusal: that is the operator's call, but it is worth a sentence.

**Naming an environment inverts the precedence, deliberately.** The default order is flags, then
`DATABASE_URL`/`PG*`, then the file — the file supplies what the environment does not, which is
what was asked for. But `--environment staging` is an instruction, and an operator who gives it
on a box where `DATABASE_URL` happens to point at production must not be quietly connected to
production; so a *named* environment whose section says anything is authoritative for that run
and the ambient variables are ignored entirely — entirely, not per field, since a section giving
host and database while `DATABASE_URL` still supplied the URL would be the same accident wearing
a smaller hat. Explicit `--db-*` flags still win over everything, and an empty placeholder section
takes over nothing. `serve` prints which environment it connected with, and from which file,
before it connects: which database this process is about to write to is the one fact worth saying
out loud.

The tests are in three layers, because the failure modes are: parsing and the refusals as unit
tests in `config_file.rs`; the precedence rules in `db.rs`, driven through `DbConfig::extract`
with a fixture file and written to hold whatever `DATABASE_URL` the suite happens to run with;
and [crates/sc-cli/tests/config_file.rs](crates/sc-cli/tests/config_file.rs) end to end against a
real Postgres, where a file naming three environments — two of them pointed at a dead port on
purpose — boots the catalog on the third. That last one is the only place the claim can actually
be checked: the suite runs with `DATABASE_URL` set to a *different* database, so a `users` table
in the per-test database whose name only the file knew is the proof that naming an environment
beat the ambient variable, rather than the code merely saying it does.

## The GraphQL provider, written down where the design lives

§13.4 of the technical design described a provider that did not exist yet, in a paragraph. It now
describes the one that does: the schema shape a caller actually sees, the naming rule (and why an
underivable name is an omission with a diagnostic rather than something mangled to fit), the
aggregate lowering with the SQL it produces, the level-batched execution, the four authorization
rules that are genuinely new because an aggregate and a projected join are new ways to *observe*
rows, and the four limits with their defaults and the reason each is counted where it is. Design
documents are read by people deciding whether to change something, so it says why each of those
went the way it did rather than what the modules are called.

[docs/GRAPHQL_API.md](docs/GRAPHQL_API.md) keeps its own job — the libraries surveyed, the
deviations from Hasura, the alternatives rejected — and is marked **implemented**, with a note at
each of the three places the implementation went somewhere the record did not: the explorer
became an admin endpoint because `connect-src 'self'` forbids the admin page from reaching an
app's subdomain at all; a database integer is `BigInt` rather than GraphQL's 32-bit `Int`, and a
decimal is a string, because a scalar must not lose information silently; and a GraphQL
aggregate's subquery aliases are `_sc_g…` so that one statement can carry both a calculated field
and an aggregate without a collision. A design record that quietly disagrees with the code is
worse than no record, and rewriting it to match would erase the decisions it exists to hold.

[docs/tutorial-graphql.md](docs/tutorial-graphql.md) is the other half, in the shape the other
tutorials take: two tables and a relation, an application with **both** providers on one
subdomain, the milestone's own query run in the explorer, and then the same query typed in the
React app so a column removed in the admin UI is a `tsc` error naming the call site. It says what
the server did with each query — two statements, and one if you drop the child list, because the
constrained count was never a round trip of its own — and it carries a `curl` recipe, since a
GraphQL endpoint answers `200` with its errors in the body and that is the first sentence
somebody debugging one needs. Two of its steps exist because everything before them runs as an
admin, the one caller no rule applies to: what a caller who is *not* one sees (the nullable child
list, the refusal that is never a quiet zero, a key that is a read of the table it reaches), and
what one query is allowed to cost.

## An explorer for it, with no more authority than the admin holding it

An application that enables GraphQL now has a screen: a query editor, a variables pane, a
response pane, and its own schema browsed from introspection, reached from a **GraphQL** button
on the applications list (and only on the rows that have one — a row should not offer a dead
end). It opens on something runnable, because a click on a root field in the schema pane writes
the query for it, naming the leaf columns and nothing that would need a nested selection set.

**Not GraphiQL, and not a `fetch` to the app either.** The admin SPA is served under
`default-src 'self'`, so the CDN bundle every GraphQL server ships cannot load; vendoring
GraphiQL into each *application* would be a large dependency in a bundle whose users never see
this screen. The same policy's `connect-src 'self'` also forbids the admin page from calling
`staff.example.com/graphql` at all, and relaxing it so one screen may talk to every subdomain is
a poor trade for a debugging tool. So the screen posts to `runApplicationGraphql`, an admin
endpoint that finds the application's **mounted** GraphQL provider and calls the very
`ApiProvider::handle` a request to that subdomain reaches. The schema, the limits, the row layer
and the §7 authorization are not restated anywhere — the handler's only decision is who is
asking, and it answers `ctx.user`: the signed-in admin.

**Which the screen says, in as many words.** An admin clears every table's role floor and every
ownership rule, so a query that works in the explorer is not evidence that it works for the
application's users. The caption names the person the queries run as and says this shows the
most any caller can see — an explorer quietly holding more authority than the caller being
debugged is a trap, and the sentence lives in the tested model rather than in the markup so that
it cannot quietly go missing.

The panes are the parts that have to be right, so they are a model (`graphqlExplorer.ts`) rather
than a component: malformed variables are refused *here*, naming the pane they came from, rather
than coming back as "invalid JSON body" from a round trip; a GraphQL endpoint answers `200` with
its errors in the body, so a refusal is rendered as a refusal; and a **partial** result — Phase
4's nullable child list, parents returned with the child table's refusal on that field — is
rendered as both panes at once, because showing either half alone would hide the design.

Proved against a real server as well as in `vitest`: the same document through the explorer and
through the application's own mount, in the same admin's session, is the same answer;
introspection through it describes the application's schema and not a table outside its declared
subset, which is neither browsable nor answerable; a write through it is the row layer's write,
read back over REST; and an application that is not built, or that does not enable the provider,
is told which of the two it is.

## GraphQL, enabled: an application turns it on and its build types itself against it

Everything before this was a provider nobody could reach. An application now enables `graphql`
beside `rest` — two mounts, one subdomain, one table subset, one authorization layer — because
`app_providers_with` builds it from the *same* resolved tables and the *same* JavaScript
evaluator the REST projection gets. Not "also configured the same way": the same values, so the
two APIs over one application cannot disagree about which tables exist or about a row an
ownership formula decides. A `File` field's `url` points at the REST mount the bytes are already
served from, so the second protocol does not become a second download path.

**Two providers on one mount is refused.** The mount check only ever refused `/`; a request
resolves to the longest matching mount, so REST and GraphQL both on `/api` is a whole API that
is mounted, generated a client for, and unreachable — with nothing to say so. It is named at
save time now, like every other configuration error.

**The provider list is served, not remembered.** `listApiProviders` returns what
`app_providers_with` switches on, and the application form renders it as a select. A provider
name was the one field of an application whose typo survived the save and turned up later as
"unknown API provider" from a mount that failed; a picker built from the same list cannot offer
a name that will not mount. Picking one fills an empty mount with that provider's usual
sub-path, and a stored name this server does not register stays selected rather than being
silently rewritten to something else.

**The build writes the schema into the app's source tree.** `src/saltcorn/schema.graphql`, from
`Schema::sdl()` of the projection that will actually be mounted, beside `client.ts` and
`hooks.ts` and regenerated with them — so a column added in the admin UI is in the contract the
app compiles against at the next build, with nobody exporting an SDL by hand. Only for an
application that enables the provider: a `schema.graphql` in the tree of an app that serves no
GraphQL is a file every editor would believe and no server would honour.

**The client is a hundred lines of `fetch` and no dependency** (decision 7). `graphql(document,
variables)` posts to the app's own mount with the session cookie and the same double-submit CSRF
header the REST client carries, and throws the server's own errors — a GraphQL endpoint answers
`200` with the errors in the body, so a status code says nothing. `graphqlRequest` returns the
whole response for the case where a partial result *is* the answer, which is what Phase 4's
nullable child lists produce. The types are TypeScript's own work: `gql.tada` is configured in
the generated `tsconfig.json` against the emitted SDL — a language-service plugin `tsc` ignores,
so nothing was added to `package.json` and the project builds identically whether or not the
developer installs it. There is no codegen step to run, and a query the schema no longer answers
fails the app's own `tsc --noEmit`.

Proved by running it: a scaffolded project whose page issues the milestone's query — a
department, its employees, and a constrained aggregate over them — installs, type-checks and
builds; and through the assembled router, one application answers that query on `/graphql`
while `/api` answers the REST call for the same rows in the same session, with a column added
through the admin API appearing in the served SDL on the *next request* over the
`SchemaObserver` seam, no restart and no rebuild.

## GraphQL: the whole read is the caller's, and a query has a price

Phase 5 left one thing refused: a child table under row-level security, counted inside a parent
read that was **not** a caller-context transaction. Its policies would have run with no caller
set, and answered whatever they make of nobody — so the aggregate said so instead of answering.
It answers now. A read that projects a correlated subquery over a protected table asks for the
transaction its own table did not need: `RowQuery::in_caller_context` says the *statement*
reaches RLS, and `rows::in_context` — which has always been "the table decides" — now grants a
transaction to a statement that reaches one. A restricted caller's `departments { name
employees_aggregate { count } }` counts their own employees, per department, in one statement;
an anonymous caller counts none, because an unset GUC has never meant "no restriction".

**A key is not a way around a floor.** Auditing that path turned up a real leak: an outgoing
`Key` projects a Ⱶ-join subquery over the target table, and nothing was checking that the caller
may read the *target*. `employees { department { name } }` handed over departments to a caller
whose `{ departments { name } }` was refused by name. `ownership::join_guard` is the rule now,
and it is the sibling of `aggregate_guard`: the caller meets the target's read floor, or the
target's policies decide inside the caller's transaction, or it refuses naming the table. There
is deliberately no third option. A join subquery is built from the schema shape and has no
`WHERE` this provider owns, so an ownership *formula* — which decides row by row — cannot be
carried into it, and a caller whose access comes from one is told to query the table directly
rather than handed a row the formula withholds, one column at a time.

**Four limits, per application, with defaults that are set** (`GraphqlLimits`). Depth and
complexity are `async-graphql` validation rules compiled into the schema, so a too-deep or
too-wide document is refused with **no statement issued** — asserted by counting statements, not
by reading the message. The row cap bounds every list field, an absent `limit` taking it and a
larger one clamped to it. The fourth is counted rather than predicted: the number of database
round trips is not a property of a document, because one child list field is one statement per
level however many parents asked for it, so `StatementBudget` is charged at each read and write
and refuses the one that would exceed it — the point of a budget being the work not done. The
defaults admit the standard introspection query, which is deeper than anything written by hand,
because **introspection stays on**: the SDL is served at `{mount}/schema.graphql` and describes
tables the application already exposes over REST.

The two library refusals are rewritten to name the bound they hit. "Query is nested too deep."
is true and useless to the person holding the query; the bound is this application's
configuration, and this is the only place that knows it.

**Nothing a caller writes reaches SQL as an identifier**, and there are tests that say so rather
than a comment. A response key is a GraphQL name by the parser's own grammar, so an alias
spelling a SQL fragment is not a document; the test asserts the stronger thing — that the
statement is byte-for-byte identical apart from the aggregate's own result alias, which *is* the
response key by design. A fragment produces the same read as the selection it stands for. A
variable is a value: it is coerced against the column and bound as a parameter, so
`'; DROP TABLE employees; --` matches nothing and leaves the table where it was. The one place a
caller's input is genuinely about an identifier — `count(distinct: Column)` — is an enum in the
schema and is checked against the catalog again anyway, because the catalog is the only thing
that may decide a column name.

## GraphQL mutations, which are the row layer with a second front door

`insert_X(object:)`, `update_X_by_pk(pk_columns:, set:)` and `delete_X_by_pk(id:)` are in every
application's schema, and each is a thin call into `ownership::insert_row_as` /
`update_row_as` / `delete_row_as` — the same entry points the agent's write tools use, which go
through `rows::create_row_ctx` / `update_row_guarded` / `delete_row_guarded`. So a rich type's
`max_length`, a `File` field's store and folder rules, §7.3's "granted on the existing row *and*
on the row as it would become", RLS's `WITH CHECK` and the table's triggers all fire for a
GraphQL caller **because they are the same code**, not because this provider remembered them. A
test inserts the same row over GraphQL and over REST and compares the rows *and the events they
raised*; nothing in the GraphQL module coerces a value or decides who may write.

**A refusal is labelled, not just worded.** A mutation is run by an application on behalf of
somebody filling in a form, and that application has to tell "this row is not yours" from "the
server fell over" without parsing English. So a write's error carries
`extensions.code` (`BAD_USER_INPUT` / `FORBIDDEN` / `NOT_FOUND` / `CONFIGURATION_ERROR` /
`INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR` — the conventional GraphQL vocabulary, not one invented here),
`extensions.table`, and `extensions.field` for a validation failure. The field name is lifted
out of the row layer's own `` `age`: must be at most 120 `` — the message it already writes
because it is shown to a *user* — and is only reported when the name it finds is really a column
of the table, since a `field` pointing at an input that does not exist is worse than none.

**An insert and an update answer with the row read back.** A write's `RETURNING` carries the
row's own columns and nothing else, because it was never given the selection set — so a
`manager { email }` on the result would have to be answered `null`, which says "no manager"
rather than "not asked". Instead the response is projected by the *same* read `_by_pk` performs,
which is why `insert_projects(object: …) { name tasks_aggregate { count } }` works and returns
what a query would have. A delete has no row left to read, so it answers with the columns the
row layer removed and **refuses** a selection reaching past them.

That last one needed the row layer to stop deciding a wire shape: `delete_row_guarded` returned
`{"deleted": true}` while holding the row it had just read for the delete event — the only copy
of it anyone will ever get. It now returns the row, and the REST projection wraps it in its own
acknowledgement at its own boundary, where that contract belongs.

**Every table gets its mutations, and the schema is still one** (decision 4). A table whose
`min_role_write` this caller does not meet is in the SDL and refuses at resolve time naming
itself, because a caller who gains a role should not need a different schema. What a table does
*not* get is a mutation the row layer could never carry out: no `update`/`delete` without a
single primary key to address a row by (the rule `_by_pk` already follows), and no insert
without a writable column to name. `XInsertInput` and `XSetInput` differ by exactly the primary
key, which addresses an update rather than being set by it; a calculated field is in neither,
turning a runtime refusal into something a caller's editor tells them; and a `File` field is
written as its path and read as a `FileValue`, because there is nothing to write to a URL. Every
input field is nullable, including one whose column is `NOT NULL`: what is required of an insert
is not what is required of the *caller*, and demanding a sequence-backed key would refuse every
insert into a table that has one.

## GraphQL aggregates, computed by the database in the query that asked

The milestone's motivating question answers, and it answers in one statement:

```graphql
departments {
  name
  employees_aggregate(where: { salary: { lt: 50000 } }) { count }
}
```

Each requested aggregate becomes a correlated subquery **projected as another column of the
departments query** — `(SELECT count(*) FROM "employees" "_sc_g1" WHERE "_sc_g1"."department" =
"departments"."id" AND "_sc_g1"."salary" < $1) AS "employees_aggregate.count"` — so a query
over N departments costs one read, not N, and the child's predicate is folded into the
subquery's `WHERE` rather than applied to rows fetched and discarded. The rule is not "the root
projects the aggregates" but "whichever read reaches the row projects them", so a batched child
list carries its own: `departments { employees { tasks_aggregate { count } } }` is still two
statements.

**The provider does not aggregate.** Every expression comes from `sc-expr`'s builders — the
same ones a Ↄ-chain in a calculated field or an ownership formula goes through — so `sum` over
no rows is `0` and `avg` over no rows is null wherever the question is asked. The correlated
form was already there; a root `X_aggregate` needed the uncorrelated one, and rather than write
a second decision table `correlated_aggregate` and the new `aggregate_expr` share the one that
says what each function answers over an empty relation.

**Where the values ride back** is the selection's own response-key path: `count`,
`total.salary`, `employees_aggregate.count`. That is what makes an alias work — the same
relation aggregated twice under different `where` arguments gets two subqueries with two
aliases and two response keys, and the resolvers find their values by stripping their own key
the way a Ⱶ-joined row already strips its own prefix. A response key is a GraphQL name, so
nothing a caller writes reaches SQL as anything but a quoted alias.

**An aggregate never counts a row the caller may not read**, and where that cannot be arranged
it is a refusal naming the table rather than a number. A count is computed by the database over
whatever the `WHERE` leaves, so the rule has to *be* a predicate: the role floor admits
everything, a translatable ownership formula becomes a predicate ANDed into the subquery, and
an **untranslatable** one — a formula only the JavaScript evaluator can decide — refuses. This
is where `sc-expr` grew `translate_rooted`: a predicate evaluated inside a subquery that
aliased the table cannot name the table, so the whole formula is translated rooted at the
alias, exactly as `join_path_expr_rooted` already did for a single join path. A child table
under RLS whose parent's read is not a caller-context transaction refuses for the same reason —
its policies would not apply, and the number would be whatever they make of no caller. A silent
`0` is worse than a refusal, because nobody investigates it.

One narrowing on the wire: Postgres widens `sum(bigint)` to `numeric`, and the schema promises
`BigInt`. The sum comes back as the number the SDL says it is, and a sum that genuinely does
not fit in 64 bits stays an exact decimal rather than being truncated into a wrong one.

## GraphQL child lists, one query per level

`departments { name employees { name } }` answers, and it answers in **two**
statements rather than one plus one per department. The child field resolves through
`async_graphql::dataloader::DataLoader`: each parent's resolver only says which rows it wants
— the relation, this field's arguments, this parent's key — and the loader turns the siblings
that asked the same thing into one `SELECT … WHERE department IN (…)`.

**What makes two loads one read** is the key: relation, arguments, parent. Two siblings share
the first two and collapse; the *same* relation asked twice under different `where` arguments
does not, because it is a different question, and it gets its own read and its own answer.
Neither a `RowQuery` nor a `Value` is hashable — they carry expressions, floats and decimals —
so the batch key is their `Debug` rendering, which is structural, deterministic, and
distinguishes `Int(1)` from `Text("1")` so no parent can be handed another's children.

**A `limit` on a child list is per parent**, which is what `row_number()` was added to
`sc-query` for: "three employees each" is not "three employees". It arrives as a new
`RowQuery::partition`, and the row layer renders it by numbering the rows *inside* the read —
the same read the ownership predicate is ANDed into — and comparing the numbering outside it.
So the window can never rank a row the caller may not see. The JavaScript-evaluator path,
which already withheld `limit`/`offset` until after the evaluator had spoken, now withholds
the per-parent bound for exactly the same reason.

**A child list nobody bounded is an error when it reaches the cap, where a root list simply
truncates.** The difference is not inconsistency: a root list's cap bounds the response the
caller asked for, while a child list's cap is *shared between the parents*, so reaching it
means some department's list was cut short and nothing in the response says whose. The
message names the relation and says that a `limit` is what bounds each parent on its own.

**The child's rules are the child's.** The read is `ownership::read_row_values_as` over the
child table, so a caller who may not read `employees` is refused there — not because this
provider remembered to check. That refusal is an error on *that field* with every parent still
returned, which required one deviation from Hasura's shape: a child list field is nullable
(`[Employees!]`, not `[Employees!]!`), because GraphQL propagates an error on a non-null field
up to its parent, and a query for departments should not lose the departments over the
employees. Partial results are the point.

One thing came back from the Ⱶ-join side: a child list *behind* a join (`department { employees
{ … } }`) only works if the parent's query projected the joined row's key, so the selection
walk now projects it — the same reason a `File` field on the far side of a join already
projected one. Where the relation is keyed on something other than the primary key the
resolver refuses by name rather than answering an empty list.

## GraphQL reads rows

The GraphQL provider's root fields now answer. `departments(where: …, order_by: …, limit: …,
offset: …)`, `departments_by_pk(id: …)`, a row's columns, and — the part that matters —
its outgoing keys.

**Not a second data path, demonstrated rather than asserted.** A root list is
`ownership::read_row_values_as`: the same function the agent's `query_table` reads through, the
same "meets the role floor OR the ownership formula grants this row", the same RLS routing into
a caller-context transaction. `_by_pk` is that query with a primary-key equality ANDed in.
`read_rows_as` did not move; it became a *rendering* of the new values-returning entry point,
because a GraphQL read needs two things the JSON wire shape cannot give it — a `Decimal` that
stays exact rather than passing through a JSON number, and the extra projected columns a
joinfield rides back on.

**An outgoing key is projected, not fetched.** A requested `manager { email }` reads the
selection set, asks `sc-expr` for the Ⱶ-join's correlated subquery, and projects it as another
column of the *same* `SELECT`, aliased by the join path itself (`managerⱵemail`); the `manager`
resolver then hands its children that row with the prefix stripped, so a second hop composes by
doing exactly the same thing again. Only the leaves the caller named are projected. The test
that this is true counts statements rather than milliseconds — it records the SQL the catalog
runs, asserts there was one of it, and asserts which columns it asked for. A joinfield that
quietly became a query per parent would still be fast on four rows.

A null foreign key is `null`, not an error and not an object of nulls: the key column rides back
with the row and ends the walk before a leaf is looked at. That is the Ⱶ operator's own contract.

**Filter literals are coerced against the column, not against JSON.** `{ due: { lt: "2026-01-01" } }`
binds a *date*, because the value goes through `rows::column_value` — the same function an
insert's body goes through — so the comparison the database performs is the one the column's
type means. The deliberate exception is `like`/`ilike`, whose argument is a pattern rather than
a value: holding `"%urgent%"` to a `String` field's `options` or `regex` attribute would refuse
a perfectly well-formed query. Null semantics are SQL's — `eq`/`ne` are `=` and `<>`, and
`is_null` is how nullness is asked about — because a null-safe `eq` here would make the same
filter mean different things in a `where` and in an ownership formula.

**A `limit` is a request, not a permission.** An absent one becomes the application's row cap
and a present one is clamped to it, so a list field with no bound cannot be how a table ends up
streamed into a response by accident. (Phase 7 makes the cap configuration; this is the default
it will have.)

One shape change reaches outside this provider: `ApiProvider::handle` now takes the catalog as
the `Arc` the server already holds. `async-graphql`'s field resolvers are `'static` closures
built once at mount, so the catalog reaches them as request data or not at all; a provider that
only reads it borrows through the `Arc` and pays nothing. The resolvers look the table up in the
live catalog on every request, which is what keeps the schema *data*: a column added this
morning is in the row the resolver reads.

## The GraphQL schema, before anything answers it

`sc-api` gains a **GraphQL provider** beside the REST one — `POST /graphql` and
`GET /graphql/schema.graphql`, two endpoints because that is genuinely all a GraphQL API is —
and every field of it refuses. That is the point of the phase: the deliverable is the
*contract*, and a resolver that returns a plausible zero is worse than one that says it is not
implemented, for the same reason decision 5 forbids a quiet zero from an aggregate.

**`async-graphql` 7.2 with `dynamic-schema`**, pinned in the workspace with its reasoning. Our
schema is data — an admin or an agent creates a table at runtime and the API must have it at
the next mount — which rules out every macro-driven library, since a compile-time schema means
generating Rust and restarting the server. `default-features = false` drops the web-framework
integrations and the CDN playground: this crate speaks `ApiRequest`/`ApiResponse` and never
axum types, and an application's CSP is `default-src 'self'`.

**Names are derived in one place, and an underivable name is an omission with a diagnostic.**
GraphQL names are `/[_A-Za-z][_0-9A-Za-z]*/`, which Ⱶ and Ↄ are not, so there is a derivation —
and the derivation never mangles. Mangling invents collisions: two tables that differ only in
the characters being stripped become one type, and a schema that quietly answers for the wrong
table is worse than one that admits it is missing a table. PascalCasing a type name can collide
too, so that is checked and reported the same way. The inverse-relation rule is the one a caller
notices: a child is `<child>` when exactly one of its keys points here and `<child>_by_<key>`
when more than one does or when a column already has the name — the same disambiguation Ↄ
spells, arrived at from the other direction.

**The type mapping's rule is that a scalar must not lose information silently.** GraphQL's `Int`
is 32 bits by specification and ours are `bigint`, so an integer column is a `BigInt` scalar,
not an `Int` that works until the ids get large; a decimal is a string-carried `Decimal`, not
the `Float` that would round it. That rule then decided something Hasura does differently:
**`avg` gets its own result type**. Hasura shares one `NumericFields` between `sum` and `avg`,
but the mean of a `BigInt` column is not a `BigInt`, and a schema that says it is has promised
to round somebody's number. A `Key` projects as the target table's object type; a `File`
projects as a path and the URL the *REST* provider already serves the bytes at, because a
GraphQL field must not become a second file-download path.

Both endpoints are public, and that is the same decision as decision 4 rather than a relaxation:
the schema describes what the *application* exposes, and the gate is the table's own — its role
floor and its ownership formula, at resolve time. An endpoint-level gate here would be a second,
coarser rule that disagreed with the first. Introspection stays on for the same reason it is
safe: it describes the tables REST already exposes.

The SDL is checked in as a snapshot over a deliberately awkward fixture — a child with two keys
back to its parent, a `File` field, a column of every scalar family, and a table whose name
GraphQL cannot spell so that its *absence* is part of the contract too. Every later phase of
this milestone edits the schema builder; the snapshot is what will say whether anything else
moved with it.

## One builder for "an aggregate over a child table"

The GraphQL milestone's first phase, and it is a refactor rather than an invention: the
correlated-subquery builder that `employeesↃdepartment.filter(r => r.salary < 50000).length`
has always lowered to is now a **public function over an explicit spec**, and the formula
translator calls it too.

The alternative was tempting and wrong. A GraphQL aggregate selection could have been turned
into JavaScript source — `"employeesↃdepartment.filter(r => r.salary < 50000).length"` — and
fed to the existing parser; the translator parameterises literals, so there would have been no
injection. But it would have made correctness depend on JS-escaping user data into a source
string, to reach a subquery builder that was private only by accident. So `sc-expr` grows
`correlated_aggregate(AggregateSpec)`: child table, key field, parent (a table name *or* an
alias, because a GraphQL child aggregate correlates to whatever the parent query called the
parent), parent field, alias, function, `DISTINCT`, the value expression and an extra predicate
already qualified with the alias.

What the builder carries is the part that would have drifted. The empty relation's answer is a
property of the function, not of the caller — `sum` is `0` and `avg`/`min`/`max` are `null`,
per AGG_EXPRS.md's semantics table — and a second implementation of that table would eventually
disagree with the first, quietly, in the direction of a number nobody checks. `Translator::
aggregation` now assembles a spec and asks; the Ↄ path's golden SQL strings and the reified-
evaluator parity tests are the proof that nothing changed, and a new test asserts the two entry
points produce the *identical* `Expr` — not merely SQL that matches today. The two paths that
still build their own subquery (`includes`, which is a membership test, and `maxBy`/`minBy`,
which picks a row rather than aggregating) correlate through the same `correlation` function.

Two smaller pieces the later phases need. `sc-query` gains `Expr::Window` — `func(args) OVER
(PARTITION BY … ORDER BY …)`, with the name structural as `Agg::func` is — because `row_number()`
partitioned by the parent key is the only way to give a *nested child list* a per-parent `limit`
in one round trip: `Agg` has no `OVER` and there is no `LATERAL`. And `join_path_expr_rooted`
exposes what the translator has always done internally for an aggregation arrow's
`r.publisherⱵname`: the same join path correlated from a subquery's **alias** rather than from
a table's own name, which is exactly what a GraphQL filter over a child table is written
against.

## A plan for the GraphQL API

The agents milestone is finished and archived to `docs/TODO-post-mvp-6.md`; `TODO.md` is now the
seventh: a **GraphQL provider** beside the REST one. The reason it is worth a milestone is not
protocol coverage — it is that REST cannot express a *shape* a caller chooses, and in particular
cannot answer "for each department, the number of employees earning below 50 000" without a route
invented per question.

`docs/GRAPHQL_API.md` records the survey behind it. On the Rust side the choice is
**`async-graphql` 7.2 with the `dynamic-schema` feature**: our schema is data — an admin or an
agent creates a table at runtime and the API must have it at the next mount — which rules out
every macro-driven library, `juniper` included, since a compile-time schema means generating Rust
and restarting the server. `apollo-compiler` parses and validates but does not execute, and would
have us write variable coercion, introspection and the error format for no capability we lack; it
stays as the fallback if we ever own execution. Seaography 2.0 builds the same thing over SeaORM
and is the prior art that the dynamic API carries a real schema. On the browser side, an
application's `default-src 'self'` decides more than taste: the generated client is a
dependency-free `fetch` wrapper emitted beside the typed REST client, the build writes
`schema.graphql` from `Schema::sdl()` so `gql.tada` types queries with no codegen step, and the
explorer is an **admin screen** rather than the CDN-loaded GraphiQL every server ships.

**The aggregations already exist.** `sc-expr`'s Ↄ inverse-relation chains (docs/AGG_EXPRS.md)
translate exactly the motivating question into one correlated subquery, so the provider lowers a
GraphQL aggregate selection into the same builder and projects it as another column of the parent
`SELECT` — the constrained child count is computed by the database, in the query that fetched the
parents. That makes the plan's first phase a refactor rather than an invention: expose the
subquery builder structurally, and make the translator call it too, so the formula path and the
wire path cannot drift. Reads stay on `ownership::read_rows_as` and writes on the row layer's own
entry points, so §7.3 applies because it is the same code.

Two rules the plan will not compromise on, both of them about aggregates being a new way to
observe rows: an aggregate must never count a row the caller may not read (under RLS the
correlated subquery is subject to the child's policies — a property to test, not assume; without
RLS the child's ownership predicate is ANDed into the subquery), and a child ownership formula
that cannot be translated **refuses** the aggregate rather than returning a number computed over
rows the caller cannot see. Depth and complexity limits are mandatory rather than advisable: an
arbitrary query language is an arbitrary cost, which REST's fixed routes never were.

## An application is created with the agent that builds it

Creating an application now creates an agent for building it, and **which agent that is belongs to
the framework**. `framework_builder_agent` sits beside the framework's other declarations — its
settings, its default CSP — and answers the same way, from the framework's name, because an
application is created long before there is a built instance to ask.

Both code frameworks declare the same shape, because it is what building an app of theirs consists
of: the `coding` trait scoped to the source directory `app_source_from_config` resolves — React's
derived project directory, `code`'s stated one — and `build_application` on the app's own
subdomain. So a new React application called Todo arrives with an agent called `build-todo` that
can read, search and edit `apps/todo` and build the Todo application, and nothing else: two apps
sharing a store are two agents, neither able to touch the other's source. `may_edit` is on, since
changing the source is the point of it; `may_run_scripts` stays off, since running the project's
other scripts executes code the agent did not write and building has its own tool. The system
prompt is the framework's too — React's says that `src/saltcorn/` is generated and rewritten on
every build, which is the convention a model would otherwise break with its first edit.

**What the framework declares is data.** The traits live two layers above `sc-app`, so a framework
names them and their settings (`BuilderAgentSpec`) exactly as an application names its API
providers; the server assembles that into an agent and saves it through the same `save_agent` the
Agents screen calls, so the same validation applies and the result is an ordinary agent an admin
can edit, re-point or delete. Because the names are strings on one side and Rust on the other,
`sc-core-traits` — the one layer that can see both — holds them to each other in a test, which is
what turns a renamed setting into a build failure instead of an `agent_error` on every application
created afterwards.

**It is created beside the application, never instead of it.** The creation response carries `agent`
or `agent_error` the way it already carries `scaffolded` / `scaffold_error`, and the form's banner
reports both: a deployment with no LLM provider connected still gets its application and is told in
one sentence why it has no builder agent. Which provider it uses is not something a framework can
know, so the server takes the first connected one and the admin changes it like any other setting.
An agent of that name already there is left alone — an admin who had already made a `build-todo`
keeps theirs.

**Deleting the application deletes the agent**, because a builder scoped to an application can do
nothing once the application is gone; left behind, it is an entry in the agents list whose only
remaining property is that it does not work. What decides it is the trait rather than the name: only
an agent still carrying `build_application` for that subdomain is that application's builder, so an
agent an admin made themselves under that name, or re-pointed at another application, survives —
while edits to the real builder do not buy it survival, since an agent that still names this
application is still this application's. Its runs are kept, as they are when an agent is deleted
directly, and the delete response names what went so the list screen can say so.

## A chat can be popped out of its page and kept in the corner

A conversation with an agent is almost always *about* something else in the admin — the table it
just described, the trigger it says is failing, the build it broke. Until now the chat was a page,
so following any of that up meant leaving the conversation: the transcript is at
`#/agents/x/chat` and nothing else is.

**The chat now pops out.** A button in the top right of the chat page hands the conversation to a
store that outlives the router, and it reappears as a window in the bottom-right corner while the
page navigates on to wherever the answer pointed. Gmail's popped-out reply, with Gmail's three
controls on the window's title bar: minimize to a thin bar along the bottom, full screen to a
centred modal over a dimmed page (the same button brings it back, as does Escape), and close. Up
to three sit alongside each other along the bottom, oldest on the left.

**A window is the same chat, not a second one.** `AgentChat` renders both, and the differences are
the chrome and the conversation rail — 17rem of navigation between conversations belongs beside a
transcript that has the page, not inside a window a third that width, so it returns when the
window goes full screen. What a window does *not* do is move: every mode is a class on an element
that stays where it was rendered, because each window holds a live socket and a transcript that
exists nowhere else until the turn ends, and a window that changed parent on its way to full
screen would drop the conversation to get there. Minimizing hides the transcript rather than
unmounting it, for the same reason.

**Popping out mid-turn is refused**, with the button saying why. It closes one socket and opens
another, and the answer being streamed is coming down the one being closed — a button that quietly
lost an answer would be worse than one that asks you to wait. Popping out a conversation reopened
from the history brings it back to life rather than carrying the read-only view across: a window
is somewhere to carry on. A run already open in a window is raised rather than opened twice, which
is what keeps one conversation from being written by two sockets.

**The chat screen keeps clear of the corner** while a window is docked over it. Every other screen
is a document with a chat floating over the bottom of it, which is what a floating chat is for —
but the chat screen's composer is pinned to that exact corner, so a window there is a page whose
send button cannot be clicked. It gives up as much width as the row of windows actually occupies,
capped, because three windows are wider than most screens.

Also fixed, because a title bar with four of them made it impossible to ignore: **Tabler's
icon-only buttons had no box.** `.btn-icon` zeroes the horizontal padding and then pulls the icon
back in by that same padding on every side, which on `.btn-sm` leaves the button two pixels of
border with the glyph overflowing it — it looked nearly right and was nearly impossible to hit,
and two side by side overlapped. Every icon button in the admin is now square and hittable.

`chatWindows.ts` holds the model — which windows exist, what mode each is in, one full screen at a
time, the ceiling — as pure transitions over plain data, so it is tested without a browser the way
the transcript is (`chatWindows.test.ts`). A Rust test keeps the class names in the two components
and the rules in `admin.css` from drifting apart, including the one that fails silently: a window
must not carry `.chat-page`, which would tell the whole admin it is a chat screen.

## The agent chat is a chat interface, not a page of cards

The chat panel worked and looked like the rest of the admin: a bordered card holding the
transcript, a textarea and three buttons in its footer, a second card of history beside it, all
inside a page that scrolled as a whole. Everything a hosted coding agent's web UI does — Kimi,
z.ai — it did not, and the difference is not decoration: a card in a scrolling page means the box
you type into moves away from you as the answer grows, which is the one thing a chat must not do.

**The screen now claims the viewport.** The transcript scrolls inside it, the composer stays where
the hand left it, and the page furniture (Tabler's footer) is out of the way. It is the one admin
screen not built from `PageHeader` + cards, and it says so for itself: `admin.css` keys the
full-height rules off `:has(> .chat-page)` rather than threading a prop through the router for one
route, and a Rust test asserts the class names in the screen and the rules in the stylesheet still
agree — a divergence no compiler would notice, whose symptom is the composer below the fold.

**The transcript reads as a conversation.** What the person said is a tinted bubble, bounded and
right-aligned; what the agent said is full-width prose with no bubble at all, because it is the
content of the page and a border around every answer is a border around everything. Fenced code in
an answer is rendered as a code block with a Copy button — not a Markdown renderer, and
deliberately not the beginning of one, but an agent that reads and writes files answers with code,
and code in the body font with its indentation collapsed is the one part of an answer that cannot
be read at all. An unterminated fence is still a block, so a streaming answer does not reflow under
the reader when the turn ends. Reasoning is a quiet disclosure above the answer; a tool call is one
quiet pill naming the tool, with three animating dots while it runs, opening onto its arguments and
its result.

**History is a rail, not a card.** Past conversations sit on the left where a chat interface keeps
them, grouped Today / Yesterday / Previous 7 days / Previous 30 days / Older — a list of forty
timestamps is not a list anyone reads — with the destructive delete revealed on hover rather than
sitting under the pointer of someone reaching for the conversation. It collapses, and below
Tabler's `lg` breakpoint it slides over the transcript as a drawer instead of taking half the
screen. The transcript follows a streaming turn only while the reader is *at* the bottom of it: a
tool-using turn that runs for a minute must not yank the view back down from what it said two tool
calls ago.

**The composer is a capsule with a toolbar row inside it**, and that row is the point. A trait may
in future contribute **buttons and dropdowns to the box a message is written in** — "search the web
for this one", "which branch am I working on" — so the shape is in place now rather than being
retrofitted by redesigning the composer under the first trait that wants one:

- `ComposerControl` is **data**, of two kinds: a `toggle` and a `select`. A trait declares controls
  the way it declares a tool's schema; the server forwards them in a `controls` event; the panel
  renders whatever arrives and never knows which trait sent it — §11.2's rule for the trait config
  form, applied where a trait speaks to a person mid-conversation.
- A control's value **travels with the next message** (`{"type":"message","text":…,"controls":…}`),
  which is what makes it a modifier on what is about to be said rather than a button that does
  something on its own. A control that acts immediately would be a client frame of its own and is
  not invented until a trait asks for one. An agent with no controls sends exactly the frame it sent
  before, so nothing changes for the traits that exist.
- A declaration that arrives again mid-conversation **keeps a choice already made**, unless the
  redeclared control no longer offers it; a queued message keeps the controls it was *composed*
  with; and a malformed control costs the composer that one control rather than the toolbar.

No trait declares a control yet — no built-in trait has a mode — and no server change was needed:
unknown fields are ignored on the way in, and the event simply never arrives.

## One `coding` trait instead of six

`read_file`, `write_file`, `list_files`, `edit_file`, `search_files` and `run_project_script` were
six agent traits with the *same* configuration on each: a file store and a sub-directory. An admin
building a coding agent therefore answered one question six times, and a change of mind about the
root was six edits, five of which could be forgotten — while the capability actually being granted
was one thing, "this agent works on the code in `web/todo`". They are now a single trait,
**`coding`**, with one form.

**What may be done there is a checkbox, not a separate trait.** Reading, listing and searching come
with the trait; **`may_edit`** adds `write_file` and `edit_file`, and **`may_run_scripts`** adds the
script runner. Both default to off, so a read-only coding agent remains the default shape — the one
property of the six separate grants worth keeping. Running a script is its own grant rather than
part of the edit one because it executes code the agent did not write. This is
`manage_table_admin`'s shape for `manage_table_admin`'s reason: grants that share a scope belong on
one trait rather than on several that can disagree about where they point.

A withheld tool is **not declared to the model** — it plans around reading rather than trying an
edit and being refused — and a call that arrives for one anyway is refused naming the checkbox that
would allow it, because "an administrator must turn on `may_edit`" is something a user can act on
and "not permitted" is not.

**The tool names are unchanged**, and still derived from the scope: `read_file_apps_web`,
`edit_file_apps_web`, `run_script_apps_web`. So the trait enabled twice over two directories is two
distinguishable sets of tools, the same directory twice still collides and is refused on save
(§11.2), and every derived name — not just the shortest — is checked against the 64 characters both
vendors accept. The three bounds that were spread across the old traits (`max_chars` on the read,
`max_results` on the search, `timeout_seconds` on the script) are three fields on the one form.

**`build_application` stays its own trait.** It is configured on a different axis — an
application's subdomain, not a store — and folding it in would mean an agent that builds two
applications out of one source tree needed two `coding` instances, which would then collide on the
file tools' names.

No compatibility shim: the prototype has no stored agents to migrate, and the six old trait names
are simply gone. The six tool implementations moved to `src/coding/` as ordinary modules — same
behaviour, same errors, same descriptions — with `files.rs`'s scope, path-resolution and §9 access
plumbing untouched. Tests: the grants pinned both ways (what is offered, and what a call whose
grant is off is told), one scope reaching every tool, a name from another instance's scope refused
with the names that do work, and the existing coding suite — round trip, the three edit outcomes,
the confinement, the search's bound, the access rule on a listing and a search, the script refusal
— re-run through the one trait. The `coding_agent.rs` end-to-end run now configures two traits
where it configured five.

## `manage_table_admin`: the agent that builds the schema

Agents Phase 7, completed. The milestone's earlier phases were held to "if this ends with a trait
that constructs a table, something has gone wrong"; this phase revises that boundary deliberately,
now that the machinery is proven. An agent given `manage_table_admin` creates the connected tables
and their fields of a whole schema in one act, edits what is already there — including, under its
own grant, the access rules of §7.3 — drops what it is granted to drop, and answers questions
about the schema **without ever seeing a row**.

**The real work was moving the rule out of `sc-server`.** Creating a table and creating a field
existed only as closures inside the admin handlers: `createTable`'s default primary key,
`createField`'s `resolve_field_type` → DDL → `_sc_fields` overlay sequence, `parse_field_kind`,
`validate_calc_field`, `needs_overlay`, and `updateTable`'s `validate_ownership_settings` and
`sync_table_rls`. `sc-core-traits` is layer 9 and cannot name `sc-server`, so a trait that
re-implemented any of that would be a second answer to "what does creating a field mean", and the
two would drift within a release. It is all now **`sc_api::schema_edit`** (layer 8), beside `rows`
and for the same reason; the handlers are thin callers of it, exactly as the REST provider is a
thin caller of `rows`. Six admin endpoints — `createTable`, `updateTable`, `createField`,
`updateField`, and the new `dropTable`/`deleteField` — hand it one `Operation` each.

**A batch is one transaction, one reload and one refusal.** `schema_edit::apply` takes an
*ordered list* of operations, because a schema is a set of connected tables and a per-operation
tool turns a twelve-table ERP into forty round trips. Every operation resolves against a
**projected** catalog (`sc_catalog::SchemaProjection`), so a key pointing at a table created three
operations earlier and an ownership formula naming a field added two operations earlier both
validate — the batch validates against the schema it *ends* with — and only then is any DDL
issued, through one `Transaction` (`Catalog::apply_schema_batch`), with the RLS policy SQL joining
it at the end because it may reference columns the operations above it create. A refused operation
rolls the whole batch back and names itself by index. The catalog reloads **once** rather than once
per operation, which for a twenty-field batch is nineteen introspections it no longer does. The
`_sc_tables`/`_sc_fields` overlay rows are written after the commit and cannot join that
transaction, so `createField`'s partial-failure message — the columns exist, their settings did
not save, edit or drop them and retry — is now the batch's, naming the operation.

**Dropping is new capability, not just a new caller.** There was no `dropTable` and no
`deleteField` anywhere: `SchemaChange::DropTable`/`DropColumn` rendered in `ddl.rs` and nothing
called them. `Catalog::drop_table` and `Catalog::drop_field` now do, each deleting the overlay rows
with the thing they describe — an overlay left behind would be indistinguishable from §1.1's
deliberately-kept orphan — and each refusing **by name and before the DDL** what the database would
otherwise refuse with a foreign-key error nobody can act on: a table another table's `Key`
references (listing those fields), a primary key, a field a calculated field reads, a built-in
column of `users`/`_sc_roles`. The endpoints and the admin UI's Drop table / Delete field buttons
ship with them, because an agent must not be able to do something the admin UI cannot.

**Mounted applications re-project through a seam.** `createField` used to call
`AppMounts::refresh_table` itself, which worked only while an HTTP request was the only way to
change a schema. `sc-catalog` now carries a `SchemaObserver` seam — the shape `TableEvents`
already has, and for the same layering reason — that the server registers on the catalog at boot
and `schema_edit` notifies after a successful batch. The handlers' own `refresh_table` calls are
gone rather than double-firing beside it, and a field an *agent* drops takes the endpoints a
mounted app projected from it with no restart.

**The trait names no table, and that is the phase's most load-bearing deviation.** Every other
built-in names its target, because "which tables may this agent see?" must be answerable off the
agent's definition. This one cannot: the tables it makes do not exist when it is configured. So it
is scoped by *what it may do* — four checkboxes, `allow_create`, `allow_edit`, `allow_drop` and
`allow_access_changes`, the last two off by default — on **one** trait rather than four, because
the operations share a batch and a batch that half-applied for want of a grant is the state the
transaction exists to avoid. A batch containing an ungranted operation is refused whole, naming
the operation and the checkbox. Access changes are their own grant, *above* dropping, because a
drop announces itself and a widened role floor does not.

**Both tools refuse a caller who is not an admin.** Every other trait leans on §7.3 to decide what
a caller may see; a schema has no ownership formula to fall back on, and the admin API guards every
catalog endpoint with `admin()`. Without this, an agent exposed to a role-80 user through a chat
view would hand them the table editor. `_sc_*` tables are invisible to `describe_schema` and
refused by `edit_schema`; `users` and `_sc_roles` are described and may gain a field but are never
dropped and never lose a built-in column.

**`alter_table` is read-modify-write, and omitted means leave** — a deliberate divergence from
`updateTable`'s whole-object contract (§13.1), recorded there beside the contract it diverges from.
The endpoint takes every setting because the admin UI edits a table it has loaded; a model has no
loaded table, and under that contract `{op: alter_table, table: "clients", min_role_read: 40}`
would blank the ownership formula and turn RLS off. Enabling RLS is refused unless the formula
translates for all four operations under the GUC env; **disabling** it always succeeds and says so
in the result, in those words, because that is the one operation here whose damage is invisible in
the schema afterwards.

**Three things the tool schema decides rather than leaving to the model**, each because a guess
costs a turn or a wrong column: the type names are an **enum built from the live registry** (the
same list `listFieldTypes` builds its picker from), so `varchar(255)` cannot be invented; a foreign
key is **`references: <table>`**, with the storage type taken from the target's primary key rather
than asked for, so the pair cannot disagree; and a created table gets the **identity primary key
unasked**. The operation items are flat objects with per-`op` optional fields documented in their
descriptions rather than a `oneOf` discriminated union — providers vary in how well they handle
`oneOf`, and Rust validation naming the missing field for the operation at index *n* is a better
error than a schema the provider silently flattens.

**Tests** are Rust against a real Postgres: a two-table batch whose foreign key actually
constrains rows; a batch whose third operation is invalid leaving nothing applied and naming
index 2; the overlay rows going with a dropped table and a dropped field; a drop refused while
another table references it, listing the referencing fields; a drop refused for `allow_drop` off
and taken with it on; both tools refusing a non-admin; `_sc_agents` and `users` refused;
`describe_schema` on a table full of rows reporting none of their values and no count of them;
`dry_run` refusing the same way and changing nothing; an `alter_table` naming only `min_role_read`
leaving the formula, the RLS flag, the label and the write floor exactly as they were; one batch
that adds a field and then writes a formula naming it; RLS refused for a formula that does not
translate with nothing written, taken for one that does with the four policies emitted and two
callers reading different rows, and disabled again dropping them and saying so. In `sc-server`:
`dropTable` and `deleteField` over the endpoints with their refusals, and a mounted app losing a
`File` field's endpoints when the field is dropped and picking up a tightened role floor on the
next request.

## The agent as a trigger body, and the agents tutorial

Agents Phase 6, completed — and with it the milestone. An insert on a table can now fire a
trigger whose action is an **agent**: the row's data reaches the prompt, the agent's tools run,
and the run it leaves behind is readable afterwards in the chat panel's history.

**`run_agent` is one more registered action**, taking an agent's name and a **prompt formula**
evaluated in the event's scope — the same language as an `only_if`, a `where` and every field
value, reading `row`, `old`, `user` and `payload` under the same rule. So an agent becomes a
trigger body through machinery that already existed (§10.2) and stays editable as an agent. It
returns the final assistant message, the run id and a `conclusion` (`answered` | `max_steps` |
`aborted`); it does **not** stream, because an action returns a value and a caller who wants
deltas is a chat client.

**It lives in `sc-core-traits`, and is registered apart from the built-in actions.** Same layer
as the traits and for the same reason — it drives a loop whose tools reach the row layer — but
through `register_agent_actions`, because it needs two things no other action does: the trait
registry the agents were validated against (one registry, or an agent would be accepted by the
admin API and refused by the trigger) and how this deployment connects a provider. The visible
consequence is a boot order: `install_agents` now runs before `install_triggers`, which takes the
assembled `AgentServices`. The `ProviderConnector` seam moved down from `sc-server` to `sc-agent`
so this crate can reach it, which is also what keeps decision 7 intact — every test here drives a
whole run against a script rather than a vendor.

**Checked on save and on load, like everything else configured.** The agent must resolve by name
and the prompt must resolve in the scope the trigger's event will give it, so `` `${row.title}` ``
on a `none` trigger is a message on the form rather than a firing that fails, and an agent deleted
afterwards leaves the trigger out of the live set with a reason on it. The agent is resolved
against **storage** at save time and against the live set at fire time — `run_trigger`'s split
read the other way round: one broken thing produces one error, in the place it can be fixed.

**A triggered run carries the trigger's authority** (decision 5) and records no user, which is
what an audit needs to be able to say. The agent's own `min_role` is the floor on who may *chat*
with it and does not gate this; what guards a triggered run is the trigger's `min_role` — which
is also what guards it when an application exposes it as `POST {mount}/actions/{name}`, with no
change to §13.2 at all. That claim is now a test rather than a sentence: the same endpoint, the
same 403 for a role below the floor, the same generated `runSummarise()` client method, and the
run id in the answer resolving to a run whose first message is what the prompt formula built.

**What a triggered run cannot do is run a trigger.** It is given no dispatcher, so an agent whose
traits include `run_trigger` answers that one tool with a configuration error the model reads and
reports. Handing the dispatcher back down into an action it is running would close a
trigger → agent → trigger cycle with nothing counting the depth, since a tool call is not a firing
and carries no chain. Carried past the milestone with the fix named: a run that carries a firing
chain, which §10.3's engine will want anyway.

**Two documents.** `docs/tutorial-agents.md` walks an admin from an API key to an agent that reads
their tasks, runs their triggers, edits their app's source and then runs on its own — with a
"trips people up" section for the redacted key, a trait whose table was renamed, `max_steps`, and
the fact that a tool sees only what its caller may see. And §11 now records what was *built* where
it deviates from what was planned: §11.2's (`AgentTrait::tools` takes the catalog, `TraitContext`
carries the evaluator and the dispatcher, the machine and the driver are two types, `RunCaller`
has no default) and §11.5's, beside the blocks Phases 1, 3, 4 and 5 wrote as they landed.
`repo_hygiene.rs` asserts both — that the tutorial still teaches each step of the loop, and that
every phase's deviations are still written down.

## Coding traits: an agent that can build the application

Agents Phase 5, completed. An agent pointed at a file store can now read, list, grep and edit the
project in it, build the application whose source it is, and run one of that project's own
`package.json` scripts — and the whole cycle works: the end-to-end test has an agent grep for
where a type is declared, edit the file, build, read the type error it caused, fix it and build
clean, with every step in the run's transcript.

**Seven traits, one scope.** `read_file`, `write_file`, `list_files`, `edit_file`,
`search_files` and `run_project_script` are each configured with the same two settings — a file
store (a pick-list, resolved from the catalog like every other store setting) and an optional
sub-directory — and `build_application` names an application by its subdomain. Separate grants
rather than one "code" trait, for Phase 3's reason: a read-only agent is the default shape, and
an admin reading a definition can see that it may edit `web/` and may not run anything without
reading any code. Tool names are derived from the scope (`edit_file_apps_web`, `build_todo`), so
one trait enabled over two directories is two distinguishable tools and the same directory twice
is a collision refused **on save**.

**The configured sub-directory is a second confinement.** The store already refuses a path that
escapes *it*; the root refuses one that escapes the *directory the admin gave the agent* — by
any spelling, `../x`, `web/../x` or `/../x` — and every path the model is shown is relative to
that root, because a prefix it may not change is one it will eventually send back and be refused
for. Access is §9's rule unchanged, applied with the run's caller: a directory a user cannot open
is not listed, not searched and not read through an agent, which is pinned by a test where the
same store gives two callers two answers.

**`edit_file` is exact-string replacement, and the refusals are the feature.** A match that is
absent is an error saying so with the file's length ("read it again"); a match that appears twice
is an error saying *twice* and telling the model to include more surrounding lines, or to set
`replace_all` if it really means both. Neither writes anything. The alternative — a fuzzy edit —
is a corrupted file nobody notices until much later, and a model that can read the refusal can
recover from it in one turn.

**A failed build is the most useful thing an agent can be told**, so `build_application` returns
it as a *result* rather than an error: `built: false`, the tools' own output, and the
file/line/message triples parsed out of it (the Rust sibling of the IDE's `buildDiagnostics.ts`,
understanding the same three shapes — `tsc`, esbuild's one-liner, rolldown's boxed frame).
It builds the **stored** application through the same `sc_app::build_application` the admin's
Build button runs, so there is one build path and one set of diagnostics to explain. It does
**not** mount what it built: mounting is the server's (§13.2), and an agent's build answers "does
this compile?", not "serve this to the world" — an admin publishes the result by pressing Build,
which is also where they get to look at the diff first.

**No shell, and `run_project_script` is why there does not need to be one** (decision 6). It runs
`npm run <script>` for a script the project's own `package.json` already declares; the refusal
for anything else names the scripts that exist. So the runnable set is the project's, the model
chooses from a list rather than composing a command line, there is no way to pass arguments, and
a script that hangs is killed at the configured timeout with the run reporting that rather than
holding the conversation open.

**The search is one endpoint, shared three ways.** `sc_files::search_store` walks the store where
the bytes are — literal or regex, case and whole-word, a file glob, bounded in results, file size
and files visited, skipping binaries and not descending into `node_modules`, `.git`, `dist`,
`build`, `target` or `.venv` — and applies `filter_visible` per directory, so a search cannot
report a line out of a file the caller could not have opened. On top of it sit the
`search_files` trait and a new admin endpoint, `POST /api/file-stores/{store}/search`, which is
the endpoint the IDE's find-in-files wanted (carried past the last milestone): the workbench's
search now registers a provider that asks the store in **one request** instead of walking the
tree through the filesystem provider a directory at a time. Two details settled there: the
endpoint narrows by a single glob, so a query naming several is searched whole and filtered in
the client (a wrong answer is worse than a slower one), and the client's glob rule is the
server's rule spelled the same way, because two rules that disagreed would make an answer depend
on how many patterns were typed.

Tests: Rust — `sc-files` for the walk (excluded directories, binary files skipped, the access
rule on a search, the bound reported), `sc-core-traits` against a real local store for
read/write/list, `edit_file`'s three outcomes, the escaping path, the search, the two callers,
and `run_project_script`'s refusal and its run; `build_application` against a real application
row and a stand-in bundler that fails the way `tsc` does; and the whole grep-edit-build-fix-build
cycle as one run against the scripted `FakeProvider`. `sc-server` drives the new endpoint through
the assembled router. `vitest` — the IDE's search: which globs the server narrows by and which
are applied to what it returns, the glob rule, and a truncated search reporting that it was.

## The chat interface: agents in the admin UI

Agents Phase 4, completed. An admin can now create an agent in the browser, chat with it, watch
the text stream in and a tool call expand with its arguments and its result, stop a long answer,
reload the page and find the conversation in the history. Everything the previous three phases
built is now reachable without writing Rust.

**Two transports, split where the shapes differ.** Configuration is typed endpoints — `listAgents`
/ `createAgent` / `updateAgent` / `deleteAgent`, `listAgentTraits`, and the run history
(`listRuns`, `getRun`, `deleteRun`) — because each is a request and an answer. The *turn* is a
**WebSocket** at `/admin/agent-chat`, because it is not: deltas go out while a message or a stop
may come in, and the endpoint model (§13.1) has no shape for that. The socket follows the
language server's precedent exactly (§12.1) — admin-only, decided from the session cookie before
the upgrade, and the *only* refusal answered with an HTTP status, because a browser cannot read
the body of a failed handshake.

The protocol, as it settled: the client sends `start` (which agent, and optionally which run to
continue), `message` and `abort`; the server sends `text`, `reasoning`, `tool_call`,
`tool_result`, `done` and `error`. Four decisions inside it are worth stating, because each is a
thing that could reasonably have gone the other way:

- **A failure is an event on an open socket, never a dropped connection.** A provider that
  refuses, a key that is wrong, an agent whose trait names a dropped table: each arrives as
  `error` in the transcript, followed by the `done` that releases the composer. A chat window
  that silently stops is unfixable by the person watching it, and a closed socket is
  indistinguishable from a network that dropped. The two things that *do* close the socket are
  the conditions under which no chat is possible at all — no catalog, or no agents installed —
  and both close with the reason in the frame.
- **A tool call is announced once**, at the moment the loop is about to run it, not when the model
  streams it. Both would be truthful and the transcript would show every tool twice.
- **Abort is read while the model is still streaming**, because the turn is driven inside a
  `select!` against the socket's own receiver. Dropping the drive future drops the provider stream
  with it, and every step had already written the run row (§11.2) — so the transcript after a stop
  is the transcript up to the stop, and the run is `aborted` rather than orphaned in `running`.
  A client that simply **goes away** mid-turn gets the same ending without the events: nothing
  will advance that run, and a row left saying "running" would say it for ever in the history.
- **The agent is resolved per turn, not per socket.** An agent is editable while its conversations
  exist, so a trait added between two messages is offered on the second — and an agent that
  stopped validating stops answering, with the reason the admin can act on rather than a tool
  that quietly is not there.

**A `ProviderConnector` seam, and why it is production code.** Decision 7 says no test in this
tree may need an API key or spend a token, and §11.4's whole subject is what happens *after* the
provider answers. So how an agent's provider is connected is a trait — `StoredProviders` in a
server, a scripted `FakeProvider` in the tests — and the seam is at the *connection*: the loop,
the tool dispatch, the run rows, the row-layer reads and the socket under test are all the
production ones. A stub HTTP endpoint would have tested the adapter instead.

**`AgentServices` is assembled once, like the trigger dispatcher.** `install_agents` bootstraps
`_sc_agents` and `_sc_runs`, assembles `sc-core-traits`' built-in set, and reports any stored
agent that does not validate — and the result rides on `AppMounts` beside the dispatcher and the
evaluator, so the admin handlers and the chat socket validate against **one** registry. An agent
that saved against one trait set and ran against another would be refused in one place and
accepted in the other, which is exactly the bug this arrangement prevents.

**Deleting an LLM provider now checks the agents that use it.** `sc-llm` cannot see `_sc_agents`
from a layer below, so the server collects the referring agents and passes them in — the
`extra_referents` slot Phase 1 left with an honest note saying no agent existed yet. **Deleting
an agent does not delete its runs**: a run's subject is the agent's *name* (§11.4), and a
transcript that vanished with the definition would take the record of what happened with it.

**In the UI**: `Agents` (list — with a broken agent still listed and marked, because that screen
is where the repair happens), `AgentForm` (provider, model, prompt, the sparse attributes, and a
trait picker where each enabled instance renders its own `config_spec` through the existing
`SettingsFields`, so no screen knows a specific trait and a trait can be added twice), and
`AgentChat` (transcript, collapsible tool entries, composer, stop, and the run history with an
old run reopening read-only and a deliberate Continue). The model behind the panel —
`agentChat.ts` — is a socket wrapper and a pure fold over the event stream, so the part that has
to be right is tested by `vitest` against a stubbed socket rather than a browser: deltas appended
in order into one assistant entry, a new entry after a tool so the turn reads as it happened, a
call paired with its result by id, an error rendered alongside what preceded it, a second message
refused mid-turn, an abort leaving the transcript intact, and a stored run rebuilt into the same
transcript the socket streamed.

Tests: Rust — the admin API against a real Postgres (a save validated against the same registry
the chat runs with, refusing an unknown provider, an unknown trait, a missing table and two
traits whose tools would collide; an agent whose table was dropped still listed with its reason;
runs listed without their transcripts, read whole, and outliving their agent); and the socket
itself against a real listener and a real WebSocket client, driving a whole turn through the
`FakeProvider` including a real `query_table` read of a real table, a provider that refuses, a
stream that fails mid-answer, an abort, and the two auth refusals. `vitest` for the chat model.
`ui/admin` gained a `test` script and `vitest`, as `ui/ide` has.

## `sc-core-traits`: writing rows, and running a trigger

Agents Phase 3, completed. An agent can now be given four more grants over the world it was
only reading: `insert_row`, `update_rows` and `delete_rows` over one configured table, and
`run_trigger` over one configured trigger — which connects an agent to the whole of §10, and to
workflows unchanged once §10.3 lands, because a workflow is a trigger.

**Three write traits, not one.** A read-only agent is the default shape of an agent, and each
way of changing data is a separate entry in the trait list with a form of its own, so an admin
reading a definition can see that it may insert into `orders` and may not delete from it without
reading any code. Every one of them goes through **`sc-api`**, so a trait's write is the write
an API caller makes: coerced, validated against the field types, `File`-field-checked, and
**observed by the table's own triggers** — pinned by a test in which an agent's insert fires the
trigger that files the arrival.

Four properties bound what a write can do, and each is a test:

- **The caller's access is the tool's access, on a write as on a read.** `sc_api::insert_row_as`
  / `update_row_as` / `delete_row_as` are the write half of `read_rows_as`, and they share §7.3's
  rule rather than restating it: meets `min_role_write` **or** the ownership formula grants the
  row — checked on the existing row *and* on the row as it would become, so an update cannot move
  a row out of the caller's own ownership even when every value in it is valid. Under
  `owner === user.email` Ada renames her own book, cannot touch Bob's, and gets the same
  nothing-matched a genuinely absent row gives, because a tool must not become a way to probe
  which rows exist. The REST provider keeps its own orchestration, which turns each denial into
  an HTTP status; the *rule* is the shared part, which is the half that must not be able to
  differ between two callers of it.
- **A `where` is required — on `update_rows` as well as `delete_rows`.** The TODO asks it of the
  delete alone, but a whole table rewritten by an omitted argument is the same accident as one
  emptied by it, and §10.1 already refuses both on the *actions*. A model that means every row
  writes a condition that matches every row.
- **`max_rows` refuses rather than truncates.** One row past the ceiling and **nothing** is
  written, with the count in the message and an instruction to narrow the filter. A read that
  returns the first 50 of 200 is a short answer; a write that changes the first 50 of 200 is a
  half-applied change nobody can find the other half of. Matching *nothing*, by contrast, is a
  count of zero rather than a failure — an idempotent call must not look broken the second time
  it is made.
- **The rows are selected through the caller's own read**, then written one at a time by primary
  key. So a tool can only change rows the same caller could have been shown, and each changed row
  raises its own event with its own payload (§10.2's decision 2), exactly as the actions of the
  same names do.

The allow-list works the other way round from the read trait's, deliberately: on a write trait it
bounds what may be **set**, not what may be **matched**. "The row whose title is Dune" is how a
model addresses a row it may not rename, and a filter's reach is already bounded by the caller's
access — whereas on a read trait a hidden field left filterable could be read back one comparison
at a time. `insert_row`'s arguments are the row itself, flat, and its schema carries **no
`required` list**: a `NOT NULL` column may have a database default, the catalog does not record
defaults, and demanding every required column would make the model invent values the database was
going to supply.

**`run_trigger` runs the dispatcher's trigger, not a copy of it.** The call goes through the same
`TriggerDispatcher` every other event fires on, so the `only_if` runs, the cascade depth is
counted, a disabled trigger stays disabled, and what the action returned is what the tool returns.
Three consequences, each recorded in §11.3 and each tested:

- **`TraitContext` carries the dispatcher**, put there by `Runner::with_triggers`, exactly as it
  carries the evaluator and for the same reason — a capability the deployment has or has not got,
  whose absence is an honest configuration error (`require_triggers`) rather than a second way to
  fire an event. This is why `sc-agent` now depends on `sc-action`; nothing in it knows which
  triggers exist.
- **The trigger's own `min_role` still gates it.** Exposing an agent to a role does not thereby
  expose everything the agent can reach: an absent `min_role` is admin-only, as it is everywhere
  else in §10, and a run started by a trigger carries that trigger's authority and clears the
  floor — decision 5 read the other way round.
- **The trigger is resolved from storage on save and from the live set at call time.** A trigger
  that is stored but does not currently validate leaves *its own* live set with a reason; the
  agent that names it stays valid, and calling the tool reports the trigger's problem in the
  trigger's own words. One broken thing should produce one error, in the place it can be repaired.

Two supporting changes. The `where` vocabulary, the configuration keys and the field descriptions
now live in one module (`table.rs`) that all four table traits share — not to save the lines, but
because each of them is a promise to the model, and four dialects of one tool set would be four
chances to get it right somewhere and wrong somewhere else. And the crate's tests share an `Env`
that assembles a real database, the real V8 engine and a real trigger dispatcher, so a test that
stubbed the rule out would be testing nothing.

Phase 3's "done when" now runs end to end: an agent given `query_table` over a real table and
`run_trigger` over a real trigger answers a question about the data — seeing only what the person
chatting may see — and then performs the action as that person, with the whole exchange readable
in its run's context.

## `sc-core-traits`: the built-in agent traits, and `query_table`

Agents Phase 3, first half. An agent can now be pointed at a table and asked about the data in
it — and what it can see is exactly what the person chatting can see, on a table with an
ownership formula as much as on one without.

**A new crate at layer 9, beside `sc-core-actions` and for the same reason.** A trait that
touches rows goes **through `sc-api`**, never around it: a read is the same read an API caller
makes, under the same §7.3 rule, and the writes Phase 3 adds next go through the same coercion,
the same rich-type and `File`-field validation and the same events. That fixes the built-in
traits *above* the row layer, while `sc-agent` (layer 7) stays what a plugin needs to write a
trait of its own. `sc-agent` registers nothing; `builtin_traits()` is this crate's single
constructor, and it is the first consumer of the seam rather than a privileged one.

**`query_table`** takes one table, an optional field allow-list and a maximum row count. Its
tool takes a `where` object, an optional ordering and a `limit`. Four properties are
load-bearing and each is pinned by a test against a real database:

- **The tool describes the table.** Its name is derived from the configuration (`query_books`,
  not `query`), and its description and JSON schema are generated from the table's own fields:
  every field with its type, `order_by` as an enum of exactly the fields that have a column, and
  a `limit` whose `maximum` is the configured ceiling. A model left to guess column names will
  guess, and a guess that misses costs a turn the person watching pays for. This is what made
  **`AgentTrait::tools` take the catalog** — a declaration built from the configuration alone
  cannot say any of it. It stays infallible: a trait whose table was dropped still returns its
  tool under the name the configuration gives it, because "this agent has no tools" is not a
  repairable message and "this agent names a table that is gone" is.
- **The caller's access is the tool's access, and the trait cannot skip it.** The read goes
  through the new `sc_api::read_rows_as`, which is the §7.3 rule the REST API already applies —
  shared rather than re-implemented, because a second copy of "meets the floor OR the formula
  grants it" is a second place for it to be subtly wrong, and the wrong one is the one nobody
  is looking at. The same `books` table read by two chatting users returns each of their own
  rows and neither of the other's, on the translated path and on the reified one; a trigger's
  run carries the trigger's authority instead and sees everything (decision 5). A filter cannot
  reach past the formula, and a caller it grants nothing gets an empty table rather than an
  error that would confirm the rows are there.
- **The configuration bounds the tool, never the reverse.** The allow-list is what the model may
  see, filter on *and* order by — a field left out of it cannot be read back through a `where` —
  and `max_rows` is a ceiling a larger `limit` is clamped to, not a default it can raise. One
  row more than asked for is fetched so the answer can carry `more_rows_available`: a model that
  cannot tell a truncated answer from a complete one will state the truncated one as fact.
- **Everything refused is refused by name.** An unknown field, an unknown operator, an argument
  the schema never described, a `limit` of zero — each comes back naming what was wrong and, for
  a field, listing the ones that exist. These errors are read by a model that can only recover
  if it is told, so they are written for that reader.

Three supporting changes, each recorded in §11.3:

- **`sc_api::rows::RowQuery`** — filter, ordering, bound — is the only shape a tool can ask a
  read in. There is no projection and no SQL: a tool that could name either would be a second
  row layer with none of this one's rules. On the reified ownership path the bound is applied
  **after** the evaluator has spoken, because a `LIMIT` that counted rows the caller may not see
  would answer "10 rows" with three.
- **`TraitContext` carries the JavaScript evaluator**, put there by `Runner::with_evaluator`. A
  tool needs V8 exactly when the table it reads has an untranslatable ownership formula; one
  that could not reach an engine would have to choose between failing and skipping the check,
  and `require_evaluator` makes the first choice the explicit one. It is optional, so an agent
  that only talks does not build an isolate to say hello.
- **`query_table`'s `where` is JSON, not a formula.** Each entry is a field against a value to
  match exactly or an object with one operator key (`eq`, `ne`, `gt`, `gte`, `lt`, `lte`,
  `like`, `ilike`, `in`, `is_null`), all ANDed; `null` and `{"eq": null}` are both the null
  test, because SQL's `=` is never true of one and a model writing it means "unset". A model
  writes JSON reliably and JavaScript unreliably, and the object form is the one whose
  vocabulary the schema can enumerate.

## `sc-agent`: the agent, the trait, the loop and its runs

Agents Phase 2. An agent is now a stored record an admin can define, validate and run: a
provider, a system prompt and a list of enabled **traits**. It holds a conversation, calls the
tools its traits declare, and leaves a run behind that reloads into the same history it was
holding — all of it against a scripted provider, so no test in the tree needs an API key.

**The loop is a steppable machine, not an `async fn`** (§11.2, decision 8). `AgentLoop` owns
every decision — what to send, when to run tools, when to stop — and does no IO at all: a driver
asks `next_step()` and feeds the result back through `model_answered` or `tool_results`. The
whole state is therefore a `Serialize + Deserialize` value, and *that value is `_sc_runs.context`*.
Persisting after every step and resuming from the row are then the same mechanism rather than two,
and §10.3's durable engine inherits it. `next_step` is **idempotent**, which is what makes a
process that died between being told to act and acting come back and be told the same thing.

The shape is borrowed from `rig-agent`'s sans-IO `AgentRun`, which was surveyed again before this
phase now that rig 0.41 has split its runtime into a separate crate. The crate itself is still not
taken, for reasons now recorded in §11.1: rig type-erases tools and vector stores but **not**
models, so `Agent<M>` would need a unified model enum that makes `sc-llm`'s two adapters
redundant; `ConversationMemory` appends per *turn* where `_sc_runs` writes per *step*; and it has
nothing to say about the agent record, the trait specs or the validation, which are most of this
phase's code.

**A trait is the extension point, and this crate implements none of them.** `AgentTrait` declares
its configuration as `FormField`s — the same "settings as data" move an action and a file-store
backend make — plus `tools(config)`, `call(...)` and `on_turn(...)`. `AgentRegistry` is
`ActionRegistry`'s twin. The built-ins arrive in `sc-core-traits` (Phase 3) at layer 9, above the
row layer, because a trait that writes a row must go through `sc-api` like any other write.

Three properties of the trait seam are load-bearing and each is pinned by a test:

- **A trait may be enabled twice.** `traits` is a list of `(trait, config)` pairs stored as a JSON
  array, so "count the books" and "count the orders" are one trait with two configurations, in the
  order their tools are offered. **Tool names are derived from the configuration** (`count_books`,
  not `count`), and a collision between two enabled traits is refused **on save**, where it is
  fixable, rather than discovered when the model picks the wrong one. A tool name a provider would
  reject is refused there too — otherwise the failure is a 400 mid-conversation naming a tool the
  admin never typed.
- **`on_turn` runs before every model call**, not once per run, so a trait's contribution can
  differ on the second step from the first.
- **A tool that fails returns its error as the tool result.** `error: no collection named 'cars'`
  is something a model can act on; an exception is not. Only a failure of the loop itself ends a
  run — including a tool the model *invented*, which comes back as a result naming the tools that
  do exist.

**The caller travels with the run, and there is no default.** `RunCaller` is a user (a chat turn)
or the system (a trigger's authority), the `Runner` constructor takes one, and `Run::new` records
it — so decision 5's difference is visible at exactly one place. `sc-agent` sits at layer 7 and
deliberately stops there: the conversion to the row layer's `CallerContext` is spelled at the call
site in `sc-core-traits`, above `sc-api`, rather than smuggled downwards.

**Two tables, in the shapes their neighbours already use.** `_sc_agents` follows `_sc_triggers`:
the row *is* the definition, reads are strict, a missing or ill-shaped column is an error naming
the agent. `_sc_runs` is created here with the shape the workflow engine will also use — `kind`
discriminates `agent` from `workflow` — and is written after **every** step. Validation runs on
save *and* on load, in one function: the provider resolves, each trait resolves, each configuration
validates against its spec and then its own `validate_config`, `min_role` is on the 1–100 scale,
tool names do not collide. An agent that fails is dropped from `Agents`' live set **with its reason
kept**, and stays stored, listed and editable — editing it is the repair. Asking for it says "not
usable: no LLM provider named `openai`" rather than "not found", because those send an admin to
different places.

**`FakeProvider` is part of the crate, behind a `testing` feature** rather than `#[cfg(test)]`
(decision 7), so `sc-core-traits` and `sc-server` can drive whole runs against it. It scripts three
things — say this, call these tools, *fail* — because "the provider refused" is a path the loop and
the chat window both have to handle and no happy script exercises it. It also records every request
it was sent, which is how a test asserts what the agent actually offered the model.

**Deviations from the plan, recorded here rather than left for the end of the milestone:**

- **`_sc_runs` has no `name` column**, departing from §9's rule for metadata tables. It has
  `subject` — what the run is *of* — and a name column that always equalled it would be residue on
  every row. §9's rule is about definitions an admin names and addresses; a run is neither.
- **`_sc_runs` has an `error` column** the plan did not list. §11.4 requires a failure to be an
  event a reader can see, and a run whose state is `failed` with nowhere to say why is not one.
- **A `RunObserver` seam exists already**, unused by this phase. The chat socket (Phase 4) needs
  deltas, tool calls and results as they happen, and adding the seam later would mean rewriting the
  one function that ends a step.
- **`Conclusion` has an `Aborted` variant and `AgentLoop::abort`**, ahead of the stop button, so
  the run state a stopped conversation leaves behind is decided in the machine rather than in the
  transport.

64 tests: the machine's transitions synchronously and without a database (including the whole
state round-tripping through JSON and resuming), the record and its storage against a real
Postgres, and the loop end to end against the fake provider — a two-turn conversation with no
traits at all, a tool call and continuation, two calls in one turn executed in order, a failing
tool the run survives, `max_steps` stopping a model that never stops asking, a provider failure
leaving the row `failed` with its reason and its transcript, and a run resumed from nothing but
its row.

## `sc-llm`: the provider seam, and the providers an admin configures

Agents Phase 1. An admin can now connect an **LLM provider** — an Anthropic key, or any
endpoint speaking OpenAI's Responses API — press **Test connection**, get the model's own answer
back, and reopen the form to find the key redacted and still working. Nothing calls a model yet;
this is the seam the agent loop will be written against.

**A new crate, `sc-llm` (layer 6).** It knows providers, messages, tools and streaming, and
nothing about Saltcorn. `LlmRequest`, `LlmMessage`, `ToolSpec`, `ToolCall`, `LlmDelta`, `Usage`
and the object-safe `LlmProvider { fn model(); async fn stream(req) }` are the whole vocabulary,
and they are **ours** — nothing `rig-core` exposes appears in a public signature, so `sc-agent`
will not churn when a provider crate does. The message types are `serde`-round-trippable because
Phase 2 persists a run's history into `_sc_runs` after every step.

**Streaming is the only shape.** `LlmStream::collect` drains a stream into one `AssistantMessage`,
and that is what every non-streaming caller uses — so the tool-call assembly the two vendors do
differently is written once and tested once. The first error wins and ends the collection: a
partial answer followed by a failure is a failure, because returning the fragment as though it
were the response is how a truncated stream becomes a wrong answer nobody notices.

**Two adapters over `rig-core` 0.41, sharing one bridge.** `openai_responses` (with a
configurable `base_url`, so any OpenAI-compatible endpoint is a field value rather than a code
change) and `anthropic`. Three things the bridge is responsible for and rig is not:

- **Consecutive tool results are merged into one user message.** Anthropic requires alternating
  roles and rig does no merging, so three tool results emitted as three user messages is a request
  the vendor rejects outright. Pinned by a test that captures the actual request body.
- **A tool call is emitted only once its arguments parse.** Arguments that arrive as a JSON
  *string* are parsed; a fragment that never parsed is an error rather than a call with empty
  arguments, because running a tool with arguments the model did not ask for is the worse outcome.
- **The stop reason is derived**, not forwarded — see the deviation below.

**Providers are configured entities, like file stores.** `LlmProviderDef` + a backend registry
declaring settings as `FormField`s + `_sc_llm_providers` (the `_sc_file_stores` shape: unique
name, strict reads, validation on save) + `connect_provider(def, model)`. Structurally wrong
(missing key, unknown backend) blocks the save, where the admin can fix it; *not currently
working* (revoked key, retired model) is only knowable from a request, which is what Test
connection makes.

**`FormField::secret`, on the declaration.** So it reaches every consumer at once: the admin UI
renders a password input, `redact_attrs` replaces the value with a fixed sentinel (`••••••••`, not
a truncation — a prefix is still a leak) wherever a record is serialised, and `merge_secrets`
restores the stored value when a save hands the sentinel back unchanged. Without that second half,
an admin who opened a provider's form to fix a typo in its *name* would save the mask over the
key. The sentinel with nothing stored is dropped rather than saved, so a provider cannot end up
authenticating with `••••••••`. `sc-files`' store serialisation goes through the same pair, so an
S3 backend's secret will be redacted by declaring it rather than by editing a handler.

**Admin API + UI.** `listLlmProviders` / `createLlmProvider` / `updateLlmProvider` /
`deleteLlmProvider` / `listLlmProviderBackends` / `testLlmProvider`, and `LlmProviders` +
`LlmProviderForm` modelled on the file-store screens — no screen mentions OpenAI or Anthropic,
including for the key, which is a password input because the spec says `secret`. A failed test
connection is a **200 with `ok: false`**, not an error status: the provider refusing is the answer
to the question that was asked, and it carries the provider's own words because "invalid
x-api-key" is the actionable part and "auth failed" is not.

**The reqwest pin moves to 0.13**, because that is what rig builds on. Letting `sc-llm` pull its
own would have put two reqwests, two hyper stacks and two rustls configurations in one binary. The
0.13 rename of `rustls-tls` to `rustls` was the whole of the migration; §16's no-OpenSSL posture
is unchanged, though the default provider is now `aws-lc-rs` rather than `ring` (both pure-Rust
builds, no system libssl).

**Deviations from §11.1, recorded now rather than in Phase 6.** `StopReason` has two variants,
`EndTurn` and `ToolCalls`, and is **derived from what the stream produced** — neither provider's
streaming response carries a finish reason through rig 0.41, so a `MaxTokens` variant would be a
value nothing could ever produce. A truncated response therefore reads as `EndTurn` unless the
transport itself errors, which it does for a cut-off body (tested). Anthropic requires
`max_tokens` on every request, so the adapter defaults it to 4096 rather than letting the vendor
reject a well-formed call.

**Tests.** `sc-llm`: each adapter against a stub HTTP server replaying a recorded SSE body —
text, reasoning, a tool call split across four fragments, a `Stop` with usage — plus a 401, a
malformed body and a truncated stream each surfacing as an error rather than an empty answer, and
`collect` reassembling exactly what `stream` emitted. `_sc_llm_providers` against real Postgres,
including the key surviving a read-edit-save cycle in which the reader never saw it. `sc-server`:
the whole configuration API through the router, asserting that no response — not even the create
that carried the key — contains any part of it. **No test requires an API key or spends a token**;
the stub is a first-class part of the crate.

## A design and a plan for agents

Planning only; no code. The IDE milestone is finished, so `TODO.md` is archived as
`docs/TODO-post-mvp-5.md` and replaced by the **agents** milestone, and §11 of the technical
design — two paragraphs of intent since the document was first written — is rewritten as the
design that plan is against.

**The milestone.** An admin connects an LLM provider, builds an agent out of configured *traits*,
chats with it in the admin UI while its answer streams in, and fires it from a trigger. Its traits
query tables, run triggers, and read, grep, edit and build code in a file store. The copilot's
app-building traits and the AppConstructor are explicitly **not** in it: this milestone builds the
extension point they will be written against, and a trait that constructs a table would mean the
boundary was lost.

**Three crates, because three things are separate.** `sc-llm` (layer 6) knows providers, messages,
tools and streaming and nothing about Saltcorn; `sc-agent` (layer 7) knows agents, traits, the
loop and the runs and nothing about which vendor answers; `sc-core-traits` (layer 9) holds the
built-in traits, beside `sc-core-actions` and for the same reason §10.1 gives — a trait that
writes a row must go through the row layer, or it skips the coercion, the validation and the
triggers that layer owns.

**`rig-core` for the provider calls, behind our own object-safe seam.** The request named the
`llm` crate; the survey rejected it. `llm` 1.3.8 speaks Chat Completions only, and the OpenAI
*Responses* API is a requirement rather than a preference — it is where the reasoning models and
their compatible reimplementations are. `rig-core` 0.41 is the only surveyed crate carrying
`providers::openai::responses_api` *and* a maintained Anthropic provider with cache control and
thinking; it defaults to `rustls`, so §16's no-OpenSSL posture survives. `litellm-rust`,
`multi_llm`, `tiycore` and `llm-sdk-rs` are younger takes with neither API as deep. Writing the
two clients by hand was the serious alternative and loses on SSE framing, partial-JSON tool
argument assembly and each vendor's error shapes — not on the happy path. Only the *call* is
rig's: its `CompletionModel` is not object-safe (associated types, `impl Future`, `Clone`), so a
`Box<dyn>` chosen from stored configuration needs a seam whatever crate is underneath, and the
loop has to be ours anyway because it persists to `_sc_runs`, runs every tool as the chatting user
and streams to a browser.

**Two decisions the plan makes that are worth reading on their own.** A tool runs as the *caller*
and an action runs as the *admin*: a chat turn is a user's request, so every trait's read goes
through `sc-api::rows` with that user and §7.3's ownership and RLS apply — an agent is not a way
around them — whereas a trigger-started run carries the trigger's authority, and the two meet at
exactly one place, where the run is created. And there is **no shell**: the IDE milestone declined
to hand an admin a terminal, that decision must not arrive by the back door, so the coding traits
get `build_application` and `run_project_script` (an existing `package.json` script, by name) and
nothing that composes a command.

Three smaller things the plan puts on the table because nothing else has needed them yet:
`FormField::secret`, so a provider key is redacted where records are serialised rather than in a
screen, and a save that submits the sentinel keeps the stored value; `_sc_runs`, created in the
shape the durable workflow engine will also use, so a chat session and a resumable run are one
mechanism; and a server-side `search_files`, which is both the coding agent's grep and the
endpoint the IDE's find-in-files was carried past its own milestone wanting.

## The documentation catches up with the IDE

Documentation only, and the last phase of the milestone. Two documents were describing a product
that no longer matches: the React tutorial taught the edit loop as "open the file manager, edit a
textarea, press Build on the app's row", and §12.1 still described the IDE as it was *planned*.

**The tutorial's step 7 is now the IDE.** It opens the workbench from the `(edit code)` link the
admin already has, formats `Tasks.tsx` with prettier — including what a `.prettierrc` of the
project's own changes, and `editor.formatOnSave` — misspells a column so the generated `TasksRow`
catches it as you type, takes the quick fix, and builds from the status bar with the failure
landing in the Problems panel. The two conditions on semantics are stated where a reader meets
them rather than as caveats at the end: dependencies must be installed (which is why the build
comes first, and what the "press Build to install" notice means), and the store must have a local
directory. Source control gets a paragraph with the honest footnote that this tutorial's own
layout does not have it — the store is `/srv/apps` and the scaffold initialised the repository in
`/srv/apps/todo`, one level down, so the store as opened is not a working copy. Two new
"trips people up" entries: the first build is what turns on type semantics, and the IDE has no
file watcher, so a change made elsewhere is seen when something lists.

**§12.1 now describes what was built.** The narrative that chose `@codingame/monaco-vscode-api`
stands unchanged — it is still the reason customization lives in the host page — and the
deviations are recorded beside it: the real CSP (four relaxations, and nothing about where code
may come from), the flag that was built and then deleted (`--ide-dir` is not a deployment choice,
and there is no fallback document), prettier's configuration search being ours because
`resolveConfig` is node-only, the three diagnostic shapes the build parses and the `run_build`
stdout/stderr bug that made the feature impossible, and the three ways the language server came
out differently from the plan — refusals in the close frame, a workspace folder that deliberately
is not the server's root with a URI-translating bridge in between, and `monaco-languageclient`
skipped for the same version conflict that rejected `@typefox/monaco-editor-react`. The extension
ordering that decides whether the contributions exist at all is written down, because the failure
mode is a hang and not a throw. The SCM view gets a subsection of its own: the subset in one
sentence, what is left out and the missing backend operation behind each absence, the optional
`data` beside an operation's prose `output`, the refresh discipline, and the viewlet that could
not be omitted. §14.1 gained the paragraph those references point at — what an operation is, which
ones git declares, and why its output is prose with structure optional beside it.

Documentation is testable to the extent that it can be *lost* silently, and both of these could
be: `repo_hygiene.rs` now asserts the tutorial still teaches each step of the loop and that §12.1
still records each deviation, in the same style as the existing test over the triggers tutorial.

## Source control in the IDE: the minimal SCM view

A store that is a git working copy now has VS Code's **Source Control** view in the IDE: the
files you have changed, a message box, Commit / Pull / Push / Refresh as the view's title
buttons, and the branch in the status bar with a picker that switches it. The subset is
deliberate and it is stated in one line — **see what changed, commit it, exchange it with the
remote, switch branch** — because everything else an SCM panel usually offers needs something
the file-store backend has no operation for.

**What is left out, and why.** No index (stage and unstage per file or per hunk), no diff
editor and no gutter quick-diff, no history or blame, no discard, no merge or rebase, no
conflict resolution. The first three all want the same missing thing: a way to read a blob at
a revision — there is no endpoint that serves `HEAD:src/App.tsx`, so a "diff" would be a diff
against nothing. Clicking a changed file therefore *opens* it, which is the honest act
available. None of these is what stops an admin committing the file they just edited.

**The operations were already there; this is the view over them.** `status`, `clone`, `pull`,
`push` and `commit` are declared backend operations (§14.1) and have been driven from the admin
SPA since git stores landed. One is new — **`checkout`**, taking a `branch` name and a `create`
flag, so a missing branch name is caught by the same declared-input validation that catches a
missing commit message. It runs plain `git checkout`: **no `--force`, no automatic stash**. A
switch that would overwrite uncommitted work fails with git's own refusal, naming the files,
because the alternative is an editor that silently eats what someone just typed.

**An operation now answers twice.** `status`'s output is prose, and §14.1's reason for that
stands: a screen that rendered branches and ahead/behind counts would be a screen that knows
what those are, and could not render a plugin backend's status at all. But a source-control
view cannot list changed files from a paragraph of English. So `RunFileStoreOperationResponse`
grew an **optional** `data` field, which the git backend fills with the working copy as
structure — branch, branch list, ahead/behind, last commit, and the porcelain lines split into
`{ status, path }`. The admin UI still renders `output` and is untouched; every other backend
leaves `data` null; the IDE stops parsing prose. Every instance operation carries it, not just
`status`, because the server computes the state anyway and a view that redrew from a second
request could disagree with the operation it had just run.

Two flags were added to how status is read, both so a change can be *addressed* rather than
only counted: `--untracked-files=all`, because a new directory reported as `notes/` is not a
path anything can open, and `core.quotepath=false`, so a non-ASCII path arrives as itself
rather than as C-style escapes.

**The refresh discipline is the substance of the client side.** Every operation drops what the
filesystem layer remembers (`StoreFiles::forgetEverything`, as the Build command already did),
announces the open documents as changed so a stale editor re-reads, and redraws from the status
the operation itself reported. A **branch switch** is the strongest case: the whole tree can
change at once, so it also restarts the language client rather than reasoning about which of
tsserver's in-memory beliefs survived — one process, and cheaper in thought than the
alternative. The provider grew an `announceChanged` for this, which is the counterpart to its
`watch` returning nothing: no watcher can *notice* an outside change, but the IDE sometimes
**causes** one, and in that moment it knows exactly what a watcher would have told it.

**A store that is not a working copy gets no provider** — no commands in the palette, no title
buttons, no branch. The planned version of that was to leave the SCM service out entirely for
such a store; it turns out the Source Control view and its activity-bar icon belong to the
workbench itself, and omitting the service changes nothing except whether the view can work. So
the service is registered always, and a plain directory shows VS Code's own "No source control
providers registered."

Tested on both sides, and driven for real: Rust tests for the payload's shape and for
`checkout` (switch, create, and the refusal that leaves the working copy alone), an HTTP-level
test that `data` and `checkout` reach the endpoint, and `vitest` over the browser-side model —
payload parsing that drops what it cannot use, each operation's request, git's own words
surviving a failure, name validation, and which stores have source control at all. The whole
flow was then run in a headless browser against a real repository: edit, Refresh, the file
appears under Changes, commit (`[other 0dae1a9] commit from the IDE`), push (`branch 'other'
set up to track 'origin/other'`), and a branch created from the status-bar picker.

## TypeScript semantics in the IDE: a language server per store

Typing a type error in `src/App.tsx` now underlines it as you type, and completions and
go-to-definition work across the project's own files and its installed dependencies. Until
now the build's diagnostics *were* the type errors, which is why they were wired to the
Problems panel and not to a toast; they still are, and now they have company.

**The semantics come from a real `typescript-language-server` process on the server**, not
from tsserver in a web worker (§12.1). The deciding fact is where `node_modules` is: the
project's dependencies and its `tsconfig.json` are already on the server's disk, so checking
against them there costs one process, while checking against them in the browser would mean
dragging a React project's whole type surface across HTTP. `GET /ide/lsp/<store>` upgrades to
a WebSocket, spawns one process in the store's directory, and kills it when the socket closes;
at most eight run at once, and the ninth is refused with that sentence rather than being
allowed to take the machine down.

**A store that cannot have semantics is told so, once.** This is the one IDE capability gated
on `FileStore::local_path`, exactly as a framework's build step is — an object store cannot
host a process any more than it can host a bundler — and a project whose dependencies have
never been installed is refused too, because without `node_modules` tsserver resolves no
import at all and every file becomes a wall of "cannot find module": thousands of errors
saying one thing. Saying the one thing is better, and the fix (Build, which installs) is a
button the admin already has.

Those refusals arrive **in the close frame, not as an HTTP status**. Only the admin check is
answered before the upgrade, because a browser cannot read the body of a failed WebSocket
handshake: a reason sent that way is a reason nobody sees. So the socket is accepted and then
closed with the reason in it, and the IDE shows that sentence — the server's own words, not a
second wording of them — as a warning.

**The bridge translates two things, and the second one is the interesting one.** The socket
carries bare JSON-RPC (what `vscode-ws-jsonrpc` speaks) and the process speaks LSP's
`Content-Length` framing, which is bookkeeping. But the workbench edits the store as the
workspace folder `/<store>` while the process sees a directory on disk, and neither end can be
talked out of its own URIs. The TODO's plan was to make the workbench's folder *be* the
server's root; that was dropped, because the browser would then have to be told the server's
directory layout, the folder would differ between stores that have a local path and stores
that do not, and a relative `--file-store` path would have to be canonicalised on the way.
Instead the bridge — the one place that knows both roots — rewrites URIs as messages pass. It
walks the JSON rather than doing a textual replacement, which matters more than it sounds: a
`didOpen` carries a document's whole text, and a blind search-and-replace over that would edit
the admin's source code. A URI it cannot map (a definition in a file outside the store) passes
through untouched, so the workbench reports it cannot open that file instead of being handed a
path that does not exist.

**`monaco-languageclient` is not used, for the reason §12.1 rejected
`@typefox/monaco-editor-react`.** Its released line pins `@codingame/monaco-vscode-api@^25`
and its unreleased one `^35`, against the `36` this workbench is built on; npm would install a
second copy, and two copies of that package are two service registries — the client would
register its providers with a workbench nobody is looking at. Its actual content beyond that
is a twenty-line subclass of `vscode-languageclient`'s `BaseLanguageClient` that returns a
ready-made transport, so those twenty lines are in `languageClient.ts` and
`vscode-languageclient` (with `vscode-ws-jsonrpc` for the socket) is used directly. Nothing is
reimplemented; what is skipped is a shim whose only other content is a version conflict.

Tested on both sides. Rust: the route refuses an anonymous and a non-admin socket before the
upgrade; a store with no local path and a store that is not connected each close with their
reason; a stub language server installed where a real one would be proves each direction of
the URI translation independently (the echo is deliberately not a URI, or the test would watch
a round trip and call it a delivery); and a real `typescript-language-server` reports a real
type error on the right line — that last one skipping, as `ide_typecheck.rs` does, when
`npm install` has not been run in `ui/ide`. `vitest` covers the socket URL (including `wss:`
from an `https:` page, which is otherwise a mixed-content block that looks exactly like a
store with no semantics) and what the admin is told when the socket closes silently.

## Prettier, and a Build button that leaves its errors in the Problems panel

The workbench could edit a store; it could not do anything a project needs doing. It now
formats with **prettier** and **builds the application** whose source the store holds, and
a failed build's type errors land in the Problems panel by file and line — which is the
whole reason they were never wired to a toast.

**No extension was packaged.** This is the payoff of embedding `@codingame/monaco-vscode-api`
rather than serving a static workbench (§12.1): `registerExtension(<manifest object>)` plus
`setAsDefaultApi()` makes the `vscode` import in `ui/ide`'s own modules that extension's API,
so a formatter and a command are two ordinary function calls. The manifest is a literal in
`extension.ts`. There is no `.vsix`, no bundling step, and nothing to install.

**The extension is declared before `initialize` and activated after it**, and that ordering
is the whole difference between this working and not. Registered first, it joins the built-in
set the workbench brings up with itself. Registered afterwards — which is what the first
attempt did, and what upstream's README shows — it is a *delta* against a running workbench,
which takes the extension registry's lock and waits for every extension host to accept it,
including the web worker host this bundle has no extension for and which never starts. The
result is not an error: `setAsDefaultApi()` simply never resolves, the workbench looks
perfectly healthy, and the formatter and the Build button silently do not exist. The console
says only `handleDeltaExtensions has been holding on to the lock for 30s`. Since the failure
mode is a hang rather than a throw, a wait longer than fifteen seconds now logs what to
suspect.

**Prettier runs in the browser**, from `prettier/standalone` with the estree, babel,
typescript, postcss, html and markdown plugins — deliberately not the project's installed
copy, which a store need not have. Registering a `DocumentFormattingEditProvider` (rather
than a command of our own) is what makes Format Document, the context menu and
`editor.formatOnSave` all work at once.

The price of standalone prettier is that `resolveConfig` is part of its *node* half, so the
search is ours: `prettierConfig.ts` walks from the file's directory to the workspace root,
consulting `package.json`'s `prettier` key before the dotfiles in each, and the nearest
ancestor wins. Three details are worth stating because they are what a project actually
contains. A `package.json` **without** a `prettier` key is not a configuration and does not
end the search — that is the monorepo package inheriting the root's settings. Comments and
trailing commas are accepted, because hand-written configs have them. And a `.js`, `.yaml`
or `.toml` configuration — which a browser cannot execute or parse — is *found*, reported
once as "not being used, and here is why", and not silently stepped over to use a different
file's settings. Where the project says nothing, the editor's own tab settings are used, so
formatting is never worse than the editor alone.

**Build** is a palette entry (`Saltcorn: Build Application`) and a status-bar button. It
matches this store against `listApplications`'s derived `source.store` **in the browser**, so
the milestone adds no endpoint; one match builds, several ask, none says so plainly rather
than doing nothing. Afterwards the filesystem forgets every listing it held: a build writes
the generated client into the source tree and a bundle into `dist/`, which is a change made
outside the editor by something done inside it, and there is no watcher to notice.

**A failure is parsed into a `DiagnosticCollection`.** The server's error carries the tail of
what the tools said (§16), and `buildDiagnostics.ts` reads three shapes out of it: `tsc`'s
`src/App.tsx(12,15): error TS2322: …`, esbuild's `file:line:col: ERROR: …`, and rolldown's
boxed report, whose position is in the frame header and whose message is the line above it —
colour codes included, since the bundler emits them with no TTY in sight. Paths are placed
back in the store relative to the application's source directory; an absolute path is mapped
only when it lies under the directory the build reported, because a diagnostic pointing at a
file the admin cannot open is worse than one only in the output channel. Everything else, and
the whole log, goes to the "Saltcorn Build" output channel unparsed.

That last part turned up **a server bug that made the feature impossible.** `run_build`
quoted stderr when stderr had said anything, and stdout only otherwise. But the React build
is `tsc --noEmit && vite build` run by npm, and `tsc` writes its diagnostics — the file, the
line, the column of every type error — to **stdout**, while npm reports the failure itself on
stderr. Preferring stderr therefore dropped exactly the part worth reading, on the exact tool
that produces the errors this feature exists to show. A failed build's error now carries both
streams, each bounded separately so a chatty one cannot crowd the other out.

That ordering bug is also a reminder of what the split test strategy does not cover: both
sides were green while the feature did not exist in the browser. It was found, and the fix
confirmed, by driving the built bundle in headless Chromium against a stub of the file-store
API — the Build button in the status bar, Format Document rewriting a `.js` file to the
project's `.prettierrc`, and a failed build's `TS2339` landing in the Problems panel at
`Ln 2, Col 26`. That harness is not checked in; whether the milestone wants one is a question
for phase 5.

Tested from both ends. `vitest` covers the configuration search (nearest ancestor,
`package.json` with and without the key, the unreadable ones), that the options found change
the output, the store→application match, and the diagnostic parser against real output from
each of the three tools. In Rust, a failing build whose diagnostics are on stdout and whose
noise is on stderr must carry the position — and, end to end with a real toolchain, a
deliberate type error appended to the scaffolded `src/App.tsx` fails the build with
`src/App.tsx(<that line>,…): error TS2322`.

## The IDE stops asking questions it can already answer

Opening a store wrote five `not found` errors into the server log: VS Code looks for
`.vscode/settings.json`, `tasks.json`, `launch.json` and `mcp.json`, then lists `.vscode`
itself, and none of them normally exist. The provider forwarded each question to the store,
which has no way to answer "no" except by failing the request.

Quieting the log was the wrong fix, and was reverted. **A 404 should be logged** — it means
something asked for a thing that is not there, which is worth seeing precisely because it
ought not to be routine. The bug was that the IDE had made it routine.

A directory listing says what a directory contains, and therefore what it does not. Once
the root's listing is known, "is there a `.vscode/settings.json`?" needs no request: there
is no `.vscode`. So `StoreFiles` now keeps listings as it fetches them and resolves a path
by walking them from the root — `stat` of a path under a missing directory is `null` with
nothing asked, and `read` establishes existence before requesting bytes. The only calls
that reach the server are for paths that exist, plus the listings themselves. A whole
session — boot, expand, open, edit, save — now leaves the log empty.

Four rules keep it honest rather than merely quiet:

- **`list` always refetches.** It is what the explorer's Refresh runs, and with no watcher
  that is the one way to see a change made outside the IDE; refresh has to mean refresh.
- **A mutation forgets** every listing along its path and beneath it, because the store
  creates missing parents on write — so a new file is visible without a refresh, and so is
  a deletion.
- **Listings are held as promises**, so the concurrent lookups a workbench fires at startup
  share one request instead of racing to duplicate it.
- **A listing is believed for five seconds**, and the page forgets everything when it
  regains focus.

The last of those is the answer to the obvious objection: what about a file created
outside the editor? A *deletion* cannot mislead — the listing still names the file, so
reading it asks the server and gets the 404 it deserves, which is exactly the case worth
logging. A *creation* can, so it is bounded three ways: anything that lists sees it at
once, focus drops everything remembered, and nothing is believed for longer than the
lifetime anyway. What an expiry costs is another *listing*, which succeeds — so bounding
staleness never reintroduces a request for a path that is not there.

The tests say what the requirement is rather than how it is met: the four `.vscode` probes
plus a listing of `.vscode` make exactly one call — `browse ""` — and three concurrent stats
under `src` make two.

## The IDE has no settings

Not a TODO item — a correction, found by running it. A server started without
`--ide-dir` had no IDE bundle, and `/ide/` answered **every** path under it — `main.js`
and `main.css` included — with a fallback document. The browser asked for a module, got
HTML, refused it on its MIME type, and rendered a blank page; the console said `Failed to
load module script`, which names the symptom and not the cause.

Both halves of that were wrong, and the flag was the bigger half. **`--ide-dir` and
`SC_BUILD_IDE` are gone.** The IDE is not a deployment choice: it is where an admin edits
an application's source, reached from a button in the admin UI, so an operator has nothing
to decide and a build that produced the admin UI without it would leave that button
leading nowhere. `SC_BUILD_ADMIN=1` now builds `ui/admin` **and** `ui/ide` and embeds both
paths; a binary built without it finds `ui/ide/dist` in the checkout it was compiled from.
Either way `saltcorn serve` serves the IDE with no flag, and `--ide-dir` is now rejected
like any other unknown argument.

The fallback document is gone too, with no page in its place: with no bundle, every path
under `/ide/` — the document as much as an asset — is a 404. The SPA has a fallback so a
client-routed deep link still loads the bundle; the IDE has no client-side routes to
deep-link into (a store is a query parameter), so there is nothing to fall back *for*. The
general lesson, which is why this is worth a note: a fallback that serves the wrong *kind*
of thing is worse than a 404, because it turns a clear failure into a blank page. A test
now asserts that nothing under the prefix is ever `text/html` when the bundle is missing.

## The IDE edits the real store

TODO phase 2. The workbench's filesystem is now the file store's own API, so the
explorer is the store's tree, opening a file reads it, and Ctrl+S writes it. Create,
rename and delete work from the explorer; the admin UI links into the IDE from the file
store list, the file manager, and an application's source.

### Two layers, because only one of them is worth testing

`storeFiles.ts` is the store as a filesystem in the *store's* vocabulary — paths, bytes,
and the four outcomes an operation can have (`notFound`, `exists`, `noPermission`,
`failed`). It has no VS Code in it at all. `fileSystemProvider.ts` is the translation:
URIs to store paths, entries to `FileType`, those four outcomes to the codes VS Code acts
on. The split is what lets the interesting half — path mapping, base64, and which HTTP
status means what — be tested in twelve `vitest` cases against a stubbed client, with no
workbench to boot.

No new endpoints: `browseFiles`/`readFile`/`writeFile`/`makeDirectory`/`deleteFile`/
`renameFile` are exactly the file manager's, which is what makes any backend editable.
Two absences are worked around rather than filled in. There is no `stat`, so a path is
stat'ed by listing its parent — which also gives the right answer for a path whose parent
does not exist. And there is no watcher and cannot be one over request/response, so the
IDE announces its own writes and everything else is seen on an explorer refresh; `watch()`
returns a no-op disposable, which keeps that a limitation instead of a failure.

The store has no timestamps, so `mtime` is a constant. That is deliberate: `mtime` exists
for VS Code to notice a file changed under an open editor, and a made-up clock would
manufacture a conflict on every save.

### A missing file is a 404 now

`LocalFileStore` reported a missing path as an I/O failure, which the API turned into a
**500**. That was wrong before and unworkable now: asking whether a path exists by trying
to read it is the ordinary thing for a client to do. Booting the workbench probes
`.vscode/settings.json`, `tasks.json`, `launch.json` and `mcp.json`, none of which
normally exist — five "the server broke" errors before the editor has drawn anything.
`read` and `list` now map `NotFound` to `Error::not_found`, so the answer is a 404 the
provider turns into `FileNotFound` and VS Code proceeds. Everything else stays a system
error with its context intact.

### What is not connected is said, not shown

A store can be defined but not connected (§9), and a workbench over one is a tree of error
dialogs. So the IDE asks `listFileStores` *before* it boots and, when the store is missing
or disconnected, renders the store's own reason as the page instead — the reason the admin
needs, in the place they were going anyway. A session that expired between the page load
and that call sends them to the admin UI, where logging in is possible.

## The workbench boots: `ui/ide`, served under `/ide/`

TODO phase 1. VS Code — the real workbench, not an editor component — now runs in the
browser as a second bundle, and the server serves it to an admin at `/ide/?store=<name>`.
The store is a placeholder in-memory folder for one more phase; everything around it is
the shape the rest of the milestone builds on.

### What is actually ours

`ui/ide` is about 350 lines of TypeScript, and most of it is a service list. The workbench
— activity bar, explorer tree, editor tabs, panels, status bar, command palette,
find-in-files, the settings editor, keybindings — is `getWorkbenchServiceOverride()` plus
the twenty-odd service overrides it expects to find beside it, from
`@codingame/monaco-vscode-api` at 36.0.0 (VS Code 1.128.1). TextMate grammars and the
theme come in as VS Code's *own* built-in extensions, published per-extension as npm
packages, so a React project's files look like code with no highlighting code of ours.

The service list is deliberately shorter than upstream's demo: no debug, no notebooks, no
chat, no terminal, no extension gallery. Each of those is a decision this milestone has
either deferred or ruled out, and leaving them out of the list is how that decision is
enforced rather than merely written down.

Two things needed knowing rather than reading: VS Code's workers are *named*, not
constructed, by the page — so `workers.ts` builds `new Worker(new URL(…))` values that
start nothing, which is what makes the bundler emit them as chunks while VS Code decides
when to run them — and its stylesheets have to arrive as strings it injects itself, which
is a six-line Vite resolver plugin. The build needs Vite 8: Vite 5 (what `ui/admin` uses)
cannot resolve the deep wildcard subpath exports these packages publish.

### The route is where the containment is

`/ide/` is not a path inside the SPA's static bundle, and the difference is not cosmetic.

It is **admin-only**. The SPA bundle is public because the login screen is in it; the IDE
is reached only from an admin UI the caller has already logged into, so there is no reason
to serve twelve megabytes of editor to an anonymous request. A navigation without a
session is redirected to `/`, where logging in is possible; an asset fetch gets the
ordinary 401/403, because a dead-end error page is what a redirect would be for a `fetch`.

It has **its own Content-Security-Policy**. VS Code computes and injects styles, runs its
editor, textmate, search and extension-host code as workers created from blobs, and hosts
the worker extension host in a sandboxed iframe; the admin UI's strict policy forbids all
of it. `IDE_CONTENT_SECURITY_POLICY` relaxes exactly those four things — inline styles,
`unsafe-eval`, blob workers, blob frames — and nothing about *where* code may come from:
`default-src 'self'` stands, there is no remote origin, and `connect-src 'self'` keeps the
IDE talking only to this server (which also admits the same-origin WebSocket phase 4's
language server needs). It is set per response on the IDE's own route, so relaxing the
policy for the workbench cannot relax it for the admin UI — and a test asserts precisely
that, including that `/ideas` is not the IDE's route.

`--ide-dir` and `SC_BUILD_IDE=1` mirror `--static-dir` and `SC_BUILD_ADMIN=1`, as separate
flags because this bundle contains VS Code and an operator who does not want it should not
pay to build it.

## The file-store IDE: design and milestone plan

Documentation only — no code yet. A file store holding a React front-end is a software project,
and the file manager treats it as a folder of files: one file at a time, a textarea, nothing
project-wide. The next milestone replaces that with **VS Code in the browser**, and the question
worth recording is which VS Code, because the answer decides how much of this we write ourselves.

Three distributions were examined. **`vscode-web`** — the static official web build — is dead: last
published 1.91.1 in July 2024, repository archived 2026-07-21, and its live neighbours are a
several-hundred-megabyte server binary to supervise and reverse-proxy.
**`@typefox/monaco-editor-react`** is the right family but a layer too low: one editor and one
language client per component, pinning `@codingame/*@^25.1.2`. **`@codingame/monaco-vscode-api`**
is at 36.0.0, tracks VS Code 1.128.1, and publishes the workbench service by service as npm
packages.

The decision is the third, and the deciding argument is **where customization lives**. A statically
served workbench can only be extended by web extensions served to it, so a filesystem, a formatter
and a build button are three extension builds — and `prettier-vscode` is a *node* extension that
cannot run in a web extension host at all. With `monaco-vscode-api` the workbench runs in the host
page's own JavaScript context: `registerExtension` takes a manifest **object**, and the build
command and the formatter are ordinary `vscode.*` calls in our bundle. The extension this feature
"probably needs" turns out to be a manifest literal.

Two consequences shape everything else. VS Code initializes once per page and cannot be unloaded,
so `ui/ide` is a **separate page** under `/ide/` rather than a screen in the SPA — which also
contains its need for a relaxed CSP, leaving the admin UI's strict policy alone. And its filesystem
is a `FileSystemProvider` over the **file endpoints that already exist**, so the IDE adds no file
API and works against every backend.

The one thing that cannot be backend-agnostic is TypeScript semantics: a real
`typescript-language-server` runs in the store's directory, keeping `node_modules` on the server,
which gates it on `FileStore::local_path` — the same rule a framework's build step already follows,
and now documented as such in §14.1. A store without one is told it has no semantics rather than
being given worse ones.

Written up as technical design **§12.1**, with the milestone's five phases in `TODO.md`; the
completed actions-and-triggers list is archived as `docs/TODO-post-mvp-4.md`.

## A file store can be a git repository

Not a TODO item. `local` was the only registered file-store backend; there is now a second,
`git`, whose store is a **working tree cloned from a remote** — configured with a repository
URL, cloned when it is saved, and driven from the admin UI with pull, push and commit.

### Operations are declared data, like settings

The registry already made settings additive: a backend declares `Vec<FormField>` and the admin
form renders what it is handed (§6.2), so `git`'s URL, branch and key path needed no new form
code. What it had no vocabulary for is *acts* — generating a deploy key, cloning, pulling,
pushing, committing. None of those is a value the admin types, and writing them as
`backend === "git"` branches in the SPA would have made an operation something only a
**built-in** backend could have: a backend supplied by a plugin, or through `sc-code` in a guest
language, could declare settings and never a button.

So there is now `sc_types::Operation`, the counterpart to `FormField`: a name, a label, a
description, a scope, and — because some operations take an argument — its own `Vec<FormField>`
for whatever it asks the admin for. `backend_operations(name)` reports them beside
`backend_config_spec(name)`, and `run_backend_operation(&mut def, name, input)` runs one. The
argument bag is checked with `validate_attrs` against the operation's own spec, so a missing
commit message is caught by the same code and with the same message shape as a missing setting.

An operation is handed the definition **mutably**, which is what lets it configure as well as
act: the deploy-key generator fills in two settings, a clone records where it cloned to, and the
caller persists whatever changed.

The **scope** is the substance rather than bookkeeping, and it is why there are two endpoints:

- `configure` runs against settings the admin is still editing, so it is addressed by *backend
  name* and carries the unsaved config in its body (`POST
  /api/file-store-backends/{backend}/operations/{operation}`). This is what makes "generate a
  deploy key" possible at all — see below.
- `instance` runs against a saved store, addressed by id like the rest of the configuration API
  (`POST /api/file-stores/{id}/operations/{operation}`). It works from the stored *definition*,
  not a connected instance, because a git store that has never been cloned has no instance and
  cloning it is precisely the operation that would otherwise be unreachable.

An operation may also be `automatic`: run when the screen opens rather than on a press, for the
one whose whole job is to report state. `git`'s `status` is that, and it returns **text** — the
generic thing a screen can render. A card showing branches and ahead/behind counts would be a
card that knows what those are, and could not render a plugin backend's status at all.

`FileStoreForm.tsx` therefore contains no mention of git: it renders a button per declared
operation, a form per declared argument, and shows whatever output comes back. `FormField` also
gained a `multiline` hint, so a setting holding an SSH public key gets a text area without any
screen knowing which settings happen to hold keys.

### Generating the deploy key comes before saving the store

Saving a git store clones it, and cloning a private repository needs a key the remote already
accepts. So the order the UI enforces is **generate, install, then save**, and that ordering is
exactly what `configure` scope exists for: the operation runs against the form, not against a
store, because there is no store yet. It returns the settings with `key_path` and `public_key`
filled in, the form adopts them, the admin pastes the public key into the repository's deploy
keys, and Create then clones with a key the remote already knows. An operation that required a
saved store would always be one step too late — the first clone would always fail, for a reason
that has nothing to do with what the admin configured.

Only the public half ever crosses the API. The private key is written to Saltcorn's data
directory with `0600` permissions and is referenced by path; `ssh` itself refuses a key that
other users on the box can read, so the mode is not decoration.

### Where the clone goes is Saltcorn's decision, and is recorded

A `local` store's directory is the admin's own, so only they can name it. A git clone is a
directory Saltcorn creates, so Saltcorn places it — under an OS-appropriate data directory
(`%LOCALAPPDATA%\Saltcorn`, `~/Library/Application Support/Saltcorn`, or
`$XDG_DATA_HOME/saltcorn`), overridable with `SC_DATA_DIR`, which is also what keeps tests out
of a developer's real one. There is deliberately no `path` setting to ask about.

The chosen path is **recorded** in the definition's attributes on the first clone rather than
re-derived from the name each time. Without that, renaming a store would compute a fresh
directory, point the store at an empty one, and abandon whatever was uncommitted in the old one.
This also made `updateFileStore` carry a definition's attributes across a save, which it
previously reset — server-managed values (§9) are not the edit form's to erase.

### Creating a store is transactional; editing one is not

An operation may declare itself part of **creating** an instance (`on_create`), and `git`'s
`clone` does: until the clone succeeds there is no working copy and therefore no store. So
creating a store runs the cheap checks (`check_file_store_saveable`, factored out of
`save_file_store` for exactly this), then whatever the backend has to build, and only then
writes the row. A failed clone leaves **nothing** — no row, no registry entry — and returns
git's own message to the form the admin is still looking at.

The alternative, saving first and reporting the failure, is what an *edit* does and it is wrong
on a create for a concrete reason: the admin corrects the URL, presses Create again, and is told
the name is already taken — by the row their own failed attempt left behind. There is nothing to
preserve on a create, since the form still holds everything they typed.

Editing keeps §1.2's rule, and the asymmetry is the substance: an existing store must stay saved
and editable even when it cannot be brought up, because editing it *is* the repair. Refusing the
save would trap an admin whose remote is briefly unreachable with a definition they can no
longer correct. What editing now also does is **disconnect** a store it could not bring up: the
handle in the registry was built from the previous definition, so leaving it there means the
store goes on serving the old URL while the admin looks at a form saying something else — the
most confusing possible answer to "did my edit take effect?".

`local` declares no `on_create` operation and so is unaffected; a backend added later that has
something to build — an object store creating its bucket — gets the same transactional create by
declaring one. The server does the whole thing without naming a backend: `GIT_BACKEND` is no
longer imported in `sc-server` at all.

### Connecting never reaches the network; saving does

`connect_from_def` stays synchronous and offline: it opens an existing clone or fails saying
there is none. It runs at boot and on every save, and a clone there would make a slow remote a
slow boot, or — on a store whose URL had been repointed — write a whole tree nobody asked for.
Cloning is the backend's own `clone` operation, which both the save path and the button run —
one implementation, so they cannot drift. It is idempotent, so re-saving never discards a
working tree.

### Every git invocation is non-interactive

A server has no terminal, so a git subprocess that asks a question does not get an answer — it
hangs, holding a request open. Every invocation runs with `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`, and every one
using a deploy key runs ssh with `BatchMode=yes`, `IdentitiesOnly=yes` (so an agent's other keys
are not offered first, exhausting the remote's auth attempts before ours is tried) and
`StrictHostKeyChecking=accept-new` (a first clone from an unknown host is the normal case here;
a host whose key *changed* is still refused).

### Tests

`crates/sc-files/tests/git_store.rs` runs the driver against a real bare repository in a temp
directory — clone, write through the `FileStore` API, commit, push, and a second clone pulling
it back down — plus the deploy key's shape and permissions. `crates/sc-server/tests/
git_store_api.rs` drives the same round trip through the assembled router as the admin UI does,
over the generic operation endpoints, and covers what only that layer decides: that saving
clones and connects, that a **failed clone creates nothing and the corrected retry succeeds**,
that the same failure while editing keeps the store and reports why, that a `configure`
operation cannot be run at instance scope or the reverse, that `local` — declaring no operations
— refuses all of them by name, and that renaming a store keeps its working tree.
Local paths are used as remotes throughout, so none of it touches the network.

## The admin UI wears Tabler

Not a TODO item. The admin SPA was plain Bootstrap with a horizontal navbar; it is now
[Tabler](https://tabler.io) 1.4.0 in its **vertical layout** — a dark sidebar of sections beside a
page wrapper, which is the shape an admin console with a dozen screens wants and the one the
navbar was going to run out of room for.

### The theme is vendored CSS, and only CSS

`ui/admin/src/vendor/tabler/tabler.min.css` is Tabler's released stylesheet, checked in with a
`README.md` recording where it came from, why it is here rather than on npm or a CDN, and how to
upgrade it. Two properties made that the right shape:

- **Same-origin or nothing.** Every response carries `style-src 'self'; font-src 'self'`, so a CDN
  link is not an option and neither is Tabler's own `@import url("https://rsms.me/inter/inter.css")`
  — which its templates *do* carry and this does not. The file is self-contained: every image in it
  is a `data:` URI and the font stack falls back to the system UI font.
- **No vendored JS.** Tabler also ships `tabler.min.js` (Bootstrap's JS plus a theme script) and
  none of it is needed: React owns the DOM, so the sidebar collapse is component state and the
  light/dark switch is the `data-bs-theme` attribute, set by a `useTheme` hook that remembers the
  choice and otherwise follows the OS. There is a light/dark toggle in the sidebar; both schemes
  are styled, because Tabler styles both.

Tabler's CSS is a superset of Bootstrap 5's, so every `react-bootstrap` component in the screens
keeps working and gains Tabler's styling — the change is a stylesheet swap in `main.tsx`, not a
component library migration. Bootstrap's own stylesheet is no longer imported.

### `layout.tsx`: the page furniture, in one place

Tabler's page header is a *sibling* of the page body, not a heading inside it, so the shell cannot
wrap a screen in one container and be done. `PageHeader` (pre-title, title, actions in a
`.btn-list`) and `PageBody` are that contract, and every screen now states its title through them
instead of an ad-hoc `<h1 className="h3 mb-4">`. Also there, because both were places where
Tabler's markup differs from Bootstrap's in a way that is invisible until it is wrong:

- **`StatusBadge`.** Tabler's `.badge` takes its *text* colour from `--tblr-badge-color`, so
  `<Badge bg="secondary">` renders grey on grey and the word disappears. The combinations Tabler
  actually ships are `bg-<tone>` + `text-<tone>-fg` and the soft `bg-<tone>-lt`; this uses the
  soft one and is now the only way a status badge is made.
- **`AlertBody`.** Tabler's `.alert` is a flex **row** (its first column is the optional icon), so
  an alert with a heading *and* a body laid them out side by side. The wrapper gives the row one
  column.

`CenteredPage` is the signed-out shape — a narrow centred column — which the login and first-user
screens now use, with the Saltcorn mark above the card.

`icons.tsx` holds the dozen Tabler icons the shell uses as inline SVG components (the sections, the
theme toggle, log out), which is how Tabler's own templates do it and what the CSP wants: markup
the bundle already carries, not a font or a sprite sheet to fetch.

### An icons-only sidebar

A chevron button at the foot of the menu — below the sections, above the account block — collapses
the sidebar from Tabler's 15rem rail to 4.5rem: icons only, each carrying its section name as a
`title` so hovering still says what it is. The chevron points left to narrow and right to widen,
and the choice is remembered (`useNarrowSidebar`, same shape as `useTheme`) because an admin who
wants the width back on the row editor wants it on the next screen too.

Tabler has no such mode, so `admin.css` states it: the rail's width and the page wrapper's
`margin-left` are one measurement written twice, and `admin_theme.rs` asserts they match — a rail
narrowed without the wrapper following leaves a band of dead space down the whole page, and nothing
else would catch it. All of it is scoped to Tabler's `lg` breakpoint, where the sidebar *is* a
rail; below that it is a collapsing drawer with no width to give back, and hiding the labels would
leave a menu of unexplained icons. The stored preference survives a narrow window — it just does
not apply until the window is wide again.

What disappears with the labels is opt-in (`.sidebar-wide-only`) rather than a blanket rule, so a
future addition to the sidebar says for itself whether it survives the narrowing. The email address
is one of them; it moves into the log-out button's tooltip.

### The logo

The real Saltcorn mark (the January 2023 logo) is vendored at
`ui/admin/src/vendor/saltcorn-logo.svg` and shown at the top of the sidebar and above the card on
the login and first-user screens. Unlike the icons it is an `<img>` of an imported file rather than
inline SVG: it is brand art with three fixed colours — nothing to gain from `currentColor` — and a
single imported file cannot drift from the mark the rest of the project ships. Vite inlines it into
the bundle as a `data:` URI (it is ~1 KB), which `img-src 'self' data:` permits, so it costs no
extra request. `src/vite-env.d.ts` pulls in Vite's ambient asset-module types so the import
type-checks.

The one trap, and the reason there is a test for it: Tabler's `.navbar-brand-autodark` applies
`filter: brightness(0) invert(1)` to a brand image, to flip a monochrome logo white on a dark
sidebar. On this logo it produces a white silhouette. The class is all over Tabler's templates, so
it is exactly what a copy-paste would reintroduce; the brand blocks deliberately omit it.

`admin.css` is the deliberately tiny list of what Tabler does not provide: `.text-pre-wrap` for the
`<pre>` blocks that carry build logs and action results (Reboot's `<pre>` scrolls instead of
wrapping, and Bootstrap's `.text-wrap` would collapse the newlines), and the `height: 100%` that
reconnects `.page`'s `min-height: 100%` across `#root`, the mount point Tabler's static pages do
not have.

### Tests

`crates/sc-server/tests/admin_theme.rs` asserts what neither compiler can: the vendored stylesheet
keeps its MIT banner, contains no `@import`, and has no `url()` that is not a `data:` URI — the CSP
hazard that a future theme upgrade would silently reintroduce — that `main.tsx` loads the theme
(and not Bootstrap's stylesheet), the shell renders the vertical layout, and every screen renders a
`<PageHeader>`; and that the logo is the vendored file, shown in all three places, with
`navbar-brand-autodark` applied in none of them. The existing `admin_spa_typecheck` covers the
rest.

## Documenting the trigger system

Phase 9, and the end of the triggers milestone. Two documents: §10 of the technical design
rewritten as **what is built** rather than what was sketched, and a tutorial that gets an admin
from an empty Triggers screen to a server that acts on its own.

### `docs/TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`

§10.1 and §10.2 were a `trait Action` with a `Context` that no longer exists and a `Trigger` with
a `body: TriggerBody` that was never built. They now describe the implementation: the `Action`
trait as it is (including `validate_config`, the part that moves every "is this configuration
meaningful?" question to save time), where the built-ins live and *why* (`sc-core-actions`, above
the row layer, so a trigger's write is the same write an API caller makes), the `Trigger` record,
`_sc_triggers` as a definition rather than an overlay, the cached live set and its fail-closed
load, the fire path with `observes` as the choke point that keeps the feature free for writes
that do not use it, the `only_if` scope, the cascade bound, the scheduler, how an application
reaches a trigger, and — kept honest — **what `TriggerBody::Workflow` will still need**, which is
durability, and is why it is a milestone rather than a fourth match arm.

Also: `sc-core-actions` joins the crate map (it was missing), `sc-action`'s line says what the
crate actually holds, the `_sc_triggers` row in §9's table describes its real columns, and §17's
"since superseded" note lists it among the stored-metadata tables.

### `docs/tutorial-triggers.md`

Four steps, browser-only, continuing the `tasks`/`todo` app the earlier tutorials build: an audit
trail written by an `insert_row` trigger with `done && !old.done` as its condition; a `none`
trigger with a role floor, exposed on the application and called from the app's own JavaScript;
and a daily sweep at 03:30 UTC. Each step states the decision it demonstrates — the caller travels
with the event, a trigger writes with admin authority, exposing is the application's decision so
an unexposed trigger is a 404 and not a 403, schedules are UTC, a missed run is caught up once,
off is not down.

**The tutorial is executed by a test.** `sc-core-actions/tests/tutorial_triggers.rs` copies the
formulas out of the document, puts them through `save_trigger` — the same validator the admin's
Save button runs — and then fires them against a real database: the audit trigger firing on
*becoming* done and not on any later save, the `none` trigger deleting the caller's finished
tasks and nobody else's, and the daily one reading back as 03:30 UTC. A tutorial breaks quietly
(the formula language gains a rule, an action renames a setting) and a test that only reads the
markdown would not notice.

Writing it found one thing worth fixing in the document rather than in the reader's afternoon: a
formula is one pure expression with **no `new`**, so `new Date().toISOString()` is not something a
field value can compute. The tutorial's audit table stores `Date.now()` in a `bigint` and says
why.

The hygiene test gains the ownership→triggers cross-link and a check that the tutorial still
covers all three kinds of trigger.

## The periodic scheduler

Phase 8. `often`, `hourly`, `daily` and `weekly` triggers stop being event kinds nothing watches:
one tokio task wakes on the minute boundary, asks each periodic trigger whether it is due, and
fires the ones that are through the **same `run_trigger`** every other direct run goes through — so
a scheduled run has the same `only_if`, the same cascade bound and the same action as any other.

### Four kinds, no cron

`Schedule` (in `sc-action`) resolves a trigger's kind plus three sparse attributes — `minute`,
`hour`, `day_of_week` — into when it fires. A cron string would be a second language to learn, to
validate and to render a form for; a trigger that genuinely needs "every third Tuesday" is one
whose *action* should decide that.

- **Everything is UTC** (decision 6). A server-side schedule has no user to have a timezone, and a
  local one would mean an hour that happens twice a year and an hour that does not happen at all.
  The form says UTC on the inputs, and the arithmetic needs no timezone database to be right.
- **`Schedule::of` is the validator too**, called on save *and* on load, so what the admin is
  refused and what the scheduler computes cannot drift apart. Out of range is refused by name; so
  is a timing value on a kind that has no use for it (an `hour` on an `hourly` trigger, anything on
  a `login` one) — the same rule a channel on a channel-less event gets, because a setting the
  server silently drops is one the admin believes is in effect. An unset value is 0, so a `daily`
  nobody configured runs at midnight rather than refusing to be saved.

### The clock is a parameter

`Scheduler::tick(now)` takes the time; the task loop is the only place `Utc::now()` is read. That
is what lets the tests drive a week of schedule in a millisecond instead of waiting for it, and it
is why the due-time rules are asserted against a table of instants.

Three decisions worth their lines:

- **A fresh trigger's clock starts now, never at the epoch** — otherwise every newly created
  trigger would be overdue by decades and fire the moment it was saved.
- **A missed run catches up exactly once.** `last_run_at` is persisted, so a server that was down
  over a nightly job's hour comes up and runs it once — not once per missed day, and not never.
  That falls out of the rule (`next_due(last_run) <= now`) rather than being special-cased.
- **A slow action is skipped, not queued.** Each firing runs in its own task, so one slow action
  delays neither the clock nor another trigger, and an occurrence that comes round while the last
  one is still running is dropped: five queued copies of a report nobody read is worse than one
  late one.

**Disabling is not downtime**, and the two behave differently on purpose: a disabled trigger's
clock still advances, so switching a nightly report off for a week and back on on Tuesday morning
runs it *tonight* rather than immediately. Nobody chooses downtime, so that one is caught up;
switching a trigger off is a decision to skip those runs.

### `last_run_at` is the scheduler's, not the admin's

A new nullable `_sc_triggers` column — reaching existing databases through Phase 7's additive
bootstrap — that `save_trigger` deliberately **never writes**. Only `record_trigger_run` does, as
one targeted `UPDATE`. That is what makes "an admin editing a trigger's action at 3pm cannot
thereby claim the daily job ran at 3pm, or that it never ran" true by construction rather than by
everyone remembering. The time recorded is the tick's, not the completion's, so a job that takes
twenty minutes does not drift twenty minutes later every day.

### Admin API and SPA

`minute`, `hour` and `day_of_week` on the trigger contract (null on the kinds that have none, and
refused on them), `last_run_at` on the read shape only, and the regenerated client. The trigger
form shows exactly the timing inputs the chosen kind takes — a day picker showing day *names* over
the server's 0 = Monday numbering — and the list gains a "last run" column plus a plain-English
schedule under the event ("at 03:30 UTC", "Sunday at 09:00 UTC").

### Tests

Unit: the due-time rules as a table of (schedule, last run, now, due?) including hour, day, month
and year rollovers, plus the timing refusals. Integration (`sc-action/tests/scheduler.rs`, 5 tests
against a real database, driving the clock): an `often` and a `daily` trigger firing exactly when
they should and not before; the run landing on the row at the time it was *due*; the
catch-up-once-at-startup case through a second scheduler over the same database (which is what a
restart is); the overlap skip, with an action slow enough that the next two occurrences really do
arrive mid-run; and a disabled trigger neither firing nor replaying. Plus the admin-API round trip
of the timing, and an edit that does not disturb the recorded last run.

## Applications expose triggers as API endpoints

Phase 7. A server-side action becomes callable from outside because an **application said so**:
`Application.triggers` is a declared subset, exactly like its tables, and each name in it is
projected as one `POST {mount}/actions/{name}` — body → the event's payload, the action's result →
the response, typed into the app's generated client as `runFoo(body)`.

Four decisions this rests on:

- **Opt-in, not automatic.** Triggers are the admin's server-side configuration; most of them exist
  precisely because nobody should have to call them. So a trigger an app does not name has **no
  endpoint**, and a request for it is a `404` rather than a `403` — there is nothing there to be
  refused at. The test pins that distinction by running the same trigger through the dispatcher
  immediately afterwards: not exposed is not the same as not defined.
- **The role floor is the trigger's own**, and an unset `min_role` means **admin**. `min_role` is
  optional on a trigger because most triggers are never called from outside at all — which makes
  its absence "nobody has decided", and that must not read as "everybody".
- **Resolution is loud.** A declared trigger that is not in the live set refuses to project, naming
  the trigger and the app, exactly as a declared table that is not in the catalog does. So does an
  app with exposed triggers projected in a context that has no trigger set: silently serving an API
  the app's own generated client does not match is the failure worth refusing.
- **Deleting an exposed trigger still works.** Blocking it would leave an admin unable to remove a
  trigger they no longer want (the Phase 3 decision). The delete succeeds, the app keeps its
  previous mount and goes on serving, the dangling reference is reported to the log, and the app
  names the missing trigger when it is next built or mounted.

`AppMounts::refresh_triggers` re-projects mounted apps on every trigger mutation, the way
`refresh_table` already does for a table's access rules — a provider encodes the role it was
projected with, so without it an admin who tightens a trigger's `min_role` would watch it save and
change nothing until the next restart. Both now go through one `reproject` helper.

### Additive bootstrap: a column a release adds

`Application.triggers` needed a new `_sc_applications` column, and the design bans a migration
framework for now — which without something would mean every existing deployment breaking on the
next read of a table that worked yesterday. `Catalog::bootstrap_table` is that something: create the
table if absent, and **create any declared column it does not have**. Nothing is dropped, renamed or
re-typed; that *is* a migration and needs a framework that can decide what to do with the rows
already there. All three `_sc_*` bootstraps (`applications`, `triggers`, `file_stores`) now go
through it, so Phase 8's periodic-timing columns arrive the same way.

The corollary is a rule, not an accident: **a field added to an existing table's declaration cannot
be `required`** — the rows already stored have no value for it. So `triggers` is nullable where its
siblings are not, `NULL` reads back as "exposes nothing" (which is what an app written before
triggers existed did), and every row this version writes stores `[]` rather than leaving it.

### Wiring

The trigger set is threaded explicitly rather than reached through a global: `sc-api` gains a
dependency on `sc-action` (the layering `sc-action`'s own docs state — it is layer 6, `sc-api` is
layer 7), `RestProvider::project_with` takes the resolved triggers and `with_dispatcher` the thing
that runs them, and `app_providers_with`/`app_endpoints_with`/`app_client_with`/`build_application`/
`scaffold_app`/`emit_react_runtime` each carry an `Option<&Arc<TriggerDispatcher>>`. That reaches
the scaffold and the React runtime deliberately: both regenerate the app's client, and one that
skipped the trigger endpoints would silently overwrite one that had them.

### Admin API and SPA

`triggers: string[]` on the application create/update/list contract (absent means expose nothing,
so an older client's body is not refused), the regenerated `client.ts`, and a **trigger picker** in
the application form — checkboxes over the server's actual triggers, each showing its action, the
path it will be served at and the role that guards it. A selection naming a trigger the server no
longer has is shown in red with what it means, rather than quietly dropped by a picker that only
knows about triggers that exist.

### Tests

`sc-server/tests/app_trigger_api.rs`, 6 tests over the real router: an exposed trigger called at the
app's mount returning the row its action wrote, with the app user as `user.email` and a typed
`runVisitLogged` in the generated client; the role floor enforced at 401/403/200 across anonymous,
reader, editor and admin, including the admin-only default; a trigger the app does not expose
answering 404 while still running through the dispatcher; a `min_role` tightened through the admin
API taking effect on the mounted app immediately; a dangling reference refusing to mount and naming
itself; and the delete-an-exposed-trigger case. Plus the additive bootstrap end to end
(`sc-app/tests/app_store.rs`): drop the column, insert a row the way the previous release would,
boot, and read it back as an app exposing nothing.

## Triggers in the admin API and the admin SPA

Phase 6. Triggers stop being something only a test can create: six endpoints on the existing typed
`Endpoint` machinery, the regenerated client, and two screens.

### Endpoints

`listTriggers`, `createTrigger`, `updateTrigger`, `deleteTrigger`, `runTrigger`, `listActions`.
Four decisions worth their lines:

- **The list is the stored rows, not the live set.** A trigger that fails validation is dropped
  from the live set — and listing the live set would make it vanish from the one screen that
  exists to repair it. Each row carries `error: string | null` from the live set's issues, the
  same shape a file store's "defined but not connected" takes.
- **Every mutation reloads the live set**, so "what the list shows is what will fire" is true of
  the screen the admin is looking at, not of the next restart. The API test asserts exactly that:
  it creates a trigger over HTTP, writes a row through the *other* admin endpoint, and checks the
  trigger fired.
- **`runTrigger` is addressed by id and resolved to a name.** The row is what the admin clicked; a
  rename must not make the button run somebody else's trigger. The posted body is the event's
  payload, the admin is the caller, and an action that fails comes back as an **error** — a 200
  carrying a hidden failure is precisely what a "test this now" button exists to prevent.
- **`listActions`** reports each action's name, description and `config_spec` in the same
  `FormField` vocabulary a framework's and a file-store backend's settings use, which is what lets
  the trigger form render a configuration form for an action it has never heard of.

The dispatcher reaches the handlers through `AppMounts` — already threaded into both the router and
`admin_handlers` in Phase 5 — so no signature changed. A server assembled without one refuses these
endpoints by name rather than answering with an empty list, which would make a save look like it
worked.

### SPA

- **`Triggers.tsx`** — name, event (with its channel and `only_if` beneath it), what it runs, and a
  status badge: Enabled, Off, or **Not usable** with the reason. Run is offered only for a `none`
  trigger, because every other kind needs an occurrence to say anything about; the result (or the
  action's error) is shown inline.
- **`TriggerForm.tsx`** — event picker, table picker and `only_if` shown **only** for table events
  (the other kinds have no row, so offering them and then refusing the save would be worse than not
  offering), action picker, and the action's settings rendered from `config_spec`. No action's
  settings are known to this screen.
- Nav entry beside Tables and Applications; `tsc --noEmit` and `vite build` pass.

### Two fixes in the shared settings renderer

Both were latent bugs for any `json` setting, and an action that always has one (`insert_row`'s
field→formula map) is what surfaced them:

- `readConfig` now **stringifies** an object rather than dropping it — editing a trigger would
  otherwise blank its `values` map on save.
- A `json` field renders as a monospace textarea rather than a one-line text input.

### Tests

`sc-server/tests/trigger_admin_api.rs`, 5 tests over the real router: the round trip (create →
fires → rename and switch off → does not fire → delete → deleting twice is a not-found); five save
refusals each naming what is wrong with nothing stored; a trigger whose table is dropped underneath
it staying listed with its reason; `runTrigger`'s result and its error; and `listActions`
describing all five built-ins.

## The other events: `none`, `login`, `startup`, `error`

Phase 5. A trigger no longer has to be about a row: something signing in, the server coming up, a
request failing, or simply someone asking are all occurrences now, and each is raised from the one
place it actually happens.

### Where each is fired, and why there

- **`login`** — `router::apply_response`, which is where a session actually starts. The admin
  API's login and an application's own both pass through it, so one line covers both and no
  handler has to remember to raise it. Fired *after* the session exists: an event that says
  someone logged in must not fire for a login that then failed.
- **`startup`** — `sc_server::fire_startup`, called by `saltcorn serve` once the catalog, the file
  stores, the applications and the triggers are all up and before the listener is announced. A
  startup trigger finds a server that works, and what it writes is visible to the first request
  rather than racing it.
- **`error`** — the four places an `Error` becomes a response, and **only** those. A 404 for an
  unrouted path or a 401 from the auth gate is a *rejection*, not a failure; an alerting trigger
  that fired on every probe of a wrong URL would be useless for what it is for. The payload is a
  wire contract — `{kind, message, method, path}`, with `kind` the existing `application`/`system`
  split — and the caller rides on the event as it does everywhere else.
- **`none`** — `TriggerDispatcher::run_trigger`, by name. The posted body becomes the payload and
  the action's result is the answer; unlike `fire`, the failure is **returned**, because somebody
  asked and is waiting for it. Phases 6 and 7 put an endpoint in front of this.

None of these needed a seam like the row layer's: everything that raises them already sits above
`sc-action` and can simply hold the dispatcher. It rides on `AppMounts`, which is the handle the
router and the admin handlers both already had.

### The `error` event's re-entrancy guard

An error raised while handling an error event does not fire another — otherwise one misconfigured
trigger becomes an infinite loop at the single worst moment. The guard is a **task-local**, not a
flag on the dispatcher: it is about *this* error's own handling, and a shared flag would drop a
genuinely concurrent unrelated error (two requests failing at once are two errors, and both
deserve their event). It lives inside `fire`, so it cannot be bypassed by raising an error event
some other way.

### `payload` is now part of the formula scope

Not planned, and the phase does not work without it. The ambient vocabulary was `row`/`old`/`user`,
so a `none` trigger could not read the body it was posted and an error trigger could not write the
message to a table — "the posted body becomes the event payload" was true of nothing but
`run_js_code`. So:

- `sc-expr` gains `Ambient::Payload`, and `trigger_shape` declares it for **every** event kind
  (every event has the field; it is null where there is nothing to say).
- It is declared with **no field set**, which is the opposite of `row`'s and deliberate: nothing
  declares what a sender put in a payload, so `payload.x` resolves and reads null when it is not
  there, where `row.x` is checked against real columns.
- A payload that is **not an object** — an array, a scalar — binds as null in a formula, whose
  whole vocabulary is `payload.x`. `run_js_code` gets the payload as it is and is the tool for one.

### Also

- `run_trigger` refuses a **disabled** trigger by name. `Triggers::require` resolves one (that is
  how it stays fixable in the UI), so the check belongs at the run: an admin who switched a trigger
  off and then pressed Run expects the switch to win.
- `Event::login`/`startup`/`error` constructors keep each event's shape in `sc-action` rather than
  at the call sites that raise them.

### Tests

- **`sc-server/tests/other_events.rs`** (5, through the real router): a login trigger recording
  `user.email` for a real sign-in and not for a rejected one; a startup trigger that fires only
  when the boot path fires it; an error trigger seeing a forced **application** error (a table that
  is not there) and a forced **system** one (a NOT NULL violation the API cannot foresee) with the
  path and the caller, and **no** row for an unauthenticated request; a throwing error trigger that
  costs neither the response nor the recording trigger beside it; and a `none` trigger run by name
  with its payload, refusing an unknown name and a disabled one differently.
- **`sc-action/tests/error_reentrancy.rs`** (2): the guard itself, with a purpose-built action that
  raises an error event *from inside* one — which no built-in action can do. It fails (5 runs
  instead of 1) with the guard removed, which is the only way to know a guard test is testing
  anything.

## Table events: a row write now fires the triggers that listen for it

Phase 4. Inserting, updating or deleting a row through **any** API surface raises an event, and
the triggers stored in `_sc_triggers` run against it. The parts have been in place since Phases
1–3; this is the wire between them, and it runs in the direction the layering demands — the row
layer (layer 8) raises an event through a seam the **catalog** holds, and never names the
dispatcher (layer 6).

### The emit seam (`sc-catalog`)

- **`TableEvents`**, with two methods rather than one. `emit(&Catalog, TableWrite)` handles a
  committed write; `observes(table, op)` is a **synchronous predicate** the row layer asks
  *first*. That second one is the whole reason the feature is free for the writes that do not use
  it: it is what lets an update fetch its pre-image only when something will read it, so a
  deployment with no triggers pays one map lookup per write instead of a `SELECT` (GOALS: firing
  an event is a lookup, not a query).
- **`TableWrite`** carries the table, the op, the row, the pre-image and the caller — as a struct,
  and with rows as **JSON**, which is the shape an event hands to a formula, to an action's
  configuration and over the wire to a `fetch`.
- **`WriteOp`** is its own three-variant enum, not `sc_expr::Operation`: a read is not a write,
  and a type that cannot say otherwise is one more state at every match.
- `Catalog::set_table_events` installs the listener once at boot; `emit` takes `&Catalog` so the
  dispatcher does not hold one back (a handle each way is a cycle that never drops).

### The caller travels with every write

- **`CallerContext` moved out of `rls.rs`** into `caller.rs`, because it stopped being about RLS.
  It answers three questions about one write: whose authority (`role`), which user (`user`), and
  **what led here** (`chain` — the trigger names whose actions caused it, which is what bounds a
  cascade). `user_json: Option<String>` became `user: Option<Json>`; only the GUC wants text, so
  the stringify moved to the one caller that does.
- **The table now decides the caller-context transaction, not the caller.** The rule used to be
  "run in a policy transaction whenever a context is given", which was only right while a context
  meant RLS. Now every write carries one, so an ordinary table must not be wrapped in a
  transaction and a `SET LOCAL` no policy will ever read. Both halves of that fail silently, so
  it has a unit test.
- The REST provider and the admin row endpoints now pass a caller on every write. The admin one
  still runs at role 1 — the row editor has to work on a `FORCE`d table — but carries the
  signed-in admin's fields, so an event says *which* admin.

### Dispatch (`sc-action`)

- **`TriggerDispatcher`**: the registry, the live `Triggers` behind an `RwLock` (so Phase 6's save
  can swap the set in while events fire), and the engine. It implements `TableEvents`.
- One dispatch, per matched trigger in name order: check the depth (`Event::firing` — past
  `MAX_DEPTH` it does not run and the error names the whole chain), evaluate the `only_if`, run
  the action with the chain that firing returned. `dispatch` returns a `TriggerRun` each —
  `Ok(Some(result))` ran, `Ok(None)` the `only_if` declined, `Err` failed — so the emit path can
  report failures and a test can assert on them.
- **One trigger's failure is one trigger's failure.** The next trigger still runs, and none of it
  reaches the write, which committed before dispatch began (decision 1). A request that inserted a
  row gets its row back even when the audit trigger it fired is misconfigured. Reporting is
  stderr until §16's error log exists.
- An **`only_if` evaluator error means the trigger does not run** — the fail-closed contract an
  ownership formula has, for the same reason: "could not decide" is not "yes".

### One consequence worth its own note: the JSON⇄`Value` bridge moved down

An `only_if` reads the event's row, which arrives as JSON and must be typed **by its columns**
before a Ⱶ-path prefetch can correlate on it — a `uuid` key compared as text is a SQL error, not a
mismatch. That is a fact about types and values, not about an API, so `json_to_value` and
`value_to_json` moved from `sc-api::convert` to **`sc-types::json`** (layer 3). `sc_api::convert`
re-exports both, so no call site changed. `sc_action::typed_value` is now the single reading both
the dispatcher and the row actions use.

### Boot and tests

- **`sc_server::install_triggers`** is the wiring: bootstrap `_sc_triggers`, register the built-in
  actions, load and validate the stored set (reporting each unusable trigger, as a file store that
  will not connect is), install the dispatcher. Called from `saltcorn serve`. Until it is called
  **nothing observes a write**, which is what keeps `build-app`, the test suite and any admin
  script from firing anything.
- **`sc-server/tests/table_triggers.rs`** — 7 tests over the real router, a real database and the
  real V8 engine, with every row written and read as an HTTP request: the audit row carrying the
  event's row and its caller; update and delete staying silent on a table whose only trigger is an
  insert one; `old.title` and `row.title` in one formula; a delete audited after the row is gone;
  `pages > 100` firing for one row and passing over the other in silence; `authorⱵtier === "gold"`
  walking the Key link; the self-feeding trigger stopping at exactly `MAX_DEPTH` firings with the
  request that started it still returning 201 and the refusal naming the whole chain; and a
  throwing trigger that costs neither the write, nor the request, nor the second trigger on the
  same event.

## `run_js_code`: the last core action, and a third method on the JS engine

The escape hatch for the computation an elementary action cannot anticipate — and the **last item
in Phase 3**, so the core action set is now complete: `insert_row`, `update_rows`, `delete_rows`,
`fetch`, `run_js_code`.

- **`sc-expr`: `JsEvaluator::run_code`**, beside `eval` and `eval_value`, taking a new **`CodeCall`**
  (the source, plus the values in scope as JSON). Same thread, same isolate, same watchdog, same
  no-ops/no-extensions sandbox — the body is wrapped in a function, so a top-level `return` is legal
  and what it declares is local to the run (a trigger firing twice must not be a redeclaration
  error). The result is `JSON.stringify`d back.

  It is **not** a `FormulaCall`: a formula is one expression in the language this crate defines,
  parsed and validated and normalised and evaluable two ways; a code body is opaque JavaScript the
  host hands over verbatim. Collapsing them would have meant a `FormulaCall` whose `formula` was
  sometimes not a formula.

  Two details worth their lines: a **returned Promise is refused by name** (`JSON.stringify` of one
  is `{}`, which would look exactly like a result, and the sandbox has nothing to await); and
  binding **names** are checked as plain identifiers, because unlike the values — which ride in as
  JSON, as a formula's do — the names are spliced into `const` declarations. Failure messages now
  say `JavaScript code failed`/`timed out` where a formula's say `formula evaluation`, since the two
  send an admin to different places.

- **`sc-core-actions`: `RunJsCode`** — one setting, `code`. In scope: `row`, `old`, `user` and
  `payload`. The first three follow decision 7 exactly, so a trigger's `only_if` and its code see
  one world — **presence is scope**: `old` on an insert is in scope *and null*, while `row` on a
  `login` trigger is not bound at all, so naming it is a `ReferenceError` rather than a silent
  `undefined`. `payload` is the single addition: the formula language has no way to reach it, and a
  `none` trigger's code is exactly what wants it. What the body returns is the action's result.

- **Bounded on purpose.** No host API: the code cannot read or write the catalog, reach the network
  or touch the disk. Catalog access from guest code is `sc-code`'s milestone (§15) and this is its
  seed, not a preview of it — a test probes eight host globals from inside a body in a process that
  does have a database.

- **The timeout is the engine's, not the action's.** The isolate serves every ownership formula in
  the process serially, so a configurable per-trigger timeout would be a configurable hold on all of
  them. A runaway body is terminated by the existing watchdog and the engine serves the next run —
  and the next formula — normally.

- **Recorded limitation:** a syntax error surfaces at fire time, not on save, because compiling it
  needs the engine and `validate_config` has no access to one. What *is* refused on save is the
  blank body, which the generic spec check accepts (it is a string) and which would otherwise fire
  doing nothing at all.

- **Tests:** 7 integration tests against the real engine and a real catalog
  (`sc-core-actions/tests/run_js_code.rs`), 7 engine-level tests in `sc-expr`, and 2 unit tests for
  the action's scope and spec. Beyond the sandbox probes: statements/loop/`return` computing an
  object; the `old` and `payload` cases above; throw, `TypeError` and `SyntaxError` each an
  `Application` error naming the trigger and the setting; the watchdog case; save-time refusal of a
  blank body; and a context with no engine failing by name rather than skipping.

## `sc-core-actions`: one crate for the core actions, one crate for the machinery

No behaviour change; the whole point is that **"which crate is this action in?" now has one
answer**. Two rounds of moving got here — recorded as one entry, since the intermediate state
never shipped.

- **`crates/sc-core-actions` (layer 9, new)** holds every core action: `insert_row`,
  `update_rows`, `delete_rows` and `fetch`, with `run_js_code` to follow. It sits above `sc-api`
  because three of them must write through the `rows` layer — that *is* the point of them (type
  coercion, rich-type attributes, `File` rules, and from Phase 4 the events that make cascades
  work) — and `fetch` sits with them because splitting the set by each action's incidental needs
  made the answer to "where does an action live?" a paragraph instead of a crate name.
  `builtin_actions()` there is the single constructor a server calls.
- **`sc-action` (layer 6) now holds no actions at all** — and that is the sharper statement of what
  it *is*: the `Action` trait, the event model, the run context, the registry, the `Trigger` and its
  storage, plus the machinery every action's configuration goes through. So a **plugin's** action
  needs `sc-action` and nothing else, and the core crate is its first consumer rather than a
  privileged one.
- **`ActionRegistry::builtin()` is gone.** An empty constructor named for a set it does not hold is
  precisely the placeholder that goes stale, and there is now a crate whose job that is; `new()` is
  the only way in, and its doc says who fills it.
- **`sc-api` is back to knowing nothing about actions** (no `sc-action` dependency until Phase 7
  needs one for trigger endpoints). It gained one public function for them instead:
  **`rows::select_values`** — the rows a filter selects as `Value` maps including calculated
  fields, the evaluation-side counterpart of `list_rows_where`. One purposeful seam beats exposing
  `calc_projections` and `run_read`, which is what the alternative would have been.
- **Naming:** `sc-core-actions`, not `sc-actions`, because a one-letter difference from `sc-action`
  in every `use` line is a bug waiting to be misread.

### The shared machinery, now in `sc-action::scope`

- It holds what all of them share: the `(the event)` no-table scope,
  `action_shape`, the settings parsers (`config_str`, `optional_formula`, `required_formula`,
  `formula_map`), `check_formula`, and **`EventBindings`** — which objects an event puts in scope,
  with `old` present-but-null on an insert. That last one is the part worth centralising: it is the
  rule two crates' actions could have drifted on *silently*, one binding `old` as null where the
  other left it unbound.
- **`EventBindings` reads values two ways, from one structure.** `of(event)` reads each field as
  its own JSON shape — enough for reified evaluation, which is all an action that only reads the
  event needs. `with_values(event, …)` takes a per-field reading, which is how the row actions type
  them against real columns, because only the **translated** `where` cares: it compares inlined
  literals against actual SQL types, so `user.id` has to be a uuid there. Reified evaluation renders
  both back through `value_to_json` and cannot tell the difference — now stated where the two
  constructors are, instead of being a thing you had to work out.
- **`sc_expr::value_from_json`** — the inverse of `value_to_json`, next to it, with a test that the
  pair agrees in both directions for everything JSON can say. `sc-api`'s `json_to_natural_value` is
  gone in favour of it; the type-directed `json_to_value` stays where it was, for values that do
  have a column behind them.
- **`sc-core-actions`' `rows_scope` keeps only what a row action adds on top**: column-typed
  values, the two `where` strategies, the per-row prefetching, and the RLS authority.
- Tests travelled with their code — both integration suites are now
  `sc-core-actions/tests/{row_actions,fetch_action}.rs`, the settings-parser and event-bindings unit
  tests are in `sc-action::scope`. The bindings gained two of their own: presence-is-scope across
  insert/update/no-row events, and that a caller-supplied reading is applied to every field of
  every object.

## `fetch`: calling an HTTP endpoint, and reading its answer (Phase 3)

The fourth built-in action (TODO Phase 3). It is called `fetch` rather than `webhook` because the
**response is the point**: the parsed body is the action's result, so a directly-run trigger
returns it to its caller and a workflow step will put it in the run context. Fire-and-forget is
the same action with the result ignored.

- **New workspace dependency, as the plan called for**: `reqwest` with `default-features = false`
  and `rustls-tls` — no OpenSSL anywhere in the tree, so a deployment does not need a system
  libssl to call a webhook. `json` is the request-body serialiser only: responses are read as
  bytes and parsed with `serde_json` here, which keeps the crate's charset machinery out.
- **Configuration**: `url` (required, and refused unless it is an absolute `http`/`https` one — a
  relative URL has nothing to resolve against server-side, and `file:`/`data:` is a way to make
  the server read something local), `method` (a picker, default `POST`), literal `headers`,
  a `body` formula, and `timeout_ms`.
- **The body defaults to the event** — `{event, channel, row, old_row, payload, role, user}`,
  written out rather than derived, because that JSON is a wire contract with whatever is on the
  other end and must not change silently when a field is added to `Event`. A formula replaces it
  wholesale; `GET`/`HEAD` send none, and a body formula *on* one of those is refused at save rather
  than silently dropped. The default body needs no JavaScript engine, so one is demanded only when
  a formula is actually configured.
- **The timeout is bounded at 60 s and refused, not clamped**, when out of range. A trigger runs
  inside the write or request that fired it, so an unbounded `fetch` is an unbounded hold on that
  caller; and an admin who typed five minutes should be told, not quietly given one.
- **Failures say what happened.** A non-2xx names the status *and quotes the endpoint's own
  explanation* (truncated to 300 characters, so a page of HTML cannot become the error); a timeout
  says "timed out" rather than leaving the reader to decode a transport message; both name the
  trigger. A non-JSON 2xx body is **not** an error — it comes back as a JSON string, because an
  endpoint that answers `OK` did what was asked, and failing over its content type would make
  `fetch` unusable for half of what it is for.
- **One client, built at registration** (`Fetch::new`, which is why `builtin_actions()` is
  fallible): it holds the connection pool and the TLS configuration, and a deployment whose TLS
  stack cannot initialise should hear about it at boot rather than at the first firing.
- **Headers are literal, not formulas**: a header is a protocol detail of the endpoint being
  called, and the values that vary with the event belong in the body. Each name and value is
  checked at save time, and a configured `content-type` overrides the one the JSON body sets — a
  test pins that ordering, since it is the sort of thing a refactor silently reverses.
- **Where it lives.** As written, `fetch` went into `sc-api` beside the row actions, to reuse their
  configuration-and-scope machinery; the entry above is where it and every other core action ended
  up — `sc-core-actions`, with the shared machinery in `sc-action`.
- **A limitation worth recording:** `sc-expr` has no object *literal*, so a body formula computes
  a scalar, an array, or an ambient object as a whole (`row`, `payload`, `row.title`) — not a new
  object shape. The default envelope covers the common case; building objects in formulas would be
  an `sc-expr` change, not a `fetch` one.
- 6 integration tests against a **real socket** — a one-shot listener on port 0 that reads the
  request and hands it back to the test — plus unit tests over the configuration parsers. The
  request is asserted on its bytes (start line, content type, custom header, JSON body), because
  every way this action can be wrong is in the bytes, and a mocked client would be asserting facts
  about a request that never crossed a socket.

## The elementary row-writing actions: `insert_row`, `update_rows`, `delete_rows` (Phase 3)

The first three built-in actions (TODO Phase 3), and the one `where` semantics both of the
plural ones share. A trigger cannot fire yet — that is Phase 4 — so every one of these is
driven directly through the `Action` trait in its tests, which is exactly how the admin UI's
"run this now" and a workflow step will drive them too.

- **They live in `sc-api`, not `sc-action`** (`crates/sc-api/src/actions/`) — superseded: they are
  in `sc-core-actions` now, see the entry above. The reasoning that put them above the row layer
  still holds, and is what that crate's placement rests on. An action that
  writes a row must write it through the **`rows` layer**, so the target table's type coercion,
  rich-type attributes, `File`-field rules and — from Phase 4 — its own triggers all apply. That
  layer is layer 8, so `sc-api` gained a dependency on `sc-action` and implements its trait;
  nothing in `sc-action` names `sc-api` in return, so the emit seam the catalog will hold stays
  the only path from a write back to a trigger, and the trigger machinery stays at layer 6.
  `sc_api::actions::builtin_actions()` is the one constructor a server calls: the layer-6
  registry plus these three. A test proves it, and that registering them twice is refused.
- **`insert_row`** — a target table and a field→formula map, each formula computed from the event
  (`row.title`, `old.title`, `user.email`, `row.pages * 2`) and the whole row then handed to
  `rows::create_row_ctx`. The result is the **inserted row**, key and all, so a workflow step can
  refer to what it just made. "The formulas range over no table" is implemented as a formula
  scope named `(the event)` — an empty table shape — so `title` where `row.title` was meant is
  ``unknown identifier `title` `` at save time rather than a null at fire time.
- **`update_rows` / `delete_rows`** — a `where` formula and (for the update) a field→formula map
  of assignments, both in the **target table's** scope: `status` is the row being written,
  `row.status` is the event's, and a test where both tables have a `status` field pins that the
  two select different rows. Each selected row is written **by primary key, one at a time**, which
  is what will give it its own event with its own payload; a target table with no single-column
  primary key is therefore refused on save. `delete_rows` requires its predicate — an omitted
  `where` would mean "delete everything", which no missing setting should be able to cause.
- **The `where` semantics, once, for both**: translated into the `SELECT`'s `WHERE` when it
  translates (the event's row and caller inline as literals — no new SQL construct), and
  fetch-then-filter through the reified evaluator when it does not, exactly as an ownership read
  does. Three things settled in the writing: an evaluator error **fails the action** where the
  same error in an ownership check denies (there is no safe answer to "which rows did the admin
  mean"); the fetched rows carry their calculated fields and, per row, whatever Ⱶ-path or
  Ↄ-relation the formulas read, through `sc_catalog::prefetch_bindings` — the function a trigger's
  `only_if` already uses, so there is one answer to "what does a reified formula get bound"; and
  an action's writes carry **admin authority** on an RLS-enforced table (`ROLE_ADMIN` plus the
  event's user, so a policy reading `user` still sees who caused it), because a trigger is the
  admin's configuration and the audit row a user may not insert is precisely what one is for.
- **`Action::validate_config`** (new, defaulting to `Ok(())`) with a `ConfigCheck` carrying the
  catalog, the configuration and the event's channel. `validate_trigger` calls it after the
  generic `config_spec` check, so everything a spec cannot express — this table does not exist,
  has no primary key, has no field `x`; this formula does not parse, does not resolve in *this
  event's* scope, or uses the operation flags — is refused **in front of the admin** and
  re-checked on load, where a trigger whose world changed drops out of the live set with a reason.
  `sc_action::trigger_shape` now takes `Option<&str>` for the channel, so one function answers
  "what is in scope" for an `only_if` and for an action's formulas: a configuration valid on an
  `insert` trigger is refused on a `login` one, and a test asserts exactly that.
- **`sc_api::convert::json_to_natural_value`** — reading a JSON value with no column behind it,
  for an event's row from a table the catalog no longer has. Where a column *is* known the values
  go through `json_to_value` instead, which is what recovers the uuid a `user.id` needs to compare
  against a `uuid` column in SQL rather than a string that would not.
- 9 integration tests on real Postgres with the real V8 engine, plus 5 unit tests. One of them is
  there to keep another honest: the "same rows symbolically and reified" test compares two
  spellings of one rule, so a unit test asserts that those two spellings really do take the two
  different strategies — otherwise it could pass while the fallback was never run at all.

## Actions & triggers: `_sc_triggers`, ambient objects, and the live trigger set (Phase 2)

The trigger itself (TODO Phase 2): what it is, where it is stored, everything that can be wrong
with it, and the cached set an event will be matched against. Two of the six pieces are changes to
crates *below* `sc-action`, because the formula language had to learn one thing first.

- **`sc-expr`: `user` generalised into ambient objects** (TODO decision 7). The new `Ambient` enum
  (`user`/`row`/`old`) replaces the `user`-shaped special cases throughout: free-variable
  collection records properties per object, validation checks each object's members against its
  declared fields (`` `old.shoe_size`: `old` has no field `shoe_size` ``), the translator has one
  set of `ambient_*` methods where it had four `user_*` ones, and the reified binder binds each
  in-scope object as an object or `null`. The `Guc` env is untouched — an RLS policy has no
  triggering row, so only `user` has a GUC rendering, now explicit in one place
  (`Translator::guc_types`).
- **Scope is declared, not assumed.** `SchemaShape.ambient` maps each object to its fields; `user`
  is always in scope (every formula has a caller), `row`/`old` only where a trigger's shape
  declares them. So an ownership formula naming `row` still gets `unknown identifier` rather than
  silently reading null, and a table with a field *called* `row` keeps that field everywhere
  except inside a trigger's formula — the same shadowing `user` has always had, now pinned by a
  test. `old` in scope but **null** (an insert) is distinct from `old` out of scope, exactly as
  `UserEnv::Inline(None)` is distinct from no user env.
- **One `Env` instead of three trailing parameters.** `translate_with_calc` is gone: the user env,
  the ambient values and the calc fields became `Env::new(&user).with_ambient(…).with_calc(…)`, so
  `sc-expr` exposes one `translate` and one `translate_value` where it had three entry points.
- **Parity extended over the new objects** (`symbolic_and_reified_agree_on_the_ambient_objects`,
  10 cases): `row.x` compared against a column of the filtered table, `old` null on an insert
  (null member *and* `old === null` true), the "this field just changed" only-if, a null field of
  a present object, and all three objects in one formula. Parity is the gate the trigger
  machinery stands on, so the new scope had to pass it before anything used it.
- **`sc-catalog::prefetch_bindings`**: the Ⱶ-join and Ↄ-relation resolution that `sc-api`'s
  ownership checks built for the write path moved down a layer, because a trigger's `only_if`
  needs exactly the same bindings and `sc-action` sits below `sc-api`. One implementation, two
  callers — the drift it prevents would have been silent (a path that resolves in one crate and
  binds null in the other). The moved code now uses `sc_expr::value_to_json`, the JS mapping the
  evaluator's own bindings use, so a `Decimal` child field binds as a number rather than a string:
  a small parity fix that came out of the move.
- **`Trigger` + `_sc_triggers`** (`sc-action`): the §9 storage pattern — id/name/description/
  attributes, `name` UNIQUE (it is what an app, an API path and a Run button reference), strict
  reads, a name-clash check that names the other trigger before the constraint can, and NULL for a
  cleared optional field so "cleared" and "never set" read alike. `enabled` is a sparse attribute
  whose accessor removes the key when enabling.
- **Validation, in one place, on save** — and the same function again on load. The action resolves
  in the registry and its configuration validates against the spec it declares; `min_role` is on
  the 1–100 scale; a table event names a real table and a non-table event names none (both
  directions: a `login` trigger given a table is a misunderstanding worth naming); an `only_if`
  parses and validates with `user`/`row`/`old` in scope and the **operation flags refused by
  name** — the trigger's own event *is* the operation. An `only_if` on a channel-less event is
  refused: there is no row to test, and accepting a condition that can never be true is worse than
  saying so.
- **`Triggers`, the cached live set**: loaded and validated once, `reload()`ed after a save,
  matched by `(kind, channel)`. A stored trigger that no longer validates is **dropped with its
  reason kept** (`issues()`) rather than firing on a guess — fail closed, as an invalid ownership
  formula grants nothing. A *disabled* trigger is filtered at match time instead, so it stays
  listed and editable; and `require()` separates "never defined" (`NotFound`) from "defined but
  not usable", which carries the reason, because those call for different fixes.
- **Tests**: 32 in `sc-action` (23 unit + 9 integration), 99 + 6 in `sc-expr`. The headline is a
  table-driven test of **eleven save refusals**, each asserting the words the admin sees *and*
  that nothing was stored; the strict reader is exercised by writing an unknown event straight
  into the column; and the cache test drops the trigger's table behind the server's back, then
  asserts the trigger leaves the live set with `no table named books` as its issue, that
  `require` reports "not usable" rather than "not found", and that the row survives so repairing
  the table brings the trigger back.

## Repo hygiene: the CI gates go green under the current stable toolchain

No behaviour changed; both fixes are toolchain drift the workspace had accumulated.

- **`cargo fmt --all`** reformatted 15 files across `sc-api`, `sc-catalog`, `sc-expr`, `sc-query`,
  `sc-db-postgres` and `sc-server` — a newer stable rustfmt wraps long call chains and macro
  arguments differently, so `cargo fmt --all --check` (CI's first gate) had been failing on files
  nobody had touched.
- **One `#[allow(clippy::result_large_err)]`** on `RestProvider::resolve` (`sc-api/src/rest.rs`),
  which is what CI's `cargo clippy -D warnings` gate was failing on. The lint's advice does not
  apply here: the `Err` is not an error propagating up a stack, it **is** the response to send —
  the one caller either dispatches the resolved endpoint or returns that `ApiResponse` unchanged.
  Boxing it would add an indirection at seven call sites to shrink a value built once per missed
  request (and `ApiResponse` already boxes the one field worth boxing, `raw`). The reasoning is in
  a comment at the allow, so the next reader does not have to re-derive it.

## Actions & triggers: the `sc-action` crate (Phase 1)

The first phase of the actions-and-triggers milestone (TODO Phase 1; design §10.1–§10.2): the
**event → action** half, with no trigger yet. A new layer-6 crate `sc-action` holds what can
happen, what can be done about it, and which actions exist.

- **`Event` / `EventKind`** (`event.rs`): the eleven kinds (`insert`/`update`/`delete`/`none`/
  `login`/`startup`/`error` plus the four periodic ones) enumerated **once** in `EVENT_KINDS`,
  with `as_str`/`parse` and the `is_table_event`/`is_periodic` classifiers derived from it — an
  unknown kind is a configuration error naming the alternatives, because a trigger whose event
  nothing implements must not silently become a trigger that never fires. An `Event` is built
  fluently (`Event::new(EventKind::Insert).on("books").row(json)`) so the fields a kind does not
  use are unset rather than filled with placeholders. `row_object()`/`old_row_object()` project
  the ambient `row`/`old` bindings the scope rule (TODO decision 7) needs, reading a missing or
  non-object row as **empty** — which is what makes `old.x` on an insert *null* instead of an
  evaluation error.
- **The event is separate from the trigger, and the caller is data.** The row layer can emit
  events without knowing whether anything listens; and the caller travels as a role plus the
  user's fields as JSON — exactly what the formula language binds `user` to — rather than as a
  `sc_auth::User`, which keeps the crate off `sc-auth` and keeps actions implementable from a
  guest language later.
- **Cascades are bounded *and named*.** Deviation from the plan's `depth: u8`: an event carries
  the `chain` of trigger names that led to it, and `Event::firing` refuses to descend past
  `MAX_DEPTH` (5) with the whole path in the message (`a → b → c → d → e → f`). Same bound, and
  the diagnosis an admin needs instead of a bare number.
- **`Action` + `ActionContext`** (`action.rs`): the §10.1 trait — `name`, `description`,
  `config_spec() -> Vec<FormField>` (configuration is data, so the admin UI renders a form for an
  action it has never heard of) and `run(&mut ActionContext) -> Result<Json>`. The context carries
  the catalog, the event, the configuration, the trigger's name (an unattributable action failure
  is one an admin cannot fix), the cascade chain, and a `context: Attrs` the action reads and
  writes — the seam a workflow's durable run context grows into. A missing JavaScript engine is a
  configuration error naming the trigger, never a silent no-op (principle 5); `require_str`
  returns an owned `String` so "read a setting, then write the context" is not a borrow-check
  error in every action that does both.
- **`ActionRegistry`** (`registry.rs`): name → `Arc<dyn Action>` in a `BTreeMap`, so listings and
  "the registered actions are …" messages are name-ordered rather than hash-ordered. A duplicate
  registration is **refused**, not overwritten — otherwise which implementation answers a stored
  trigger would depend on load order. `builtin()` exists and is empty until Phase 3, so the
  wiring above it is built against the real constructor.
- **Tests**: 13 unit (kind round-trip, unknown kind by name, the classifiers over the whole set,
  event defaults and `require_channel` on a kind that has no channel, the empty-object row
  reading, `firing` extending the chain and the depth limit naming it as an *application* error,
  object-safety, registry ordering/lookup, unknown- and duplicate-name refusals) and 4
  integration against real Postgres (`tests/action_context.rs`): an action reaching its event,
  configuration and a live catalog; a missing required setting failing the run with the trigger
  named; no-engine refusing rather than skipping; and a stored action *name* resolved through the
  registry and run with an inherited chain. The context is integration-tested rather than
  unit-tested because `Catalog` needs a database, and a catalog-free mirror of its accessors would
  have tested a copy of the code instead of the code.

## New milestone: actions & triggers (plan only)

The ownership-formulae milestone is complete, so its task list is archived and the next one is
written. No code changed; the record lives in the new `TODO.md`.

- **`TODO.md` archived to `docs/TODO-post-mvp-3.md`** (ownership formulae, calculated fields and
  RLS — every phase `[x]`), with its root-relative doc links rewritten for its new directory.
- **New `TODO.md` — Actions & Triggers**: elementary actions only (workflows and agents are a
  later milestone), in nine phases — the `sc-action` crate (layer 6: `Action` trait, event model,
  run context, registry); `_sc_triggers` storage with save-time validation and a cached registry;
  the built-in actions (`insert_row`, `update_rows`, `delete_rows`, `fetch`, `run_js_code`);
  table `insert`/`update`/`delete` events with the `only_if` formula; `none`/`login`/`startup`/
  `error` events; the admin API and SPA; applications picking triggers to expose over their API
  (`POST {mount}/actions/{name}`, typed client method) as they already pick tables; the periodic
  scheduler (`often`/`hourly`/`daily`/`weekly`, admin-configured times); and documentation.
- **Seven decisions recorded up front**, because each is a fork the plan depends on: dispatch is
  synchronous and *after* the write commits (no durable queue until `sc-bus`); the `sc-api::rows`
  layer is the single emit choke point; `only_if` is the same `sc-expr` language minus the
  operation flags; triggers are cached like every other created entity; recursion is bounded
  (depth limit) rather than forbidden; periodic times are UTC and single-node, with a persisted
  `last_run_at` so a run missed while down fires once at startup; and **one scope rule for every
  formula in a trigger** — bare identifiers are the row the formula is about (the target table's
  row in an `update_rows`/`delete_rows` `where`), while the triggering event is ambient as
  `row`/`old` beside the existing `user`, which widens `sc-expr`'s `UserEnv` from one inlined
  ambient object to a set of them.

## Admin UI: define calculated fields

The admin table editor can now create a **non-stored calculated field** — the storage and
enforcement (Phase 8) existed but there was no way to define one from the UI.

- **Admin SPA** (`ui/admin` `TableDetail.tsx`): the "add field" form gains a **Calculated**
  checkbox. When checked, it swaps the Key/attribute/nullable inputs for a monospace **Formula**
  textarea (placeholder `pages * 2`), and the type picker — restricted to value types, no
  Key/File kinds — becomes the computed value's *display* type. The field is sent as a `calc`
  kind carrying the expression. Calc fields are listed with their expression (`calc: pages * 2`)
  and, because writing one is refused, are shown but never offered as inputs in the row editor.
- **`createField` handler** (`sc-server`): `parse_field_kind` accepts `{ type: "calc",
  expression }`, and the create path branches on it — a calc field is **virtual**, so no column
  DDL is run; only the `_sc_fields` overlay row is written. The expression is validated up front
  (`validate_calc_field`, like an ownership formula but refusing `user`/flags), so a broken
  formula is a 400 naming the field rather than a silently-dropped overlay.
- **Tests**: `sc-server` `field_api.rs` creates a calc field end-to-end through the router —
  asserts it is computed on read (`pages * 2` → 200), that no `double_pages` **column** exists
  in `information_schema`, that writing the calc field is refused, and that a bad expression and
  a `user`-using expression are each refused by name with nothing created.

## Documentation for ownership formulae & RLS (Phase 9)

The ownership-formula milestone is written up where the design and the tutorials live.

- **`docs/TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md` §7.3 rewritten as implemented**: the access rule
  (`allowed = role meets min_role OR formula true`, ownership only widening below the floor),
  the one-language/two-evaluators design (symbolic → `sc-query::Expr`, reified → `deno_core`),
  **parity as a tested property**, the fail-closed rules (invalid stored formula, anonymous
  `user`, missing GUC), the `Ⱶ` join-path mechanism, non-stored calc fields inlined into
  ownership/RLS, and the enforcement swap — runtime `WHERE` injection vs Postgres RLS with the
  `sc.user`/`sc.role` GUC scheme, `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` and the four generated policies.
- **§18.4 resolved**: the JavaScript-vs-CEL open question is closed in favour of **JavaScript**,
  with the reasoning recorded (evaluated two ways from one parse; the reified engine being V8
  means the parser must accept exactly what V8 accepts; v1/calc-field/code-adapter alignment).
- **`sc-expr` added to the §2 crate map** (layer 3, depends only on `sc-query`/`sc-error`, V8
  behind the `eval` feature) with a note on why it does not depend on `sc-catalog`.
- **New tutorial `docs/tutorial-ownership.md`** in the established browser-first style: a
  two-user app, an `owner === user.email` formula, the same rule through a `Ⱶ`-join
  (`projectⱵowner === user.email`), then flipping on RLS and reading the generated policies in
  `psql` (the forced four, the translated `SELECT` qual, the fail-closed `count(*) → 0` on a
  raw connection). Cross-linked from `tutorial-file-fields.md`; the `tutorials_are_cross_linked`
  hygiene test extended to require the new links.

## Non-stored calculated fields (Phase 8)

A field can now be a **non-stored calculated field** — no column on disk, only an `sc-expr`
expression (`pages * 2`, `publisherⱵname`, `reviewsↃbook.length`, or another calc field)
computed on read. It reuses the whole formula machinery, including Phase 7's Ↄ-aggregations,
over the calc scope: the row's own fields, Ⱶ-join paths, Ↄ-aggregations and *other calc
fields on the same table* — never `user` or the operation flags.

- **Storage** (`sc-catalog`): `DataFieldKind::Calc { expression }`, a `calc` discriminant in
  the `_sc_fields` overlay with the expression under a reserved key. The merge introduces it
  as a *virtual* field (there is no column to overlay) and refuses one whose name collides
  with a real column.
- **Validation + ordering** (`sc-catalog::calc`, at `Catalog::reload`): each expression is
  validated like an ownership formula but with **no `user`/flags**; a Kahn sort over the
  same-table dependency graph orders the fields (so one may read another) and detects cycles.
  A cycle, an invalid expression, or a dependency on an invalid one **drops** the field (fail
  closed) and reports a `FieldMergeIssue`; the survivors are left in dependency order.
- **Read** (`sc-api::rows`): the read and insert/update-`RETURNING` `SELECT`s project each
  calc field's value expression, aliased to its name, in dependency order — a calc field
  reading an earlier one **inlines** that one's expression, so the SQL is self-contained.
  Calc fields are **not writable**: a write naming one is refused by name.
- **Ownership + RLS** (`sc-expr::translate_value` / `translate_with_calc`, threading a
  `CalcFields` map): a calc-field reference inside an ownership formula is inlined as its
  defining expression, transitively, in value and predicate position, before `translate` —
  in both the runtime `Inline` env and the RLS `Guc` policy. An untranslatable inlined
  definition refuses RLS at save and at `enable_rls`, naming the construct; the runtime path
  falls back to the reified evaluator.
- **Reified value evaluation** (`sc-expr::eval`): `JsEvaluator` gains `eval_value`, returning
  the formula's value as JSON (`JSON.stringify` in the isolate) rather than a boolean verdict
  — the reference the calc parity tests check the SQL projection against.
- **sc-query**: `translate_value` reuses the existing `Expr` tree; no new variant.
- **Tests**: calc ordering + cycle units; end-to-end read of plain/Ⱶ/Ↄ/calc-to-calc values,
  the write refusal and fail-closed cycle (`sc-api` `calc_fields.rs`); SQL-vs-reified value
  parity (`sc-expr` `parity_calc.rs`); an ownership formula through a calc field enforced at
  runtime (`sc-server`) and inlined into an RLS policy, with an untranslatable calc definition
  refused (`sc-catalog` `rls_policies.rs`).
- **Out of scope, carried** (unchanged from the plan): stored calculated fields and the
  tamper-safe inlining of a stored field's *value* into a policy (they need the write-path
  recompute and triggers); the reified read-path fallback for a genuinely *untranslatable*
  calc expression (the `eval_value` machinery is in place; the built-in forms all translate).

## Formula aggregations — implemented (Phase 7)

Ownership formulae (and, once they land, calculated fields) can now aggregate over
*incoming* keys: `order_linesↃorder.sum("qty")`, `sharesↃdocument.some(s => s.shared_with
=== user.email)`. An inverse-relation identifier is spelled with the Claudian antisigma `Ↄ`
(U+2183, category Lu — one valid JS identifier, exactly like the forward-join `Ⱶ`), and is
aggregated by the curated method chain from `docs/AGG_EXPRS.md`. The whole thing is one
parse feeding both evaluators, and their agreement is a tested property.

- **sc-expr `agg.rs`** (new): parses a chain from the lowered AST (`filter*`, optional
  `map`/`distinct`, one terminal, and the member read of a `maxBy`/`minBy` row), checks it
  against the curated grammar — `reduce`/`sort`/`slice`/`find`/… refused by name with the
  alternative, a computed selector refused, an unknown child field named — and resolves the
  relation against the child table's Key. `Analysis` gains `agg_uses`
  (`AggUse { child_table, key_field, value_fields, filter_fields }`), the prefetch plan now
  and the stored-calc trigger dependencies later; `SchemaShape` gains `incoming(table)` and
  a per-table `primary_key`.
- **Symbolic translation** (`translate.rs`): one correlated subquery per chain — `length`
  → `count(*)`, `sum` → `coalesce(sum(…), 0)`, `avg`/`min`/`max` bare, `some`/`every` →
  `count(*) > 0` / `= 0` over the (negated) predicate, `includes` → `x IN (SELECT …)`,
  `join` → `coalesce(string_agg(…), '')`, `distinct` → `count(DISTINCT …)`, `maxBy`/`minBy`
  → `ORDER BY key, pk LIMIT 1` selecting the accessed member. Filters fold into the
  `WHERE`; arrow bodies translate in a child-row scope (`r.qty` → the child column,
  `r.fkⱵx` → a nested join subquery). Serves both the runtime `Inline` env and the RLS
  `Guc` env unchanged.
- **sc-query**: a new `Expr::Agg { func, distinct, args }` (the `count(*)` / `DISTINCT`
  shape a plain `Func` cannot spell).
- **Reified evaluation** (`eval.rs` prelude, `normalise.rs`): the seven invented methods are
  defined on `Array.prototype` inside the isolate, implementing the semantics table (null
  ignored; empty relation `sum` 0, `avg`/`min`/`max`/`maxBy`/`minBy` null; `maxBy` tie-break
  by the child's `id`). Relation identifiers render verbatim (bound to a prefetched array);
  a member read of a `maxBy`/`minBy` result is folded to `null` (not `?.`'s `undefined`) so
  it matches SQL's empty-subquery `NULL`.
- **Enforcement**: aggregations flow through the Phase 5 runtime WHERE-injection (reads) and
  the write path's reified check now prefetches each relation's child rows
  (`resolve_agg_relation`); RLS policies (Phase 6) generate the same correlated subqueries.
  **RLS-enablement cycle detection** (`sc_catalog::rls`): a policy that queries a child
  table whose policy queries back is refused at enable time, naming the cycle, instead of
  letting Postgres raise "infinite recursion detected in policy" at query time.
- **sc-db-postgres**: `PgParam` now coerces an integer/float bind into a `numeric` (or
  narrower integer) placeholder — an ownership formula comparing against `sum(…)`/`avg(…)`
  lands its literal in a `numeric`-typed parameter, which the strict encoder would otherwise
  reject.
- **Tests**: per-method parity over the semantics table (`tests/parity_agg.rs`, translator
  vs `deno_core`, incl. empty/null/tie cases); translator golden SQL and reified prelude
  unit tests; validation-error unit tests; an end-to-end aggregation ownership formula
  enforced through the real router (`commentsↃpost.some(…)`); and the RLS cycle refusal.
- **Known gaps, carried**: an *untranslatable* aggregation on the read path (fetch-then-
  filter) does not yet prefetch relations, and a child row's own Ⱶ-join inside a reified
  arrow (`r.fkⱵx`) is not prefetched — both translate fine, so the SQL path covers them.

## Formula aggregations — design decided (proposal G)

Design work only, no implementation yet.

- **docs/AGG_EXPRS.md** (new): design for aggregation over *incoming* keys in formula
  expressions, for both ownership formulae and calculated fields. Seven proposals (A–G)
  were developed and the file then condensed to the decided design plus a summary of the
  rejected alternatives. Decision:
  **proposal G** — relation identifiers spelled with the Claudian antisigma
  (`order_linesↃorder`; U+2183 is, like Ⱶ, category Lu and a valid JS identifier
  character) holding arrays of child rows, aggregated by a curated method chain: native
  `filter`/`map`/`some`/`every`/`length`/`includes`/`join`; invented
  `sum`/`min`/`max`/`avg`/`distinct` with an optional selector (constant field-name string
  or arrow) and ordered `maxBy`/`minBy` (null keys ignored, primary-key tie-break,
  optional-chaining member access); `reduce` and everything ambient-ordered, positional or
  effectful refused by name. One null/empty semantics table as the evaluator-parity
  contract; chains translate to single correlated subqueries (`some`/`every` →
  `EXISTS`/`NOT EXISTS`), so RLS-mode ownership via share tables costs no refetch.
- **docs/TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md**: §7.3 gains the aggregation block; §6.2's calculated-fields
  paragraph now names both traversal directions (Ⱶ out, Ↄ in).
- **TODO.md**: new Phase 7 (formula aggregations) with implementation items; calculated
  fields renumbered to Phase 8, documentation to Phase 9.

## Ownership formulae milestone — Postgres row-level security (Phase 6)

The *same* ownership formula can now be enforced by Postgres instead of the application:
flipping `rls_enabled` swaps the enforcement mechanism, never the outcome.

- **Caller context** (`sc_catalog::run_in_context`): every row operation on an RLS table
  runs in a transaction that first `SET LOCAL`s `sc.role` and (when logged in) `sc.user` as
  one JSON GUC — the shape `UserEnv::Guc` reads. New `sc-db` seams, both defaulting to a
  loud error so a backend cannot silently skip them: `Transaction::set_local` (via
  `set_config($1,$2,true)`, value bound not interpolated) and `Transaction::batch` (raw
  multi-statement DDL, for the policies). Admin row endpoints run at `sc.role = 1`, which
  clears every policy's role floor so the admin viewer works on a FORCE'd table. A path that
  forgets the GUCs sees **no rows** — a property of the policy shape, proven by a raw-connection
  probe.
- **Policy generation** (`sc_catalog::enable_rls`): `ENABLE` then **`FORCE ROW LEVEL
  SECURITY`** (the server connects as the table owner, whom RLS otherwise exempts), a
  `DROP POLICY IF EXISTS` per name (idempotent recreate), and four policies from the
  `UserEnv::Guc` translation with flags folded per operation — SELECT/DELETE `USING`, INSERT
  `WITH CHECK`, UPDATE both — each carrying the role floor
  (`NULLIF(current_setting('sc.role',true),'')::int <= floor OR (<formula>)`).
- **`sc_query::render_policy_expr`**: a new renderer that inlines literals (DDL takes no
  binds) through the dialect's `quote_literal`, refusing any non-scalar `Value` — the
  narrow, deliberate exception to "every literal is parameterised", for trusted translated
  formulas only.
- **The custom-GUC empty-string fix**: a set-once custom GUC keeps an empty-string default
  on a reused pooled connection, and `''::jsonb` / `''::int` are hard errors — so both the
  translator's `current_setting('sc.user', …)` and the role clause are wrapped in
  `NULLIF(…, '')`, folding unset-or-empty to NULL so the policy fails closed either way.
- **Enforcement swap**: `RestProvider::run` dispatches to `run_rls` before any §5 check when
  `rls_enabled` — reads unfiltered, writes unpredicated, all through `run_in_context`. A
  `USING` denial is zero rows → not-found; a `WITH CHECK` violation (`42501`) is mapped to
  not-found so denial is unprobeable. Joinfields are not refetched — the policy's correlated
  subselects do that in the database. The rows layer gained an `Option<&CallerContext>` on
  its run primitives so one path serves both the pooled and context cases.
- **Lifecycle**: `sync_table_rls` in the `updateTable` handler creates/recreates or drops
  policies to match the saved flag (keyed on pre-save state, so a plain role change emits no
  RLS DDL); `deleteTableSettings` disables a table it was enforcing; a formula edit while RLS
  stays on regenerates the policies live.
- **Tests**: `sc-server/tests/rls_enforcement.rs` (3) re-runs §5's owner scenario under RLS
  (same verdicts, database-enforced, admin bypass through FORCE), the fail-closed probe, and
  the enable→edit→disable lifecycle with `pg_policies` assertions; `sc-catalog/tests/rls_policies.rs`
  (2) asserts the primitives directly (four named policies, both `pg_class` flags, idempotent
  recreate, clean disable, GUC-gated `run_in_context`); plus renderer unit tests for
  `render_policy_expr`.

## Ownership formulae milestone — runtime enforcement (Phase 5)

Ownership formulas now govern data access through a real application: **allowed = the
caller meets the operation's `min_role` OR the formula grants the row**. Ownership extends
access below the role floor and never narrows a role's own access; at-floor callers take
exactly the pre-formula paths.

- **The rule's home is `crates/sc-api/src/ownership.rs`**, driven from the REST provider's
  dispatch. A table with a formula projects its endpoints as `Public` (the formula decides
  `user === null`), and the handler enforces floor-or-formula — with a re-check, so a stale
  projection can never be wider than the rule.
- **Reads**: below the floor, the translated predicate (`UserEnv::Inline`, `_read` folded)
  is ANDed into the SELECT — the database filters, no V8 in the loop. An untranslatable
  formula falls to the reified evaluator, with every Ⱶ-join value fetched **in the same
  query**: the translator's correlated subselect is exposed as `sc_expr::join_path_expr`
  and projected as a column aliased to the join identifier itself, so the evaluator's
  bindings arrive with the rows. Projected join columns are stripped before the wire.
- **Writes**: the existing row is fetched (with joins) and checked reified for every
  formula — parity makes the verdict equal to the symbolic one, and the row is needed for
  the WITH-CHECK merge anyway — then the translated predicate additionally rides in the
  UPDATE/DELETE's WHERE (`update_row_guarded`/`delete_row_guarded`) as the belt against a
  check-to-write race. A denied row answers with the **same not-found an absent row gets**
  (tested shape-for-shape); an update that would move the row out of the caller's
  ownership is a 403 naming it; an insert is checked against the proposed row (`_insert`),
  its join values resolved link-by-link since a proposed row cannot be projected from the
  database. The File endpoints apply the same rule (download = read; upload = update,
  checked against the row as it will be — the formula may read the field being written).
- **Wiring**: the `JsEvaluator` trait left `sc-expr`'s `eval` feature gate (only
  `DenoEvaluator` needs the engine); `RestProvider::with_evaluator`,
  `sc_app::app_providers_with` and `AppMounts::with_evaluator` thread one shared engine —
  `sc_server::default_js_evaluator()`, one isolate per server, constructed in the CLI boot
  — into every mount, and `refresh_table` re-projections keep it. A formula that needs the
  engine on a server without one **fails closed** with a configuration error.
- **Tests** (`crates/sc-server/tests/ownership_enforcement.rs`, 4, real app + real router,
  floors admin-only throughout so the formula is the only path): the owner formula across
  all four operations with probe-free denial and the 403 ownership-move refusal; the
  anonymous null-match corner observed live and closed by `user && …`; `_read || …`
  splitting reads from writes; the Ⱶ formula in translatable and untranslatable spellings
  with identical assertions (parity doing production work); File upload/download under the
  rule. Every formula is set through the admin API after mounting, so the live
  re-projection is exercised by construction.

## Ownership formulae milestone — storage and the admin surface (Phase 4)

An admin can now write an ownership formula on a table and store the RLS flag; the catalog
merges, validates and fails closed; the SPA edits it all. Enforcement is Phases 5/6 — this
phase makes the configuration real, validated, and visible.

- **Storage** (`sc-catalog`): `ownership_formula` and `rls_enabled` live in
  `TableMeta.attributes` (sparse, per §9; GOALS names attributes for `rls_enabled`
  explicitly) behind typed accessors — clearing removes the key, so an untouched table has
  no residue. The merged `Table` gains `ownership: Option<Formula>`, `ownership_error` and
  `rls_enabled`.
- **The merge validates in two steps**: `apply_overlay` *parses* the stored source (a
  broken one fails closed on the spot); `Catalog::reload` *validates* after all tables have
  merged — a Ⱶ-path crosses tables, so no table can check its own formula. A formula that
  stops validating (schema drift, a restored dump) **grants nothing** and the reason rides
  on the table itself as `ownership_error` — one home, the settings card — rather than in a
  catalog-level issue list. New `Catalog::schema_shape()` is the catalog→`sc-expr`
  projection (user fields from `users` minus `password_hash`), reused by save-time
  validation and by Phases 5/6.
- **`sc-expr` grew an `eval` cargo feature** (default on; workspace default off): the
  catalog links the parse/validate/translate half only, keeping V8 out of every crate below
  the server.
- **Admin API**: `updateTable` accepts `ownership_formula` + `rls_enabled` (required, like
  the other settings — a save states the whole configuration), validates before writing
  (parse, identifiers, Ⱶ-paths, `user.x` against the user table), and refuses
  `rls_enabled: true` when the backend lacks `DbCapabilities::row_level_security`, when
  there is no formula, or when the formula does not translate under the GUC environment for
  all four operations — a flag that could never be honoured is refused while the admin can
  still fix it (§6 makes the stored flag enforce). `table_schema()` adds those two plus
  output-only `ownership_error` and `rls_available`; orphan listings carry the formula
  fields too.
- **Admin SPA**: the table settings card gains a monospace formula textarea with the
  server's validation message inline, a warning banner when a stored formula is not in
  effect, and an RLS switch rendered only when the backend advertises the capability. The
  tables list badges `formula` / `formula error` / `RLS`. TS client regenerated; `tsc` and
  `vite build` pass.
- **Tests**: 3 HTTP integration tests (round-trip; five invalid shapes refused by name with
  nothing written; RLS-flag refusals and acceptance; `DROP COLUMN` schema drift failing
  closed and repairable) + 3 unit tests on the accessors and the merge's parse/fail-closed
  paths. The nine pre-existing settings `PUT`s across the test suite now state the new
  required fields.

## Ownership formulae milestone — reified evaluation on deno_core (Phase 3)

Formulas now actually run: `sc-expr` gains the second evaluator — a real V8 isolate via
`deno_core` 0.408 — and the parity suite proving it agrees with Phase 2's symbolic
translation.

- **`JsEvaluator` trait + `DenoEvaluator`** (`crates/sc-expr/src/eval.rs`): a `JsRuntime` is
  `!Send`, so the evaluator owns a dedicated thread holding the isolate, fed jobs over a
  channel (async callers await a oneshot) — the pattern the §15 code adapters will reuse.
  The trait is the engine seam; its contract is explicit: `Ok(bool)` is the verdict, `Err`
  (throw, timeout, unbound variable) **must be treated as deny**.
- **Sandbox, two layers.** The isolate has no extensions, no ops, and `globalThis.Deno`
  deleted at setup — `Deno`/`fetch`/`require`/`process` are asserted absent at the V8 level.
  One layer up, an identifier outside the formula vocabulary (fields, Ⱶ-joins, `user`,
  flags, whitelisted globals) is refused by the binder and never reaches V8 at all.
- **Watchdog**: a per-evaluator thread armed per call terminates runaway JS through the
  isolate's thread-safe handle (default 250ms) and the isolate recovers
  (`cancel_terminate_execution`) — tested with a raw `while(true)` (the formula language
  itself cannot express a loop, which is a feature).
- **The normalised rendering** (`src/normalise.rs`, pure, unit-tested without V8): the
  evaluator runs JS rendered *from the shared AST*, not the admin's source. `==`/`!=`
  render strict; ordered comparisons and arithmetic are null-guarded to `null` via
  single-evaluation IIFEs (JS coerces `null` to `0`; `null` rather than `false` keeps even
  the value identical to SQL's); `user.x` renders as `(user === null ? null : user.x)`;
  `!`/`&&`/`||`/`?:`/`??` render natively — two-valued JS logic *is* the spec, and the
  Phase 2 translator was adjusted at the one point the logics differ: **`!P` now translates
  as `P IS DISTINCT FROM TRUE`** (SQL `NOT NULL` is `NULL` where JS `!null` is `true`),
  with a golden test.
- **Binding**: a `FormulaCall` binds row fields, prefetched Ⱶ-join values (the evaluator
  does no I/O — an unprefetched join value is a named error, not `undefined`), `user` as an
  object or `null`, and the five flags through the same fold table as the translator.
  Values enter the script as one JSON literal — never concatenated — and an
  injection-shaped value staying a value is a test.
- **The parity suite** (`tests/parity.rs`, real Postgres × real V8): formula × row × user
  matrices over the whole translatable subset, each case asserted three ways (evaluators
  agree *and* match the expected verdict). Null corners throughout: the anonymous
  null-match and the `user && …` guard closing it, `!(pages < 100)` on a null row, null
  FKs at join depth one and two, flags per operation, GUC mode against a real
  `set_config` session with the missing-GUC case failing closed.
- 20 new unit tests (10 evaluator + 8 renderer + 2 translator) and the 2-test parity
  suite; the Ⱶ-in-V8 assertion closes the claim Phase 1 deferred.

## Ownership formulae milestone — symbolic evaluation to SQL (Phase 2)

A formula now becomes a `sc_query::Expr` predicate: `translate(&formula, op, env, shape,
table)` in `sc-expr` (`src/translate.rs`), the single translator both later consumers share —
Phase 5 ANDs its output into a statement's WHERE, Phase 6 renders it into an RLS policy.

- **The specified semantics live here** (and Phase 3's reified evaluator will render
  normalised JS from the same AST to match): `===`/`==` → `IS NOT DISTINCT FROM` (JS's
  two-valued equality; `x === null` → `IS NULL`; loose `==` translated as strict); ordered
  comparisons keep SQL null semantics; `?:` → searched `CASE`; `??` → `COALESCE`;
  `&&`/`||`/`!` are boolean and refused in value position (JS yields an operand there).
  One documented, test-pinned corner: an anonymous user's `user.x` is null, so
  `owner === user.id` matches null-owner rows — formulas that must not grant anonymously
  write `user && …` (bare `user` is object-or-null, so its truthiness is the logged-in
  test — same guard, shorter).
- **Operation flags never reach SQL**: translation is per-`Operation` (`Read`/`Insert`/
  `Update`/`Delete`) and a value-exact constant folder resolves `_read`/`_write`/… first —
  `_read || owner === user.id` is literally `TRUE` for a select and exactly the ownership
  clause for a write. Arrow parameters shadow flag names in the folder, as in the analyser.
- **Two user environments**: `UserEnv::Inline(Option<map>)` inlines the current user's
  values as parameterised literals (not logged in → `user` null, every `user.x` null);
  `UserEnv::Guc { field_types }` renders `user.x` as
  `CAST(jsonb_extract_path_text(CAST(current_setting('sc.user', true) AS jsonb), 'x') AS type)`
  — `current_setting`'s `missing_ok` makes an unset context SQL NULL, so GUC mode fails
  closed by construction. `USER_GUC = "sc.user"` is the constant Phase 6 will `SET LOCAL`.
- **Ⱶ-join paths become correlated scalar subselects**, one nested subquery per link
  (`_sc_j<n>` aliases — the reserved prefix, so an alias can never shadow a user table);
  a null FK yields no row yields NULL, which grants nothing: the optional-chaining
  contract for free.
- **`TranslateError` distinguishes verdicts**: `Untranslatable(construct)` — a shape
  property; Phase 5 falls back to the reified evaluator, Phase 6 refuses to enable RLS
  naming it — vs `Error` (unknown identifier, broken path), so a typo can never ride the
  fallback into V8. `From<TranslateError> for Error` maps both onto `Invalid`.
- **`sc-query` grew three constructs** the translation needed, each renderer-tested:
  `BinOp::IsNotDistinct`/`IsDistinct`; `Expr::Subquery` (scalar subquery); `Expr::Cast`
  with a type-name guard — the cast type is the one string in the AST neither
  identifier-quoted nor bound, so the renderer refuses anything that could not be a SQL
  type name (`uuid; DROP TABLE users` is a test).
- 18 translator unit tests (golden WHERE clauses through a Postgres-flavoured dialect,
  both envs, folding per operation, the null corners, every untranslatable construct
  named) + 3 renderer tests in `sc-query`.

## Ownership formulae milestone — `sc-expr`: parse and analyse (Phase 1)

The first phase of the ownership-formulae milestone (TODO Phase 1; GOALS "Authorization"):
a new workspace crate `sc-expr` owning the JavaScript formula language, below `sc-catalog`
in the dependency graph — the catalog will describe tables *to* it through a `SchemaShape`
projection, so the crate stays a generic library.

- **Parsing (`Formula::parse`)** runs `swc_ecma_parser` (the parser family Deno builds on,
  so the parser and the Phase-3 V8 evaluator can never disagree on grammar) and requires a
  **single pure expression**: multiple statements, non-expression statements and the empty
  formula are refused, and parse errors carry the 1-based line/column an admin can act on.
- **The crate's own `Ast`, lowered at the parse boundary.** swc's types never escape: the
  lowered tree is plain owned data (`Send + Sync`, tested) fit for cached catalog entries,
  and it is the one tree both later evaluators will consume — the symbolic translator
  (Phase 2) and the reified evaluator's normalised rendering (Phase 3), which is what makes
  evaluator parity provable. Lowering bounds the language: assignment, `new`, `this`,
  comma/spread/bitwise/`in`/`instanceof`/`**`, function and class expressions, block-bodied
  or destructuring arrows, BigInt/regex literals and WTF-8 (lone-surrogate) strings are
  refused **by name**. Arrows with simple parameters are allowed so array methods
  (`groups.some(g => …)`) stay usable on the reified path.
- **The Ⱶ join operator is an identifier character, not syntax.** Ⱶ (U+2C75) is Unicode
  category Lu, so `publisherⱵname` lexes as a *single JavaScript identifier* in swc and V8
  alike — no preprocessing anywhere. Tested against the real parser, single and chained.
- **Free-variable analysis** (collected once at parse): every identifier not bound by an
  arrow parameter, with static `user.x` accesses recorded and computed `user[x]` flagged.
- **Validation (`Formula::validate`)** classifies every free variable against a
  `SchemaShape` — a field of the table, a Ⱶ-join path resolved link by link through Key
  fields to any depth, `user` (with `user.x` checked against the user table's fields when
  the caller declares them), the five operation flags (`_read`/`_insert`/`_update`/
  `_delete`/`_write`), or a whitelisted JS global (`Math`, `undefined`, … — fields shadow
  globals, matching the reified scope to come). Anything else is an `Invalid` error naming
  the identifier and the table. The result is an `Analysis` the later phases plan from:
  fields read, resolved join paths, flags used, user usage.
- 24 unit tests across parsing, lowering refusals, Ⱶ lexing, scope binding and every
  classification/refusal path in validation.

The milestone's documentation phase — the technical design document now describes what was
built, and a tutorial walks the whole feature end to end.

- **`docs/TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md` §9.1 (new):** the `_sc_tables`/`_sc_fields` merge and precedence
  rules as implemented — the database is the authority on what it knows, the overlay on what it
  knows, and the two sets do not intersect; no row → introspection's answer exactly; the key
  choices (`name` UNIQUE, the composite field PK); strict reads and refused off-scale roles;
  system tables never merging a row; orphan rows kept and reported; the field-specific rules
  (kind as discriminant + parameters, type/column mismatches as reported inconsistencies, the
  `Key` precedence split); and liveness (saves reload the catalog and re-project mounted apps).
- **§6.1:** the rich-type registry (`registered_rich_types` / `rich_type` /
  `rich_type_config_spec`), `TypeRef::Rich` with name-as-identity and `validate_with`, the two
  shipped types (`String` with `max_length`/`options`/`regex` — subsuming the planned `Email` —
  and `Integer` with `min`/`max`), and the two boundaries: `File` is a kind, not a rich type,
  and introspection never resolves a column back to a rich type. `fieldviews()` is marked
  deferred until §6.3 ships.
- **§17:** the MVP's "no stored metadata beyond information_schema and `_sc_applications`" line
  is marked superseded, with a note saying what replaced it (the definitions tables plus the
  §9.1 overlays) and why the zero-setup promise still holds — a table or field with no overlay
  row comes out of the catalog exactly as introspection built it, so the promise was always
  "zero rows *required*", and stayed true when rows became *possible*. The §9 table's
  `_sc_tables`/`_sc_fields` rows now name what the overlays actually hold.
- **New tutorial `docs/tutorial-file-fields.md`**, in the style of the existing ones and
  continuing from the React to-do tutorial: create an `uploads` store, add a Member role and open
  the table's read/write roles to it, add a `File` field (store, folder, MIME allow-list) from
  the field-type picker, rebuild the app for the regenerated typed client
  (`uploadTasksAttachment(id, filename, body)` / `downloadTasksAttachment(id): Promise<Blob>`),
  upload from an `<input type="file">` and link the bytes by URL, and see both access rules bite
  — the table's role and a folder's path-cumulative `min_role`, whichever is stricter. Ends with
  the trip-ups: MIME comes from the extension, apps never choose the path, metadata operations
  never delete bytes.

Documentation drift is now tested: `repo_hygiene` (`crates/sc-cli/tests/repo_hygiene.rs`) gains
`documentation_links_resolve` — every markdown link to a `.md` file, in the top-level documents
and everything under `docs/`, must resolve to a file that exists — and
`tutorials_are_cross_linked`, asserting the React tutorial points forward to the file-fields
tutorial (a "What next" section added for it) and the file-fields tutorial links back. The link
check immediately caught a real break: `docs/TODO-mvp.md` still carried root-relative
`./docs/…` links from before it was archived into `docs/`, now fixed. Otherwise no code changed
in this phase; each earlier phase's CHANGELOG entry (already written as it landed) is the
per-phase record §5 asked for.

## Application-facing file access (§4)

An application can now serve and accept the bytes behind a `File` field — the first caller that
makes the path-cumulative `min_role` enforcement do observable work outside the admin, where role 1
clears every rule.

- **Addressing is by table, row id and field name**, never by raw store path: the REST projection
  gains `GET {mount}/{table}/{id}/{field}` (download) and
  `POST {mount}/{table}/{id}/{field}/{filename}` (upload) per `File` field, on tables with a
  single-column primary key. The row is what the caller is allowed to see; letting an app name an
  arbitrary path would have made the field's folder restriction advisory. The filename is a single
  path parameter, so a nested or traversing destination cannot even be expressed in the URL.
- **Downloads run at the stricter of two rules, never the looser.** The table's `min_role_read` is
  the endpoint's `MinRole` auth (as for row reads); on top, at request time, the file's own
  path-cumulative `min_role` is enforced by the same `sc_files::check_access` the admin file
  manager uses, seeded with the store's floor from its `_sc_file_stores` definition. Both checks
  must pass, so whichever is stricter decides. Bytes are served under the extension-derived MIME
  type — the same derivation the field's allow-list is enforced with.
- **Uploads run at the table's `min_role_write`, into the field's store and folder, with the MIME
  restrictions applied server-side** (`validate_file_path`; the declared `Content-Type` is not the
  control — a client-side accept filter is a convenience). The path-cumulative rule governs the
  write too. The row must exist before the bytes land (a failed update would orphan a file), and
  the row is then pointed at the stored path through the same `update_row` a JSON write takes, so
  the field's rules are enforced twice, harmlessly.
- **The endpoint model learned about bytes.** `Endpoint` gains `binary_input`/`binary_output`,
  `ApiRequest` a `raw` bytes body (set by the transport when the content type is not JSON),
  `ApiResponse` a boxed `RawBody` (bytes + content type) the server serves as-is. The generated
  TypeScript client types a download as `Promise<Blob>` (`res.blob()`) and an upload's body as
  `BodyInit`, sent unencoded with no JSON content type — no named Request/Response types for
  binary bodies, since `Blob`/`BodyInit` are the platform's own names for them. `rows` gains
  `read_field` (one cell by primary key), which resolves a `File` field's stored path.
- **Field changes re-project mounted apps live.** A mounted app's REST projection now depends on
  its tables' *fields* (a `File` field contributes endpoints), so `createField`/`updateField` call
  `AppMounts::refresh_table` exactly as the table-settings handlers do — an admin adds a `File`
  field and the running app grows its download/upload endpoints with no restart.

Tests: 4 new unit tests in `sc-api` (the projection's file endpoints and their auth mapping, no
file endpoints on keyless tables, the binary client shapes) and the Phase 4 integration test
`crates/sc-server/tests/app_file_access.rs` — three roles against a mounted app: an editor's
upload lands in the field's folder and updates the row; a reader's upload is refused at the write
rule; a disallowed MIME type is a 400 naming the field and type with nothing written; a `..`
filename is refused; and the same file is then served to one caller and refused to another twice
over, with the **table's** rule decisive against a logged-in visitor and the **directory's**
`min_role` decisive against a reader the table admits. Also gave
`crates/sc-catalog/tests/field_meta_merge.rs` the standard test-file clippy allowances it was
missing, so `cargo clippy --all-targets -D warnings` passes again.

## File fields do real work (§3.5)

The milestone's second claim becomes observable: a column configured as a `File` field validates
what is written to it, and a store a field points at cannot be deleted out from under it.

- **Write-path validation.** A row written to a `File` field (`rows::validate_file_write`, run in
  `create_row`/`update_row`) must resolve to a **connected** store — an unresolvable store is a 400
  naming the store and the field, since a reference into a store that is not there points at
  nothing — and its path must satisfy the field's folder and MIME rules. The store-independent half
  of that check is `sc_files::validate_file_path`: the path must be store-relative (no absolute
  path, no `..`), live under the field's folder when one is set, and carry an allowed MIME type
  (inferred from the extension via the new `mime_for_path`, backed by `mime_guess`). Null/empty is
  absence, left to the column's nullability. Errors name the field, because they reach an
  application's user, not only the admin.
- **The catalog-level store-delete check does real work.** `file_store_field_references` was
  written, correct, and inert; §3.2's merge made the catalog's fields carry `File` kinds, so it now
  finds them and `delete_file_store` refuses to delete a store a field points at, naming the field.
  No new code — the reference scan already ran; the overlay finally gives it something to find.
- **The tripwire flipped.** `file_field_references_are_inert_until_the_fields_overlay_exists` is
  rewritten into its successor, `a_file_field_reference_is_caught_by_the_catalog_delete_check`: the
  same setup, but the column is made a `File` field by an overlay row and the test now asserts the
  reference **is** found and the delete refused, then that forgetting the overlay frees the store.
  The old assertion is deleted, not weakened.
- **Files are never touched by metadata changes.** Deleting a `File` field's overlay and dropping
  its table both leave the bytes on disk exactly where they were — the same rule a store delete
  follows, for the same reason (a store is a connection to data that exists independently of
  Saltcorn).

`sc-files` gains a `reference` module (`mime_for_path`, `validate_file_path`) and the `mime_guess`
dependency; `sc-api` gains an `sc-files` dependency for it. Tests: 4 unit in `sc-files`
(MIME-from-extension, unsafe paths, folder confinement, MIME allow-list), the rewritten tripwire,
and 4 integration in `crates/sc-api/tests/file_field_write.rs` against real Postgres and a real
local store — a valid write accepted; a path outside the folder and a disallowed MIME each refused
naming the field with only the valid row written; a reference into an unresolvable store refused
naming it; a store delete refused while a field points at it and freed once the field is forgotten;
and a field-delete and a table-drop each leaving a real file in place.

## Admin SPA: the field editor (§3.4)

`TableDetail.tsx`'s "add field" form becomes type-aware, driven by the `listFieldTypes` endpoint
(§3.3). The `SQL type` free-text box is replaced by a **pick-list** grouped into basic types, rich
types and the Key/File kinds — one list assembled from the server's registry. Choosing a type
renders **its** declared attribute form beneath, using the same shared `SettingsFields` spec-form a
file-store backend's settings use, so there is **no per-type code** in the screen: a rich type or a
kind added to the server gets a working form with no change here. The `File` store is a
server-resolved pick-list of connected stores (its `config_spec` carries the resolved options);
folder and MIME restrictions are the plain inputs the spec declares. A Key also asks for a "stored
as" SQL type, since a key's column must match the field it references.

- The shared `buildConfig` (in `settings.tsx`) now coerces a `json`-typed setting by parsing its
  text as JSON, so a rich type's `options` or a File kind's `mime_allow` reaches the server as the
  array it declares — additive, and the one place the spec-form vocabulary is interpreted.
- The fields table gains **Type** and **Kind** columns (the Kind cell naming a `File` field's store
  and a `Key`'s target), so a mis-pointed field is visible without opening it.
- The row editor renders a `File` field as a **file picker** over the field's store — a path box
  plus a "Choose…" modal that browses the store via the existing `browseFiles` endpoint — rather
  than a free-text path, so every value is a path that exists in the store the field is bound to.

SPA-only: no endpoint or client changes. The editor typechecks (`tsc --noEmit`, the gate the
`admin_spa_typecheck` test enforces) and the production `vite build` succeeds. Editing an existing
field's overlay through `updateField` (wired in §3.3) is not surfaced in this screen yet; the §3.4
scope is the add form, the richer table, and the row-editor file picker.

## Admin API: creating and editing fields (§3.3)

The field endpoints grow from "add a plain column" to the full overlay-aware surface, so an admin
can give a field a rich type, a kind and attributes from the API.

- **`createField`** no longer takes `sql_type`/`nullable`. It takes a `type` (a basic-type or
  registered-rich-type name — the SQL type is *derived* from it, so the two can never disagree),
  an optional `kind` with its parameters, `attributes`, `label`, `description`, `required` and
  `unique`. An unknown type name is refused by name. Creating a field is now **two writes**: the
  DDL column first, then the overlay row — and if the overlay write fails, the error says the
  column exists as a plain column so the half-made state is legible rather than mysterious. The
  overlay row is written only when the field carries something the column alone does not record (a
  rich type, a non-plain kind, a label/description, attributes).
- **`updateField`** (`PUT api/tables/{table}/fields/{field}`) is new: overlay-only edits — label,
  description, rich type, kind parameters, attributes — reusing the field's existing overlay row.
  It states the whole overlay (as a table's settings do), so the SPA sends the full set each time.
  Renaming or retyping the *column* stays out of scope (a schema change).
- **`listFields`** returns the merged field: `type`, `kind` (with parameters), `attributes`,
  `label` and `description` alongside the introspected `sql_type`/`nullable`/`unique`. It degrades
  gracefully on a database with no `_sc_fields` table (no overlay → the field as introspection
  produced it).
- **`listFieldTypes`** (`GET api/field-types`) is new: the basic types, the registered rich types
  (with their attribute `config_spec`), and the `Key`/`File` kinds (with their parameter spec, the
  `File` store resolved to a pick-list of connected stores) — one list the editor's type picker is
  assembled from, the same `FormField`-spec contract `listFrameworks` has. `file_kind_config_spec`
  / `key_kind_config_spec` live in `sc-catalog` beside the storage, so the spec's field names match
  the keys the overlay folds a kind's parameters into.

The generated admin TS client is regenerated, and `TableDetail.tsx`'s create-field call is mapped
to the new `type`/`required` shape (the type picker and attribute form are §3.4). Integration
tests (`crates/sc-server/tests/field_api.rs`, 3, real Postgres): create a rich `String` field and
a `File` field and read both back merged, edit the `String` field's label/attributes in place, see
`listFieldTypes` offer basic/rich/kinds with their specs, and an unknown type refused by name with
no column left behind. The existing `admin_api` field-create call moves to the new shape.

## Merging the `_sc_fields` overlay onto introspection (§3.2)

The field overlay now reaches the fields the rest of the system sees. `Catalog::reload`, after the
`_sc_tables` merge, loads every `_sc_fields` row and applies it to the matching column via the new
`Table::apply_field_overlay`; `bootstrap_field_meta` is wired into `connect_catalog` beside the
table overlay (deferred from §3.1, as `_sc_tables`'s boot wiring was deferred to §1.2). A field
nobody configured comes out byte-for-byte as `Table::from_physical` produced it — the zero-setup
promise, now asserted at the field level by comparing a configured catalog's fields to a bare one.

The merge follows §1.2's precedence rule one level down: the database is the authority on what it
knows (a column's SQL type, its nullability, whether a foreign key stands behind it), the overlay
on what it knows (label, rich type, a kind's extra parameters, attributes). Where they would
contradict, **the merge reports rather than resolves** — it never downgrades a column silently and
never fails, because a hand-edited database or a restored dump produces exactly these
contradictions and the table must stay usable:

- A **rich type** applies only if its `sql_types()` includes the column's actual SQL type. `text`
  configured as `Integer` leaves the column its basic type and records the mismatch; an
  unregistered type name (a plugin's, perhaps) is recorded too.
- A **`Key`** overlay's target is the database's whenever the database enforces one: on a column
  introspection already made a key from a foreign key, the overlay may add only its `summary_field`
  and can never repoint the reference. On a column with *no* foreign key, the overlay supplies the
  whole reference — the case the database cannot enforce, such as a key onto a field in another
  database (a future capability), where the overlay is the only place the target can come from.
- A **`File`** overlay applies to any column (its storage is `text`, a reference the database does
  not model), and `label`/`attributes` are applied even when a type or kind issue is also reported.

Unclean merges surface through a new reporting channel mirroring `file_store_error`: the catalog
holds the last reload's `Vec<FieldMergeIssue>` (table, field, message), rebuilt every reload and
read by `Catalog::field_overlay_issues()`. A dangling row — its column dropped, or its whole table
gone — is kept and reported here, as an orphan table overlay is (§1.1). `DataFieldKind::Key`'s
overlay target is intentionally discarded in favour of the introspected one.

Tests: 8 unit on `apply_field_overlay` (a fitting rich type + attributes apply; a non-fitting or
unregistered type is reported and the column stays basic; `File` applies anywhere; a `Key` on a
plain column supplies the whole reference while a `Key` atop a real FK adds only the summary field
and keeps the DB target; a missing column and a system table are handled) and 5 integration against
real Postgres (`crates/sc-catalog/tests/field_meta_merge.rs`) — an unconfigured field compared to
`from_physical` in full, a rich type and a `File` field surviving an unrelated reload, a
type/column mismatch reported with the table still serving and cleared when the overlay is
forgotten, a dropped column reported, and a system-table row inserted behind the API ignored.

## The `_sc_fields` overlay: model & persistence (§3.1)

The field-level twin of `_sc_tables` lands (`crates/sc-catalog/src/field_meta.rs`): a `FieldMeta`
value type and `_sc_fields` table storing what an admin configures *on top of* an introspected
column — a rich type by name, a label/description, a field kind (`Plain`/`Key`/`File`), and sparse
attributes — with `save`/`load`/`list`/`delete` and the same strict reads as `_sc_tables` (a
missing or ill-typed column, or an unknown `kind`, is an `Invalid` naming the field, never a silent
default). Not yet wired into the boot path or merged onto the catalog's fields — that is §3.2, as
`_sc_tables`'s merge was §1.2.

Three shape decisions, documented on the module:

- **The natural key is composite, and it is the primary key.** A field name is unique only within
  its table, so one-overlay-row-per-field is `PRIMARY KEY (table_name, name)` — a real
  database-level guarantee (the authority under concurrent saves), using the composite-PK the DDL
  already supports rather than a composite `UNIQUE` it does not. §9 requires an `id` (UUID) column
  but not that it be the key, so `id` stays as a required, unique row handle that `save`/`load`/
  `delete` address by, while the pair does the uniqueness work.
- **A field's kind is a `kind` discriminant plus its parameters in `attributes`.** A `File`'s
  store/folder/MIME-list and a `Key`'s target are folded into the sparse `attributes` bag under
  reserved keys, not a column apiece — §9's "a sparse value goes into `attributes`" applied so more
  kinds never grow the schema. On read the kind's parameter keys are lifted back out into the
  structured `DataFieldKind` and whatever remains is the field's own rich-type attributes; those
  key names are reserved within a field's attributes, harmless since a `File`/`Key` field carries a
  basic type with no rich attributes to collide.
- **The rich type is a name, not validated here.** The `type` column holds a rich type's name or
  NULL and is left inert — a type may arrive later from a plugin, and §3.2's merge is where an
  unknown one is reported. Introspection still never *produces* a rich type (§2.1).

`DataFieldKind` gained `Eq` (all its variants are identifiers and strings), which is what lets
`FieldMeta` derive `Eq` for the round-trip assertions; the enclosing `DataField` stays `PartialEq`
only, as its `BaseField` carries an `Attrs` bag.

Tests: 9 unit (schema shape, the composite key, the full column set, `File`/plain round-trips
through the attributes bag, an unknown kind and a missing `store` each named) and 9 integration
against real Postgres (`crates/sc-catalog/tests/field_meta_store.rs`) — a `File` overlay
round-tripping store/folder/MIME by id and by `(table, field)`, a rich type and its attributes
surviving, an in-place update, a second overlay for one field refused, an ill-typed `kind`
inserted behind the API refused by name on read, a system-table field and a nameless overlay
refused, an overlay outliving a dropped column, ordered/scoped listing, and a delete that leaves
the column in place.

## Rich-type validation on the write path (§2.3)

A row write now validates each value against its field's full type **and** the field's attributes,
not just the value family. Until now `rows::column_value` — the coercion every API surface's row
write funnels through (`create_row`/`update_row`, and so an application's REST POST) — took a
field's `as_basic()` and converted JSON to a `Value`, with no attribute check and an outright
error for a rich field. It now:

- coerces the JSON through the column's **storage** type (the SQL type a rich type sits on — a
  `String` over `text`, an `Integer` over `int8`), then
- validates the value against the field's `TypeRef` and its `Attrs` via the new
  `TypeRef::validate_with` (§2.1), so a `String`'s `max_length`/`options`/`regex` and an
  `Integer`'s `min`/`max` are enforced at the write.

Both a coercion failure and a rich-rule violation are reported as an `Invalid` error — which the
router maps to a **400**, not a 422 — **naming the field**: "`age`: must be at most 120, got 999".
This message is shown to a user of an application, not only the admin, so it has to say which input
to fix; `field_error` preserves the `Invalid` kind while prefixing the field name (a plain
`.context()` would have inherited an Application kind and become a 422). Basic fields are unchanged
in practice — the added `validate_with` only re-checks the value family JSON coercion already
guaranteed.

**Test** (`crates/sc-api/tests/rich_type_write_path.rs`, 3, against real Postgres): a `people`
table given rich fields by hand — `name` a `String` (≤ 5 letters, alphabetic `regex`), `age` an
`Integer` (0–120) — writes a satisfying row, and rejects a too-long name, a non-alphabetic name,
an over-max and under-min age, and a non-numeric age, each an `Invalid` (400) naming the field,
with the offending rows absent from the table afterwards. The test drives `rows::create_row`
directly — the seam an application POST funnels into — because a rich field cannot yet be
*configured* through the admin API; §3's overlay closes that loop over literal HTTP.

## The initial rich types: String and Integer (§2.2)

The registry from §2.1 was empty; this fills it with two types, small on purpose — between them
they prove what the machinery must do. They live in `crates/sc-types/src/rich_types.rs` and
`builtin_rich_types` hands them to the registry, so `registered_rich_types()` now lists them and
`RichTypeRef::resolve("string")` succeeds.

- **`String`** over `text` — a `max_length`, an optional `options` list, and an optional `regex`
  pattern. Proves attributes drive validation several ways: a value longer than `max_length` is
  rejected (counted in characters, so a multi-byte character is one); a non-empty `options` array
  restricts the value to that set (turning the field into a select, which §3.4's editor will
  render); and a `regex` pattern must match the **whole** value — the pattern is anchored
  (`^(?:…)$`), so a validation rule of `\d{3}` rejects `"abc123"` rather than passing it. An empty
  `options` list or empty `regex` restricts nothing; a pattern that will not compile is the
  admin's mistake, surfaced by name.
- **`Integer`** over `int8` — `min`/`max`, independent. Proves numeric attribute validation; the
  error names the bound and the actual value.

Both treat `null` as a field-level nullability concern (accepted) and reject a value of the wrong
family — a `String` given an `Int`, an `Integer` given text — naming the kind.

The `regex` attribute is matched by **`regex-lite`**, added to the workspace: a field-validation
pattern needs correct matching, not the full `regex` crate's SIMD/perf dependency tree, so the
lighter crate with the same `Regex::new`/`is_match` API is the right weight for layer 3.

**`File` is not a rich type.** §2.2 resolved it as a field *kind* (`DataFieldKind::File`), not a
type: it is a reference like `Key`, not a value family, and its storage type is `text`. The
admin-facing type picker (§3.4) will merge kinds and types into one list because that is how an
admin thinks, but the model keeps them apart.

Tests (4 in `rich_types.rs`): value-family/`max_length`/`options` for `String`, a dedicated
`regex` test (full-match anchoring, empty pattern, a user-anchored pattern, and a bad pattern
reported by name), family/range for `Integer`, and each type's `sql_type()` round-tripping through
a `RichTypeRef` and a `TypeRef::Rich` so it agrees with the column's DDL type at §3.2's merge. The
§2.1 registry test now asserts both are listed and that an unknown name is still reported by
naming them.

## Rich types: the trait and the registry (§2.1)

The type system gains a second kind of type. Until now every column resolved to a `BasicType` —
a scalar family with no attributes and only "value of the right family" validation. A `RichType`
(§6.1) is one Saltcorn understands: it declares typed attributes as `FormField`s (a `max_length`,
a set of select options), validates a value against *both* the value and those attributes, and
names the SQL types it can sit on. `sc-types` now defines the `RichType` trait — `fieldviews()`
deferred, since those are React components (§6.3) and out of this milestone's scope.

Rich types live in a **registry** keyed by name, the same "settings as data" move as
`registered_backends` (file-store backends) and `registered_frameworks`: `registered_rich_types`,
`rich_type`, `rich_type_config_spec`. The point is that the admin UI (§3.3's coming
`listFieldTypes`) can render a field's attribute form for a type it knows nothing about, including
one arriving later from a plugin. This section builds the mechanism; the initial types (`String`,
`Integer`, `Email`) are §2.2, which fills in `builtin_rich_types` — until then the registry is
deliberately empty, and asking it for a type names what is registered rather than inventing one.

`TypeRef` grew a `Rich(RichTypeRef)` variant beside `Basic`. A `RichTypeRef` is a rich type's
name already resolved against the registry (via `RichTypeRef::resolve`), so `TypeRef::sql_type`,
`name` and validation delegate to the type without a lookup each time; identity is the name, which
is what lets `TypeRef` keep deriving `PartialEq`/`Eq` over a trait object. `TypeRef::validate_with`
is the new form that carries a field's attributes beside the value — the write path switches to it
in §2.3; the old `validate(value)` stays for basic-only callers and validates a rich value against
no attributes.

**A rich type is never guessed from the database.** `TypeRef::from_sql_type` still always returns
a basic type: a column is rich only because the `_sc_fields` overlay (§3.2) says so. Inferring
"this `text` column is an `Email`" from the schema is exactly the surprising magic v2 avoids, so
the mapping runs one way only — from an overlay's stored type name to a `RichTypeRef`.

Tests (7 in `crates/sc-types/src/rich.rs`): a test rich type exercises the trait (attributes drive
validation, non-text rejected, null allowed), `find` resolves by name and misses cleanly,
`RichTypeRef` delegates and its identity is its name, the empty builtin registry reports unknown
types by naming what is registered, and every registered type declares at least one SQL type (the
invariant `RichTypeRef::sql_type` relies on).

## A table's access rules are enforced on an application, and a change reaches a mounted app live

The rules were storable, merged and settable; this is the phase where they do observable work.
Nothing new is *enforced* — `RestProvider::project` has mapped `min_role_read`/`min_role_write`
onto its endpoints' auth since the MVP — but until now every table was `1`/`1`, so the mapping
had nothing to distinguish. With the overlay behind it, an application's REST API now serves a
`min_role_read: 80` table to role 80 and refuses it to role 100, with reads and writes governed
separately.

**The seam this phase exists to close** is invisible until it bites. A provider encodes a
table's access rules, and it is built from the catalog **at mount time**. So a mounted
application goes on enforcing the rules the table had when it was mounted — an admin who tightens
a role watches it save (the catalog reloads, §1.2) and watches the running app change *nothing*
until the next restart. `AppMounts::refresh_table` re-projects the providers of every mounted app
that exposes the changed table, live, and `updateTable`/`deleteTableSettings` call it. The
framework — the built bundle — is untouched: an access change alters who may reach an app's data,
not a byte it serves, so no bundler runs and this is cheap enough for every settings save. It is
the table-side counterpart to the live app remount an edit already does (§13.2, "the mount
registry is live").

**The admin's own row endpoints stay admin-only, and that is not an oversight** — the endpoint
docs now say so. A table's `min_role_read`/`min_role_write` are its *application-facing* access;
the admin API's `listRows`/`createRow`/… are the admin's own view of the same rows, reached only
by role 1 through the SPA. A table opened to role 80 for its application is still admin-only in
the admin UI. Two different surfaces onto one set of rows.

Two end-to-end tests (`crates/sc-server/tests/table_access_enforcement.rs`) drive the whole loop
at three roles through the real router — configuring on the base domain, observing on the app's
subdomain, exactly as a deployment is shaped. A default table is admin-only to roles 80 and 100;
an admin opens reads to 80 and the **already-mounted** app serves role 80 with no restart, no
rebuild and no fresh login, while writes stay admin's; opening writes to role 40 then lets role
40 write while role 80, less privileged, still cannot; and forgetting the settings re-closes the
running app, because a delete that only took hold on the next restart would leave data readable
that the admin just re-secured.

## Roles are rows in `_sc_roles`, and `users.role` is a foreign key onto them

A role was a bare integer with a convention attached: `1` meant admin because a constant said
so, `100` meant public, and the ninety-eight between meant whatever an installation's users made
them mean. `listRoles` (from the previous entry) even inferred the set from which numbers users
happened to hold. That stops working the moment a role has to *carry* something — and it does:
role-specific settings are coming, and a pick-list wants a name, not a number. A row can carry a
name and settings; an integer cannot.

So `_sc_roles` now holds them — id, `role` number (unique, on the 1–100 scale), `name`,
`description`, and `attributes` for whatever role-specific settings arrive, needing no schema
change to do so — and **`users.role` is a real foreign key onto it**. That is the part that had
to be a database constraint rather than a convention: a user whose role names nothing is a user
whose privileges cannot be described, and under concurrency the database is the only thing that
can rule that state out. `_sc_roles` is **not an overlay** (§9): a role does not exist without
its row, exactly as an application or a file store does not, so the table is the authoritative
list rather than an addition to introspection.

**The foreign key is emitted, not just modelled.** `DataFieldKind::Key` has described foreign
keys since the MVP, and introspection *derived* `Key` from one, but `to_column_def` created a
plain column — so a `Key` field round-tripped to `Plain` the moment the catalog reloaded,
claiming a relationship the database never had. `ColumnDef` gained a `references` clause,
`Key` fields now render `REFERENCES "table" ("column")` (naming the target column explicitly,
because the goals require keys onto non-primary-key columns and the implicit form always means
the PK), and `users.role` is the first field to use it. Two DDL tests and the introspection
round-trip pin it down.

**Bootstrap seeds exactly two roles** — admin and public — and no plausible-looking middle. A
seeded "staff" or "editor" nobody uses is a role every admin has to read, understand and decide
to delete. Roles are created **before** the users table, because a foreign key onto a table that
does not exist is not a constraint any database accepts; `bootstrap` does them in that order. A
database that predates this keeps its unconstrained `role` column — per GOALS, early development
evolves the initial setup rather than retrofitting migrations.

Both built-ins are undeletable (without admin nobody can administer anything; without public an
anonymous request has no role to be), and a role any user still holds cannot be deleted either —
the answer names the holder count rather than surfacing the foreign key, and nothing cascades a
user to some other role. `create_user` now checks the role exists first, turning a raw
constraint violation into "role 40 does not exist; create it before assigning users to it".

**The admin API and SPA gained roles as a thing you manage.** `GET api/roles` reports the rows
(with a `builtin` flag so the UI declines to offer a delete it would only be refused for);
`POST api/roles` and `DELETE api/roles/{role}` create and remove them. A new Roles screen lists,
adds and deletes; the Users screen's role field became a pick-list over the roles that exist,
because a user can no longer be given a role that does not; and the table settings' role selects
render each role's name. The `listRoles` shape changed from `{ role, label }` to
`{ role, name, description, builtin }` — it reports rows now, not a synthesised list.

Five integration tests in `sc-auth` (`crates/sc-auth/tests/roles_store.rs`): bootstrap seeds
exactly the two built-ins and does not overwrite a renamed one, a role round-trips including its
attributes, a user cannot hold a role that does not exist, a held role cannot be deleted, and
the table is hidden. The admin-API role flow (create → assign a user → refuse to delete the
built-ins) is covered over HTTP.

## An admin can set a table's access rules

The rules have been storable since `_sc_tables` landed and merged since `Catalog::reload` read
it; this is the phase where an admin can actually set them. `listTables` now carries `label`,
`description`, `min_role_read`, `min_role_write` and `configured`; `PUT api/tables/{table}`
sets them; `DELETE api/tables/{table}/settings` forgets them; and the table editor grew a
settings card above the fields.

The roles are in the *list*, not only the detail screen, because "which of these can the public
read?" is a question about the whole set and should not take eight screens to answer.
`configured` is the overlay's presence rather than its content: a table an admin deliberately
set to admin-only and one nobody has ever opened both report `1`/`1`, and only the first has
settings to forget. Without that flag the UI could not offer "forget these settings" honestly.

**Two endpoints that were not in the plan, both earning their place.** `deleteTableSettings` is
what "forget what I configured" means — it returns the table to the closed default, immediately,
and never touches the table or its rows. `listOrphanTableSettings` is the visible half of an
earlier decision: settings whose table has been dropped are *kept*, so a restore gets its rules
back, and a row nobody can see is indistinguishable from a leak. Both are addressed by table
name and neither requires the table to exist — an orphan has no table to open, so name is the
only handle there is.

**Roles are a query, not a constant, and not the settings-spec machinery.** The plan said the
role pick-list should come through `OptionsSource::ServerQuery`. It does not. That machinery
exists so the admin UI can render a form for settings it knows *nothing* about — a file-store
backend's, a framework's — and a table's four settings are not that case; routing them through
a spec resolver would mean building a spec-driven renderer for a static form. `GET api/roles`
answers instead, with the two ends of the 1–100 scale that mean something on their own (1 =
Admin, 100 = Public) plus every role the installation's users actually hold. All hundred numbers
would be a dropdown nobody can read; only the two named ones would make the middle of the scale
unreachable. The list grows by itself as an admin creates users, with no roles table to keep in
step, and when roles gain names of their own this endpoint is where that lands with no caller
changing.

The SPA merges a table's *current* role into the choices even when the server did not list it
(`ui/admin/src/roles.ts`). A table configured for role 40 after the last role-40 user was
deleted must still show 40 — a select that silently snapped to the nearest listed role would
change who can reach the data on the next save, and would do it without saying so.

Four integration tests over the real HTTP surface (`crates/sc-server/tests/table_settings_api.rs`):
configure → list → edit → forget, with the second save asserted to *edit* rather than duplicate
(a second row for one table is refused by the storage layer, so an editor that forgot the row id
would fail on every edit after the first); an off-scale role refused with nothing written and a
404 leaving no stray row behind; settings outliving a `DROP TABLE`, still listed, still waiting
when the table returns, and cleanable by name; and the role list growing when a user of role 40
is created.

Nothing yet *enforces* these rules on an application's data — the REST provider reads
`table.access` at mount time, and making an access change take effect on a mounted app is §1.4.

## The overlay reaches the table: stored access rules are the ones the catalog serves

`_sc_tables` had rows and no readers. `Catalog::reload` now applies them: it introspects as
before, then merges each overlay row onto the table it names. `Table` gains `label`,
`description` and `overlay` (the row's id, `None` when nobody has configured the table) beside
the `access` it always had.

**The precedence rule, now stated in one place** (`Table::apply_overlay`): the database is the
authority on everything it knows — columns, types, nullability, keys — and the overlay is the
authority on everything it knows — access, label, description, attributes. *The two sets do not
intersect*, so there is no contested value and no conflict semantics to get wrong. That is a
constraint on what may ever be added to `_sc_tables`, not a description of what it holds today.

**A table nobody has configured must come out exactly as it did before this existed**, or the
zero-setup promise is gone. The merge loop can only modify entries the introspection loop
already created, and the test asserts an unconfigured table equals `Table::from_physical` in
full rather than spot-checking a field. Three ways an overlay could reach somewhere it should
not, each closed and tested: a row for a table that is not there merges onto nothing (it does
not conjure a table, and it is still waiting when the table returns); a row for a `_sc_*` table
is ignored even though `save_table_meta` already refuses to write one, because a restored dump
or a hand-edited database can contain what the API would not; and a database with no
`_sc_tables` table at all is never queried for one.

That last case is not defensiveness — it is required. `bootstrap_table_meta` creates the table
*through* `create_table`, which reloads, so the reload runs at least once on a database where
`_sc_tables` genuinely does not exist. A reload that assumed the table were there could never
bootstrap it.

**Saving or deleting an overlay reloads the cache**, as a schema change does. Access rules are
read from the cached `Table` on the request path; without the reload an admin would set a role,
watch it save, and watch the running server go on enforcing the old one — the stored row and
the served behaviour disagreeing until something else happened to reload. Deleting reverts the
table to `AccessRules::default()` (admin-only), immediately, not to the previous value.

`connect_catalog` now bootstraps `_sc_tables` beside `users`, `_sc_applications` and
`_sc_file_stores`. Lazily creating it on first save would mean the merge silently does nothing
on exactly the databases nobody has configured yet — which is all of them, until they are.

Six integration tests (`crates/sc-catalog/tests/table_meta_merge.rs`) and three unit tests on
the merge itself. Nothing yet *sets* an overlay through the API; that is §1.3.

## The first overlay: a table's access rules can be stored

`AccessRules` has been modelled since the MVP, is read by the application REST provider on
every request, and has been `{ read: 1, write: 1 }` for every table that ever existed —
because `Table::from_physical` had nowhere else to read it from. Every app-facing table was
therefore admin-only, which makes an application serving anyone but its admin impossible to
configure. `_sc_tables` is where the other value comes from.

This is the first **overlay** Saltcorn stores, and the word is the whole design constraint. A
file store or an application *is* its row — delete the row, the object is gone. A table is not:
it exists in the database whether or not a row describes it, and it must stay exactly as usable
with no row as it was before this table existed. So `TableMeta` carries only what introspection
cannot yield — access rules, label, description, sparse attributes — and deliberately nothing
else. A `nullable` column in `_sc_tables` would break the precedence rule the merge (§1.2) is
about to rely on: *the database is the authority on everything it knows, the overlay on
everything it knows, and the two sets do not intersect.* A merge with no contested field has no
conflict semantics to get wrong. There is a unit test asserting the column list in full, rather
than spot-checking it, for exactly that reason.

`TableMeta` is a separate type from `Table`, not an extension of it. A `Table` is the merged
view — fields, primary key, source, all from introspection; a `TableMeta` is only the part an
admin configured. Collapsing them would make "no row" inexpressible, which is the state most
tables are in.

Storage follows `_sc_file_stores` column for column (`crates/sc-catalog/src/table_meta.rs`):
UUID row identity separate from the name everything references, `name` `UNIQUE` because the
merge joins on it, sparse values in `attributes`, strict reads. Three places it deliberately
differs, each because the values here decide who may read and write data:

- **Both role columns are `NOT NULL`.** A store's `min_role` is nullable because "no floor" is
  a real state for it; a table's rules are always a pair, and a NULL would be a third state
  ("inherit from what?") with no answer.
- **A role off the 1–100 scale is refused, never clamped** — on save and on read alike.
  Rounding an out-of-range role to the nearest legal one would silently decide who can reach
  the data. A row that fails to parse is an error, not a degraded default.
- **A system (`_sc_*`) table may not have a row at all.** They are hidden from users (§9) and
  their access is not the admin's to widen; refusing on save means the merge never has to
  decide what to do with one.

**An overlay whose table has been dropped is kept, and reported** (`orphan_table_meta`).
Deleting it on sight would be tidier and wrong: a table dropped and recreated — a restore, a
migration run outside Saltcorn — would come back with `AccessRules::default()`, so an
application that served it stops working with nothing anywhere saying why. Keeping the row means
recreating the table restores its configuration, and the admin UI can say "these rows describe
tables that are not here" instead of the admin finding out. Symmetrically, deleting an overlay
removes the configuration and never the table — the same rule a file-store delete follows, and
stronger here, since for an overlay the subject outliving its row is not a caveat but the point.

Eight integration tests against a real Postgres (`crates/sc-catalog/tests/table_meta_store.rs`)
assert each of those as behaviour, not as shape: a round-trip that includes both roles
separately, a second row for one table refused with the first left un-widened, an off-scale role
rejected with nothing written, a system table refused, an overlay surviving `DROP TABLE` and
un-orphaning itself when the table returns, and a delete that leaves the table and its columns
where they were.

## The generated client now satisfies the CSRF contract it was generated from

Reported from the tutorial: the React app's login failed with `403 CSRF token missing or
invalid`.

The server protects state-changing requests with a double-submit cookie — a mutating request
must echo the non-`HttpOnly` `sc_csrf` cookie in `x-csrf-token` — and `login` is a `POST` like
any other. The **admin SPA** satisfied this by wrapping `fetch` in a hand-written transport
(`ui/admin/src/api.ts`); the generated client itself sent no such header, so every other
consumer of it — starting with the scaffolded app, which has no such wrapper — could not
perform a single mutation, beginning with the one that lets it do anything at all.

**A client generated from a server's endpoint set should be able to call that server.** The
generator now emits the check itself: `requestHeaders(method, hasBody)` adds the token on
anything but `GET`/`HEAD`, read from `document.cookie`, guarded so the module still runs where
there is no `document` (a node script, a test) and still overridable through `options.fetch`.

`CSRF_COOKIE`/`CSRF_HEADER` moved down into `sc-api::auth`, where the wire contract is stated,
and `sc-server` re-exports them: the server enforcing the check and the client satisfying it
now read the same two names. The admin SPA's transport keeps its copy only for `uploadFile` —
the one request outside the typed endpoint set, raw bytes being undescribable by a
`TypeSchema`.

**The test runs the client rather than reading it** (`crates/sc-api/tests/generated_client_csrf.rs`):
Node executes the generated TypeScript directly, with `document.cookie` and `fetch` stubbed, so
the assertion is on the headers a real call produced — a mutation carries the decoded token, a
`GET` does not, and a browserless caller still works. A test that grepped the emitted text
would pass just as happily on a client that never runs, which is the failure mode this whole
entry is about.

## An API mounted at `/` no longer swallows the app it belongs to

Reported from the tutorial again: an app built and mounted correctly, and every page of it —
starting with `/` — answered `{"error":"no endpoint for /"}`. The app's REST provider was
mounted at `/`, not `/api`.

That is doing exactly what it was told. A provider's mount claims that path and everything
beneath it, so a mount of `/` claims the whole app; the router hands `GET /` to the API, which
has no endpoint there, and the framework's bundle is never reached. The admin gets a JSON 404
from a request they never thought of as an API request, and nothing anywhere says why. A blank
mount field normalises to `/`, so the same trap can be fallen into by leaving a field empty.

**The rule belongs to the framework, not the router.** An app whose framework serves a UI has
paths to lose; an API-only app does not, and `/` is the right mount for it. So `Framework` now
declares `serves_ui()` — `true` by default, part of the same registry entry as a framework's
label and config spec, so it can be answered from a stored `FrameworkRef` with no built
instance to ask. `validate_api_mounts` refuses a root mount only when that answer is `true`,
and an unregistered framework is assumed to serve a UI (the safe direction: the consequence of
being wrong is an app that cannot be browsed).

- **On save**, beside the framework-config check, so the admin hears it standing in front of
  the form: "mounts its `rest` API at `/`, which claims every path and would leave the `react`
  framework's UI unreachable; mount the API on a sub-path such as `/api`".
- **On build and on mount**, via `app_providers` — the one path both go through — so an app
  stored before this check existed fails loudly at boot (logged and skipped, like any app that
  fails to build) rather than coming up as an app that 404s every request.
- **The mount field is required** in the admin API rather than defaulted, so a blank one is a
  400 naming the field instead of silently becoming `/`.

## A scaffolded app that would not build, and the tools that should have caught it (TODO §2.6)

Reported from an actual run of the tutorial: a scaffolded application failed its build with
eleven TypeScript errors in generated files — `Property 'listTasks' does not exist on type
'ApiClient'`, and similar for `login`, `logout`, `whoami` — none of which named what was
actually wrong.

**The cause: the application enabled no API provider.** With an empty `apis` list its endpoint
set is empty, so the generated client has no methods, while the hooks and the login screen call
them. The admin's mistake was one empty field in the create form; what they were shown was a
wall of type errors in code they never wrote, pointing at three files that were all doing
exactly what they were told.

Three changes, in increasing order of how much they matter:

- **A React app with no API provider is refused up front**, at scaffold *and* at build, in the
  admin's own vocabulary: "enables no API provider … add the `rest` provider mounted at `/api`".
  This framework's whole data layer is generated from the app's endpoints, so an app without one
  cannot work, and it should hear that before npm runs rather than after `tsc`.
- **A new application now defaults to `rest` at `/api`** in the create form, so the state is
  hard to reach at all. It is a row like any other; removing it stays one click.
- **Generation is driven by the endpoint set, not the table list.** Pages, routes and hooks are
  emitted per *exposed* table, and each hook only when the client actually has the method it
  calls. The two halves of the runtime used to agree by coincidence — both re-derived "what can
  this app do?" from the tables, in the same way — and now they agree structurally, because one
  of them is computed from the other's input.

**A second bug, found while testing the first.** An application with **no tables** — created
before its data model, an ordinary order to work in — produced a `hooks.ts` whose shared
`useQuery`/`useMutation` were never called, so `noUnusedLocals` failed with `TS6133`. Both are
now exported, which is also the more honest shape: an app that adds a custom endpoint should
join the same cache rather than build a second one beside it. `generate_client` got the same
treatment — an empty endpoint set now emits a client with no unused helpers instead of three
dead declarations.

Both bugs were the same bug wearing different clothes: something generated from one source of
truth, something else from another, kept in agreement only by two pieces of code that reasoned
alike. That is the shape of every "the generated X calls a Y that does not exist" failure, and
it is the arrangement that was worth fixing rather than the two symptoms.

Verified against the reporter's own database as well as in tests: `saltcorn build-app myapp
--database-url …` now takes their real project through install, type-check and bundle.

**Why no test caught it.** Every test around the scaffold either asserted the generated *text*
or stubbed the toolchain, and the one real `npm install` + `vite build` was behind an opt-in
`SC_TEST_NPM=1` — a gate this changelog defended two entries ago and which was, in hindsight,
the reason the bug shipped. An opt-in test is one nobody runs.

So `crates/sc-app/tests/tutorial_app_build.rs` runs the tutorial for real — table created
through the same catalog calls the admin UI makes, app scaffolded, then `npm install`,
`tsc --noEmit` and `vite build`, asserting that what is served is a *bundled* index rather than
the scaffold's own. It **skips only when `npm` is absent from `PATH`**, so a Rust-only checkout
stays green and everyone else runs it. A second test covers the app shapes the tutorial does not
produce — no tables, a keyless table, date/int columns — which is where the failure lived. A
unit-level guard holds the line where `npm` is unavailable, and it checks the rule rather than
the two names: in any table set, every privately-declared thing in the runtime must be
referenced.

**Backtraces are capped at ten frames.** With `RUST_BACKTRACE` set, every logged error carried
around forty frames of async-runtime machinery below the code anyone was looking for, once per
error — and that volume is what pushes the real message off the screen. `head_frames` truncates
the rendered text (stable Rust does not expose a `Backtrace`'s frames), keeps the innermost ten,
and says how many it dropped so the trace does not merely appear to end. Anything that does not
look like a backtrace passes through byte-identical, so an unrecognised rendering loses nothing.

**New: `saltcorn build-app SUBDOMAIN`.** The admin UI can already build an application; what a
command line adds is the *run* rather than the result — scriptable, pipeable, greppable, and
usable when the app cannot be reached in a browser at all, which is exactly the state a failing
build leaves a deployment in. It prints the installer's and the bundler's output whole rather
than the tail an error message can carry. It **builds without mounting**, so running it against
a live deployment's database cannot disturb what that server is currently serving. Driven in
tests through the real binary.

The lesson worth keeping: the generator's tests asserted what it *wrote*, and what it wrote was
right. The failure was in what a compiler made of it, for a table set no test used. Generated
code needs a test that runs the generator's own toolchain over the shapes real input takes — and
that test has to run by default, or it does not exist.

## The tutorial loses its SSH session (TODO §2.5) — Phase 2 complete

`docs/tutorial-react-todo.md` is rewritten for the React framework. Its old steps 2 and 3 — log
into the server host, `npm create vite`, `npm install`, `git init`, then fill in five paths that
have to agree with each other — collapse into one step with two fields, and the document now
opens by saying what changed: **everything happens in a browser**.

The space that freed went to what an admin now needs instead of shell instructions: a tour of the
generated project, the `src/saltcorn/` boundary (ours, rewritten every build) against everything
else (yours, written once), what the typed hooks give you, the `public` flag on a route and why
its absence is the safe default, and the edit-file → build → view loop through the file manager.

**The generic path is documented separately, and framed as the escape hatch**:
`docs/tutorial-code-framework.md` covers the `code` framework — when to reach for it (Next.js,
SvelteKit, an existing project, a bundler that is not Vite), and honestly what you give up by
doing so: no scaffold, no `npm install`, no hooks, five settings you keep consistent yourself,
and a shell on the host to create the project. The hand-rolled `useEffect` + `refresh()` client
usage that used to be *the* way to write a Saltcorn app lives there now, where it reads as a fair
picture of the difference between the two frameworks. README's pointer names both and says which
is for which.

**Both tutorials now create the file store in the admin UI**, not on the command line. That has
been possible since the file-store work landed in Phase 1, but the tutorials still opened with
`--file-store apps=/srv/apps`, which quietly contradicted the claim above it that everything
happens in a browser — and made the server's start-up line look like it had more required
configuration than it does. It is now `saltcorn serve --base-domain localhost` and a **File
stores → New file store** step, with the flag mentioned once as the scripted-deployment
alternative. Verified end to end against a server started with *no* `--file-store` at all: create
the store (directory created on demand, connected with no restart) → create the app → scaffold →
build → served on its subdomain.

**The tutorial was walked, not written from the code.** Every claim in it was executed against a
running server: bootstrap → create `tasks` with `title`/`done` → create the application (14 files
scaffolded, git repository initialised) → Build (`npm install` + `tsc --noEmit` + `vite build`,
about four seconds) → sign in at `todo.localhost` → create a task through the app's own REST API
→ find it in the admin's table view. Then the schema-change loop: add a `due` column, rebuild,
and `TasksRow` grows `due?: string | null` while `pages/Tasks.tsx` — the admin's file — is left
alone.

§13.3 already describes both frameworks and why there are two; that was written with each phase
rather than after it. **Phase 2 has no open items.**

## Two frameworks, presented as the different things they are (TODO §2.4)

The admin SPA now offers React and `code` as a radio card each — a name, and a sentence saying
who it is for — instead of two equal names in a dropdown. Picking React shows two settings and
picking `code` shows five, which needed no code at all: the spec is the branch.

**The interesting constraint was doing this without the screen knowing which framework is
which.** Presenting React as the path to take and `code` as the escape hatch is editorial, and
the obvious implementation — special-case the name `react` in the SPA — would undo §13.3's
whole arrangement the moment a third framework, or one supplied by a guest language, arrived.
So the editorial content moved to where the ordering already lived: `FrameworkInfo { name,
label, description }` in the registry, surfaced by `listFrameworks`. The picker renders what it
is told, in the order it is told, and a new application starts on whatever is offered first.

**The app row now links into the file manager at the app's source**, because the loop an admin
actually works in is edit-file → build → view. Where the source *is* comes from the server as a
derived `source: { store, path }` on the application JSON — a `code` app states it in five
settings and a `react` app derives it from one, and the screen should know neither. The files
route grew an optional directory: `/files/<store>/<dir>`.

**A scaffold's outcome reuses the build banner.** The scaffold happens on the create form,
which navigates away immediately, so a one-shot `notice.ts` hands the message to the list
screen rather than growing a second banner on a screen about to unmount. To an admin a scaffold
and a build are the same kind of news about the same app, so they get the same banner —
including the case where the application was created but its project was not.

**A bug this uncovered, and it would have been quiet.** The form pre-filled the CSP box with
`default-src: 'self'` and always sent it, so *every* app overrode its framework's default
policy with the bare baseline — §2.2's react CSP was reachable by API and unreachable from the
UI that is supposed to be the only configuration path. The box now starts empty, means "no
opinion", and is omitted from the request when blank.

**Verified live rather than only in tests.** Against a running server: create a React app from
two fields → 14 files scaffolded → Build → `npm install` + `tsc --noEmit` + `vite build` in
about three seconds → the app serving on its subdomain with the full React policy
(`connect-src 'self'`, `img-src 'self' data:`, no `unsafe-*`), deep links resolving to the SPA
entry point, and its REST API refusing an anonymous caller. That is the tutorial's entire path
with the SSH session removed — which is what Phase 2 was for.

## The server writes the app: React scaffolding (TODO §2.3)

Creating a React application now generates its whole project into the file store — on the
first save, from a browser, with no shell. The MVP tutorial's steps 2 and 3 (`ssh` in,
`npm create vite`, `npm install`, `git init`, then fill in five paths) are gone; an admin picks
React, names the project, picks a store.

For a one-table app that is 14 files: `package.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `tsconfig.json`,
`index.html`, `.gitignore`, the entry point, the app shell, `auth.tsx`, a login screen, the
route list, a stylesheet, **one page per table**, and the two-file runtime under
`src/saltcorn/`. The pages and hooks are generated against the app's *actual* columns —
`TasksRow` has `title: string` and `done?: boolean | null` because that is what the table says
— so the app comes up showing real rows instead of a placeholder whose first job is to be
deleted.

**Never overwrites, and says so before writing anything.** Scaffolding into a directory with
anything in it is refused with an error naming the directory. The test puts a file in the way
and asserts it is byte-identical afterwards, because a generator that clobbers is worse than no
generator — the work it destroys is the admin's.

**Scaffolding does not fail the create.** The row is saved and valid before any file is
written, and an occupied directory or an unwritable store is something the admin fixes and
retries. Losing the application they just configured over it would be the wrong trade, so the
create response carries `scaffolded` or `scaffold_error` *alongside* the created app.

**`npm install` is part of the build now**, when `node_modules` is absent. It is carried on the
`BuildSpec` as an `InstallSpec { command, args, marker }` rather than assumed by the build step,
so `run_build` stays a framework-agnostic "run this over that tree" and a `code` app — whose
dependencies are its admin's business, stated in their own build command — is unaffected. The
marker is checked on disk rather than remembered, so a store restored from a backup installs
again instead of building against nothing. A failed install is an Application error carrying
npm's own output (§16), and the install log is surfaced separately in the build banner, because
the first build of a scaffolded app is mostly the install.

**`git init` only when the store is not already a repo.** If it is, the project sits inside it
and a nested repository would leave the source tracked by one nobody expects. A missing or
failing `git` is reported rather than fatal: refusing to keep a project that was written
correctly because version control was unavailable is the wrong trade.

**The generated/yours boundary is now enforced, not just documented.** Only `src/saltcorn/` is
rewritten — on every build, from the app's endpoints — and both files in it carry a DO NOT EDIT
header. A test adds a table to an app, regenerates, and asserts `useNotes()` and `NotesRow`
appeared while the admin's edited `App.tsx` is untouched. That is §2.1's irreversible decision
doing its job: a registry package could not have contained a hook typed from this app's
columns.

**The end-to-end test is real, and opt-in.** `SC_TEST_NPM=1` runs scaffold → `npm install` →
`tsc --noEmit && vite build` → serve against a real Node toolchain (react 19, vite 8,
react-router 7), verified passing locally. Because the project's build script type-checks
before bundling, that run is also what proves the generated hooks compile against the generated
client — a drift between what `sc-api` emits and what the scaffold calls fails there. It is
gated because it needs npm and a network, which the rest of the Rust suite deliberately does
not; the other four tests in that file run unconditionally.

Two smaller notes. `sc-api`'s `op_name` became public: the hooks call `listTasks`/`createTasks`
by name, and recomputing that convention inside the scaffold would have been a second copy of it
free to drift from the endpoints the client is generated with. And the generator itself
(`scaffold/files.rs`) is **pure** — tables and an `EndpointSet` in, file contents out — so the
templates are assertable with no store, no database and no Node, and the store-writing half has
no templates in it.

## The `react` framework: two settings instead of five (TODO §2.2)

`react` is registered alongside `code`. An app picks a **file store** and a **project name**,
and everything the `code` framework has to be told is derived: a project `todo` builds from
`todo/` into `todo/dist` with `npm run build`, and its generated client lands at
`todo/src/saltcorn/client.ts`.

**The measure of the design is how little had to change.** `app_source_from_config` grew a
dispatch on the framework name, and both arms produce the same `AppSource` — stated for `code`,
derived for `react`. Below that function nothing can tell which framework it is building, so
`build_app`, `build_application`, `build_and_mount` and the live mount registry are untouched. A
test pins this by asserting a `react` config and the equivalent hand-written `code` config
resolve to an identical `BuildSpec`. Serving is likewise reused rather than reimplemented: a
built React app is a static bundle with an SPA fallback, which is what `CodeFramework::serve`
already does.

**That reuse did expose one thing.** `CodeFramework::config_spec` hard-coded `code_config_spec()`,
so a mounted `react` app — which is a `CodeFramework` under its own name — would have reported
`code`'s five settings as its own. It now looks the spec up by name, so the instance and the
registry agree for both frameworks.

**The registry list is now ordered, and the order means something**: `react` first because it is
the path an admin should take, `code` second as the generic escape hatch. §2.4 renders that
distinction; this is where it starts, rather than two equal names in a dropdown.

**The project name is checked on save, not at build.** The first cut checked it while resolving
the build, which §1.6's own argument rules out — the admin should hear about it while looking at
the form. It cannot be expressed in the spec (§6.2 states presence, type and membership, not
patterns) and growing that vocabulary for one setting would oblige every guest-language framework
to be understood by it, so the framework checks its own name rule and both validation paths run
it. The payoff is the error an admin actually gets: `setting `project` is "../../etc", which is
not a usable directory name` instead of a build path caught escaping the file store two layers
down.

**A framework now supplies the default CSP** for an app that states none (`framework_default_csp`).
An admin who no longer chooses the bundler should not have to derive the policy that bundler
needs. `react`'s is the strict baseline plus exactly what a Vite bundle requires — `data:` for
images and fonts, since Vite inlines small assets — with `connect-src 'self'` for the app's own
API and `object-src`/`base-uri`/`frame-ancestors` tightened. **No `unsafe-inline` or
`unsafe-eval` anywhere**, which is the §2.1 tooling and styling decisions cashing out rather than
a coincidence, and a test asserts it so a future dependency cannot quietly introduce one. A
stated policy still wins: this is a default, not a fixture.

Conventions live in `sc-app/src/react.rs` as pure functions of the project name with no I/O, so
§2.3's scaffold reads its paths from there instead of restating them. Tested at unit level (spec
shape, derivations, name rule, CSP) and over HTTP (React listed first with its store pre-resolved,
the default policy applied and overridable, a traversal name refused on save). One caveat stated
plainly: the "serves its bundle and resolves deep links" test runs against the derived
directories but with `npm` stubbed by a shell script — a real `npm run build` arrives with §2.3's
scaffold → build → serve integration test, which is the first point there is a project to run it
on.

## The opinions of the `react` framework, decided (TODO §2.1)

Phase 2 opens with a decision item, not code: the `react` framework's whole value is that it stops
asking the admin questions, so the answers have to be settled before the scaffold hard-codes them.
No code changed; the record lives in TODO §2.1 and in a new "Two code frameworks, and why"
subsection of design §13.3, which also stops §13.3 describing "code frameworks" as a single
category now that there will be two entries with very different purposes.

The choices: **Vite + React + TypeScript, no SSR**; **routes as data in one file** via
`react-router` rather than a file-system convention; **the generated client plus generated typed
hooks** (`useRows`/`useRow`/`useCreate`/`useUpdate`/`useDelete`) with no data-fetching dependency;
**authenticated by default**, with a per-route `public` opt-out; **plain CSS**, replaceable, no
framework and no CSS-in-JS. Settings collapse to two — the store and the app's name — with source,
output, build command and client path derived from the name.

**The irreversible one — where the shared runtime lives — went to generated-into-the-project
rather than an npm package**, and not for the reason the question was originally framed around.
Upgradability versus hackability turned out to be the wrong axis: the runtime is *app-shaped*.
Hooks worth having are typed per table, so they are generated from this app's `EndpointSet` just
as `client.ts` already is, and a registry package cannot contain them — it could only ship generic
untyped hooks, which discards the reason to have a hooks layer at all. That also dissolves the
staleness objection to vendoring: `src/saltcorn/**` is not a snapshot but generated output,
refreshed every build, so a server upgrade cannot leave a client runtime pinned behind it. The
cost is stated rather than hidden — it is overwritten, therefore not editable in place; everything
outside it belongs to the admin and is never touched.

Two smaller notes worth keeping. **No SSR is a principled exclusion, not an omission**: §13.3
serves bundled assets, and server rendering would put a Node process in every application's
request path — a different serving model rather than a different setting. And **two of the
decisions were made by the CSP**: Vite's output has no inline script, and CSS-in-JS would have
forced `style-src 'unsafe-inline'` into the default policy of every scaffolded app, which is a
poor thing to hard-code on an admin's behalf.

The through-line, which is the test for the next opinion: each choice is either derived from the
app's own schema, or a dependency not taken. Generated things regenerate and need no version
negotiation with the server; things not depended on cannot drift out of step with it. An opinion
that can only be honoured by a package the admin must keep in step with the server is the wrong
opinion.

## Error messages reach admins whole (Phase 1 loose ends)

Closing out the three items Phase 1 left open — one fix, two decisions.

**The truncation was never about file stores.** §1.5 found that a failed store connection reported
only its outermost context, and fixed that one site. The audit it prompted found the real source:
`error_response` in `sc-server/src/router.rs` sent `err.to_string()` for **every** endpoint error,
so every admin-facing message in the product lost its cause. It could not simply be switched to
the causal chain, because the same function serves the admin API *and* every application's API,
and those have different readers — an app's users are not operators, and the causes below the top
message are internals (SQL, paths, driver text).

So `error_response` now takes an explicit `Audience`: `Admin` gets `err.causes()`, `App` gets the
top-level message, and the log gets the whole chain with source locations either way. Making it an
argument rather than a default means adding a route has to answer the question rather than inherit
a silent choice. `Error::causes()` joins `Error::chain()` as the user-facing counterpart. Verified
against a running server: reading a missing file now reports
`reading "nope.txt" from store docs: No such file or directory (os error 2)` where it previously
stopped after `from store docs`.

**Decided: no `_sc_fields` overlay in this milestone.** The deciding fact is that it would be
necessary but not sufficient — `createField` accepts only `name`/`sql_type`/`nullable`, so a
field's *kind* cannot be declared through the API at all, and storing a `File` kind would still
leave `File` fields unreachable. Making them reachable means field-kind editing in the API and the
table editor, then §6.3's fieldviews to render one: a feature area, not a loose end. The overlay
also overturns the catalog's "no stored metadata beyond `information_schema`" invariant, which is
load-bearing for the zero-setup promise, and brings merge/precedence semantics and cache
invalidation with it. Nothing in the milestone's definition of done needs it. The tripwire test
remains the signal for whoever picks it up.

**Decided: `min_role` enforcement cannot be closed here.** It is implemented, unit-tested and
wired into every file endpoint, but all of those are admin-only, so the caller's role always
clears every rule. Its first real exercise is application-facing file access, which does not exist
yet. Moved to a new "Carried past this milestone" section in the TODO rather than left looking
like unfinished Phase 1 work — along with the overlay and `OptionsSource::ClientCode`, so the
decisions stay visible instead of being lost as ticked boxes.

**Phase 1 is complete**: no open items remain in it.

## `OptionsSource`, and the framework `store` setting becomes a pick-list (TODO §1.6)

The last of Phase 1. Now that file stores are a listable, persisted set, the compromise §13.3
documented at length — "the `store` setting is free text because a static spec cannot express a
runtime list" — is retired.

- **`OptionsSource` in `sc-types`** (§6.2): `None | Static | ServerQuery`. `FormField.options`
  became `options_source`, with `static_options()` and `query()` accessors.
- **A server query is a *name*, not an expression.** The point of declaring settings as data is
  that the data stays inert: the admin UI renders a spec it does not understand, and a spec may
  come from a guest language through `sc-code`. An embedded query language would make a settings
  spec executable — a much larger design, and not a thing to evaluate on a plugin's behalf. So a
  query names a source the server already knows how to answer, and the set of names is the
  server's to define (`sc_catalog::resolve_options`, `QUERY_FILE_STORES`).
- **`ClientCode` is deliberately absent.** It is for options that depend on other values in the
  form — a dependent dropdown — which by definition cannot be pre-resolved and needs the form
  runtime to evaluate per keystroke. Nothing needs it yet, and this module's convention is to
  leave out what has no consumer (the same reason `fieldview` and `visibility` are still missing).
- **The server resolves, so the client needs no evaluator.** This is the open question §1.6 asked
  and the answer that makes the rest cheap: the spec is resolved to a concrete list *before* it
  leaves the server, at both points a spec is used — handing it to the admin UI, and validating a
  config on save. The SPA renders a select purely because `options` is non-empty, with no
  store-specific code in any screen and no query evaluator, so **this did not have to wait for
  `ui/form-runtime` (§12)** and nothing gets built twice.
- **An unknown store is now rejected on save**, where the admin is still looking at the form,
  rather than surfacing later as a bundler failure. The error lists the real stores, so a typo
  shows as a near-miss.

**Two design points the work forced:**

*Validation split in two.* `validate_framework_config(catalog, fw)` resolves queries then
validates (save time); `validate_framework_config_structure(fw)` checks shape only (build time).
Re-asking "does this store exist?" at build would make a build fail for a reason unrelated to
building, when the honest error there is the one the build already gives — the store cannot be
resolved. This falls out of the model rather than being bolted on: an unresolved query has no
static options, and `validate_attrs` only checks membership against options it has.

*The pick-list is defined stores ∪ connected stores, not connected only.* If it were
connected-only, an unmounted disk would block editing every application that uses that store —
including the edits needed to repair it — and an unrelated outage would start rejecting saves of
apps nobody had touched. A defined-but-unreachable store stays a valid choice, the same principle
§1.2 established for saving one.

**A coupling caught and removed.** Resolving options reached for `_sc_file_stores`
unconditionally, which made `listFrameworks` return 500 when that table was absent — an endpoint
failing over a table with no visible relationship to it. `choosable_file_stores` now asks the
catalog whether the table exists (a cache lookup, not a query) and treats its absence as "no
stored definitions", which is what absence means; a genuine database error still propagates rather
than being quietly reported as "no stores exist".

Tests: the framework spec declares a query and an unresolved one restricts nothing (unit);
an unknown store rejected on save with the real stores listed, and a defined-but-unconnected store
still choosable (`app_store.rs`); the pick-list arriving resolved over HTTP
(`file_store_admin_api.rs`). Several existing tests now define the stores their apps name — which
is the change working: an application can no longer be saved pointing at a store that is not there.

## Admin screens for file stores and the file manager (TODO §1.5)

The admin UI half of Phase 1: file stores are now managed entirely in the browser, and the file
manager the MVP built endpoints for — and then shipped no screen for — exists.

- **`screens/FileStores.tsx`** — every store an admin has configured, plus any connected by
  `--file-store`. It exists to make two states visible that had no way of being seen before: a
  store **defined but not connected** (directory unmounted since it was saved) is listed with its
  reason and stays editable, because editing it is the repair; and a store **connected but not
  defined** (from the flag, ephemeral by design) is listed read-only, since its null id means there
  is no row to edit or delete. Offering buttons that would 404 is worse than saying "not editable".
- **`screens/FileStoreForm.tsx`** — pick a backend, get its settings. No backend-specific code, so
  an S3 or git-remote backend added to the registry gets a working form with no change to this
  file. A save that succeeds but leaves the store unconnected shows "Saved, but not connected"
  with the reason and keeps the form open, rather than treating it as failure — that follows §1.2's
  split between "you mis-configured this" and "this is not currently reachable".
- **`src/settings.tsx`** — the `FormField[]` renderer, **factored out** of `ApplicationForm` rather
  than copied, and now used by both. One settings vocabulary (§6.2) deserves one rendering; two
  copies would be two places to drift, and this is the single stand-in that `ui/form-runtime` (§12)
  eventually replaces in one file.
- **`screens/FileManager.tsx`** — breadcrumb navigation, upload, download, new folder, rename,
  delete, and editing a text file in place. That last one is what `readFile`'s UTF-8 `text`
  shortcut was built for in the MVP and nothing had ever called. The permissions dialog shows a
  file's **effective** minimum role beside the rule set on it, because a parent folder can be
  stricter and an admin reading only the entry's own rule would conclude a locked file is
  reachable. It round-trips the free-form attributes untouched, so opening the dialog to change an
  access rule cannot silently drop them.
- **`api.ts` gains `uploadFile`** — the one hand-written call in the SPA, because its route is
  outside the typed `EndpointSet`. It lives beside the other browser concerns rather than inside a
  screen, so the exception is in one obvious place.

**Verified by running the real server**, not only by tests: created a store through the same API
the form posts to, drove mkdir/upload/write/browse/rename/delete/meta, confirmed the uploaded bytes
round-trip byte-identical by sha256, observed all three list states, and walked the repair flow —
editing a broken store's path until it connected, then renaming it and confirming the old name
disconnected.

**A bug that only running it could find.** A store that failed to connect reported its error as
`connecting file store \`gone\`` — the context and nothing else, because `Display` on a context
error renders only the outermost layer and leaves the cause behind `source()`. On a screen whose
entire purpose in that state is to tell the admin what to fix, that message is useless: it does not
say the directory is missing, or which directory. Added **`sc_error::format_causes`**, the whole
causal chain on one line with no code locations — the user-facing counterpart to `format_chain`,
which is multi-line and carries `file:line` because whoever reads a log can also read the source.
Connection failures now record and report through it, so the admin sees
`connecting file store \`gone\`: file store root /definitely/not/here: No such file or directory`.
Anywhere else `e.to_string()` on a context error reaches the UI has the same defect; that is now a
TODO item, since it is invisible until someone reads a message and finds it says nothing.

## File operations and the path-cumulative access rule (TODO §1.4b)

The second half of §1.4: operating on a store's *contents*, and finally enforcing the access rule
`FileMeta` has documented since the MVP.

- **`FileStore` gains `mkdir`, `delete` and `rename`** (it had only `read`/`write`/`list`/
  `get_meta`/`set_meta`), with `LocalFileStore` implementations and `makeDirectory` / `deleteFile` /
  `renameFile` endpoints over them. The semantics are the interesting part: `mkdir` is idempotent
  (the caller wanted a directory there and there is one) but refuses to shadow an existing file;
  `delete` returns whether anything was there, so a caller need not race an existence check against
  the delete, and refuses the store root however it is spelled (`""`, `"/"`, `"."`); `rename`
  refuses to overwrite an existing destination, because a silent replace on a file manager's
  drag-and-drop is a lost file with no undo.
- **`getFileMeta` / `setFileMeta`** make `FileMeta` reachable at last. Both report
  `effective_min_role` — what actually applies, given the store floor and every parent directory —
  alongside `min_role`, what is set on this entry. Reporting only the latter would let an admin
  believe a file is reachable when its folder has locked it.
- **The path-cumulative rule is enforced** (`crates/sc-files/src/access.rs`) on read, browse,
  write, mkdir, delete, rename, meta and upload. Roles are inverted (`1` admin … `100` public), so
  the effective floor is the *most restrictive* rule on the whole path: a nested rule can only ever
  tighten. That direction is the point — if a nested rule could widen access, restricting a folder
  would not restrict it, since anything inside could opt back out. A browse is **filtered** rather
  than refused, because a readable directory may hold entries the caller cannot open and listing
  their names leaks exactly what the rule was set to hide. Access errors name neither the
  restricting component nor whether the target exists, so the endpoint cannot be used to probe for
  files the caller may not see.
- **Binary upload** at `POST /upload/{store}/{*path}`, outside the typed `EndpointSet` because the
  endpoint model is JSON-only (`TypeSchema` is `Value`/`Struct`/`Array`/`Optional`, and
  `HandlerResponse` carries `Json`). It dispatches **through the handler registry** (`uploadFile`)
  rather than owning its own catalog handle — routing around the endpoint set must not also mean
  routing around the catalog or the access model, so there is still one code path for "write a
  file" however the bytes arrived. `HandlerCtx` gained a `raw_body` for this. Bodies are capped at
  256 MB; `writeFile`'s base64 path stays for small files and stays in the generated client.
  The accepted cost: this is the first admin operation absent from the generated TypeScript
  client, so the SPA will hand-write this one call.

**A gap worth stating plainly rather than papering over.** Every file endpoint is
`AuthRequirement::admin()`, so the caller's role is always `1` and an admin clears every rule —
which makes the enforcement **inert through the admin API today**. It is wired in anyway: it is
correct by construction, costs nothing, and starts doing real work the moment a non-admin role can
reach a store. Enforcement living only in some future caller would be the same mistake as the rule
living only in a doc comment, which is precisely what `FileMeta::min_role` was until this change.
The rule itself is tested directly at the `sc-files` level, where non-admin roles can be exercised.

Tests: 8 new unit tests in `sc-files` (mkdir idempotence and the file-conflict refusal, delete
reporting and root refusal, rename creating parents / refusing to clobber / rejecting traversal on
both sides, cumulative access, nested rules only tightening with the store floor composing, and
listings hiding entries) plus 5 integration tests in
`crates/sc-server/tests/file_operations_api.rs` (the operations end to end, traversal rejected on
*every* operation including the new ones, metadata round-tripping with the effective rule, binary
upload landing bytes verbatim, and upload rejecting an unauthenticated caller — the risk that comes
with hand-rolling a route outside dispatch).

## Admin API for file-store configuration (TODO §1.4a)

File stores can now be created, edited and deleted over the admin API, which is everything the
§1.5 file-stores screen needs. §1.4 was split: file operations (`mkdir`/delete/rename), the
`FileMeta` endpoints and path-cumulative `min_role` enforcement are §1.4b, so the access-control
work does not land in the same review as routine CRUD.

- **`createFileStore` / `updateFileStore` / `deleteFileStore`**, addressed by **id** — the row
  identity, which survives a rename — while the file manager stays addressed by **name**, which is
  what the admin picked and what a `File` field or an application references.
- **`listFileStoreBackends`** returns each backend with its `config_spec`, mirroring
  `listFrameworks`, so the create/edit form renders controls for a backend it knows nothing about.
- **`listFileStores`** now reports id, description, backend, config, `min_role`, `connected`,
  `error` and `is_git_repo` rather than just `{name, is_git_repo}`.
- **The two composition steps §1.3 could not perform now happen here**, because this is the only
  layer that knows both the before and after of an edit: a **rename disconnects the old name**
  (otherwise the old handle keeps serving under a name that no longer has a definition), and a
  **delete disconnects after removing the row**. Create and update also connect immediately, so an
  admin finds out now — not at next boot — whether the directory is reachable; a failure to connect
  is reported in the response rather than failing the save, per the §1.2 split.
- **Delete composes both halves of the reference check**: `sc-catalog` scans its own `File` fields,
  and this layer adds `applications_using_file_store`. A store still holding an application's
  source cannot be deleted, and the error names the application.
- `ui/admin/src/client.ts` regenerated.

**A design change the work forced.** `listFileStores` was going to be driven by the stored
definitions. That would have made a `--file-store` store **invisible**: it is connected and
browsable but has no row, so a developer running with the flag would have seen an empty screen and
had no way to reach the store they had just connected — silently undoing the ephemeral-store
workflow §1.3 had just preserved. The listing is now the **union** of defined and connected stores,
and `id` is nullable. A null id is precisely what tells the UI that a store cannot be edited or
deleted, because there is no row behind it.

Tests: 8 integration tests in `crates/sc-server/tests/file_store_admin_api.rs`, driven through the
assembled router with the session and CSRF cookies a browser carries — create/list/connect, an
unreachable store saving and reporting why, a mis-configured store rejected (including a `min_role`
outside the scale, rejected rather than clamped), edit repointing live while a rename disconnects
the old name, delete removing the row *and* stopping service while leaving the bytes alone, an
application blocking a delete, backends listed with their spec, and a flag-connected store listed
with a null id. `file_manager.rs` gained the `_sc_file_stores` bootstrap its setup now needs.

## File stores connect and disconnect live (TODO §1.3)

Stores configured in the admin UI now come up at boot and can be re-pointed or removed while the
server runs. The MVP could only connect a store by restarting the process with a different
`--file-store` flag; that restriction is gone.

- **`Catalog::disconnect_file_store`** — the counterpart to `connect_file_store`, and what a
  deleted or renamed store needs. Without it a definition could be removed while its handle kept
  serving, so the file manager would happily browse a store the admin had just deleted. (Re-pointing
  an existing store never needed it: connecting the same name replaces the handle.)
- **Connection errors are remembered.** The catalog records why a store is not connected
  (`record_file_store_error` / `file_store_error`), cleared whenever it connects or is
  disconnected. A store that failed must not vanish silently — its definition is still there, the
  admin still needs it in the list, and the reason is the only thing telling them what to fix. A
  stale reason would be just as bad, shown as if it were current, hence the clearing.
- **`connect_all_file_stores`** loads every definition and connects each, returning
  `FileStoreConnections { connected, failed }`. **One store that fails does not stop the others or
  the server** — the same rule `mount_all` applies to an application whose build fails (§13.2): a
  single unmounted disk is for the admin to fix in the UI, not a reason for a working server to
  refuse to boot. Only failing to *read the table* is an `Err`, since that means the metadata
  itself is unreachable.
- **Boot path**: `connect_catalog` now bootstraps `_sc_file_stores` alongside `users` and
  `_sc_applications`, and `saltcorn serve` calls `connect_stored_file_stores` (which logs each
  outcome) before the `--file-store` flags.
- **`--file-store` coexistence, resolved**: the flag stays ephemeral and unpersisted — a
  developer's scratch directory, and what the tests use — is applied *after* the stored stores, and
  a name already connected is now a **startup error** rather than a silent override. Refusing is
  the important part: `connect_file_store` replaces on a repeated name, so a clash would let the
  flag silently shadow a store configured in the UI. The admin would edit a store, see it save, and
  watch the server go on serving a different directory with nothing anywhere explaining why. An
  error at boot costs one restart; that costs an afternoon.

**Two composition steps deliberately left to §1.4**, because nothing at this layer can enforce
them: `save_file_store` does not touch the registry, so **renaming** a store must disconnect the
old name (otherwise the old handle keeps serving), and **deleting** must disconnect after removing
the row. The second is proven separate by `disconnecting_stops_a_store_resolving`, which asserts
the row is gone while the handle still browses. Both are now checklist items under §1.4.

Tests: 5 integration tests in `crates/sc-catalog/tests/file_store_live.rs` — stored stores connect
and resolve, one failing store leaves the others up and records why, an edit repoints without a
restart, disconnecting stops resolution while leaving the directory untouched, and a repaired store
loses its stale error — plus `boot_connects_stored_stores_and_refuses_a_flag_that_shadows_one` in
`crates/sc-cli/tests/serve.rs` covering the bootstrap, the boot order and the clash rule.

## File-store backend registry (TODO §1.2)

Backends now declare their settings as data, so the admin UI can render a settings form for a
backend it knows nothing about — including one supplied later by a guest language through
`sc-code` — with no per-backend special case. The same "settings as data" move that makes "the
admin picks a framework" work (§13.3), applied to stores. New `crates/sc-files/src/backend.rs`.

- **Registry**: `registered_backends()` and `backend_config_spec(name)`, mirroring
  `registered_frameworks` / `framework_config_spec`. There is deliberately **no
  `FileStoreBackend` trait** — a backend is a name, a spec and a constructor, all of which the
  registry functions provide, and a trait would need an *instance* to ask for a spec that must be
  available before any instance exists. That is the same reason `code_config_spec` is a free
  function. The MVP registers one backend, `local`; the registry is what makes adding S3 or a git
  remote a change in `sc-files` rather than a change in the admin UI.
- **`local` settings**: `path` (required) and `create` (default false). Creating a directory on the
  server's filesystem is a side effect an admin should opt into — silently creating one turns a
  typo'd path into a new empty store instead of an error.
- **`validate_file_store_config`** is now called from `save_file_store`, exactly as
  `validate_framework_config` is called from `save_application`, and names the store, its backend
  and the offending setting.
- **`connect_from_def`** is the one place a definition becomes a live `Arc<dyn FileStore>`.
  `sc-cli`'s `--file-store NAME=PATH` flag now goes through it instead of constructing a
  `LocalFileStore` itself, so a flag-connected store cannot drift from a stored one. Those
  definitions remain unpersisted — the flag stays an ephemeral, process-lifetime convenience, and
  how it coexists with stored stores is §1.3.

**Two kinds of wrong, deliberately kept apart** — this deviates from what §1.2 originally
specified ("an unreadable path *or* a missing setting" rejected on save), and the deviation is the
interesting part:

- *Structurally wrong* — a missing `path`, a `path` that is a number, an unknown setting, an
  unknown backend. The admin's typo, knowable without touching the filesystem, the same answer on
  every machine. **Blocks the save**, so it is caught while the admin is still looking at the form.
- *Currently unreachable* — a well-formed definition whose directory has been unmounted, renamed,
  or not created yet. Often not the admin's fault, can become true *after* a successful save, and
  can stop being true with no edit. **Reported by `connect_from_def`, never enforced at save.**

Enforcing reachability at save time would make a store whose disk was unmounted *uneditable* — the
admin could not open it to repoint its path, which is the one thing they need to do — and that
contradicts the §1.1 model where a definition is inert data that may describe a store which does
not currently work. So §1.4's save endpoint should connect after saving and return the reachability
result alongside the saved store: immediate feedback, without blocking the save.

Tests: 11 unit tests in `backend.rs` (spec names/labels/required-ness and the `create` default,
registry round-trip, unknown backend naming what is available, missing/ill-typed/unknown settings,
validation not touching the filesystem, connecting, an unreachable path failing to connect without
being a config error, `create` making the directory only when asked, and connect validating first),
plus `a_misconfigured_backend_is_rejected_on_save` in `crates/sc-catalog/tests/file_store_store.rs`,
which also asserts the other half: a well-formed definition pointing at a non-existent directory
*is* saved.

## File stores become persisted, editable objects — model & persistence (TODO §1.1)

First step of making file stores editable in the admin UI. In the MVP a store existed *only* as a
`--file-store NAME=PATH` process argument held in an in-memory catalog registry: nothing to
persist, nothing to edit, and no way to add or re-point one without changing the command line and
restarting. A store now has a definition with a lifecycle, which is the prerequisite for every
later part of Phase 1 (backend registry, live connect/disconnect, admin API, admin screens).

- **`FileStoreDef` (`sc-files`)** — the stored *definition* of a store: name, backend, backend
  config (`Attrs`), optional store-wide `min_role`, description, sparse attributes. Deliberately
  distinct from the connected `Arc<dyn FileStore>` instance it produces: a definition is inert data
  that may describe a store which does not currently work (a path since unmounted), and that is a
  state the admin UI must show and let them fix, not an error that refuses to load. `FileStoreDefId`
  is the row identity (UUID, per §9), separate from the *name* that `FileStoreId` references, so a
  store can be renamed without its row changing identity.
- **`_sc_file_stores` (`sc-catalog`)** — the second stored-metadata table, by the same §9 argument
  that gives `_sc_applications` its existence: `information_schema` knows about tables, not about
  which directories an admin has connected, so there is nothing to introspect a store from and its
  row *is* its definition. Idempotent `bootstrap_file_stores`, a legacy database gaining the table
  on first call with no migration step. `name` is `UNIQUE` — it is the key everything resolves
  through — with a pre-flight check so a clash names the offender rather than surfacing a raw
  constraint violation. Reads are strict: a missing or ill-typed column is an `Error::invalid`
  naming the store and the column, never a silent default (an out-of-range `min_role` is a corrupt
  row, not something to clamp — clamping would quietly change who can reach the store).
- **CRUD**: `save_file_store` (insert or update in place), `load_file_store`,
  `load_file_store_by_name`, `list_file_stores`, `delete_file_store`.
- **Placement**: the value type is in `sc-files`, the persistence in `sc-catalog`. `sc-files` cannot
  hold the persistence — it has no `Catalog` and cannot gain one, since `sc-catalog` already depends
  on it. `sc-catalog` is the lowest crate holding both the `FileStore` trait and a `Catalog`, and it
  already owns the connected-store registry.
- **Deleting removes the row and nothing else.** A store definition is a connection to data that
  exists independently of Saltcorn — very often an admin's own directory, possibly a git working
  tree — so disconnecting it is not consent to destroy it. A delete is refused if anything still
  references the store, with the error naming the referents.
- **The reference check is split across two crates, deliberately.** `sc-catalog` scans its own
  tables for `File` fields (`file_store_field_references`); an *application*'s references live in
  `sc-app`, above it, so `sc_app::applications_using_file_store` collects those and callers pass
  them to `delete_file_store` as `extra_referents`. An application counts as a referent three ways:
  its declared store subset, a static dir, and a framework's `store` setting — a store serving an
  app's source is precisely the one that must not vanish underneath it.

**Gap found and pinned down, not papered over:** `DataFieldKind::File` is modelled (§6.2) but
persisted nowhere. `create_table` reloads the catalog from introspection and `Table::from_physical`
can only derive `Plain` or `Key` (from a foreign key) — a column has no way to say "I am a path in
store `uploads`". Storing that needs the `_sc_fields` overlay, which §9 puts out of MVP scope. So
`file_store_field_references` is correct but **inert** today, and the application-level check is
what actually protects a store. This is documented on the function and asserted by
`file_field_references_are_inert_until_the_fields_overlay_exists`, which is written as a tripwire:
when the overlay lands the assertion flips and the test fails, forcing the delete path to be
revisited rather than quietly staying half-enforced.

Tests: 9 integration tests against a real Postgres (`crates/sc-catalog/tests/file_store_store.rs`)
covering round-trip with every column, update-in-place including clearing `min_role` back to NULL,
duplicate-name rejection, name/backend validation, ordering, delete semantics, and both halves of
the reference check; plus unit tests for the schema (§9 required columns, `name` unique, `min_role`
nullable, columns and values staying positionally in step) and for `FileStoreDef`; and
`applications_are_found_by_every_way_they_reference_a_store` in `crates/sc-app/tests/app_store.rs`.

## Fix: applications table not created at startup (admin UI 500 on load)

Loading the admin UI's Applications screen failed with `relation "_sc_applications" does not exist`
— the query behind `SELECT * FROM _sc_applications` had no table to read. The `_sc_applications`
table has an idempotent `sc_app::bootstrap`, but the CLI boot path never called it: `connect_catalog`
(`crates/sc-cli/src/lib.rs`) bootstrapped only the `users` table. `mount_all` hit the same missing
table at boot but by design logs-and-skips, so the server came up and the failure only surfaced
later, on the first admin request.

- **`connect_catalog` now bootstraps both platform tables** — `sc_auth::bootstrap` (users) and
  `sc_app::bootstrap` (`_sc_applications`), symmetric and idempotent, so every boot ensures the
  tables exist (a legacy database gains them on first `serve`, no migration step). Added a direct
  `sc-app` dependency to `sc-cli` for this.
- The boot smoke test (`crates/sc-cli/tests/serve.rs`) now also asserts the applications table
  exists after `connect_catalog` and that `list_applications` — the exact query that was failing —
  returns an empty list rather than erroring.

## Opt-in backtraces on errors (gated by `RUST_BACKTRACE`)

`Error` now also carries a `std::backtrace::Backtrace`, the companion to the always-on source
location. `Backtrace::capture()` self-gates on `RUST_BACKTRACE`/`RUST_LIB_BACKTRACE`: with the
variable unset it is `Disabled` — no stack walk, and `format_chain` output is byte-for-byte
unchanged — and with it set, the full stack is captured and appended under a `backtrace:` line. So
the location answers "which line?" for free on every error, and a backtrace answers "how did we get
here?" whenever an operator opts in, with no code change. This slots into the plumbing added above:
one field, captured in the existing `Error::at` funnel, rendered in `format_chain`; no external
caller was touched. New accessor `Error::backtrace()`.

## Errors carry their source location (zero-overhead `#[track_caller]`)

Every `Error` now records the source location where it was created — the failing `file:line` — so a
logged error points straight at the code that produced it, without a debugger or a repro. The
location is captured at **compile time** via `#[track_caller]` (a `&'static Location` threaded
through the constructor/`?` call chain), so it costs nothing at runtime and, unlike a captured
`Backtrace`, needs no `RUST_BACKTRACE` and walks no stack — it is always present.

- **`Error` is now a struct** (`crates/sc-error/src/lib.rs`): `{ repr: Repr, location }`. The former
  variants moved to a public `Repr` enum, matched via the new `Error::repr()`; the new
  `Error::location()` returns the capture site. Every constructor (`Error::database`, `invalid`, …),
  both `Context::context`/`with_context` methods, the `bail!` macro, and the `io::Error` conversion
  are `#[track_caller]`, so the location is the *caller's* line (e.g. the `.context(…)` or
  `Error::database(…)` site), not somewhere inside `sc-error`.

- **Location appears in the log, not the client body** (`format_chain`): each `Error` in a rendered
  chain gets a ` (at file:line)` suffix, so `error_response` (and any `eprintln` of the chain) shows
  where each layer originated. `Display` is deliberately unchanged, so the JSON error a client
  receives carries no source paths.

- **Callers updated for the struct**: the few sites that pattern-matched variants now match
  `err.repr()` — `error_status` in `sc-server`, the framework-config remap in `sc-app`, and the
  `matches!` assertions in `sc-files`/`sc-api` tests.

## Error visibility — no silent failures at the HTTP boundary (principle 5)

Every error that reaches an HTTP response is now logged to the console with its full cause chain,
instead of only being encoded into a terse JSON body. Previously a request could fail with a `500`
and `{"error":"database error: query failed: db error"}` while **nothing at all** was printed on
the server, leaving an operator with no message and no stack of causes to act on.

- **`sc_error::format_chain` / `Error::chain`** (`crates/sc-error/src/lib.rs`): walk an error's full
  `source()` chain into one multi-line string (top message, then each `caused by:` cause). The
  `Display` of an `Error` shows only its outermost message; the real cause lives in `source()`, so
  anything logging at a boundary needs this to emit everything it knows.

- **Postgres driver errors keep their real message and the failing SQL** (`crates/sc-db-postgres/src/{exec,introspect}.rs`):
  `tokio_postgres::Error` renders as the near-useless string `"db error"` — the actual SQLSTATE and
  server message are only in its source chain. The three driver call sites now format the error with
  `format_chain`, so a failed query surfaces the true cause (e.g. `relation "..." does not exist`)
  rather than `db error`. Each error also now carries the **offending SQL statement** on its own
  `sql:` line, so the log shows which statement broke. The bind *values* are deliberately not
  included — only their count (`(N bind parameter(s))`) — because they may hold secrets (passwords,
  tokens) and this message is also returned to the client. This is exactly what was hidden when
  creating a new application failed.

- **Server logs every boundary error** (`crates/sc-server/src/router.rs`): a new `error_response`
  helper logs the error (with its cause chain) before mapping it to a JSON response — used by every
  handler error arm. System errors (`500`) are labelled `internal error`; others `request error`.
  The previously-discarded `Err(_)` session-lookup and login-failure paths now log via `log_failure`
  instead of failing silently. Logging goes to stderr with the existing `saltcorn:` prefix, matching
  the rest of the server's diagnostics.

## Phase 10 — admin SPA: the Applications screen (design §12/§13.2/§13.3)

The admin UI can now manage applications end to end, closing the "created in the admin UI" loop
GOALS asks for: an operator lists, creates, configures, builds, edits and deletes applications
without touching Rust or a config file.

- **Applications screen** (`ui/admin/src/screens/Applications.tsx`): a list of every application
  with its subdomain (a live link to `<subdomain>.<the admin's host>`, so it opens the running
  app), framework, and a per-app build state. Each row has Edit, Build and Delete. Because the
  server persists no build status, a freshly loaded or freshly created app shows **"Not built
  yet"** rather than pretending a save deployed it; a build flips it to Built (or Build failed)
  for the session, and the build's outcome — the bundler's log on success, its **diagnostics on
  failure** — is surfaced in a dismissible banner.

- **Create/edit form** (`ui/admin/src/screens/ApplicationForm.tsx`): the screen that proves *no
  screen knows a specific framework's settings*. The admin picks a framework from `listFrameworks`
  and the form renders whatever that framework's `config_spec` declares (label, required, type,
  options, default) as a plain form — there is no `code`-framework-specific code in the UI. It
  also edits the subdomain, the table and file-store subsets, the enabled APIs (provider + mount,
  as add/remove rows), the static directories, and the CSP (one `directive: sources` line each).
  Save posts to `createApplication`/`updateApplication`; a rejection (duplicate subdomain, invalid
  framework config) shows the server's own message.

- **Richer client errors, so diagnostics reach the UI** (`crates/sc-api/src/typescript.rs`): the
  generated client discarded the response body on a failed request, throwing only
  `"<op> failed: <status>"`. It now builds the error through a shared `clientError` helper that
  reads the server's `{ error }` body and appends it, so a caller can surface, e.g., a failed
  build's bundler diagnostics. `ui/admin/src/api.ts` gains `errorMessage(err, fallback)` to
  recover that message (and `errorStatus`'s regex no longer anchors on the status, since a message
  may follow). The committed `ui/admin/src/client.ts` was regenerated.

- **Routing/nav** (`ui/admin/src/App.tsx`): an "Applications" nav item and hash routes for the
  list, `/applications/new`, and `/applications/:id/edit`.

- **Verification.** The `admin_spa_type_checks` test now compiles the new screens against the
  generated client contract, and `npm run build` bundles the SPA; the `sc-api` client-sync and
  TypeScript type-check tests confirm the generator change is consistent for both the admin and
  code-framework clients.

## Phase 10 — admin API for applications: CRUD, build+mount, framework specs, typed error kinds (design §13.1/§13.2/§16)

The admin UI's contract for managing applications now exists as typed endpoints, so an
application can be created, configured, built, edited and deleted entirely over HTTP — the
configuration path GOALS asks for ("applications are created in the admin UI"). This is the
server/API half; the admin SPA screens that consume it are the next subphase.

- **Application endpoints** (`crates/sc-api/src/admin.rs`, `crates/sc-server/src/handlers.rs`):
  `listApplications`, `createApplication`, `updateApplication`, `deleteApplication`, and
  `buildApplication`. Create/update persist the `_sc_applications` row (an app can be
  saved-but-unbuilt, a normal state); `build` resolves the build step from the app's framework
  config, runs the bundler and mounts the result **live** into the same [`AppMounts`] the router
  resolves against, so a freshly built app serves on its subdomain with no restart; delete
  removes the row and unmounts it, so its subdomain stops resolving. The id is a UUID path
  parameter and is never taken from the body — the row's identity is not something a payload
  reassigns.

- **`listFrameworks`** returns each registered framework with its `config_spec` rendered as
  data (`{ name, label, type, required, default, options }` per setting), so the create/edit
  form can render controls for a framework it knows nothing about (§13.3). Backed by a new
  `sc_app::registered_frameworks()`, the single place that enumerates them.

- **Build errors are Application errors, not System errors (§16).** `sc-error` gains
  `ErrorKind { Application, System }` and `Error::kind()`, the classification §16 says lands with
  `sc-error` from the start: `Invalid`/`Config`/`Query`/`NotFound`/`Auth` are Application,
  `Database`/`File`/`Serde`/`Internal` are System, and a `Context` inherits the kind of the
  `Error` it wraps. The router's `error_status` now maps an unhandled Application error to a
  client-fixable **422** and a System error to **500**, so a failed build comes back as a 422
  carrying the bundler's own diagnostics rather than a generic 500 — the admin sees what to fix.

- **`admin_handlers` now takes the live `AppMounts` handle** in addition to the catalog, since
  the build/delete handlers mount and unmount. `main.rs` passes the same handle it gives the
  router; the admin-only tests pass an `AppMounts::new(catalog)`.

- **Tests.** A new `crates/sc-server/tests/admin_applications_api.rs` drives the whole story over
  HTTP against a real Postgres: list frameworks → create → build (serves on its subdomain
  without a restart) → edit + rebuild → a failing build returns 422 with the bundler diagnostics
  while the old bundle keeps serving → delete (subdomain stops resolving, second delete 404s). A
  second test asserts every application endpoint rejects anonymous and non-admin callers.
  `sc-error` gains unit tests for the kind split; the committed `ui/admin/src/client.ts` was
  regenerated from the new endpoint set.

## Phase 10 — live mounting: applications mount, rebuild and unmount with no restart (design §13.2)

The configuration path finally reaches all the way from the database to a running app. Before
this, an app was only servable by embedding `sc-server` in a bespoke binary and handing a frozen
`AppMounts` to `build_router_with_apps`; `saltcorn serve` mounted the admin only, so
`--base-domain` did nothing. Now the mount registry is **live**: the CLI loads every
`_sc_applications` row at boot and builds+mounts each, and an app can be built, rebuilt or
unmounted against a running server — "a full restart should never be required" (GOALS).

- **`AppMounts` is live shared state** (`crates/sc-server/src/apps.rs`). Its `by_subdomain` map
  moved behind an `RwLock<HashMap<String, Arc<MountedApp>>>` and its mutators take `&self`:
  `mount` (insert, refusing a subdomain collision), `remount` (insert-or-replace, for an edit or
  rebuild), and `unmount`. `get` hands back an `Arc<MountedApp>` the caller holds after the read
  lock is released, so a mount/unmount never blocks an in-flight request and a request already
  dispatched against a since-replaced app keeps serving the version it resolved. The lock
  recovers from poisoning rather than propagating a panic — a torn write leaves at worst a stale
  entry the next mount overwrites, which must not take the whole registry down.

- **`build_and_mount` + `mount_all`** (same module). `build_and_mount(apps, app)` is the runtime
  create/edit path: resolve the build step from the app's framework config, run the bundler, and
  `remount` the result. Because the build runs to completion *before* anything is mounted, a
  **failed rebuild leaves the previously mounted version serving** and returns the bundler's own
  diagnostics (§16) — an `Err` never disturbs what is already up. `mount_all(apps)` is the boot
  path: it lists every stored app and builds+mounts each, logging and **skipping** any that fails
  to build so one broken app cannot stop the server or the others.

- **`build_router_with_apps` takes `Arc<AppMounts>`** (`crates/sc-server/src/router.rs`), a
  shared live handle rather than a value frozen at build time; the router resolves each request
  against the registry's *current* contents, so an app mounted after the router was assembled
  serves immediately. `resolve_app` returns the owned `Arc` for the reasons above.

- **`saltcorn serve` honours `--base-domain` for real** (`crates/sc-cli/src/main.rs`,
  `crates/sc-server/src/serve.rs`). `serve` now takes the live `AppMounts` handle and the CLI
  builds it from the catalog, `mount_all`s every stored app when a base domain is set (mounting
  is gated on it — without one no request could address an app), and keeps a clone for later
  runtime mounts. The README's "no effect yet" caveat is gone.

- **Tests.** A new `crates/sc-server/tests/live_mounting.rs` drives the whole lifecycle against a
  real Postgres through a router built **once**: an app is created (build+mount) and appears on
  its subdomain with no rebuild; an edit re-mounts the new bundle; a failing rebuild is reported
  with the bundler's diagnostics while the old bundle keeps serving; an unmount makes the
  subdomain stop resolving. A second test proves boot mounts every stored app and a failing one
  is skipped rather than fatal, then mounts the fixed app live. The existing `app_serving`
  tests were updated to the `&self`/`Arc<AppMounts>` API.

## Phase 10 — a framework's settings become data: `config_spec`, save-time validation, config→build (design §13.3)

The framework half of "configuration as data" now works end to end: a framework declares its
settings, an app's config is checked against them on save, and the build step is resolved from
that config rather than hand-built in Rust. This closes the loop the previous entry opened —
`FormField` had no consumer; now it has its first.

- **`Framework::config_spec() -> Vec<FormField>`** (`crates/sc-app/src/framework.rs`), with
  `CodeFramework` declaring `code_config_spec`: `store`, `source`, `output`, `command`, and an
  optional `client` — §13.3's "which file store, which subdirectory, which build command". It
  is a **free function** as well as a trait method, because the admin UI and the save-time check
  need a framework's settings *before* there is an instance to ask (a `CodeFramework` only
  exists once its bundle is built, and the point is to configure it before that); the trait
  method delegates, so the two cannot drift. `framework_config_spec(name)` is the registry
  lookup — an unknown framework is a config error, mirroring how an unknown API provider is.

- **`validate_framework_config` runs on save.** `save_application` now validates the primary
  framework's config — and every extra framework's — against its spec before writing, so a
  missing or ill-typed setting is an `Invalid` error naming the framework and the setting, at
  the point the admin can fix it. The same mistake found at build time is a bundler error and
  at serve time is a broken app. The error message is rebuilt rather than nested, because
  `Error::Invalid` renders its own "invalid:" prefix (double-wrapping said it twice) and
  `Error::Context` shows only the context, which would hide the setting name — the part that
  matters.

- **`app_source_from_config`** (`crates/sc-app/src/build.rs`) resolves an `AppSource` and its
  `BuildSpec` *from* a stored app's framework config — the join between "an app is a row the
  admin filled in" and "an app is a thing that builds". Before this the store,
  sub-directories and build command were hand-built at the call site, which is exactly the
  configuration path §13.2 rules out. The `command` setting is split on whitespace into the
  executable and its arguments (the bundler is spawned directly, not through a shell, so this
  is a split, not shell parsing — no quoting, globbing or substitution).

- **Validation lives in `sc-types`**: `FormField::validate` / `validate_attrs`, backed by a new
  `BasicType::accepts_json`. The JSON check is stricter than the `Value` one and deliberately
  so: JSON natively has bools, numbers and strings, so a `Bool` setting wants `true` not
  `"true"`; the families JSON cannot represent (uuid, date, decimal, …) travel as strings and
  are checked by parsing them exactly the way `catchall::parse` parses a form input. `null` is
  "no value" for every type, so requiredness is a separate check. `validate_attrs` also rejects
  an `attrs` key the spec does not describe — a setting the admin believes they set but which
  does nothing is worse than an error.

- **On the `store` setting, an admitted limitation.** `config_spec` takes no `&Catalog`, so a
  framework's settings are static — and the `store` setting *should* be a pick-list of the
  connected file stores, which a static spec cannot express. For the MVP it is free text,
  validated as text and resolved against the catalog when the build runs (an unknown store
  fails the build, not the save). Threading a `&Catalog` into every framework's `config_spec`
  to fix this one setting is out of proportion; §6.2's `OptionsSource::ServerQuery` is the
  post-MVP answer. Written into §13.3 rather than left implicit.

- **The Phase 9 tests now resolve their build settings from config.** `build_app`, `app_client`
  and the server's `app_serving` state the store/sub-directories/command only in a
  `FrameworkRef` and call `app_source_from_config`; no test hand-builds a `BuildSpec` any more
  (the one remaining literal is the catalog-free `run_build` unit test, which takes a spec by
  design). New tests: the resolver (valid config → build spec, each missing/ill-typed/unknown
  setting → an `Invalid` naming it, the command split, the source-dir default), the framework
  spec (the settings §13.3 names, instance-vs-free-function agreement, unknown framework), the
  JSON type check, and an integration test that a misconfigured framework is rejected on save
  and never becomes a row.

## Phase 10 — settings are `FormField`s; `BaseField`/`Attrs` move to `sc-types` (design §6.1/§6.2/§13.3)

The vocabulary every configurable extension point declares its settings in. Nothing consumes it
yet — `Framework::config_spec()` and validation-on-save are the next items — but it is the type
they are both defined in terms of.

- **There is no `AttrSpec`. Settings are declared as `FormField`s** (`crates/sc-types/src/field.rs`).
  The design had `AttrSpec` as a separate settings vocabulary, and building it made the overlap
  with §6.2's `FormField` obvious: both are "name, label, type, required, and how to pick a
  value", because both answer *what should the admin be asked?*. §6.2 already says a form field
  "may derive from a `DataField` **or be standalone**" — a setting is exactly the standalone
  case. So a `Framework` (§13.3), `Action` (§10.1), `Agent` (§11.1), `ModelProvider` (§14.2),
  `FieldView` (§6.3) and a `RichType`'s attributes (§6.1) all declare `Vec<FormField>`. One
  vocabulary, one form for the admin UI to render, one type a guest-language extension must
  know about — instead of two that would declare `name`/`label`/`type_` twice and drift once.
  **Design §6.2/§6.1/§10.1/§11.1/§13.3/§14.2 updated** accordingly.

- **`FormField`**: `base: BaseField`, `required`, `default`, `options`, with chaining setters and
  a `resolve` that reads a supplied value or falls back to the default. Two deliberate
  departures from §6.2's sketch: it gains a `default` (a settings form needs one, and a
  `DataField`'s DDL default projects onto it), and it carries a plain `options: Vec<Json>` where
  §6.2 has an `OptionsSource` of *static | server query | client code* — only the static case is
  reachable with no form runtime, so the enum would have one meaningful variant. `fieldview` and
  `visibility` are left out entirely: they name types that do not exist (fieldviews and formulas
  are post-MVP), and inventing them with no consumer would fix a contract nothing can test.

- **A `FormField` describes JSON, not a Rust value.** `default` and `options` are
  `serde_json::Value`, because the values it describes as settings live in an `Attrs`, are
  stored in a JSON column and reach the admin UI as JSON — plain JSON is what ends up in the
  bag, whereas `Value` serialises tagged (`{"type":"text","value":"web"}`), which is not a shape
  to hand a form renderer. `type_` stays a `TypeRef`, so a control is chosen for a setting
  exactly as for a column and rich types slot in later without a change here.

- **`Attrs` and `BaseField` moved from `sc-catalog` down to `sc-types`**, both re-exported from
  `sc-catalog`. `sc-types` is layer 3 and cannot depend on layer 4, but `FormField` carries a
  `BaseField` and describes `Attrs` entries. `BaseField` never needed layer 4 in the first
  place — it touches only `TypeRef` and `Attrs`. What did, and what stays in `sc-catalog`, is
  `DataField`: its `Key`/`File` kinds reference catalog identifiers and it bridges to
  `Column`/`ColumnDef`. The move is invisible — `sc_catalog::{Attrs, BaseField}` still resolve
  and **no call site changed** — and a test pins that the re-exports are genuinely the same
  types rather than look-alikes. `Attrs` was also `sc-catalog`'s only use of `serde_json`, so
  that dependency went with it.

- **On the self-reference**: `BaseField.attributes` is an `Attrs`, and what may go in it is
  described by that type's `attributes()` — a `Vec<FormField>`. So a `FormField` both carries a
  `BaseField` and describes what another field's attributes may hold. This is the same
  self-description a JSON Schema has and it is well-founded: the recursion bottoms out at basic
  types, which have no attributes. Written down in §6.2 rather than left to be rediscovered.

## Phase 10 — applications are stored rows (`_sc_applications`; design §9/§13.2)

The storage half of Phase 10: an application is now something the database holds, not only
something a Rust caller constructs. This is the groundwork for creating apps in the admin UI —
nothing mounts from the table yet (that is the live-mounting item), and `saltcorn serve` still
mounts the admin only.

- **`_sc_applications` table + `sc_app::bootstrap`** (`crates/sc-app/src/applications.rs`),
  built like `sc_auth::bootstrap`: idempotent, driver-agnostic (it goes through the `Catalog`
  rather than issuing DDL), and inventing no database-side defaults. It carries the §9
  required columns — UUID `id`, `name`, `description`, `attributes` — plus a column each for
  the values every app has: `subdomain`, `framework`, `extra_frameworks`, `tables`,
  `file_stores`, `apis`, `static_dirs`, `csp`. Only `subdomain` is a scalar the database can
  index; the rest are structured lists and maps and are stored as JSON, which is not a
  compromise but the shape they are read in — a whole app or nothing, with no query filtering
  on them. This is the **one** stored-metadata table the MVP adds: the `_sc_tables`/`_sc_fields`
  overlays stay out of scope, because a table exists without a metadata row and an application
  does not.

- **`subdomain` is `UNIQUE` in the database.** Routing dispatches on the subdomain, so two apps
  claiming one is not a state the server can serve. `AppMounts` cannot be the authority on that:
  it is in-memory and cannot see another transaction, so two admins saving concurrently would
  both pass an in-memory check. `save_application` still checks first, so the ordinary case is
  an `Invalid` error naming the app that already holds the subdomain rather than a raw
  constraint violation — the constraint is the backstop, not the message.

- **`Application` gained `description`, `attributes` and `static_dirs: Vec<StaticDir>`**, and
  **`AppId` is now a UUID** rather than a slug (§9's rule for stored metadata: it is a row now).
  Its identity is therefore its row and its routing key is its `subdomain`, so the two are no
  longer the same string wearing two hats. `Application::new` dropped its `id` argument and
  mints one; `with_id` reconstructs an app that already has one. The new `StaticDir`
  (`mount`, `store`, `path`) is the "any number of statically-served subdirectories" half of the
  admin's configuration surface, deliberately separate from the framework's bundle.

- **`FrameworkRef.config` is now `Attrs` (a JSON object)** rather than `BTreeMap<String, String>`,
  so it can hold what a framework's `config_spec()` describes (§13.3) — bools, numbers and lists,
  not just strings — and so the admin UI can post its rendered form back as JSON.
  `FrameworkRef::with` takes any `Into<serde_json::Value>`; `config_str` reads a setting back as
  a string, and returns `None` for a non-string rather than stringifying it.

- **Load/save/delete** (`crates/sc-app/src/store.rs`): `save_application` (insert or update in
  place, keyed on the `AppId`), `load_application`, `load_application_by_subdomain` (the lookup
  routing needs), `list_applications` (what the server will mount at boot, ordered by subdomain)
  and `delete_application`. Reading is strict — a missing or ill-shaped column is an `Invalid`
  error naming the app and the column, not a silently defaulted field, because an app *is* its
  row and a half-understood row is a misconfiguration the admin needs told about. Saving is not
  building or mounting: an app can be saved and unbuilt, which is what a newly created app is
  until its first build.

- **Tests**: `crates/sc-app/tests/app_store.rs` (5 integration tests against a real database) —
  an app with every column populated round-trips identically (including non-string framework
  config, attributes and a non-default CSP); an edit updates in place rather than adding a row,
  and keeping its own subdomain is not a clash with itself; a duplicate subdomain is rejected
  with an error naming both the subdomain and the incumbent, is not written, and is *also*
  rejected by the database when a raw insert goes around the pre-check; apps list and delete,
  freeing the subdomain for another app; and a legacy database with no `_sc_applications`
  bootstraps cleanly, idempotently, and without disturbing its introspected tables. Plus unit
  tests for the schema's §9 columns and the JSON-typed framework config.

## Planning — applications belong in the admin UI (design §9/§12/§13.2/§13.3/§17, TODO Phase 10)

Planning and design only; no code changed, so nothing here is testable yet — the tests are
specified as Phase 10 items.

- **GOALS now says applications are created in the admin UI**, which the design did not say
  and the code does not do: Phase 9 made an application *servable*, but the only way to
  define one is to embed `sc-server` in a bespoke Rust binary and hand
  `build_router_with_apps` an `Application` value. That is why `--base-domain` currently does
  nothing on `saltcorn serve`. **Design §13.2 rewritten** around the configuration path from
  database to mounted app: an application is created and configured in the admin UI — not in
  Rust, not by a CLI flag — stored in `_sc_applications`, and built and mounted **without a
  process restart** (GOALS: "full restart should never be required"). Editing an app
  re-mounts that app alone; a failed rebuild leaves the previously mounted version serving.
  Saving configuration and building it are distinct operations with distinct outcomes, so a
  saved-but-unbuilt app is a normal state the UI shows rather than a save that silently
  deployed nothing.

- **`_sc_applications` is not an overlay, and that is why the MVP needs it** (§9, §17). "No
  stored metadata beyond information_schema" was always a statement about the
  `_sc_tables`/`_sc_fields` *overlays*: a table exists in the database whether or not a
  metadata row describes it, so the overlays can be deferred while tables still work. An
  application has no underlying reality to introspect — its row is its only definition — so
  it cannot be deferred the same way. The distinction is now written down in §9 rather than
  left as a thing to rediscover, and §17's MVP scope carries the one deliberate exception.

- **`Framework::config_spec() -> Vec<AttrSpec>` (§13.3).** GOALS requires each framework to
  have its own settings (a React app needs the file store or subdirectory holding its code; a
  v1 app needs none of that), and the admin UI has to render a form for whichever framework
  the admin picked without a per-framework special case — including frameworks supplied by a
  guest language. So a framework declares its settings as data, exactly as `Action`, `Agent`
  and `ModelProvider` already do in the design, and the config is validated on save rather
  than at build or serve time. **`AttrSpec` does not exist in the code yet** — the design has
  leaned on it since §6.1 without a consumer, and Phase 10 is the first; flagged as its own
  TODO item in `sc-types` rather than left to surprise the implementer, along with the
  layering snag it walks into (`Attrs` is currently an alias in `sc-catalog`, which `sc-types`
  sits below and cannot depend on). `ui/form-runtime` is out of MVP scope, so the MVP renders
  the same `AttrSpec` data with a plain form.

- **`Application` gains what GOALS asks for**: `static_dirs` ("any number of subdirectories
  that are served statically" — previously modelled nowhere), plus the `description` and
  `attributes` §9 requires of a stored entity. Two consequences for existing code, both now
  TODO items: `AppId` becomes a UUID (it is a stored row now, and §9 mandates a UUID `id`),
  leaving `subdomain` as the unique routing key; and `FrameworkRef.config` becomes `Attrs`
  rather than a string map, so it can hold what a `config_spec` describes.

- **TODO: new Phase 10 "Applications in the admin UI"; the old Phase 10 is now Phase 11.**
  Broken into storage, framework-settings-as-data, live mounting, admin API, and admin SPA,
  each with its own tests — including that creating an app over HTTP serves it on its
  subdomain with no restart, and that a failing build keeps the old bundle up. Phase 11 gains
  a tutorial item, deliberately sequenced *after* Phase 10 so it can be written admin-UI-first
  with no Rust in it, and its manual pass now goes through creating the app in the admin UI.

## MVP Phase 9 — Applications & framework (`sc-app`)

- **Serving applications from the Saltcorn process (design §13.2/§13.3).**
  `sc-server` can now serve applications, each on **its own subdomain**. A
  `MountedApp` is the triple that makes an app servable — its `Application`
  record, the `Framework` serving its UI, and the `ApiProvider`s it declares
  (built via `sc_app::app_providers`, so a mounted app's API is exactly the one
  its record declares and its generated client is typed against). `AppMounts` is
  the registry, and `build_router_with_apps` assembles a router over it;
  `build_router` is unchanged and mounts nothing, so the admin-only deployment is
  untouched.

  Routing is by `Host`: `blog.<base_domain>` reaches the app whose subdomain is
  `blog`. Anything else — the base domain, an unknown subdomain, a deeper name,
  or a host that merely *ends* with the base domain's text (`blog.notexample.com`)
  — is the admin, so mounting an app can never take the admin away from an
  operator. `--base-domain` is required for this and app routing is **opt-in**:
  without a base domain to anchor it, a request's own `Host` header would get to
  choose its app. Mounting an app with no base domain set, or two apps claiming
  one subdomain, fails at boot rather than serving something unreachable or
  arbitrary.

  Within an app, its API providers win over its framework for the paths they
  claim (longest mount first), so `/api/*` is its data and everything else is its
  UI. A provider's response is shaped exactly like a handler's, so it goes out
  through the same `apply_response`: an app's `login` sets its session cookie the
  very same way the admin's does, which is the transport half of the API-auth
  item. Each app stamps **its own `CspPolicy`** (§13.2) on its responses; the
  server's CSP layer became `if_not_present` rather than `overriding` so it can,
  while everything that sets no policy — the whole admin surface — still gets the
  strict default (still asserted by the existing header test).

  **The app never touches the database**: its framework serves static bundled
  bytes and ignores the catalog it is handed, and every byte of data it shows
  arrives through a provider that enforces the declared table subset and the §7
  layer.

- **Phase 9 acceptance test (`sc-server/tests/app_serving.rs`).** The whole story
  end-to-end over HTTP against a real Postgres: an app is built from a git-repo
  file store by an actual bundler invocation, mounted, and then — through the
  assembled router, with the cookies a browser would carry — **the build's assets
  are served** (index.html at `/`, its JS, the emitted client, and the SPA
  fallback for a deep link), an **unauthorized request is rejected** (`401`
  anonymous), the **API auth round-trip** works (bad password `401` → `login`
  `200` + session cookie → the same request now succeeds → `whoami` → full CRUD →
  `logout` ends it), and a logged-in public-role user is still `403` on an
  admin-only table — authenticating is not authorization. The rejected write is
  confirmed to have inserted nothing.

  Worth noting for anyone writing an app: the CSRF double-submit means a client
  must hold the `sc_csrf` cookie before it may mutate anything, and serving the
  SPA is what hands it over — so an app's first request has to be a page load, as
  a browser's is. The test does this explicitly rather than papering over it.

  **Known gap:** `--base-domain` has no effect on `saltcorn serve` yet, because
  nothing loads application definitions from `_sc_applications` — apps are mounted
  programmatically today. The routing and serving work and are tested; the
  configuration path from database to mounted app is missing. Flagged in the
  README rather than left to be discovered.

- **API auth: an app authenticates against `sc-auth`, authz honored (design
  §7.2/§13.4).** `RestProvider` now projects the app's **own** `login` / `logout`
  / `whoami`, so a React app authenticates through its own API rather than
  borrowing the admin's (which is admin-gated and on another subdomain). `login`
  is the one `Public` endpoint — it is how a caller stops being anonymous — and it
  verifies credentials with the same `sc_auth::authenticate` the admin login
  uses, for **any** role rather than admins only. These are projected for every
  app: an app whose users cannot log in could only ever serve public-role data.
  A table cannot shadow them, since table operations are always prefixed (a table
  named `login` projects `listLogin`).

  A bad password and an unknown user get the same `401` with the same body, so
  the API does not disclose which emails are registered. A malformed login
  (missing/blank/non-string credentials) is an `Invalid` error — a bad request,
  not a failed login.

  **The provider never touches cookies.** It returns a `SessionAction`
  (`Keep`/`Start(user)`/`End`) and the transport mints and carries the token —
  the same seam `sc-server`'s admin login already used, so login/logout stay pure
  and testable without a socket. `SessionAction` accordingly moved from
  `sc-server` down to `sc-api` and is now shared: an app's `login` and the
  admin's describe the same session change and the dispatcher applies both the
  same way (`sc-server` re-exports it, so its public API is unchanged).

  **Authenticating is not authorization**, and there is a test that says so: a
  logged-in public-role user clears `whoami` but is still `403` on an admin-only
  table's rows, and the forbidden write never reaches the database. Cookie/token
  carriage is the transport's half and lands with the `sc-server` mount (the
  "serve the built React app" item).

- **Shared auth vocabulary (`sc-api::auth`).** Logging in looks the same whichever
  API is asked, so the credentials/user-summary schemas, the wire rendering of a
  user, and the body parsing now live once below every surface instead of once
  per surface: `credentials_schema`, `user_summary_schema`, `user_summary_json`,
  and `credentials`. The admin endpoint set and `sc-server`'s handlers use them,
  and so does `RestProvider`. `user_summary_json` emits only id/email/role, so a
  user row's admin-added columns and password hash cannot ride along — pinned by
  a test. The admin's checked-in `client.ts` is byte-for-byte unchanged, so this
  consolidation altered no contract.

- **An application's typed TypeScript client (design §13.1).** `sc-app` gains the
  wiring that turns a declared [`Application`] into running API machinery, which
  nothing did before: `app_tables` resolves its declared table subset against the
  catalog, `app_providers` builds one `ApiProvider` per `ApiConfig`, and
  `app_endpoints` collects their projections into the app's single `EndpointSet`.
  `app_client` generates the app's TypeScript consumer client from that set using
  the same `generate_client` the admin SPA's client comes from — an app's client
  is not special-cased machinery.

  The admin's client is a **checked-in** artifact guarded by a drift test,
  because its endpoints are compile-time constants. An app's endpoints depend on
  which tables it declares, so its client cannot be committed: it is emitted into
  the app's source tree on every build. `AppSource::with_client(path)` says where
  it lands, `emit_client` writes it (through the `FileStore`, so the store's own
  traversal sandboxing applies), and `build_application` emits it **before**
  invoking the bundler — the app's source does `import { createClient } from
  "./client"`, so a client written after bundling would be useless.
  `BuildReport::client_path` reports where it went.

  Misconfiguration fails loudly rather than yielding a quietly smaller API: a
  declared table absent from the catalog, an unknown provider name (`graphql` is
  in the design but not the MVP), and two providers projecting the same endpoint
  name (the name keys both client methods and dispatch) are each an error.

  The existing `tsc --noEmit --strict` typecheck test now covers **both** client
  surfaces — the admin set and a REST projection — sharing one runner. The app
  case needs no database (a projection is a pure function of its tables) and
  proves the projection's types are real: passing a string to `updatePosts(id:
  number)` fails with `TS2345`.

- **`ApiProvider` trait + REST provider (design §13.4).** `sc-api` now defines
  `ApiProvider` — `name()`, `mount()`, `endpoints()`, and an async
  `handle(req, cat, user)` — with transport-agnostic `ApiRequest`/`ApiResponse`
  (method, path, query, JSON), so a provider is testable without a socket and
  `sc-server` maps HTTP onto it. The MVP ships one provider; the trait exists now
  so GraphQL/gRPC/tRPC/MCP slot in without a shape change.

  `RestProvider::project(mount, tables)` projects an app's declared table subset
  into REST in the shape of the design's quality bar (Hasura/PostgREST/Supabase):
  `posts` becomes `GET/POST /api/posts` and `PUT/DELETE /api/posts/{id}`. The
  table is a **literal** path segment rather than a `{table}` parameter — an app's
  API exposes the app's own tables, so they belong in the contract and get typed
  client methods (`listPosts`, `createPosts`, …) instead of a stringly-typed
  table argument. A table with no single-column primary key gets only the
  collection endpoints: there is no way to address one of its rows, so
  `PUT`/`DELETE` would be promises the API could not keep. The projection is an
  ordinary `EndpointSet`, so an app's typed client comes from the same
  `generate_client` the admin API uses.

  **Authorization** is taken from each table's own `AccessRules` (§7): reads
  carry `MinRole(min_role_read)`, writes `MinRole(min_role_write)`, enforced
  before anything runs — an under-privileged caller gets `403`, an anonymous one
  `401`, and a rejected write never reaches the database. A table the app did not
  declare has no route at all (§13.2), not merely a failing one. Dispatch
  distinguishes an unclaimed path (`404`) from a path that exists for another
  method (`405`).

  **Custom routes** (guest code / SQL) are carried and typed but answer `501 Not
  Implemented` — the MVP stub the TODO allows, failing honestly rather than
  pretending. **Actions are not projected**: the actions registry (`sc-action`)
  is explicitly outside MVP scope, so there is nothing yet to project; the item's
  "actions" clause lands with it.

- **Row CRUD unified across API surfaces (`sc-api::rows`).** Reading and writing a
  table's rows as JSON is the substance of *both* the admin API's row endpoints
  and an app's REST API — same tables, same query layer, same wire shape — so it
  now lives once in `sc-api`, below both, as `list_rows`/`create_row`/
  `update_row`/`delete_row`. `sc-server`'s admin handlers became thin wrappers
  over it (`handlers.rs` shrank by ~130 lines) and `RestProvider` calls the same
  functions, which is what makes "an app's API and the admin API are the same
  machinery" true in the code rather than only in the docs. `convert` (JSON ↔ the
  query layer's `Value`) moved from `sc-server` to `sc-api` for the same reason,
  and `ValueType::from_basic` maps a column's `BasicType` to its wire type (1:1,
  with `BasicType::Other` travelling as text per its own contract).
  `PathSpec::match_path` matches a concrete path back to an endpoint and captures
  its params, so a provider that owns its dispatch need not re-derive path
  matching from `segments`. `sc-api` accordingly gains `sc-db`/`sc-query`/
  `sc-types`/`serde_json`/`async-trait` (+ chrono/rust_decimal/uuid for
  `convert`).

- **Code-framework build step wired (design §13.3).** An app's React source lives
  in a git repo inside one of its file stores; `sc-app`'s new `build` module runs
  the bundler over it and turns the output into the `AssetBundle` a
  `CodeFramework` serves. `AppSource` (file store + `BuildSpec`) says where the
  source is; `build_app` resolves the store through the `Catalog`, invokes the
  bundler in the spec's `source_dir`, and loads the bundle from its `output_dir`,
  returning a `BuildReport` (bundle, absolute output dir, whether the store is a
  git repo, and the bundler's stdout/stderr for build logs).
  `build_code_framework` is the one-call path from source to a `CodeFramework`
  serving the result, with the `BuildSpec` attached so the app can be rebuilt.
  Following the `serve`/`handle` split already in the crate, the catalog-free
  `run_build(spec, root)` holds the logic and is unit-testable without a
  database.

  Failure modes are all errors rather than silent no-ops (principle 5): a missing
  source dir, a bundler that cannot be launched, a non-zero exit (the error
  quotes the bundler's own diagnostics — the tail of stderr, or stdout when
  stderr is empty — not just the exit code), and a build that exits `0` but
  produces no output directory or an empty one. Build paths are sandboxed to the
  store root with the same `..` rejection the file store applies to reads and
  writes, since a `BuildSpec` is admin-supplied configuration.

  Tokio gains the `process` feature workspace-wide: the bundler is an external
  process, awaited without blocking a runtime thread.

  Note: this item's "output served by `sc-server`" is the serving half — mounting
  an app's bundle into the server's router — which is the separate Phase 9 item
  "Serve the built React app from the Saltcorn process". The build produces the
  bundle and `CodeFramework` serves it; the server mount is still to come.

- **`FileStore::local_path` (design §14.1).** New trait method returning a
  store-relative path's on-disk location, defaulting to `Ok(None)`. Nearly
  everything should stay backend-agnostic behind `read`/`write`; the build step is
  the one job that cannot, because it hands an external bundler a working
  directory. A store with no local path (an object store) therefore cannot host a
  buildable app, and `build_app` says so explicitly instead of failing obscurely.
  `LocalFileStore` implements it through the existing `resolve`, so traversal
  sandboxing is unchanged.

- **`Application` model (design §13.2).** `sc-app` now defines `Application` — an
  app's id, name, subdomain, primary `FrameworkRef` (plus optional
  `extra_frameworks`), its access subset of `tables` and `file_stores`, its
  `apis`, and a `CspPolicy`. Multiple apps share one data layer and each sees only
  its declared subset (v2's replacement for v1 schema-per-tenant multi-tenancy):
  `can_access_table`/`can_access_file_store` enforce the subset. Supporting value
  types: `AppId`, `FrameworkRef` (a serialisable name+config reference the runtime
  resolves to a `dyn Framework`), `ApiConfig` (provider + normalised mount
  sub-path), and `CspPolicy` (strict `default-src 'self'` by default, with a
  deterministic `header_value()` renderer). A builder API (`Application::new` +
  `with_table`/`with_file_store`/`with_api`/`csp`) keeps construction terse.

- **`Framework` trait + `CodeFramework` (design §13.3).** The `Framework` trait
  owns an application's primary UI: `name()`, an async `handle(req, &Catalog)`,
  and `build() -> Option<BuildSpec>`. `CodeFramework` is the MVP code-framework
  implementation — it serves a pre-built `AssetBundle` of static files (a bundled
  React/Svelte/… SPA). `AssetBundle` keys assets by relative path, derives each
  `Content-Type` from its extension, and can be loaded from a directory
  (`AssetBundle::from_dir`, which recursively walks the built output and adopts
  `index.html` as the SPA fallback). Serving applies `/` → `index.html`, falls
  back to the SPA entry point for unmatched routes (so client-side deep links
  resolve), answers only `GET` (else `405`), and returns `404` when there is no
  match and no fallback. The catalog-free serving logic lives in a public `serve`
  method that `handle` wraps, so it is unit-testable without a database. Data
  never flows through the framework — the client talks to the API providers.
  `BuildSpec` (bundler command/args + source/output dirs) is carried now; wiring
  the actual build invocation is a later Phase 9 item.

## Docs

- `docs/TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`: fold in two new GOALS additions. (1) **Error
  classification + error log** — every `Error` carries an `ErrorKind`
  (Application vs System) and is logged to a new `_sc_errors` table in the primary
  database; documented in §16 (cross-cutting error handling), added to the §9
  metadata-table catalogue (uncached runtime stream), and tied to the existing
  `error` trigger event. Noted as non-MVP, but the `ErrorKind` split lands with
  `sc-error` from the start. (2) **Tabler admin theme** — §12 now specifies the
  admin SPA is themed with Tabler (tabler.io), a Bootstrap-5 kit that layers on the
  existing react-bootstrap decision rather than replacing it.

- Operator `README.md`: install prerequisites (Rust 1.85+, PostgreSQL 13+/16,
  optional Node for the admin UI), one-time database setup (create role + database,
  local or Docker), building the server with/without the admin bundle
  (`SC_BUILD_ADMIN=1`), running `saltcorn serve` (full flag/env reference), the
  create-first-user flow (UI and API), the `/health` route, and troubleshooting.
  Covers the Phase 10 "README quickstart" item early since the boot path it
  documents now exists.

## MVP Phase 8 — Files (`sc-files`)

- **File store connected to the catalog.** The `Catalog` now owns a named registry
  of connected `FileStore` handles (not their contents — files keep no database
  row, design §9): `connect_file_store`, `file_store`/`require_file_store`, and
  `file_store_names`. `sc-catalog` gains an `sc-files` dependency for the trait
  object. The CLI can connect local stores at boot with a repeatable
  `--file-store NAME=PATH` flag (parsed by `sc_cli::extract_file_stores`, wired by
  `sc_cli::connect_file_stores`), so `saltcorn serve --file-store docs=/srv/docs`
  makes that directory browsable through the file manager.

- **File-manager API + handlers.** Four new admin endpoints (design §13.1/§14.1),
  admin-only and flowing through the same typed-JSON dispatch as the rest of the
  admin API: `listFileStores` (`GET /api/file-stores`), `browseFiles`
  (`POST /api/file-stores/{store}/browse` with `{ dir }`), `readFile`
  (`POST …/read` with `{ path }`), and `writeFile` (`POST …/write` with `{ path,
  base64 | text }`). Reads/writes carry bytes as base64 (with a UTF-8 `text`
  shortcut for editing text files) rather than special-casing binary routes, so the
  generated TypeScript client covers files like everything else. `sc-server` gains
  `base64`/`bytes` dependencies; `ui/admin/src/client.ts` regenerated.

- Tests: new `sc-server/tests/file_manager.rs` integration suite (real Postgres for
  auth) connects a `LocalFileStore` to the catalog and, as a logged-in admin,
  lists stores, writes a text file, browses root + sub-directory, reads it back
  (text + base64), round-trips arbitrary non-UTF-8 bytes via base64, and asserts
  the error cases (unknown store → 404, both `text` and `base64` → 400). A second
  test round-trips per-file xattr `FileMeta` through the catalog-resolved store
  handle and confirms the metadata never surfaces as a file in a browse listing.

- **Cross-platform xattr access.** New `sc-files::xattr` module: async `get` /
  `set` / `remove` wrappers over the `fsquirrel` crate, which stores extended
  attributes as POSIX xattrs (`user.` namespace) on Linux/macOS/FreeBSD/NetBSD/
  Android and as NTFS Alternate Data Streams on Windows — the four platforms the
  MVP targets (design §9/§14.1). `fsquirrel` was chosen over the more popular
  `xattr` crate because `xattr` has no Windows support; on Unix `fsquirrel`
  delegates to `xattr`, so the mature path is inherited. The blocking syscalls run
  on tokio's blocking pool. `LocalFileStore` now stores each file's serialised
  `FileMeta` in a single `saltcorn.meta` extended attribute on the file itself,
  replacing the earlier JSON-sidecar placeholder: no sidecar files, no reserved
  directory, and `get_meta` returns defaults when the attribute is absent. Tests:
  direct get/set/overwrite/remove round-trip, plus the existing `FileMeta`
  round-trip now exercising real xattrs.

- **`FileStore` trait + descriptors.** New `sc-files::store` module defines the
  async `FileStore` contract (technical design §14.1): `name`, `read`, `write`,
  `list`, `is_git_repo`, and `get_meta`/`set_meta`. Supporting value types: `Entry`
  (name, root-relative path, `is_dir`, size) for directory listings, and `FileMeta`
  (per-file `min_role` for path-cumulative authz plus a free-form `attributes`
  map) as the per-file metadata that replaces a database row (design §9). Paths are
  root-relative with `/` separators; drivers must reject absolute paths and `..`
  traversal.

- **Local-directory driver.** New `sc-files::local::LocalFileStore` backs a named
  store with a local directory. `new()` canonicalises and validates the root;
  `resolve()` sanitises every store-relative path (rejecting `..` escapes and the
  reserved metadata directory) before any I/O. `read`/`write` use `tokio::fs`
  (write creates parent dirs); `list` returns sorted `Entry`s with sizes and hides
  the metadata directory; `is_git_repo` detects a `.git` entry. Per-file metadata
  is round-tripped through a JSON sidecar under a hidden `.scmeta/` tree — a
  portable placeholder that the next TODO item swaps for cross-platform extended
  attributes without changing the trait surface. `get_meta` returns
  `FileMeta::default()` when unset; `set_meta` requires the target file to exist.

- Tests: write/read round-trip, sorted listing with sizes and sub-directory
  relative paths, path-traversal rejection, metadata round-trip + defaults +
  sidecar hidden from listings, `set_meta` on a missing file, and git-repo
  detection.

## MVP Phase 7 — CLI (`sc-cli`)

- `saltcorn serve` now brings the data layer up and mounts the live admin API,
  instead of the placeholder empty handler registry (design §16, §17). The
  command connects the primary Postgres database, initialises the
  [`Catalog`](sc_catalog::Catalog) from its live schema, ensures the `users`
  table exists (`sc_auth::bootstrap`), and serves the admin `EndpointSet` backed
  by `admin_handlers(catalog)`. Previously admin routes answered `501` because no
  database was wired in; they now answer for real.
- Database connection configuration (`sc-cli/src/db.rs`, `DbConfig`). A `serve`
  invocation takes either a full connection URL (`--database-url`, falling back to
  `DATABASE_URL`) or the individual parts (`--db-host` / `--db-port` / `--db-user`
  / `--db-password` / `--db-name`, falling back to the conventional `PGHOST`,
  `PGPORT`, `PGUSER`, `PGPASSWORD`, `PGDATABASE`); a URL, when present, wins
  wholesale. `DbConfig::extract` pulls just the database flags out of the argument
  list and returns the remainder, which `ServerConfig::from_args` parses as before,
  so a typo in either group still fails loudly.
- No silent failures on a bad database. The connection pool is built eagerly but
  the first real connection happens when the catalog introspects; that failure is
  surfaced with the **password-redacted** target (`DbConfig::target`) for context,
  so an unreachable or misconfigured database fails at startup with a clear message
  rather than a server that boots and then 500s.
- Operational health route. `sc-server`'s router now serves `GET /health` →
  `200 {"status":"ok"}` as a fixed, unauthenticated route outside the typed API
  (for CLI smoke tests, load balancers, and orchestrators); it takes precedence
  over the SPA fallback.
- `sc-cli` gained a library half (`src/lib.rs`) exposing `DbConfig` and
  `connect_catalog`, so the binary stays thin and integration tests drive the same
  boot path.
- Tests: `DbConfig` argument parsing / env-fallback / password redaction unit
  tests, plus an integration smoke test (`crates/sc-cli/tests/serve.rs`) that boots
  the full CLI path against a real per-test Postgres and asserts `/health` answers,
  the users table is bootstrapped, and connecting to a dead port fails with the
  redacted target and no leaked password.

## MVP Phase 6

- Admin SPA (`ui/admin`; Phase 6 "Admin SPA" subphase; design §12, §16). A
  React + TypeScript + react-bootstrap single-page app that consumes the
  **generated** typed API client — no bespoke API code, no server-rendered admin
  HTML — served by `sc-server` as a static bundle.
  - `ui/admin/`: Vite project. `src/client.ts` is the generated typed client
    (emitted from `sc_api::admin_endpoints()`); `src/api.ts` wraps
    `createClient` with a browser `fetch` that carries the session cookie
    (`credentials: same-origin`) and the CSRF double-submit header
    (`x-csrf-token` echoing the `sc_csrf` cookie). `src/App.tsx` bootstraps auth
    state via `authStatus` and gates the three top-level states — create-first-user,
    login, and the authenticated shell — routing between admin screens with a tiny
    hash router (no router dependency).
  - Screens (`src/screens/`): `FirstUser` (creates the initial admin, who is
    logged straight in), `Login`/logout, `Tables` (list + create), `TableDetail`
    (fields list + add-field, plus a row editor that creates/updates/deletes rows
    addressed by their `id`), and `Users` (list + create with an integer role).
    Styling is Bootstrap classes only — no runtime inline styles — so the strict
    `style-src 'self'` CSP holds; the bundle emits a single same-origin
    `/main.css` loaded via `<link>`.
  - Generation & wiring. `sc-api/examples/emit_admin_client.rs` is the single
    command that (re)writes `ui/admin/src/client.ts`. `vite.config.ts` pins the
    output to stable `/main.js` + `/main.css` entry points matching the server's
    `BOOTSTRAP_HTML` fallback (now also linking `/main.css`). `sc-cli/build.rs`
    runs the `ui/admin` production build **opt-in** (`SC_BUILD_ADMIN=1`) and
    records the bundle dir in `SC_ADMIN_BUNDLE_DIR`; `saltcorn serve` defaults
    `--static-dir` to it, so a bundle-built binary serves the SPA out of the box
    (Rust-only builds need no Node toolchain and fall back to the bootstrap doc).
  - Tests: `sc-api/tests/admin_client_sync.rs` asserts the committed `client.ts`
    matches the generator (no drift); `sc-server/tests/admin_spa_typecheck.rs`
    runs the SPA's strict `tsc --noEmit` over the screens + generated client
    (skips when `ui/admin` deps are not installed, mirroring the existing TS
    type-check test); `sc-server/tests/router.rs` gains coverage that a configured
    static bundle is served and unknown (client-routed) paths fall back to the
    bootstrap document. `cargo test`/`clippy --all-targets`/`fmt` clean; the SPA
    `npm run build` and a `saltcorn serve` smoke test confirm the bundle is served
    with the strict CSP.

- Admin API handlers (`sc-server`, `sc-auth`; Phase 6 "Server" subphase, second
  half; design §13.1, §7). The concrete code behind every `sc_api::admin_endpoints()`
  endpoint — the server-side half of the typed API — resolved by name against the
  `HandlerRegistry` at dispatch time:
  - `sc-server/src/handlers.rs`: `admin_handlers(catalog)` builds the registry,
    one entry per endpoint `name`, each closing over a shared `Catalog`:
    - bootstrap/auth: `authStatus` (any-user-exists + current user), `createFirstUser`
      (creates the initial admin and logs them straight in), `login`
      (`authenticate_admin` → session, else 401), `logout` (ends the session).
    - catalog: `listTables` (non-system tables), `createTable` (gives a new table a
      default database-generated identity `id` primary key so the row editor can
      address rows — the endpoint contract carries only a name), `listFields`,
      `createField` (raw `sql_type` → `TypeRef::from_sql_type`).
    - row CRUD: `listRows`/`createRow`/`updateRow`/`deleteRow`, addressing a row by
      its single-column primary key (composite keys are post-MVP), with `RETURNING`
      so the mutated row is echoed back; a missing row on update/delete is a 404.
    - users: `listUsers`, `createUser`.
    Handlers stay free of HTTP plumbing — they read `HandlerCtx` and return
    `HandlerResponse`, deferring session cookies to the dispatcher.
  - `sc-server/src/convert.rs`: `json_to_value`/`value_to_json` bridge the JSON wire
    shape to the query layer's `Value`, coercing incoming scalars to the variant a
    column's `BasicType` calls for (uuid/date/timestamp/decimal parsed, malformed →
    400) and rendering values back as natural JSON (not `Value`'s tagged form).
  - `sc-auth`: new `create_user(catalog, email, password, role)` — the admin-driven
    counterpart to `create_first_user` (caller-supplied role, no empty-table
    precondition); UUID minted in Rust, password argon2id-hashed before insert.
  - Tests: `sc-server/tests/admin_api.rs` drives the whole admin story end-to-end
    through the assembled router against a real Postgres (per-test isolated DB) with
    a cookie-carrying client (session + CSRF double-submit like a browser):
    bootstrap → create table + field → row create/list/update/delete (+404 on a gone
    row) → create/list users → logout → non-admin login rejected → wrong password
    rejected → admin re-login unlocks admin routes. `convert.rs` unit tests cover the
    JSON⇄`Value` round-trips and malformed-input rejection. `cargo test`/`clippy
    --all-targets`/`fmt` clean.

- Server infrastructure (`sc-server`, `sc-cli`; Phase 6 "Server" subphase, first
  half; design §13.1, §16). The axum server that mounts the typed API and serves
  the React admin SPA — everything except the concrete admin handlers, which are
  the second half:
  - `src/config.rs`: `ServerConfig` (bind address, static bundle dir, session
    TTL, secure-cookie flag) with `from_args` CLI parsing (no arg-parsing
    dependency); unknown/typo'd flags fail loudly.
  - `src/handler.rs`: the `HandlerRegistry` (name → async handler) that endpoint
    dispatch resolves `HandlerRef::Named` against, plus `HandlerCtx` (path/query
    params, parsed JSON body, authenticated `User`) and `HandlerResponse`.
    Handlers stay free of HTTP plumbing and manage login/logout declaratively via
    `SessionAction` (Keep/Start/End), so the dispatcher owns all cookie writes.
  - `src/router.rs`: `build_router` assembles a single axum fallback that
    dispatches **every** endpoint through one `matchit` router built from the
    `EndpointSet` values (the "routes need not be known at compile time"
    machinery — admin API and future app routes share it). Per request it
    enforces `Endpoint.auth` against the session (Public / LoggedIn / MinRole →
    401/403), parses the body, resolves the handler (absent or guest-code/SQL →
    501), and applies the session action. Non-API GETs fall through to the
    `ui/admin` bundle via `tower-http` `ServeDir`, else the minimal, inline-free
    bootstrap document (`BOOTSTRAP_HTML`; no server-rendered admin HTML). Strict
    CSP + `X-Content-Type-Options`/`X-Frame-Options`/`Referrer-Policy` applied via
    `tower-http` `set-header`.
  - `src/security.rs`: strict `Content-Security-Policy` (no `unsafe-inline`),
    the double-submit-cookie CSRF middleware (mutating requests must echo the
    `sc_csrf` cookie in `x-csrf-token`), and the shared cookie conventions
    (`SameSite=Strict`, `HttpOnly` session cookie, `Secure` behind TLS).
  - `src/serve.rs`: binds the listener and runs `axum::serve` with graceful
    shutdown on Ctrl-C / SIGTERM (tokio `signal`).
  - `sc-cli`: `saltcorn serve [--bind …] [--static-dir …] [--session-ttl-hours …]
    [--secure-cookies]` builds the config, mounts `sc_api::admin_endpoints()` with
    an (as-yet empty) handler registry, and serves.
  - Tests: `config.rs` unit tests cover flag parsing/validation;
    `tests/router.rs` drives the assembled router with `tower::oneshot` (no DB) —
    bootstrap doc + security headers, public handler dispatch, unimplemented → 501,
    wrong method → 405, admin route → 401, insufficient role → 403, CSRF
    block/allow, and a full login→session→admin-route flow. Also smoke-tested the
    real `saltcorn serve` binary over HTTP (headers, 501/401/403, clean SIGTERM
    shutdown). `cargo test`/`clippy --all-targets`/`fmt` clean workspace-wide.

- Endpoint model & typed TypeScript client (`sc-api`; Phase 6 "Endpoint model &
  typed client" subphase; design §13.1). Endpoints are now reified as **data**,
  not just handler functions, and the same machinery drives both the admin API
  and (later) application APIs:
  - `src/schema.rs`: `ValueType` (the scalar leaf set, mirroring the non-null
    `Value` families, each with a fixed TypeScript mapping) and `TypeSchema`
    (`Value` / `Struct` / `Array` / `Optional`) — a small, serializable schema
    sufficient to describe args and results and to emit TS. Includes convenience
    constructors and an `empty()`/`is_empty()` "no value" shape.
  - `src/endpoint.rs`: the `Endpoint` value (`name`, `Method`, typed `PathSpec`,
    input/output `TypeSchema`, `AuthRequirement`, `HandlerRef`) with a builder;
    `PathSpec` (literal segments + typed params, with `pattern()` for
    matchit/axum and `params()` for the generator); `Method`; `AuthRequirement`
    (`Public` / `LoggedIn` / `MinRole`, with `admin()` from `sc_auth::ROLE_ADMIN`);
    `HandlerRef` (`Named` Rust handler, or guest-code / SQL for custom routes).
    `EndpointSet` is the runtime registry (register/iterate/find, duplicate-name
    guard) — routes need not be known at compile time.
  - `src/admin.rs`: the admin API (`admin_endpoints()`) built as fixed `Endpoint`
    values through the **same** `EndpointSet` machinery — auth-status/first-user/
    login/logout, tables + fields, row CRUD, and users — establishing the
    contract the server handlers and generated client are both held to.
  - `src/typescript.rs`: `generate_client()` emits a single TS module —
    per-endpoint request/response type declarations plus an `ApiClient` interface
    and a `createClient` factory (fetch-based, path params interpolated into the
    URL, typed bodies/results). Output is deterministic and valid strict-mode TS;
    the factory is contextually typed by `ApiClient`, so a drift from a declared
    endpoint is a compile error.
  - Tests: unit tests cover path/pattern building, endpoint defaults + registry
    behaviour (incl. the duplicate-name panic), the admin contract, and the
    `TypeSchema`→TS rendering. `tests/typescript_typecheck.rs` generates the admin
    client, writes it beside a strict usage module that calls a cross-section of
    endpoints, and runs `tsc --noEmit --strict` — proving the generated client
    genuinely type-checks against the declared endpoints (verified locally against
    TypeScript 5; the test skips cleanly when no `tsc` is resolvable via `SC_TSC`
    or `crates/sc-api/node_modules`). `cargo test`/`clippy`/`fmt` clean.

- Reconcile existing code with the React-admin-UI / typed-API pivot (Phase 6
  "reconcile" subphase; design §6.3, §12, §13.1), before building the server on it:
  - Confirmed the `sc-markup` symbolic-HTML crate is dropped everywhere — no
    `crates/sc-markup` directory and no reference in the workspace manifest or any
    planning notes (the design/TODO removals were done in the prior doc pass).
  - `sc-types` (`src/catchall.rs`): reworded the module/`display` doc comments that
    described the future `FieldView` as a "symbolic markup tree". Fieldviews are now
    React (TypeScript) components; the catch-all path is documented as returning
    plain text, not HTML. No behaviour change.
  - `sc-server` / `sc-api` stub crates: updated their pre-pivot module docs to the
    new framing (axum server that mounts the typed API and serves the `ui/admin`
    React SPA; `sc-api` as the endpoint model + providers + TypeScript consumer
    generation). Confirmed all three stubs (`sc-server`/`sc-api`/`sc-app`) carry no
    server-HTML assumptions.
  - Test: added `tests/harness/tests/workspace_invariants.rs`, a DB-free guard that
    asserts no `sc-markup` crate/reference exists and that the reconciled sources
    contain no leftover "markup"/"symbolic" language — so the pivot cannot silently
    regress. `cargo test` + `cargo clippy --all-targets` clean on the touched crates.

## Design

- Chose the Rust web framework: **axum** (on hyper + tower), with `axum-extra`
  (cookie jar), `tower`/`tower-http` (`set-header` for strict CSP headers, `fs`
  `ServeDir` for the `ui/admin` bundle), and `matchit` (axum's path router, used
  directly to dispatch the runtime-registered `Endpoint` routes that aren't known
  at compile time). Picked over actix-web (separate `actix-rt` runtime) and Rocket
  (macro-driven routing) for coherence with the already-selected tokio/hyper stack
  and to carry the reified endpoint registry (design §13.1) rather than fight a
  built-in router. Pinned in the root `Cargo.toml` `[workspace.dependencies]`;
  recorded in `docs/TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md` §16 and the Phase 6 tasks in `TODO.md`
  (replacing the "axum/actix" placeholder).

- Design docs updated to follow a GOALS.md revision (`docs/GOALS.md`, uncommitted
  at the time): the admin UI pivots from server-rendered "web 1.0" HTML to a
  **React + TypeScript SPA over a typed JSON API**, and the planned `sc-markup`
  symbolic-HTML / CSP-tree + JS-extraction crate is **dropped entirely**. New
  requirements captured: APIs expose tables, actions, and custom code/SQL routes
  per permissions, and **all APIs generate TypeScript type declarations / a typed
  consumer library** (admin UI and per-app APIs alike). Added an "HTTP server
  framework" concept — endpoints reified as **Rust values** (`Endpoint` /
  `TypeSchema`) with typed args/results, supporting runtime-registered dynamic
  routes while the compile-time-known admin API reuses the same machinery.
  - `docs/TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md`: removed `sc-markup` from the crate list; rewrote
    §6.3 (fieldviews are React components), §12 (admin SPA + CSP-via-React +
    form-runtime), and §13 (new §13.1 endpoint model + typed API generation,
    renumbered subsections); updated §2, §4, §16, §17, and added open question
    §18.5 (how v1 views render CSP-safe HTML without `sc-markup`).
  - `TODO.md`: reworked Phase 6 into "typed API + React admin SPA" (with a
    reconcile-existing-code checklist at its start), extended Phase 8's API
    provider tasks (custom routes + TS consumer generation), and updated the MVP
    definition of done and the out-of-scope list.

## MVP Phase 5

- Phase 5 integration-test rollup (`sc-auth`): added `tests/auth_flow.rs`, a
  consolidated end-to-end acceptance walk against real Postgres covering the
  whole users-and-auth story in one place — create-first-user (including the
  no-user precondition and refusal of a second first-user), login failure (wrong
  password and unknown email), login success (authenticated user matches the
  created admin), logout (session resolves, then is gone), and **session
  expiry** (a short-TTL session resolves while fresh and lapses after the TTL
  elapses). Session expiry previously had only unit coverage; this adds the
  integration-level check the TODO called for. The per-feature integration tests
  (`first_user`, `login`, `role_gate`, `user_roundtrip`, `users`) remain as
  focused tests. This completes Phase 5.

- Admin-only login role gate (Phase 5, `sc-auth`): added the MVP role gate that
  restricts admin-UI login to administrators (technical design §7.3).
  - `User::meets_role(min_role)` — a general privilege check encoding the 1–100
    "lower = more privileged" convention (a user passes when `role <= min_role`),
    matching `AccessRules`; reused by later per-CRUD authz.
  - `authenticate_admin(catalog, email, password)` — authenticates then requires
    admin. Returns `Ok(Some(user))` only for valid admin credentials; wrong
    credentials *and* a valid non-admin both collapse to `Ok(None)`, so the admin
    login screen leaks nothing about which check failed (including whether a
    non-admin account with that email exists). `Err` stays reserved for
    infrastructure faults.
  - Tests: a unit test for `meets_role` across admin/non-admin gates, plus one
    integration test against real Postgres — an admin passes the gate, a wrong
    admin password is rejected, and a role-40 user with correct credentials is
    accepted by the ungated `authenticate` yet rejected by `authenticate_admin`.

- Login/logout auth + session logic (Phase 5, `sc-auth`): delivered the
  credential and session substance behind login/logout (technical design §7.2).
  The HTTP session **cookie** and **auth middleware** themselves live in the
  server and are wired in Phase 6 (`sc-server`, which does not exist yet); they
  build directly on the pieces added here.
  - `password::verify_password` completes the argon2id primitive: it verifies a
    plaintext against a stored PHC hash, returning `Ok(false)` for the ordinary
    wrong-password case and reserving `Err` for a genuinely broken stored hash.
    This finishes the "Password hashing (argon2id) + verification" TODO item too
    (the hash side shipped with create-first-user).
  - `authenticate(catalog, email, password)` looks a user up by email
    (`SELECT *`, so the returned `User` carries every admin column) and checks
    the password. Returns `Ok(None)` for every ordinary failure — unknown email,
    no stored password, wrong password — and `Err` only on infrastructure faults.
    It applies no role gate (the admin-UI gate is a separate later item).
  - `session` module: an in-memory `SessionStore` (single-process MVP; an
    `RwLock<HashMap>` keyed by an opaque 256-bit token from two v4 UUIDs).
    `login(user)` mints a token with a TTL (`with_ttl`; `DEFAULT_TTL_HOURS` = 24,
    the `Default`), `user_for(token)` returns the snapshotted user when the token
    is known and unexpired (purging expired tokens lazily), `logout(token)` ends
    a session, and `sweep_expired()` is optional housekeeping.
  - Tests: added unit tests for `verify_password` (right/wrong/empty password;
    broken-hash error) and the `SessionStore` (login→resolve, unknown token,
    logout, expiry via zero TTL, token distinctness), plus one integration test
    against real Postgres walking login → session → logout: wrong/unknown
    credentials rejected, correct login authenticates to the created admin, its
    token resolves back to the user, and logout invalidates it.

- Create-first-user flow (Phase 5, `sc-auth`): added the logic behind the
  "create first user" screen (technical design §7; GOALS acceptance story) in a
  new `first_user` module, plus the argon2id password-hashing primitive it
  depends on in a new `password` module.
  - `password::hash_password` hashes a plaintext password with argon2id
    (`Argon2::default` = argon2id v0x13, recommended params) and a fresh random
    OS-CSPRNG salt, returning a self-describing PHC string
    (`$argon2id$v=19$…`). `password::is_valid_hash` reports whether a stored
    string parses as a PHC hash. (Verification — the read side used at login —
    lands with the login item.) This required enabling the `std` feature on the
    workspace `argon2` pin so `getrandom`/`OsRng` are available for salt
    generation.
  - `any_user_exists` runs a `SELECT id FROM users LIMIT 1` so the server can
    decide between the login and create-first-user screens. `create_first_user`
    validates non-blank email/password, hashes the password, and inserts an
    **admin** (role 1) with a Rust-generated UUID — all inside a transaction that
    re-checks the empty-table precondition, so it cannot mint a second "first"
    admin (concurrent-bootstrap hardening via table locking is unnecessary for
    the single-process MVP). Returns the created `User` (email in `extra`, hash
    never carried).
  - Tests: three DB-free unit tests for hashing (PHC/argon2id shape; random-salt
    uniqueness; garbage rejected) plus one integration test against real Postgres
    walking the whole flow — no user → blank inputs rejected → first admin
    created → user now exists → second attempt refused → the stored password is a
    verifiable hash, never the plaintext.

- `User` struct (Phase 5, `sc-auth`): added the `User` value model (technical
  design §7.1) in a new `user` module — `id: Uuid`, `role: u8` (1–100), and
  `extra: BTreeMap<String, Value>` for the admin-defined columns. The model makes
  only the two guaranteed assumptions about the table's shape; everything else
  (including `email`) rides opaquely in `extra`.
  - `User::new(id, role)` validates the role range; `User::from_row(&Row)` reads
    the UUID `id` and integer `role` (validating both type and range) and
    collects every other column into `extra`. The **password hash is
    deliberately excluded** from `extra` so a `User` — the object passed through
    requests/sessions — never carries the hash into logs, session state, or API
    responses. Convenience: `is_admin()` and `get(field)`. Derives
    `Serialize`/`Deserialize` (ready for session storage) and `PartialEq`.
  - Tests: three DB-free unit tests (role validation; `from_row` extracts
    id/role/extra while dropping the hash; rejects bad role, wrong `id` type, and
    missing columns) plus one integration test against real Postgres that inserts
    a user row through the table provider and round-trips it back into a `User`,
    asserting the hash stays out of `extra`.

- Users table bootstrap (Phase 5, `sc-auth`): built the skeleton crate out with a
  `users` module holding the users-table schema and its one-time `bootstrap` into
  the `Catalog` (technical design §7.1). The table is deliberately minimal and
  driver-agnostic — it is created through the catalog like any other table, with
  no Postgres-specific defaults:
  - `id` — UUID primary key, `NOT NULL`. The non-deletable identity; the UUID is
    generated in Rust when a user is created (a later Phase 5 item), not by a
    `gen_random_uuid()` DB default, so the schema stays portable.
  - `role` — `int8`, `NOT NULL`. Integer 1–100 (1 = admin, 100 = public); the
    range is enforced in application code via the exported `role_in_range` helper
    (with `ROLE_ADMIN`/`ROLE_PUBLIC` constants) rather than a DB `CHECK`, which
    the MVP schema layer does not yet render.
  - `email` — `text`, `NOT NULL`, `UNIQUE`. The initial identifier field; the
    admin may later delete it and substitute another.
  - `password_hash` — `text`, nullable. Stores the argon2id PHC string
    (hashing/verification land in a later Phase 5 item); nullable so
    password-less accounts (e.g. future OAuth-only users) are representable.
  - Column and table names are exported as constants (`USERS_TABLE`, `COL_ID`,
    `COL_ROLE`, `COL_EMAIL`, `COL_PASSWORD_HASH`) so later flows and the server
    reference them symbolically. `bootstrap` is idempotent: it returns an
    existing `users` table unchanged (no schema reconciliation for the MVP).
  - Tests: two DB-free unit tests (schema shape; role-range bounds) plus one
    end-to-end integration test against real Postgres that drops any pre-existing
    `users` tables (the local multi-tenant v1 test template carries several),
    bootstraps, and asserts the created columns/PK both in the catalog and in a
    fresh driver introspection, including idempotency of a second `bootstrap`.

## MVP Phase 4

- Catalog: fields, tables, the `TableProvider` trait, and the `Catalog` hub
  (Phase 4, `sc-catalog`): built the skeleton crate out into the data-layer hub
  (technical design §8), completing all seven Phase 4 items. Four modules, plus
  seven DB-free unit tests and one end-to-end integration test against real
  Postgres:
  - `field.rs` — `BaseField`/`DataField`/`DataFieldKind` (§6.2), the `Attrs`
    JSON-object alias, and the id newtypes (`TableId`, `FieldId`, `DbId`,
    `FileStoreId`). `DataFieldKind` models `Plain`, `Key { target_table,
    target_field, summary_field }`, and `File { store, folder, mime_allow }` —
    the `Key`/`File` kinds are modelled now even though the MVP UI only exercises
    `Plain`. `DataField` has builder methods (`required`/`unique`/`primary_key`/
    `label`) and converts both ways with the driver's column types:
    `to_column_def()` (using `TypeRef::sql_type()` from `sc-types`) and
    `from_column()`. **Deviation from §6.2**, documented in the module: the
    design sketches these under `sc-types`, but the `Key` kind references a table
    and field by id — a catalog concept — so keeping the field types here (beside
    `Table`) avoids `sc-types` depending on catalog ids and matches the Phase 4
    TODO grouping.
  - `table.rs` — `Table` (§8.2) as pure data: id, name, database, `source`,
    fields, primary key, `access`, attributes. `Table::from_physical` maps an
    introspected `PhysicalTable` into the catalog: primary-key membership from the
    physical key, nullability → `required`, `TypeRef` from each `sql_type`, and a
    single-column foreign key → a `Key` field (no summary field, which is overlay
    metadata). `TableSource` records which provider kind backs the table
    (`Database` only for MVP — the design's serialisable stand-in for the
    behavioural provider). `AccessRules` defaults to admin-only (role 1) since an
    introspected table carries no overlay; `is_system()` flags `_sc_*` tables.
  - `provider.rs` — the `TableProvider` trait (`fields`/`query`/`write`; §8.3)
    and the trivial `DriverTableProvider` that forwards straight to a
    `DatabaseDriver`. Non-trivial and materialised providers are post-MVP, so the
    trait carries only the read/write surface.
  - `catalog.rs` — `Catalog` owns the primary `Arc<dyn DatabaseDriver>` and an
    `RwLock`-guarded cache of tables. `init` loads the cache from introspection
    (no metadata beyond `information_schema`; a freshly connected DB is usable
    with zero setup); `reload` rebuilds it (doing the DB I/O before taking the
    lock, never across an await); `get`/`require`/`tables` read it; `create_table`
    and `create_field` issue schema changes and refresh; `provider` hands out a
    `DriverTableProvider` for a table. No `id` column is invented — primary-key
    columns are the fields marked `primary_key`.
  - Integration test (`tests/catalog.rs`) walks the whole story against a real
    Postgres: create a table out-of-band and have `Catalog::init` discover it,
    `create_table` + two `create_field`s through the catalog, assert the cache and
    a fresh driver introspection agree on columns/order, then round-trip a row
    through the table's provider (INSERT then SELECT).
  Added deps (`async-trait`, `serde_json`) and dev-deps (`sc-db-postgres`,
  `sc-test-harness`, `tokio`). `cargo test -p sc-catalog` (7 unit + 1 integration),
  `cargo clippy -p sc-catalog --all-targets`, and `cargo fmt` all pass. **Phase 4
  (`sc-catalog`) is now complete.**

## MVP Phase 3

- Type system: basic types, `TypeRef`, and the catch-all display/edit path
  (Phase 3, `sc-types`): fleshed out the skeleton crate into the MVP type layer
  (technical design §6.1–6.3). Per the milestone there are **no rich types** —
  everything is basic. Three pieces, all covered by 13 unit tests:
  - `BasicType` (`basic.rs`) is the `Value`↔Postgres type mapping. `from_sql_type`
    resolves a driver `sql_type` string — the Postgres `udt_name` that
    introspection emits (`int8`, `varchar`, `timestamptz`, …) — to one of eleven
    scalar families, each mirroring a `sc_query::Value` variant. The match is
    case-insensitive and covers the common aliases (every integer width →
    `Int`, `json`/`jsonb` → `Json`, etc.). A type it doesn't recognise is never
    an error: it becomes `BasicType::Other(name)`, carrying the backend name so
    it stays usable through the catch-all path as text ("a Basic type is any DB
    type not mapped to a rich type"). `sql_type()` gives the canonical Postgres
    type for DDL (collapsing aliases, e.g. every integer → `int8`), `value_kind()`
    the expected `Value::kind`, `of_value()` the reverse mapping, and
    `accepts()`/`validate()` check a value's family (Null always accepted;
    mismatches are a descriptive `Error::Invalid`, no silent coercion).
  - `TypeRef` (`type_ref.rs`) is the "rich or basic" type a field carries
    (design's `BaseField.type_`). Only the `Basic` variant exists in the MVP, but
    it is modelled as an enum (not an alias for `BasicType`) so a future `Rich`
    variant slots in without touching field definitions. Delegates
    `sql_type`/`name`/`validate` to the wrapped basic type; `as_basic()` returns
    an `Option` for forward compatibility.
  - `catchall` (`catchall.rs`) is the full `FieldView` trait reduced to its two
    essential operations over plain strings, standing in until the symbolic
    markup tree and bundled client behaviour land post-MVP. `display(&Value)`
    renders any value as text (Null → empty, bytes → lowercase hex, timestamps →
    RFC 3339); `parse(&BasicType, &str)` converts a submitted form string back to
    a `Value` of the target type, with an empty/blank string → `Value::Null` so
    clearing a field is expressible, common boolean spellings accepted, and
    malformed input reported as `Error::Invalid`. A round-trip test asserts
    `parse(ty, display(v)) == v` for every basic value kind.
  Added the crate's runtime deps (serde/serde_json/uuid/chrono/rust_decimal).
  `cargo test -p sc-types` (13 pass), `cargo clippy -p sc-types --all-targets`,
  and `cargo fmt` all clean. **Phase 3 (`sc-types`) is now complete.**

## MVP Phase 2

- Integration tests: create table, add fields, introspect, CRUD rows (Phase 2,
  `sc-db-postgres`): added `tests/crud.rs`, a single end-to-end walk of the whole
  Phase 2 story against a real Postgres, closing the last Phase 2 item. The
  per-operation behaviour already had focused coverage across the sibling test
  files (dialect rendering, `query`, `introspect`, `apply_schema`, transactions),
  but **UPDATE and DELETE had only ever been rendered, never executed** against a
  live database — this test exercises them for real. It creates a table with an
  explicit key (no invented `id`), adds two fields via `AddColumn` (one with a
  `DEFAULT false`, one nullable), introspects and asserts the resulting columns/
  order/primary key/nullability, then runs the full CRUD cycle through the
  driver: INSERT two rows (RETURNING their ids), SELECT them back and check the
  applied column default and NULL, UPDATE one row's `done`/`priority` and confirm
  only that row changed, DELETE the other (RETURNING its id) and confirm one row
  remains, and finally re-introspect to confirm the table still reflects the
  added fields. Fixed one lint along the way: a non-`#[test]` helper using
  `.expect()` isn't covered by clippy's test exemption, so `task_table` now
  returns `Result` and uses `ok_or_else` like the other files' helpers. `cargo
  fmt --check`, `cargo clippy -p sc-db-postgres --all-targets -- -D warnings`,
  and `cargo test -p sc-db-postgres` (12 unit + 7 integration across five files)
  all pass. **Phase 2 (`sc-db`, `sc-db-postgres`) is now complete.**
- Transactions: `begin()` → commit/rollback, and the full `DatabaseDriver`
  trait impl (Phase 2, `sc-db-postgres`): added `PgDriver::begin`, returning a
  `Box<dyn Transaction>` that scopes queries and schema changes to one Postgres
  transaction (technical design §5). New `transaction.rs` defines
  `PgTransaction`: rather than borrow a `tokio_postgres::Transaction` from the
  pooled client (which would make the handle a self-referential struct), it
  **owns** its pooled connection and drives `BEGIN`/`COMMIT`/`ROLLBACK`
  directly. `commit`/`rollback` consume the boxed handle and take the connection
  out (leaving `None`), so the transaction is finalised exactly once and a
  `Drop` after an explicit finish is a no-op; a handle dropped **without**
  finishing rolls back (spawned on the current runtime, or a short-lived thread
  if none) so the connection returns to the pool clean rather than
  mid-transaction. To share the exact execution path between the driver and a
  transaction — both hold a `deadpool` connection that derefs to a
  `tokio_postgres::Client` — the query/DDL logic moved into a new `exec.rs`
  (`run_query`/`run_ddl`), and `PgDriver::query`/`apply_schema` now call it.
  With `begin` in place, `PgDriver` now implements the whole `sc-db`
  `DatabaseDriver` trait (introspect, query, apply_schema, begin, capabilities,
  dialect); the trait methods delegate to the inherent methods (which shadow
  them in resolution, so there is no recursion), so the crate keeps its
  ergonomic inherent API while the catalog can hold an `Arc<dyn DatabaseDriver>`.
  Added `async-trait` and `tokio` as regular dependencies (the transaction's
  drop-rollback needs the tokio runtime handle). Three integration tests: commit
  makes a table+row visible to a separate pooled connection while an uncommitted
  one stays invisible (DDL is transactional in Postgres), and rollback discards
  a table that its own transaction could see; an abandoned handle (dropped
  without finishing) rolls back its table while leaving the pool usable; and the
  driver is exercised end-to-end through an `Arc<dyn DatabaseDriver>` trait
  object (capabilities, dialect render, create/insert/begin/commit/select).
  `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`,
  and `cargo test -p sc-db-postgres` (12 unit + 6 integration across four files)
  all pass. This completes the Postgres `DatabaseDriver` implementation; only the
  Phase 2 integration-test umbrella item remains.
- `apply_schema`: create/drop table, add/drop column (Phase 2,
  `sc-db-postgres`): added `PgDriver::apply_schema`, which renders one
  `SchemaChange` to a single Postgres DDL statement and runs it over the
  simple-query protocol (technical design §5). New `ddl.rs` does the rendering:
  table and column *names* are quoted through `PgDialect`, while a `ColumnDef`'s
  `sql_type` and `default` are treated as structural SQL fragments from trusted
  schema definitions (`int8`, `varchar(255)`, `now()`, …) and pass through
  verbatim — DDL cannot be parameterised in any case. The goals' cardinal rule
  is honoured by omission: `CreateTable` emits **exactly** the columns given and
  **never** invents an `id` column; a primary key is declared only when asked
  for and may be composite (`PRIMARY KEY ("a", "b")`). A column renders as
  `"name" type [NOT NULL] [DEFAULT …] [UNIQUE]`; `DropTable`/`DropColumn` honour
  `if_exists`. Rendering validates that every primary-key column is one of the
  declared columns, returning `Error::invalid` before Postgres would. Wired as
  an inherent `async fn apply_schema(&self)` on `PgDriver`. Five unit tests
  cover the rendered SQL for each variant (composite-PK create asserting no
  `"id"` leaks in, column default, PK-column-not-declared rejection, drop
  table/column with and without `if_exists`); a new integration test
  (`tests/apply_schema.rs`) drives the whole lifecycle against real Postgres and
  verifies each step by introspecting — create a table with a composite PK and
  confirm the columns are exactly those declared (no invented `id`) with correct
  order/nullability/PK, `AddColumn` with a default, `DropColumn`, then
  `DropTable` — and checks that dropping a missing column/table errors without
  `if_exists` and succeeds with it. `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy -p
  sc-db-postgres --all-targets -- -D warnings`, and `cargo test -p
  sc-db-postgres` (12 unit + 3 integration) all pass.
- `introspect()` → `PhysicalTable` (Phase 2, `sc-db-postgres`): added
  `PgDriver::introspect`, which reads the connected database's live schema into
  `sc-db` `PhysicalTable`s (technical design §5). Per the goals there is **no
  discovery/registration step** — every user base table reachable through the
  connection is returned. New `introspect.rs` runs four parameterless catalog
  queries on a pooled connection and collates them by `(schema, table)`: the
  table list and the columns come from `information_schema` (using `udt_name`
  for the backend's own type name — `int8`/`text`/`timestamptz`/`bool` — so it
  round-trips with what `apply_schema` will emit, plus `is_nullable` and
  `column_default`); the primary keys and foreign keys come from `pg_catalog`
  instead, because the goals require **composite primary keys** and **foreign
  keys to non-primary-key columns**, and only the catalog's `conkey`/`confkey`
  attribute-number arrays give the key columns in the correct order (the
  `information_schema` constraint views lose that ordering). `unnest(…) WITH
  ORDINALITY` preserves per-key column order, and the multi-array
  `unnest(conkey, confkey)` form pairs each local FK column with the referenced
  column it points at. System schemas (`pg_catalog`, `information_schema`) are
  excluded. Wired as an inherent `async fn introspect(&self)` on `PgDriver`
  (the full `DatabaseDriver` trait impl still follows once transactions land).
  Integration test (`tests/introspect.rs`) creates `sc_it_org` (single-column PK
  + a UNIQUE non-key `code`) and `sc_it_member` (composite PK `(org, user_id)`,
  a `bool NOT NULL DEFAULT true` column, and two FKs — one to the PK, one to the
  non-PK `code`), then asserts the introspected columns/types/nullability, the
  captured default, the ordered composite PK, and both foreign keys (looked up
  by local column so ordering is irrelevant) — including the FK that targets a
  non-primary-key column. The test asserts on its own distinctively named tables
  rather than the exact table set, so it holds regardless of what else the
  database contains (which is exactly the "no discovery step" property).
  `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy -p sc-db-postgres --all-targets -- -D
  warnings`, and `cargo test -p sc-db-postgres` (7 unit + 2 integration) all
  pass.
- Postgres connection + pooling; run `Statement` → `RowStream` (Phase 2,
  `sc-db-postgres`): landed `PgDriver`, the first working piece of the Postgres
  backend (technical design §5). It wraps a `deadpool-postgres` pool
  (`connect(url)` / `from_config(&Config)` / `from_pool(pool)`, `max_size(8)`,
  fast recycling) and exposes `query(&Statement) -> Result<RowStream>`: render
  the statement with `PgDialect`, check out a pooled connection, bind the ordered
  literals, run it, and decode the result rows into `sc-db` `Row`s wrapped in a
  `RowStream`. Any statement kind works — an INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE with
  `RETURNING` yields its rows, without `RETURNING` yields an empty stream. Also
  added `capabilities()` advertising Postgres's full basic set
  (`row_level_security`/`composite_pk`/`listen_notify`/`returning`). Rows are
  materialised into the stream for the MVP; the `RowStream` signature keeps a
  later switch to server-side streaming invisible to callers. New `value.rs`
  holds the wire-level `Value` ⇆ Postgres mapping (basic types only this
  milestone): `PgParam(&Value)` implements tokio-postgres' `ToSql` by dispatching
  each variant to the underlying type's own `to_sql_checked` (so a non-null bind
  is type-checked against its column exactly as that Rust type would be), with
  `Value::Null` short-circuiting to a *typeless* SQL `NULL` — unlike
  `Option::<T>::None`, which only accepts one column type, so a NULL bind lands
  correctly in a text/bool/etc. column. `decode()` reads a result column by its
  Postgres `Type` (bool, int2/4/8, float4/8, text/varchar/bpchar/name, bytea,
  uuid, json/jsonb, date, time, timestamp(tz), numeric) into the matching
  `Value`, maps SQL `NULL` to `Value::Null`, and returns an explicit error for an
  unmapped type rather than silently coercing. Pinned `bytes` in the workspace
  (named only in the `ToSql` signatures) and pulled `uuid`/`chrono`/`rust_decimal`
  /`serde_json` into the crate for the mapping. Test harness: added an optional,
  backward-compatible `SC_TEST_TEMPLATE` env override to `sc-test-harness` so a
  dev box whose `template1` has a glibc collation-version mismatch (which makes
  `CREATE DATABASE` fail — the known local issue noted in Phase 0) can clone from
  a database it owns; CI leaves it unset, so behaviour there is unchanged. New
  integration test (`tests/query.rs`) drives the whole path against a real
  Postgres via the harness: create a table, `INSERT … RETURNING` two rows
  (including a NULL bind into a text column and both boolean values), `SELECT`
  them back ordered and assert every decoded `Value` (int/text/bool/NULL), then
  fire a parameterised `WHERE` carrying a `'; DROP TABLE item; --` payload and
  confirm it matches no row **and** leaves the table intact — injection safety
  proven end to end, not just in rendering. `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy
  --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`, and `cargo test -p sc-db-postgres`
  (7 unit + 1 integration) all pass, as do the harness's own 3 tests. Next Phase
  2 items — `introspect`, `apply_schema`, transactions — build on this pool.
- Postgres `SqlDialect` implementation (Phase 2, `sc-db-postgres`): added
  `PgDialect`, the concrete Postgres dialect that renders a `sc-query`
  `Statement` to `(sql, binds)` (technical design §4/§5). Because the
  `SqlDialect` trait already carries a shared default `render` that walks the AST
  and emits clause order, operator spelling, `RETURNING`, `CASE`, `IN`, and the
  JSON access path (`->`) — all of which are already valid Postgres — the
  concrete dialect supplies only the two things that genuinely differ between
  backends: identifier **quoting** (double quotes, embedded quotes doubled, so a
  reserved word or odd identifier is safe and case-exact) and **placeholders**
  (the numbered `$n` form tokio-postgres binds positionally). `PgDialect` is a
  zero-sized, `Copy` struct with a `const new()`, so `dialect()` on the future
  driver can hand back a reference cheaply. Injection-safety is inherited from
  the query layer: every `Value` leaves through the shared renderer's bind
  vector, never interpolated. Seven unit tests exercise quoting (plain, reserved
  word, embedded quote), `$n` placeholders, and full-statement rendering for
  each kind — a joined/filtered/ordered `SELECT` with `LIMIT`/`OFFSET`, an
  `INSERT … RETURNING`, `UPDATE`/`DELETE` (the latter with `RETURNING`), JSON
  arrow access with parameterised path steps, and a SQL-injection payload that
  stays a single bind and never appears in the SQL text. Connection/pooling,
  introspection, `apply_schema`, and transactions are the following Phase 2
  items. `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy -p sc-db-postgres --all-targets -- -D
  warnings`, and `cargo test -p sc-db-postgres` (7 tests) all pass.
- `DatabaseDriver` trait + `DbCapabilities` (Phase 2, `sc-db`): landed the
  database-layer *contract* — the async trait one connected database implements,
  plus every data type it exchanges with the rest of the system (technical
  design §5). The concrete Postgres backend (connection/pooling, real
  `introspect`, `apply_schema`, transactions) is deferred to the remaining
  Phase 2 items in `sc-db-postgres`; this crate owns only the trait and its type
  surface, keeping the layering clean. Five new modules. `driver.rs` defines
  `DatabaseDriver: Send + Sync` (async via `async_trait`) with the six methods
  from the design — `introspect() -> Vec<PhysicalTable>`, `query(&Statement) ->
  RowStream`, `apply_schema(&SchemaChange)`, `begin() -> Box<dyn Transaction>`,
  `capabilities()`, and `dialect() -> &dyn SqlDialect` — object-safe so the
  catalog can hold it behind `Arc<dyn DatabaseDriver>` (§8). The `Transaction`
  trait carries in-transaction `query`/`apply_schema` and consumes itself on
  `commit`/`rollback` (`self: Box<Self>`) so a transaction is finalised exactly
  once. `capabilities.rs` defines `DbCapabilities` (`row_level_security`,
  `composite_pk`, `listen_notify`, `returning`) with a `none()` constructor so
  adding a future flag never silently flips existing drivers to "supported"; the
  authz layer (§7) and message bus (§16) branch on these rather than assuming a
  Postgres feature set. `schema.rs` models both directions of schema work:
  `PhysicalTable`/`Column`/`ForeignKey` as the *result* of introspection, and a
  `SchemaChange` enum (`CreateTable`/`DropTable`/`AddColumn`/`DropColumn`, serde
  tagged on `op`) with a `ColumnDef` builder as the *input* to `apply_schema`.
  Two design invariants are encoded structurally: primary keys and FK column
  lists are always `Vec<String>` (composite PKs and FKs to non-PK columns are
  first-class, as the goals require), and `CreateTable` carries an explicit
  (possibly empty) primary key so creating a table never invents an `id` column.
  `row.rs` defines `Row` (named `Value`s addressable by name or index, sharing
  one `Arc`'d column-name vector across a whole result set so streaming a large
  set doesn't re-allocate the column list per row) and `RowStream`, an async
  `futures::Stream` of `Result<Row>` that maps straight onto tokio-postgres row
  streaming, with `from_rows`/`empty`/`try_collect` helpers. Added `futures` to
  the workspace deps (only the lightweight `Stream` trait + combinators) and
  `serde` derives on the schema descriptors so introspection output stays
  inspectable/reconstructable by code adapters. Tests: an in-memory `MockDriver`
  (+`MockTx`) implementing the full trait proves it is object-safe and usable
  end to end — held as `Arc<dyn DatabaseDriver>`, it advertises capabilities,
  starts with an empty introspect (no discovery step), applies a `CreateTable`
  with a composite PK and no id column, reflects it in a later introspect,
  streams named/positional rows out of `query`, renders a statement through
  `dialect()`, and opens/commits a transaction; plus a row length-mismatch
  rejection and a serde round-trip of `PhysicalTable`/`SchemaChange`. `cargo fmt
  --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo test -p sc-db`
  (3 tests), and `cargo build --workspace` all pass. Completes the
  `DatabaseDriver` trait and `DbCapabilities` items of Phase 2.

## MVP Phase 1

- `SqlDialect` trait + rendering (Phase 1, `sc-query`): completed the query
  layer with the trait that turns a `Statement` into `(sql, binds)` (technical
  design §4). Per the design, rendering is a trait so each dialect owns only the
  bits that actually differ — identifier quoting and bind-placeholder syntax —
  exposed as two required hooks (`quote_ident`, `placeholder`); everything else
  (clause order, operator spelling, `RETURNING`/`CASE`/`IN`/JSON structure) is a
  shared default `render` implementation walking the AST via a private
  `Renderer`. Kept the concrete Postgres dialect out of this crate (that's Phase
  2 / `sc-db-postgres`); the trait and shared walk live in `sc-query`, with a
  test-only `TestDialect` (double-quoted idents, `$n` placeholders) exercising
  it. The cardinal invariant is enforced structurally: the **only** path a
  `Value` takes into the SQL string is through `Renderer::bind`, which appends to
  the bind vector and emits a placeholder — so literals can never be
  interpolated. JSON path steps are parameterised too (`data -> $1 -> $2` with
  the field name/index as binds), so even a field name can't break out into SQL;
  and `LIMIT`/`OFFSET` render as binds. `render` validates and returns
  `Error::query` for degenerate statements (empty projection, no INSERT
  columns/rows, an INSERT row whose arity doesn't match its columns, empty
  UPDATE assignments). Six unit tests cover each statement kind (joined/filtered/
  ordered `Select` with `LIMIT`/`OFFSET`, multi-row `INSERT … RETURNING`,
  `UPDATE`/`DELETE`), the JSON/`IN`/`CASE` shapes, a SQL-injection payload that
  stays a single bind and never appears in the SQL text, and the empty-projection
  error path. `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`,
  and `cargo test -p sc-query` (17 tests) all pass; `cargo build --workspace` is
  green. Phase 1 (`sc-query`) is now complete.
- `Statement` AST + `Expr` (Phase 1, `sc-query`): landed the enum-based query
  AST that `Value` feeds into (technical design §4). Two new modules. `expr.rs`
  defines `Expr` with the nine MVP variants — `Col(ColRef)`, `Lit(Value)`
  (always parameterised on render, never interpolated), `Param(usize)`,
  `Binary{op,l,r}`, `Unary{op,e}`, `Func{name,args}`, `In{e,set}`,
  `Json{target,path}` (JSON is first-class, not an add-on), and
  `Case{operand,arms,else_result}` — plus supporting `ColRef` (optional table
  qualifier, so composite keys and FKs to non-primary-key columns are
  expressible), `BinOp`, `UnOp`, `JsonStep`, `InSet` (list or subquery), and
  `CaseArm`. `statement.rs` defines `Statement` (`Select`/`Insert`/`Update`/
  `Delete`, each boxed so the enum stays small) with `Select` carrying
  from/columns/joins/filter/group/having/order/limit/offset, and the supporting
  `Source` (table or aliased subquery), `Projection` (wildcard or `expr AS
  alias`), `Join`/`JoinKind` (`on` is a general `Option<Expr>`), `OrderBy`/
  `OrderDir`/`Nulls`, `Insert` (multi-row + `RETURNING`), `Update`/`Assignment`,
  and `Delete`. Every type is `serde`-round-trippable (enums externally tagged,
  `snake_case`) so a statement stays inspectable and reconstructable by code
  adapters. Added ergonomic constructors/combinators (`Expr::col/qcol/lit/eq/
  and/or`, `Select::from(..).filter(..).join(..).limit(..)`, `Source::table`,
  `Projection::expr`, `Delete::from`, `Insert::row`, `From<Select> for
  Statement`, etc.) to keep call sites readable without a fluent builder layer.
  Note: the blanket `From<T: Into<Value>> for Expr` was intentionally avoided —
  it collides with the std reflexive `From<T> for T` under coherence — in favour
  of concrete `From<Value>`/`From<ColRef>` impls. Four new unit tests build a
  joined/filtered/ordered `Select`, the three mutating statements with
  `RETURNING`/filters, the JSON/`IN`/`CASE` expression shapes, and a full serde
  round-trip. `cargo fmt --check`, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`,
  and `cargo test -p sc-query` all pass clean; `cargo build --workspace` is
  green. (Rendering to SQL — the `SqlDialect` trait and its parameterisation
  tests — remains the next Phase 1 item.)
- `Value` enum (Phase 1, `sc-query`): landed the universal row value type that
  the rest of the query AST is built on (technical design §4). A single
  `serde`-serializable enum with the twelve MVP variants — `Null`, `Bool`,
  `Int(i64)`, `Float(f64)`, `Text(String)`, `Bytes(Vec<u8>)`,
  `Json(serde_json::Value)`, `Uuid`, `Date(NaiveDate)`, `Time(NaiveTime)`,
  `Timestamp(DateTime<Utc>)` (maps to `timestamptz`), and `Decimal` (maps to
  `numeric`). JSON is a first-class variant per the design, not an add-on. The
  enum is `PartialEq` but deliberately not `Eq`/`Hash` because `f64` has no
  total order/equality. Added ergonomic `From` conversions for each backing type
  (plus `&str`, `i32`, and `Option<T> → Null`) and small accessors
  (`is_null`, `kind`, `as_text`/`as_int`/`as_bool`). Serialised with an
  externally-tagged `{ "type", "value" }` shape so a statement stays inspectable
  and reconstructable by code adapters. Pulled `serde`, `serde_json`, `uuid`,
  `chrono`, and `rust_decimal` (all already pinned in `[workspace.dependencies]`)
  into `sc-query`. Unit tests cover the `From` conversions, `Option`→`Null`
  mapping, accessors, the stable serde tag shape, and JSON round-trips of every
  rich variant (uuid/date/time/timestamp/decimal). `cargo fmt --check` and
  `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` both pass clean on the crate.

## MVP Phase 0

- Integration-test harness (Phase 0): added a shared helper crate
  `sc-test-harness` under `tests/harness` (workspace member, `publish = false`)
  implementing the "real Postgres, reset per test" strategy from the technical
  design (principle 4). `TestDb::new()` creates a **freshly named, empty
  database per test** (`sc_test_<uuid>`) from a maintenance connection and hands
  back a `deadpool-postgres` pool bound to it; `Drop` closes the pool and drops
  the database (`DROP DATABASE … WITH (FORCE)` on a dedicated teardown thread,
  since `Drop` is sync and may run inside the tokio runtime). Full isolation —
  including DDL and `information_schema` introspection — so tests run in
  parallel with no shared state. The base connection is read from `DATABASE_URL`
  (the same variable CI sets) with a local default. Three integration tests
  assert a usable database (DDL + CRUD), cross-database isolation, and that the
  database is gone after teardown; all pass against a clean `postgres:16`. Note:
  running locally needs a reachable Postgres with `CREATEDB` rights — set
  `DATABASE_URL` accordingly (a fresh `postgres:16`, e.g. via Docker, avoids the
  glibc collation-version mismatch that a system Postgres upgraded across glibc
  releases will otherwise raise on `CREATE DATABASE`).

- Decided the async runtime and pinned core deps (Phase 0). The workspace runs
  on the **tokio** multi-threaded runtime; every async trait in the design is
  expressed with `async_trait` over it. Chose **tokio-postgres +
  deadpool-postgres** as the Postgres driver rather than sqlx — `sc-query`
  already renders a `Statement` into `(sql, binds)`, so sqlx's compile-time
  query macros add no value, while tokio-postgres gives native `$n` binding and
  row streaming that map directly onto the design's `RowStream`, keeping the
  dependency graph minimal. Added a single `[workspace.dependencies]` block to
  the root `Cargo.toml` pinning `tokio`, `async-trait`, `serde`/`serde_json`,
  `uuid`, `argon2` (stable 0.5; 0.6 is only an RC), `tokio-postgres` (with the
  `uuid`/`chrono`/`serde_json` `ToSql`/`FromSql` features), `deadpool-postgres`,
  and `chrono`/`rust_decimal` (temporal + decimal backing for the `Value` enum).
  Wired tokio into the `saltcorn` binary (`#[tokio::main] async fn main`) so the
  runtime decision is exercised, not just declared. New `core_deps` integration
  test in `sc-cli` asserts each core dep is pinned once in
  `[workspace.dependencies]`, that the driver is tokio-postgres and not sqlx,
  and drives a `#[tokio::test]` end to end.

- Repo hygiene (Phase 0): added `rustfmt.toml` (stable-only options, edition
  pinned to 2024, `max_width = 100`, Unix newlines) so `cargo fmt --check` on a
  stable toolchain matches the workspace. Added a GitHub Actions CI skeleton
  (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) with three jobs — `fmt` (`cargo fmt --check`),
  `clippy` (`cargo clippy --all-targets -D warnings`), and `test` (`cargo test`
  against a real Postgres 16 service, ready for the integration harness) — with
  build caching and superseded-run cancellation. Extended `.gitignore` to cover
  Node/frontend artifacts (`node_modules/`, `dist/`, `build/`, bundler debug
  logs, `.env*`, `.DS_Store`) for the React apps served by `sc-server`. Added a
  `repo_hygiene` integration test in `sc-cli` that walks up to the workspace
  root and asserts these artifacts exist and declare their required gates
  (`clippy.toml` was already present from the `sc-error` work).
- Implemented `sc-error` (Phase 0, layer 0): a single `Error` enum with coarse,
  location-based variants (`NotFound`, `Invalid`, `Config`, `Database`, `Query`,
  `Auth`, `File`, `Serde`, `Internal`, and a `Context` variant that preserves a
  wrapped source), a `Result<T, E = Error>` alias, and per-variant constructors.
  Added a `Context` extension trait (`.context()` / `.with_context()`) for both
  `Result` and `Option` that keeps the underlying error as a `std::error::Error`
  source chain, plus `bail!` / `ensure!` macros and a `From<std::io::Error>`
  conversion. Enforced principle 5 mechanically: `[workspace.lints.clippy]` denies
  `unwrap_used`/`expect_used`, each member crate opts in via `[lints] workspace =
  true`, and `clippy.toml` exempts test code. Unit + doc tests cover the source
  chain, lazy `with_context`, macros, and `Send + Sync`.
- Created the Cargo workspace with the MVP crate skeletons (Phase 0). Root
  `Cargo.toml` defines shared package metadata (`workspace.package`) and internal
  path dependencies (`workspace.dependencies`) so the strict layering from the
  technical design (§2) is expressed in the dependency graph. Member crates:
  `sc-error`, `sc-query`, `sc-db`, `sc-db-postgres`, `sc-types`, `sc-catalog`,
  `sc-auth`, `sc-files`, `sc-api`, `sc-app`, `sc-server` (libs) and `sc-cli`
  (binary, built as `saltcorn`). Each crate is a compiling stub with a smoke test;
  `cargo build --workspace` and `cargo test --workspace` pass.
